Next release - in progress
--------------------------

19 Oct 26  A REMOTE SELECT THAT THE OTHER SERVER ANSWERS WITH AN ERROR NO LONGER STOPS THE PROGRAM.

             What        When a program makes a list of the records of a file on another SD server (SDNet) and the server answers either request of
                        the SELECT - the SELECT itself or the read of the list - with an error of the kind that takes an ON ERROR clause, the program
                        was stopped with "READLIST generated an abort event" (or "SELECT generated an abort event"), which an ON ERROR clause cannot
                        trap. It now gets the same error as every other remote file statement: message 11500 with the server's own words, or "status 0"
                        when the server sent none. The client class (the BASIC class that talks to an SD server) lets an object that has been made
                        quiet carry on after such a reply, as it already did for a READ, a WRITE and a DELETE; the remote file handler is the one
                        user that makes its object quiet.
             Why it      A server that was broken, or that was not to be trusted, could end a client's program at will, and the reason for a refusal never
             matters    reached the person.
             Does not    An object that is not quiet - the default, and every program that uses the client class directly - is stopped exactly as
                        before. It does not change which requests a server refuses or what it says. It was found by a test written for the case; no real
                        server was seen to send this reply.

19 Oct 26  A REMOTE FILE CHANGE THAT THE OTHER SERVER REFUSES NOW SHOWS WHY.

             What        When a program changes a file on another SD server (SDNet) and that server's answer to the change is an error, the error message
                        now carries the server's own words. Before, it said only "status 0", whatever the reason. The case that matters is a login on
                        the other server that has the files route but not the right to change files (sdfileswrite): the person at the client now reads
                        "This login cannot change or lock files here: ask the administrator for files-write-on (request 16)". Message 11521, that
                        sentence, was 175 characters and the client shows 100, so it is now 98. The same holds for any other error the server
                        answers with a text of that kind, such as MAXRLOCK on a lock request or a CLEARFILE of a file the server cannot write. An
                        error the server answers with no text still shows "status <n>", as before. The server also empties its error text at the start of every
                        request now: it used to send the text of its last refusal again with an unrelated error (a write with an id that is too long
                        answered "Invalid call name" after a call with no name), which would have been shown to the person.
             Why it      "Status 0" does not say that the fix is a word the administrator of the other machine has to give; the sentence does.
             matters
             Does not    It does not change which requests are refused, or who may be given the right. A program that traps the error with ON ERROR
                        still sees STATUS() 7000, as before: the text is in the message that is shown when the program does not trap it.

19 Oct 26  MODIFY.ACCOUNT FILES-OFF AND FILES-ON NOW DEAL WITH A LEFTOVER WRITE-GROUP MEMBERSHIP, ITS LINE IS IN LOWER CASE, AND A REFUSED LOGIN NAMES BOTH ROUTES.

             What        (1) Someone could be in the Linux group sdfileswrite and not in sdfiles - a removal that stopped half way, or a change made by
                        hand. That left them inert until the next files-on, which added sdfiles and made the leftover into the right to change files on
                        this server without the administrator saying so. files-off now removes the write group first and then sdfiles, reading each
                        back, and does the work for a person who is in only one of them (it used to answer "already had that access" and leave the
                        membership); if the second removal fails the person is left in sdfiles alone - read access, never write access without read -
                        and the command says the change failed AND says what did happen: it prints "Account <name> has files-write no" and writes
                        the audit line "modify.account files-write-off account=<name>" for the removal that worked, then reports the failure. (The
                        audit line for files-off or files-on itself is written only when the whole command worked.) files-on removes a leftover write
                        membership before it adds sdfiles, and stops with the same failure message if it cannot; if the write membership went and the
                        add of sdfiles then fails, the person is left in neither group and the command says so in the same way. When the write
                        group went, a line "Account <name> has files-write no" comes before the usual one. (2) The usual line is now "Account <name> has files yes" (or no): it said "FILES" in capitals. (3) A login
                        that is in neither sdapi nor sdfiles is refused as before, but the audit reason now says "not a member of sdapi or sdfiles"; it
                        named only sdapi.
             Why it      A permission the administrator never gave must not come back at the next, unrelated command, and the audit line should
             matters    send the administrator to the right group.
             Does not    The wording of the other lines of modify.account is unchanged. files-write-on and files-write-off do what they did. The real
                        group changes still need root; they were run against a scratch group file and a stand-in for the helper, and on a real machine
                        only by the witness scripts.

19 Oct 26  THE API'S FILE LOCK AND UNLOCK (REQUESTS 32 AND 33) ACT ON THE FILE THEY ARE ASKED ABOUT.

             What        A client that locked or unlocked a whole file through the API (the wire requests filelock and fileunlock) locked or
                        unlocked whichever file the server had opened last, not the one the request named. It was found by opening three files, locking
                        the first, and asking from a second session which files were locked. They now act on the file named. The client library
                        has no function for these two requests yet, so the programs that could meet this are ones that speak the protocol directly.
             Why it      A lock on the wrong file protects nothing and blocks someone else's work.
             matters
             Does not    It does not add a client function, and it does not change record locks.

19 Oct 26  SDNET: A REPLY THAT CANNOT BE TRUE IS REFUSED AT ONCE, AND THE TWO WAITS ARE SETTINGS (NETWAIT, NETREPLY).

             What        (1) A remote file's client now checks the length a reply announces before it reads the body. A server that announces less than
                        its own ten-byte header, or more than the largest string SD holds, is treated as a lost connection immediately; before, the
                        client read for as long as the server sent, and waited 30 seconds for a body that was never coming. The ordinary client class
                        is unchanged except for lengths below ten. (2) The seconds to wait for a connection (5) and for each reply (30) were fixed in
                        the programs. They are the new sd.conf settings NETWAIT and NETREPLY, 1 to 3600 seconds, with those defaults, read by
                        set.server ... connect, set.netlogin and every remote file; CONFIG lists them. A zero is refused, so SD does not start with
                        NETWAIT=0 (openQM reads that as wait for ever). A CLEARFILE waits the longer of five minutes and NETREPLY.
                        (3) A value of 65541 (65536 + 5) for NETWAIT or NETREPLY in sd.conf was read into a 16 bit field, came out as 5 and passed the
                        1 to 3600 test, so SD started with a 5 second wait instead of refusing the value; the fields are 32 bit now and SD does not start.
                        (PWDELAY and DHCACHE are read the same way and have the same fault; they are not changed here.)
             Why it      A compromised or broken server can no longer hold a client for a long time with a bad length once the login is over, and an
             matters    administrator who has a slow link or a slow server can say how long is too long.
             Does not    It does not cap the size of a legitimate record, and the setting is per computer, not per server definition. NETWAIT and
                        NETREPLY have nothing to do with the QMClient programs. The ceiling is set after the login, not during it: the login exchange
                        itself still has no limit on the length a reply announces (only the test for a length under ten bytes), so a pinned server
                        that is hostile can still announce a large length in a login reply and send slowly, for NETREPLY seconds a read.
                        A server that completes the TLS handshake and then sends nothing at all, not even its acknowledgement byte, is NOT given up
                        on after NETREPLY seconds: the client waits until the server sends something or closes the connection. (The session tickets
                        that TLS 1.3 sends after the handshake end the first read early, and the read that follows has no time limit. Against the
                        test server, which closes a silent connection after 5 seconds, a client set to wait 3 waited 5; with the tickets turned off
                        it waited 3.) A server that sends the acknowledgement and then goes silent IS given up on after NETREPLY seconds, as
                        intended (3.0 seconds measured with NETREPLY=3). The first case is measured and reported to the owner, and not fixed here.

19 Oct 26  A GUIDE, A GENERATED REFERENCE AND A CLAUDE CODE SKILL FOR AI AGENTS THAT WORK ON AN SD SYSTEM ARE IN THE SOURCE (agent-pack/).

             What        An AI agent asked to write SD BASIC meets a language that looks like OpenQM, UniVerse or D3 and is not, and guesses names SD
                        does not have. The source now holds, under sdb_ai/sd64/agent-pack/: a guide of about eight thousand words (the language, the system
                        around it, what SD does not have, and twenty-three worked programs, every one compiled and run on a private SD); a reference
                        generated from the sources, not typed (every TCL verb and query keyword, the VOC record types, the dictionary item types and
                        conversion codes, the BASIC statements and functions, the messages, the system's keys and settings, 3,932 entries each with its
                        source); a Claude Code skill made from the two; a CLAUDE.md template for a project that lives in an SD account; and a recipe
                        for giving an agent an account of its own. mkpack.py rebuilds the reference, and a check fails when the committed copy no longer
                        agrees with the verbs, messages and tables it was made from. Two new documentation pages describe it: User 41 and Administrator 18.
             Why it      An agent that is handed facts from this version of the code, and the gates stated as walls it must not try to defeat,
             matters    makes the commonest mistakes cheap to avoid.
             Does not    The installer does not install it: it is in the source tree and the release, not in /usr/local/sdsys. It gives no agent any
                        access to a system: an agent signs in as its own SD account, with the gates the administrator has opened for that account
                        and no others. There is no sdctl and no MCP server yet, and the .mcp.json file in the pack is a shape that does not work yet,
                        and says so. SD calls no AI model. Nobody has measured whether the guide improves a model's results, and Claude Code has
                        not been run with the skill.

19 Oct 26  THE INSTALL ASKS WHETHER TO OPEN EVERY ACCESS GATE FOR THE INSTALLING USER'S OWN ACCOUNT, AND THE ANSWER IS NO UNLESS YOU TYPE y.

             What        A new SD account starts with every access gate closed - ssh, the API, the shell, OS.EXECUTE, SD.ACCOUNTS, saving changes
                        to a VOC, a program file or a dictionary, SDNet, and writing the shared account's files - and that includes the account the
                        installer makes for the person running it. On a first install the installer now asks one more question, beside its two
                        about remote access: "Open every access gate for <you>'s SD account? (y/N)". Only y or yes (any case) says yes. After the
                        account is made, a yes gives it each of those gates with modify.account, one word at a time (both, sh-on, os-on, sd-on,
                        voc-on, bp-on, dict-on, net-on, shared-write-on, files-on, files-write-on), and the installer then reads the register
                        and the Linux groups back: it prints "Opened every access gate for <you>" only if every one is open, and otherwise a
                        warning that names each gate that did not open and the word to give for it. A refused word does not stop the install.
             Why it      On a machine you are testing on, the installing user otherwise had to sign in as sdsys and type eleven commands before the
             matters    API, ssh or the editors would answer, and the verbs for that (modify.account, remote.api) are not in an ordinary account's
                        VOC at all, which looks like a missing verb.
             Does not    It is not asked, and nothing changes, when the installing user's account already exists: an upgrade keeps what it has. The
                        default, Enter, and every answer except y and yes leave the account closed exactly as before. It opens the installing user's
                        own account only - every other account is still made closed - and sdsys closes any gate again with modify.account
                        <account> <word>-off (none closes ssh and the API). It does not decide who can reach the computer from the network: that is
                        still the two questions about ssh and the API above it.

19 Oct 26  THE IDE SHOWS WHICH PANE IS ACTIVE WITH A [*] OR [-] IN ITS TOP RIGHT CORNER.

             What        Each pane that can have the keyboard now carries a small mark in its top right corner: [*] on the one that has it and
                        [-] on the other three. The four are the files (on the frame above the tree), the editor (on the frame above it), the tab
                        strip (at the right end of the strip) and the messages (at the right end of their bar), and Tab moves the [*] round them
                        in that order. Only the two marks that change are drawn again.
             Why it      A keyboard user pressing Tab could not tell where the keys would go. The status line names the pane and the editor
                        highlights its cursor, but a mark on the pane itself is what the eye finds first.
             Does not    It is not clickable, and it does not change the Tab order or what any key does.

19 Oct 26  THE IDE: CHOOSING A RECORD OR A FILE NO LONGER FLASHES THE WHOLE SCREEN, A CLICK ON THE MENU BAR OPENS THE MENU, THE PANE WITH THE FOCUS IS NAMED, AND THE EDITOR SHOWS ITS CURSOR.

             What        Four reports from using the IDE. (1) Every choice in the tree wrote every widget again - a file wrote 3,078 characters to the
                        terminal, a record 6,635 (a screen is 5,880), Down in the tree 1,026, the tree itself three times over - which showed as the
                        whole screen flashing. The screen library now keeps in step with what the terminal shows, and a form that is drawn again
                        writes only the text that changed: a file 570, a record 780, Down in the tree 54. (2) The menu bar is not a widget, so a click
                        on it was dropped and the menu opened only after F10 or Esc. A click on a title now opens that menu, and a click on the
                        bar beside the titles does nothing. (3) With the keyboard only there was no sign of which pane Tab had moved to: the
                        status line now begins with the pane, [Files], [Tabs], [Editor] or [Messages]. (4) The editor now shows the character at
                        its cursor in the highlight style while it has the focus, so the place shows on a terminal that draws its own cursor poorly
                        or not at all. SCREEN MENUBAR takes CLICK x for a program that is told of a click on its bar: the menu whose title is over
                        column x opens instead of the first.
             Why it      The flash made the IDE look unfinished and tired the eye; the dropped click and the missing focus signs made it hard to use
             matters    without remembering which key does what.
             Does not    The saving is for forms (SCREEN FORM and what is on one) on a screen opened with SCREEN OPEN CLEAR. SCREEN TEXT, BOX, the
                        dialogues, and any screen opened without CLEAR write what they are told, as before. A form that is drawn again does not
                        repair what the program PRINTed over it; SCREEN REFRESH does that, as it always did. The Tab order is unchanged, and the
                        terminal's own cursor is still put in the editor. A program compiled before today that uses SCREEN MENUBAR must be
                        compiled again (the statement passes one more value).

19 Oct 26  ED, THE CLASSIC LINE EDITOR, NO LONGER USES ALL OF A CPU FOR EVER WHEN ITS INPUT HAS ENDED.

             What        An ED session whose input was a pipe (a script, a test, a login that was not a terminal) went on for as long as it was
                        left once the pipe had ended, printing its ----: prompt without end and using all of a CPU: 6.1 seconds of CPU in 6.1
                        seconds, and 8.0 in 8.0 on a private SD. ED took the empty line that an ended input gives for an empty command, which
                        only means "show the next line", and asked again. It now leaves the record as Q does when it is answered Y, but asks
                        nothing because nobody is there: the record is not written and its lock is given back. It does not go on to
                        the next record of a list of names, a select list or a "Record name?" prompt. The line-number questions of LOAD and
                        UNLOAD (start line, end line) cancel the command when the input ends. An UNLOAD used to take the whole record there
                        and write it to the target record, with nobody to say so; a LOAD from a record with no lines could not be answered at
                        all, so it asked for ever.
             Why it      A session that spins holds a user slot, its record locks and a CPU until somebody kills it, and nothing on the screen
             matters    says so. The questions that were not asked of the person (Overwrite? Are you sure you want to delete? OK to quit?) were
                        already answered no, as Enter answers them, so the end of the input did not delete or file a record; a test holds the
                        delete and the quit.
             Does not    The end of the input is not an answer to "save the changes?": text typed into ED and not filed with FI is lost, as it is
                        with Q answered Y. A script that wants its work kept ends with FI (or SAVE). Nothing changes for a person at a terminal:
                        pressing Enter at the ----: prompt is still an empty command and shows the next line, because a terminal does not end
                        like this. The UNLOAD end-line prompt still has no colon after it, unlike the start-line prompt and LOAD's (left as it
                        was).

19 Oct 26  SED AND THE IDE NO LONGER USE ALL OF A CPU FOR EVER WHEN THEIR INPUT HAS ENDED, AND SCREEN WAIT TELLS THE END OF INPUT FROM THE TIME RUNNING OUT.

             What        A SED or IDE session whose input was a pipe (a script, a test, a login that was not a terminal) went on for as long as
                        it was left once the pipe had ended, using all of a CPU and drawing nothing new: 8.0 seconds of CPU in 8.3 seconds on a
                        private SD. SED took the empty key that an ended input gives for "no key yet" and asked again. The screen library's SCREEN
                        WAIT gave that empty key back as a timeout whenever the program had asked for a timeout, and the IDE asks for one every
                        second. SED now ends as it does when its cursor lock is left: the record locks given back, the terminal's modes given
                        back, the screen cleared, nothing asked, because nobody is there to answer. SCREEN WAIT now answers end, not timeout, so
                        the IDE ends and so does a program that waits with a timeout and acts on the end event.
             Why it      A session that spins holds a user slot, its record locks and a CPU until somebody kills it, and nothing on the screen
             matters    says so.
             Does not    The end of the input is not an answer to "save the changes?": text typed into SED and not saved is not saved, and the IDE
                        leaves its unsaved text as it is. It does not change a wait that really times out (SCREEN WAIT still answers timeout
                        when the time runs out), and SCREEN KEY still gives one empty answer for both the time running out and the input ending,
                        as its documentation says, so a program that loops on SCREEN KEY with a timeout still has to count the empty answers
                        itself, as CED does. (ED, the classic line editor, spun in the same way and was changed after this: see the entry
                        above.)

18 Oct 26  THE API'S "OPEN AN SDNET FILE" REQUEST (NUMBER 29) NO LONGER OPENS ANY ACCOUNT'S FILE FOR ANY LOGIN, EVEN WITH NETFILES BIT 2 SET.

             What        Request 29 is the old form of "open the file named account;file". It was refused unless bit 2 of NETFILES in sd.conf was set,
                        and the bit was the ONLY test. With it set, any API login could open and read the VOC-named file of any account: one it was
                        not allowed to enter with logto, a suspended one, one given as a path, one whose NET switch was off, and it ignored
                        SDCLIENT. We ran it on a private SD and the planted record came back from the disk. It now asks the rules that logto
                        asks (a member of the account's group, the account not suspended), wants a register record for the account (a path has
                        none), wants the account's net-on switch on (SDSYS's own account is excepted), and refuses under SDCLIENT as request 4 does.
                        Each refusal is on the audit trail as api refused request=29 reason=....
             Why it      The bit looks like a switch for a feature; it was in fact the whole wall around every account's files for anyone with an
             matters    API login. As delivered the bit is 0, so nothing was open on a delivered system; the hole was there for an
                        administrator who set it, which is what the documentation told him not to do.
             Does not    It does not change what is delivered (bit 2 stays 0 and the request is still refused), the other requests, or how a program
                        opens a file on another SD server (the N pointer, the NET switch and set.server), which never used this request.

18 Oct 26  MODIFY.ACCOUNT NO LONGER DOES THE FIRST OF TWO WORDS AND SAYS NOTHING OF THE SECOND; RESTORE.ACCOUNT NO LONGER OVERWRITES A DIFFERENT GLOBAL PROGRAM.

             modify.     modify.account fred voc-off bp-off used to do voc-off and ignore bp-off without a word, and modify.account group add a b
             account    added a and ignored b.  It now refuses, before anything is changed, and names the word it would not carry out: "modify.account
                        does one action at a time, and nothing was changed: bp-off was not carried out (give each action in a command of its own)".
             Why it      A verb that changes who may do what and reports a partial result as if it were the whole is worse than one that refuses.
             matters
             restore.    When an account was restored, a globally catalogued program in its archive was supposed to be left alone if a DIFFERENT
             account    program already held that name on this system ("different program already there, left alone").  The check looked the name up
                        in capitals, the catalogue keeps lower case, so it never found anything and the archive's program replaced the one on the
                        system; the message also named it in capitals.  The name is now looked up in lower case (an archive written before the
                        lower-case change is read the same way), so a different program is left alone and listed, as intended.
             Does not    Neither changes what a correct command does; they change what a wrong or unusual one does.  RESTORE.ACCOUNT still needs
                        sudo, and the restore of an account's files is untouched.

18 Oct 26  A LOGIN ON ANOTHER SD SERVER CAN CHANGE FILES THERE ONLY IF THAT SERVER'S ADMINISTRATOR ALLOWS IT (THE WRITE GROUP); AND A REMOTE DICTIONARY CAN BE OPENED.

             What        A files-only login (the Linux group sdfiles, the route SDNet uses) is now a READ-ONLY login on the server it reaches unless
                        the remote user is also in a new Linux group, sdfileswrite.  Without it the server refuses writes, deletes, record locks,
                        file locks, clearing a file, the locking forms of a read (and a READV that takes a lock) and does not carry them out; with
                        it they work as before.  Reading, selecting, file information, the indices, testing a lock and releasing one need sdfiles
                        alone.  A user in sdapi has the whole API as before, with or without the new group.
             Why it      Until now a server defined "write" on the CLIENT was the only thing between a stored login and a change: the client
             matters    enforced it and the server did not know.  Now the administrator of the server being reached decides: off for a new
                        account, given by SDSYS.
             Words       modify.account name files-write-on / files-write-off, and files-write-on after files-on in create.account user.  After a
                        change SD says "Account fred has files-write yes" (or no), read back from the group file.  files-write-on needs the person to
                        be in sdfiles already (the group on its own gives nothing) and says so; files-off takes the person out of sdfileswrite as
                        well, so a later files-on does not hand the write back.  A group or other account is refused the words as it is for files-on.
             What you    A server defined "write" on a client STOPS WRITING the day the target runs this release, until the remote user is given
             will see    files-write-on there (one word).  The refusal is "This login can read files but is not allowed to change them (the
                        administrator of this server can allow it: modify.account account files-write-on): request n is not allowed".  The person
                        at the client sees only that the change failed (status 0); the reason is in the target's audit trail, where a refused change
                        is recorded as api refused request=16 route=sdfiles write=off user=... and the login line says write=on or write=off.  The
                        group is read at login, so a change reaches the person at their next login.
             Also        Opening the dictionary of a file on another SD server (dict <file> in lower case, which is how the client sends it) failed
                        before, so COUNT, LIST, SORT and SELECT on a remote file stopped with "Cannot open dictionary".  It now works.
             Does not    It does not touch accounts that have the API (sdapi), and it does not change what a client sees for a read.

18 Oct 26  SDIDX AND SDFIX, THE STAND-ALONE FILE TOOLS, NO LONGER DO SEVERAL WRONG THINGS.

             sdidx       When it moved indexes out of a file's own directory, or deleted them, it removed the old %2, %3 ... through a name buffer
                        only 33 bytes long, so on almost any real data path the old index file stayed behind while the program reported success.
                        The leftover then stopped you moving the indexes back (error 17).  The buffer now holds any path the file header can hold.
                        If you used an older sdidx, look for a stray %2 beside %0 and %1 and delete it by hand once you have checked the index is
                        where the header says.  sdidx -d answered n at "Delete ... (Y/N)?" used to say "All indices deleted" and clear the header
                        anyway, leaving the index files on disk with nothing pointing at them; it now stops ("Indices not deleted", exit status 1),
                        and input that ends at the question counts as n.  sdidx -m and -d used to delete the whole old index directory with
                        everything in it; they now delete only the index files the header lists and remove the directory only if that leaves it
                        empty.  sdidx -p now prints what -q prints.  A relative index path given to -m or -p is made absolute from the current
                        directory before it is stored; a path argument longer than 255 characters is refused.
             sdfix       -F could not delete an index file in the file's own directory: it printed "Corrected" and removed nothing; it can now.
                        The repair for "Invalid split and/or merge loads" wrote split 50 and merge 80, the very values the check rejects; it now
                        writes the defaults SD gives a new file, split 80 and merge 50.  -Q looped for ever if its input ended while it was asking
                        "Fix?" (and at the dump-and-rebuild question and the interactive prompt): the end of input now counts as "no" and says so.
                        The interactive W command reads its address and value as the hex its help always said and writes the bytes in the order
                        typed (it read decimal and swapped bytes).  -R together with -F or -Q is refused with a message (run the fix first, then -R
                        alone).  sdfix now refuses a file that is open within a running SD, as sdidx always did.  A file whose path is 252
                        characters or more is refused: with a path that long the names of the higher index files were cut short to the overflow
                        subfile's name, and -F then deleted that subfile together with the real index.  The exit status is 1 if any named file
                        failed.
             sdconv      Named a hashed file, it used to print nothing, change nothing and exit 0, which read as a successful byte-order conversion.
                        It now says "<file> is a hashed file: sdconv converts compiled programs only, nothing was changed" and exits 1.  It
                        never did convert hashed files in this SD (the call had been lost in a compiler-warning clean-up), this SD cannot open a
                        big-endian hashed file, and both Linux and Windows are little-endian, so nothing is lost.
             Does not    Neither tool takes a backup and neither can be undone; sdidx still cannot move one index and leave the others; sdfix
                        still only reports an index tree that is damaged; and sdconv still converts compiled programs only.

18 Oct 26  ENCRYPTING A FIELD: AN ORDINARY PROGRAM CAN NOW MAKE A KEY, AND A LISTING CAN SHOW THE FIELD DECRYPTED FOR WHOEVER HOLDS IT.

             What        Three new BASIC functions, open to every program: SDSALT() gives a fresh random salt; SDKEY(passphrase, salt) gives the 256-bit key
                        for that passphrase and salt, in the form SDENCRYPT() and SDDECRYPT() accept (the key is slow to make on purpose, so make it once
                        per run, not once per field); SDRANDKEY() gives a random key.  The client library gains SDChecksum, a checksum computed on the
                        client side.  Until now SDENCRYPT() was usable only with a key that an ordinary program had no way to make.
                        On top of that a dictionary item can carry the conversion XE (or XE;name for a named key).  LIST, SORT, COUNT, an I-type that
                        says OCONV(field,"XE;name") and ED then show the field decrypted for a session that holds the key and the stored cipher text for
                        a session that does not; ICONV with XE encrypts a value.  The key is never written in the dictionary.  It is held in a key ring in
                        the memory of the session's own process.  A program fills the ring with CALL !KEYSET(name, key, id); !KEYCLEAR and !KEYLIST empty
                        and list it.  A person at a terminal types a passphrase at a hidden prompt with the new verb ENCRYPT.KEY SET name salt (add CONFIRM
                        the first time); ENCRYPT.KEY CLEAR and ENCRYPT.KEY LIST empty and list the ring, and LIST shows only names and key ids, never a key.
                        The ring is emptied by LOGTO, by sign-on and when the session ends, and it is never written to disk, the audit trail or a $COMO
                        file.  A phantom does not inherit it and has to set its own key.  A second key set under a name does not replace the first: the
                        newest encrypts and older ones still read what they made.
             Why         An application can keep a salary or an identity number encrypted in the file while a person who holds the passphrase still sees it
                        in an ordinary listing.  A stored value carries a key id and a check, so SD can say "no such key", "wrong key" or "damaged" (STATUS()
                        10208 to 10213) and never shows a wrong decryption.  Without a key, OCONV leaves the stored value, so a listing shows cipher text and
                        not a blank.  ICONV with no key returns the null string and never the plain text, so a field declared encrypted cannot be filled
                        with plain text by mistake.  SD now also wipes the buffers it copies passphrases, keys and SCRAM secrets into before it frees them.
             Does not    SD does not look after the key.  There is no vault, no recovery and no administrator override: a forgotten passphrase or a lost salt
                        means the data is gone.  A SORT BY the field sorts the cipher text; WITH field = "value" finds nothing even with the key, because the
                        value is encrypted with a fresh nonce each time; TOTAL adds the cipher text, which counts as zero; an index on the field holds the
                        cipher text; COPY moves it untouched.  Someone with root, or a debugger on the process, can still read the key from memory.  The
                        ENCRYPT.KEY passphrase has no strength rule, because nothing is stored or compared and a long phrase beats any mixed-class rule.
                        This is not encryption of the files themselves: for a stolen or discarded disk see the Administrator page on encrypting the data
                        SD keeps on disk (LUKS or fscrypt under the SD directories).

19 Oct 26  THE IDE CAN HAND A RECORD TO SED AND TAKE IT BACK (CTRL-E), SHOWS ITS CURSOR AT ONCE, NO LONGER REWRITES THE SCREEN FOR EVERY KEY, AND HAS A HORIZONTAL SCROLL BAR.

             What        Ctrl-E in the IDE (or Go, Edit in SED) opens the record in SED.  SED edits the record on disk, so a record with unsaved
                        changes is saved first: the IDE asks "Save them, then edit in SED?" and Cancel leaves everything as it was.  SED's first
                        successful save ends the edit and the IDE comes back with the new text in the editor, the compile errors forgotten (they
                        named lines of the old text) and "Back from SED: ... is saved" in the status bar; leaving SED with Ctrl-Q returns too, with
                        the record as it was.  A record that someone else holds locked opens in SED read only, with SED's message, as it does
                        from the command line.  Nothing else about SED changes: the IDE tells it by a flag in the process (a named common), not
                        by a word on the command line.  The IDE's editor leaves Ctrl-E to the IDE (End is the key for the end of a row there).
                        Three reports from the owner are fixed in the same sitting.  The cursor: the IDE showed none at the start because its
                        status line, written ten times a second, left the cursor at the end of the bottom row; the cursor now stays where the
                        widget with the focus put it.  The flash: choosing a record in the tree rewrote the whole screen two and a half times
                        (about 15,000 cells; a 140 by 42 screen is 5,880) and an idle IDE rewrote its status line ten times a second; a record
                        is now drawn by the widgets that changed (about 5,500 cells), a tab switch about 3,500, an idle IDE nothing, and an
                        editor no longer repaints its whole area for a key it does not use (a function key).  The bar: the editor has a horizontal
                        scroll bar in its last row, with arrows (a click scrolls 8 columns) and a thumb as long as the visible part is of the
                        longest line; its text has one row less.  F3, Ctrl-L and a new record now show the focus at once.
             Why it      Moving between the IDE and SED had meant leaving one and starting the other.  A cursor you cannot see, a screen that
             matters    flashes at every record and a long line you cannot scroll to are what a person notices first in an editor.
             Does not    Programs that use SCREEN MEMO are drawn exactly as before (the bar and the reserved key are options the IDE sets for itself),
                        and nothing about a record changes unless SED saves it.

19 Oct 26  THE IDE, CED AND THE DEBUGGER ARE LAID OUT FOR 140 BY 42, SURVIVE A WINDOW MADE SMALLER, AND DO NOT TAKE KEYS WHILE YOU CANNOT SEE THE CURSOR.

             What        The IDE and CED are laid out for 140 columns by 42 lines (the IDE's floor was 60 by 16), and a window smaller than that is
                        refused at the start in words ("IDE needs a window of at least 140 columns by 42 lines; this one is 100 by 30"), with nothing
                        drawn; the debugger does not refuse, it says the same in one line and is line by line for the session.  A window made smaller
                        while one of them runs shows the upper left corner of the layout and nothing past the edge, and while the cursor is off
                        the screen the program takes no keys and shows a warning until the window is made bigger; Ctrl-Q, y there leaves, the IDE
                        and CED without saving and the debugger as F3 does.  A message box, a menu and a question put up by those programs follow
                        the same rule: the cursor goes to the selected button while a box is up, and a box in a part of the screen the window does not
                        show is closed on the way out instead of being typed into blind.  A SCREEN OPEN program of your own is not held to any of
                        this: it asks for the size it needs with minsize.
             Why it      The same rule SED already had (below), applied to the rest of SD's own full-screen programs, with one refusal text.
             matters
             Does not    It does not ask your terminal to resize itself, and a window bigger than 140 by 42 shows the layout in its upper left.

19 Oct 26  SED IS 140 BY 42, SURVIVES A WINDOW MADE SMALLER, AND DOES NOT TAKE KEYS WHILE YOU CANNOT SEE THE CURSOR.

             What        SED is laid out for 140 columns by 42 lines, SD's new default terminal size (it was 120 by 36).  A window smaller than that
                        when SED starts is refused in words ("SED needs a window of at least 140 columns by 42 lines; this one is 100 by 30") and SED
                        draws nothing; ED is the editor for a small window.  If the window is made smaller while SED is running (a dragged corner, a
                        tiling window manager), SED looks at it about once a second and every time it draws, clears the screen and draws the upper left
                        corner of its layout, and nothing past the edge: nothing wraps and nothing lands on the wrong row.  Nothing pans; the upper
                        left is always the anchor.  Make the window bigger and the whole layout is drawn again.  While the cursor is off the screen
                        (on a line below the window, past its right edge, or at a search or line-number prompt on a row the window does not show) SED
                        does not take keys, because you would be typing where you cannot see: it shows a warning in reverse video in the upper left
                        corner and throws away every key and mouse click.  Make the window big enough to show the cursor and the warning goes by
                        itself, with the cursor where it was.  Ctrl-Q is the one key that does anything: it asks "Leave? (changes are NOT saved) y/n"
                        in the same box, and y leaves SED with the record unlocked and nothing saved.
             Why it      The terminal default became 140 by 42 and SED, which had been the one program that did not follow a resized window, could
             matters    draw off the edge of a smaller one and let you type blind.  The way out asks its question in its own box because SED's own
                        questions may be on a row you cannot see.
             Does not    It does not ask your terminal to resize itself.  A window bigger than 140 by 42 shows SED in its upper left corner with the
                        rest blank.  A session with AUTOLOGOUT set waits for the keyboard alone so the time-out can still fire, so it notices a new
                        window size at the next key rather than within a second.

19 Oct 26  THE DEFAULT TERMINAL IS NOW 140 COLUMNS BY 42 LINES (it was 120 by 36).

             What        A session that has no terminal to ask - a phantom, a piped script, a service, the API - now starts with a display 140
                        columns wide and 42 lines deep, and a LIST fills 41 lines before it pauses instead of 35.  term default puts a session back to
                        140 by 42 on any window.  The terminfo entries that named a width and depth (xterm, vt100, ansi, dumb and the rest) say 140
                        and 42 now, and the term help text and the documentation say so.
             Why it      The full-screen programs are being made for a larger window, and 120 by 36 was the size of a terminal from an earlier age.
             matters
             Does not    A real terminal still decides its own size: a window of 80 by 24 is an 80 by 24 display, and term w,h still sets any width
                        from 20 to 32767 and any depth from 10 to 32767, exactly as typed.  LINES and COLUMNS in the environment still win over the
                        terminfo entry for a session with no terminal.  The 132-column terminal types keep their own depth, and ms-vt100 keeps its
                        25 lines.  To get the old default back for a session, type term 120,36.

18 Oct 26  THE SCREEN LIBRARY HAS CONTAINERS AND VIEWPORTS: A FORM CAN BE BIGGER THAN THE WINDOW IT IS SHOWN IN.

             What        SCREEN CONTAINER f, id makes a bordered rectangle that owns widgets: SCREEN IN f, id says that the widgets added next
                        are inside it (SCREEN IN f, '' ends it, and a container added inside another nests).  Their AT is counted from the
                        first cell inside the border.  They move with the container (SCREEN MOVE), are hidden, disabled or put on a tab page
                        with it, and are taken out with it (SCREEN DELETE, which says how many went).  What they draw, and where a click
                        reaches them, is cut at the container's edge.  SCREEN VIEWPORT f, id ... CONTENT cw,ch is a container whose inside
                        is a window on a larger sheet: a bar appears in the right column when the sheet is higher than the window and in the
                        bottom row when it is wider.  With the viewport focused the arrow keys, Page Up and Down, Home and End scroll it; the
                        wheel scrolls three rows and Shift-wheel three columns, a click on a bar's arrow moves one and on its track a page,
                        and the thumb can be dragged.  A widget that takes the focus (Tab, a click, SCREEN FOCUS before the form is shown)
                        is scrolled into view.  A scroll is a change event of the viewport's id; SCREEN SCROLL f, id TO x,y / BY dx,dy moves
                        it from the program and SCREEN GET answers where it is.
             Why         The full-screen administrator and developer apps and the screen builder need a form larger than a 140 by 42
                        terminal, and a canvas of any size.  Measured: 200 by 60 of sheet (630 widgets) scrolls in about a tenth of a second.
             Also        A combo box's list is drawn over the whole screen, not inside the viewport it is in.  While the focused widget is not
                        wholly in view the cursor is hidden and comes back with the widget.
             Does not    It does not make a widget taller than the window usable in a viewport: such a widget scrolls itself but has no
                        cursor there, so give a memo or table a size that fits.  Dragging a widget with the mouse (for the screen builder)
                        is a separate piece.  A form that uses none of this is drawn exactly as before.

18 Oct 26  THE SHARED ACCOUNT: FILES THAT EVERY ACCOUNT CAN OPEN BY NAME, WITHOUT LOGTO OR A POINTER (S.66).

             What        The installer now makes a group account named shared (its directory is /home/sd/group_accounts/shared and its
                        Linux group is sdg_shared).  The files in it can be opened by every account by file name: OPEN "inventory"
                        in any account finds the file shared holds under that name when the account has no entry of its own for it.
                        The account's own VOC entry always wins, so a file the account already has under that name is the one it gets.
                        LIST, SORT, SELECT, COUNT and a BASIC program's OPEN all find it, and OPEN DICT opens its dictionary.
                        SDSYS puts files there by signing on to shared (logto shared) and making them as in any account.
             Gates       Two new per-account switches, shown in the account's register record (fields 13 and 14).  shared.read is ON for
                        every account, new and existing, until SDSYS turns it off with modify.account name shared-read-off: it is the
                        one switch here that a blank means ON for, because the point of the account is files open to all.
                        shared.write is OFF until SDSYS turns it on with modify.account name shared-write-on.  The words shared-read-on
                        and shared-write-off undo them.  create.account user name shared-read-off shared-write-on sets them at creation;
                        the report line is "Account fred has shared.read yes, shared.write no".  A group or other account takes the
                        words as well.
             What each   Reading a shared file needs shared.read.  A WRITE, DELETE, CLEARFILE or any verb that saves a record into a
             allows      shared file needs shared.write.  A shared dictionary needs shared.write AND dict.edit (the S.64 switch).
                        The structure of a shared file (making one by pathname, clear.file, configure.file, an index, a trigger, a
                        resize, a rename or a delete) is changed only by SDSYS at a terminal on the machine: it is refused by the
                        kernel for everyone else, a program marked internal included, whatever the switches say.
             Lookup      Only a file whose entry in shared's VOC is an ordinary file pointer is found, and only when it lies inside the
             rules       shared directory.  Q pointers, N pointers, paths elsewhere, multifiles, a "name,component" request, the VOC
                        and program files of shared, and its private catalogue are never reached from another account: its verbs,
                        sentences and programs are not shared.  A name that begins with a symbol ($ & ! * _ %), and every name in the
                        standard VOC, is never looked up, so $savedlists, $hold and the like stay each account's own.  A file's
                        records are locked exactly as in any account, so two accounts see each other's locks.
             Not looked  OPENSEQ, DELETESEQ and CALL "file prog" do not follow shared; the lookup is an OPEN, and an OPEN only.  The
             up by       administrator's own OPEN does not follow it either: SDSYS reaches the files with logto shared.
             Audit       The first open of each shared name in a session writes "shared open file=name" to the audit trail; a refused
                        name writes nothing.  The administrator's words write the usual modify.account line.
             list.shared A new verb, in SDSYS's set, lists the files of shared that an account with shared.read can open by name, one
                        to a line, and a count.  With no shared account in service it says that.  It is not in the VOC of an ordinary
                        account; if SDSYS puts it there, an account with shared.read off is told so and sees nothing.
             Safer       There is no way into shared for an ordinary user except the lookup: the account's Linux group is sdg_shared
             design      (members root and sdsys only), not sdusers, so logto shared, a Q pointer to it and sd -ashared are for SDSYS
                        alone.  That is what makes taking shared.read away a real wall.  The directory and its files belong to sdsys
                        with group sdusers (2775 / 664), so the kernel can read them for everyone and, with shared.write, write them.
                        A person who wants a member of staff to look after shared gives them membership of sdg_shared with
                        modify.account shared add name, and shared-write-on to the account named shared; they can then change records
                        but never structure.
             Names       The name shared is reserved.  create.account refuses it (11516) and delete.account shared is refused (11517);
                        the installer makes the account with a one-time marker that it removes, as it does for the installing
                        user's account.  An installation that already has an account named shared that is not SD's own is stopped
                        by the installer before it changes anything, with a message naming the register record to look at.
             Upgrading   An existing account has no value in the new fields, which reads as shared.read yes and shared.write no, so
                        no account loses or gains anything but the new read access; nothing is written to the existing accounts.  The
                        installer creates shared when the install is made again and keeps it when the accounts are kept.  Removing SD
                        with the accounts removes the group sdg_shared as well; keeping the accounts keeps it.
             Cautions    A file is checked for shared.read when it is opened: a file already open stays usable after SDSYS withdraws
                        the switch, until the person's next entry.  A program (a LIST, say) still writes the compiled form of a
                        new I-type or A-type into a shared dictionary by itself, as it does into any dictionary, so a reader may
                        cause that write; an account with sh or OS.EXECUTE on can reach the files at operating-system level like
                        any other account directory it can read; the API's sdsys login can write shared records but cannot change
                        structure.  Anything placed in shared can be read by every account, so put nothing there that is not meant
                        for everyone.
             Not done    The shared account is not replicated to other servers, and a catalogue of shared programs is not shared.
                        SD Core Solo has one account and does not have it.
             Tested      The installer's two new blocks were run against stand-ins for sudo and the account tools, and the scripts
                        were syntax checked.  NOTHING HAS RUN ON A REAL INSTALL: the account's creation by the installer, its
                        ownership and modes, the lookup, the gates and the API entry have not been seen to work.
             Messages    11513 to 11520 are new.

18 Oct 26  TWO WAYS AROUND THE OS ACCESS GATES WERE CLOSED: A CONVERSION CODE, AND A PRINT UNIT'S SPOOLER STRING.

             Why         Found by an evaluation of the OpenQM 4 manual against SD and then reproduced on a private SD with an ordinary
                        account that has SH and OS.EXECUTE off.  Neither needed a password or an administrator.
             1           OCONV and ICONV with a conversion code of "U" and a name call the catalogued subroutine of that name.  The routine
                        that does it is internal, so a name beginning with a dollar sign, which only internal code may CALL, was reached
                        by an ordinary program.  Such a name is now refused as a conversion code, the same way a name that is not
                        catalogued is (status 2).  A user's own catalogued conversion subroutines work as before.
             2           SETPTR ... SPOOLER (and SETPU) set the start of the command a print job is handed to.  It went to the shell
                        unchecked, so an ordinary account could run any command as its own Linux user.  It is now used only by a session
                        that holds the OS access gate (the administrator, or an account SDSYS gave OS.EXECUTE); everyone else prints
                        through the spooler in sd.conf, or lp.
             Not done    OPEN.SOCKET still connects from any account: whether a program may open a network connection by default is a
                        decision for the owner and is recorded as a question.

18 Oct 26  SDNET, FIFTH SLICE (E): FILES ON ANOTHER SD SERVER CAN BE WRITTEN AND LOCKED, FOR A SERVER DEFINED WITH WRITE (S.65).

             What        For a server that SDSYS defined with the word write (set.server ... write), a file opened through an N pointer
                        accepts WRITE, WRITEU, MATWRITE, WRITEV, DELETE, CLEARFILE, the locking reads (READU, READL, READVU, MATREADU
                        and the rest), RECORDLOCKU and RECORDLOCKL and RELEASE.  A busy record takes the LOCKED branch, or the
                        statement tries again every quarter of a second.  The delivered mode is read, and with read every one of
                        these is refused exactly as before and nothing is sent: writing is something SDSYS turns on, per server.
             Limits      The mode is the CLIENT's setting.  The server it points at is not told, and its files-only route lets a login
                        write and delete whatever the mode says; its own voc.edit, dict.edit and bp.edit switches still apply, and the
                        client's own switches apply by the remote file's name.  Nothing here stops a modified client.
             Rules       A change or lock inside BEGIN TRANSACTION is refused (a remote change cannot be rolled back).  A change that gets
                        no answer is never sent again: its outcome is not known, and the message says so.  FILELOCK, GETLOCKS, the
                        index statements and CREATING.SEQKEY stay refused.  A write-mode file is never read from the record cache.
             Not yet     Only a test server has been used; no two machines have talked to each other.

18 Oct 26  SDNET: A SHORT RECORD CACHE FOR REMOTE FILES, AND LOGTO CLOSES THEIR CONNECTIONS (S.65).

             What        A process now keeps the last 64 records it READ from files on other SD servers for 2 seconds, and the fact that
                        a record was not there.  A second READ of the same record from the same open file in that time costs no request:
                        a SORT over a remote file made half the requests.  The entries of a file go when it is closed or SELECTed again.
             What it     A READ can show a record up to 2 seconds old.  A record over 32 KB is not kept, READV is not cached.
             costs
             LOGTO       Going into another account with LOGTO, and coming back, now closes every connection to other servers and makes
                        every remote file opened before unusable (a READ on it is an error); a new OPEN dials again.  Until now the
                        connections stayed until the process ended.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, FOURTH SLICE (D): LIST, COUNT, SORT AND SELECT WORK ON A FILE ON ANOTHER SD SERVER (S.65).

             What        SELECT, SELECTV and SSELECT of the remote file give every record id the server holds as an ordinary select
                        list.  OPEN 'DICT' of the VOC pointer opens the file's dictionary from the same server.  With those two, the
                        query verbs LIST, COUNT, SORT, SELECT, LIST.ITEM and LIST DICT run on the VOC name, WITH clauses and BY included.
                        FILEINFO(f, FL$TYPE) of a remote file is the new FL$TYPE.NET (7), and INDICES(f) is empty.
             Cost        Each record a query needs is one request to the other server, and the whole list of ids comes in one reply.  A
                        query that reads every record of a large remote file is slow because of that; an id given on the command line
                        is read without a select.
             Not yet     Writes, locks, transactions and the server's indices are still refused; LOGTO does not yet drop the connections.
                        Only a test server has been used so far.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, THIRD SLICE (C): A FILE ON ANOTHER SD SERVER CAN BE OPENED AND READ (S.65).

             What        A VOC entry whose first field is N, with the server's name in field 2 and the file's name on that server in
                        field 3, is a pointer to a file on another SD server defined with set.server.  OPEN by that name gives a file
                        variable that READ, MATREAD, READV, FILEINFO and CLOSE work on; the records come from the other server over the
                        TLS connection and the stored login of set.netlogin.  Nothing is sent before the NET switch, the server's
                        accounts list, the stored login and the pinned key have been checked.
             Not yet     It is read only.  WRITE, DELETE, CLEARFILE, locks, SELECT, transactions and the query verbs LIST, COUNT, SORT and
                        SELECT on such a file stop with a message, and nothing is sent.  Only a test server has been used so far; no two
                        machines have talked to each other.
             A fix       A program that kept a class object in named common (the new remote-file code does) made the process end with a
                        crash at logout, because the object's destroy code ran after the stack it needs had been freed.  The objects are
                        now released first.  No program written so far was affected.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, SECOND SLICE (C): A SECOND, NARROWER API ROUTE THAT REACHES FILES AND NOTHING ELSE (S.65).

             Why         Until now a person could log in to a server's API only as a member of the Linux group sdapi, and then the
                        whole API was theirs: execute commands, call subroutines, enter other accounts, read and write sequential files,
                        and work with files.  A login that SDNet keeps for another server, if it were taken from a machine, would give
                        whoever took it all of that on the server it was made for.  There is now a second route, the Linux group
                        sdfiles.  A person who is in sdusers and in sdfiles and NOT in sdapi can log in over the API, and the session
                        is held to files.
             The rules   sdapi wins: a member of both groups has the whole API, as before.  A person in neither group is refused
                        exactly as before.  The SDSYS login is never made files-only.  If the group file cannot be read the login is
                        refused rather than guessed.  Both groups are empty for a new account until the administrator adds the person.
             Allowed     Open and close a file; read, with or without a lock; write, write and keep the lock, delete and delete and
                        keep the lock; select, read the next id, read and save lists; lock a record or a file and ask whether a
                        record is locked; clear a file; the index requests; file information and reading one field; the mark
                        mapping; the server's revision; ask for the error text; quit.  Setting the account is allowed ONCE, which is
                        the entry to an account after login: a second one would be a logto and is refused.
             Refused     Call a subroutine, execute a command, answer its prompt or end it, the other logins, open a file on another
                        SDNet server, read a variable, system(), trans(), transactions, evaluate and the conversions, sequential
                        files, and any request this release does not know.  The decision is one small function holding an ALLOW
                        list, so a request added to the API later is refused to a files-only login until somebody decides it belongs.
                        A refused request is not carried out; the client is told "This login is for files only: request n is not
                        allowed" and the connection stays open.
             What it is  It is NOT a read-only route: a files-only login can write and delete.  Everything else about the session is
             not        as for any other API login: the account's switches, SDCLIENT in sd.conf and the API listener all still apply.
             Words       modify.account name files-on / files-off, and files-on in create.account user.  After a change SD says
                        "Account fred has FILES yes" (or no), read back from the group file and not taken from the request, and a
                        change that the group file does not show is reported as a failure.  The words ssh, api, both and none leave
                        files-on alone: modify.account fred none takes away ssh and the API and not the files-only login; files-off
                        takes that away.  A group or other account is refused the words.  create.account looks for the group before it
                        makes the Linux user, so on a machine without it nothing is left behind.  A change reaches the person at
                        their next login; to remove it now, files-off and end their connection.
             Audit       api login route=sdfiles user=name for a login, api refused request=n route=sdfiles user=name for a refused
                        request, and modify.account files-on account=name for the administrator's act.
             Installing  The installer makes the group sdfiles, empty, beside sdssh and sdapi, and the privileged helper accepts it
                        by exact name for addgroup, delgroup and groupadd and refuses groupdel of it as it does for the others.  AN
                        EXISTING INSTALLATION HAS NEITHER UNTIL THE INSTALLER IS RUN AGAIN; until then files-on says "sdfiles is not a
                        valid Linux Group" and changes nothing.  The upgrade does not put anyone in the group.
             Archives    The account archive carries route.files: 1 or 0 for a user account, and restore.account puts an account it
                        has to create back in the group.  The key is not called files because that is already the count of files in
                        the archive.  An older archive has no such line and restores the account out of the group.
             Not yet     Nothing on the client side uses the route yet: set.netlogin still makes the ordinary login exchange and no
                        SDNet client opens a file on another server, so no one has connected by this route.
             Tested      The helper's group rules were run as an ordinary user (180 rows, and 8 mutants of the group rules killed),
                        the installer and helper scripts were syntax checked, and the free checks that read the programs as text pass.
                        NONE OF THE SD PROGRAMS FOR THE ROUTE WAS COMPILED OR RUN: the login with a real user in sdfiles, the refusal
                        of a request on a live session, files-on and files-off against a real group file, the installer making the
                        group and the archive round trip have not been seen to work.  There is no sandbox row for the route yet.
             Messages    11480 (the refusal) and 11482 (the FILES state line) are new.
             Documents   The Administrator guide's Accounts and Security page has a section on the route with the full lists of what
                        is allowed and refused and the checks in the order the login makes them; its SDNet page, the Remote Access
                        page and the Getting Started account and API pages mention it; the TCL syntax card lists the new words.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, SECOND SLICE (B): A PERSON'S OWN LOGIN ON A SERVER, KEPT AS DERIVED VALUES AND NEVER AS THE PASSWORD (S.65).

             Why         To use a server SDSYS has defined, a person needs a login on it.  set.netlogin server asks for the user name
                        and password the person has on that other server, makes ONE TLS connection to the server, runs the same
                        SCRAM-SHA-256 login the API uses, and keeps the values that login derives.  delete.netlogin server removes
                        them.  Both are in every account's VOC, because they are the person's own.  Nothing yet USES a stored
                        login, and no remote file can be opened.
             Not the     The password is typed hidden, worked into the derived values and cleared; it is never written, printed,
             password   logged or audited.  What is kept is the salt and iteration count the server chose and the two keys derived
                        from the password (ClientKey and ServerKey).  Be clear what that is: whoever reads the file can log in as
                        that remote user to THAT ONE server, and can try passwords against the keys offline, as fast as the iteration
                        count lets them; they cannot learn the password from the file and cannot use it on another server.  Use a
                        strong remote password that is not used anywhere else.
             Where       /var/lib/sd-net/<linux user>/<server>, one line, mode 600, in a directory owned by the person with mode
                        700.  It is OUTSIDE every account directory, so an account program that has neither the sh nor the
                        os.execute switch cannot read it with OPENSEQ.  AN ACCOUNT THAT HAS EITHER SWITCH CAN READ IT, exactly as it
                        can read ~/.ssh, because it can read anything its Linux user can.  Two accounts of one Linux user share the
                        one file for a server.
             Trust       Only an internal subroutine, $netcred, touches the file.  Before each use it asks a new C test, the SDEXT
                        function SD_FILE_PRIVATE (key 111, internal programs only): a plain file and not a link, owned by the person
                        running, no group or world permission, in a directory that is owned by the person, not a link and mode 700,
                        which is what ssh requires of a private key.  A file or directory that fails is never opened.  A login made
                        for one server key is refused once the definition holds another.
             The order   set.netlogin checks, asking for nothing: the NET switch (or the administrator), that the server is defined,
                        that the account is in its accounts list, and that the directory is ready.  Then it asks for the two
                        prompts.  Then it connects, and BEFORE IT SENDS ANYTHING compares the key the server presents with the pin
                        in the definition: a different key ends the command with nothing sent and the password unspent.  Then the
                        exchange, in which it also checks that the server's own signature is right.  Nothing else is sent: no
                        account is entered on the other server.  On any failure nothing is stored and an older login is untouched; on
                        success an older login for that server is replaced without a question.
             Directory   The directory is made by a new step of the privileged helper, sd-elevate netcred-dir (no argument; it takes
                        who is asking from sudo, refuses root, sdsys and system users, and is safe to run again), and sdusers gets
                        exactly that one more command in the sudoers file.  AN EXISTING INSTALLATION DOES NOT HAVE THAT PERMISSION
                        UNTIL THE INSTALLER IS RUN AGAIN; until then set.netlogin stops at the directory step and says so.
             Limits      It works in a local session of an ordinary Linux user; the administrator (sdsys) has no login of its own to
                        keep, and an API session does not pass the ownership test.  delete.server leaves people's login files, which
                        are inert while the pin differs.  The remote user is the other server's ordinary API user: the server has no
                        files-only login yet, so the stored values reach whatever that user reaches there.
             Audit       set.netlogin server=name remote=user and delete.netlogin server=name, on success only, and never a password,
                        key, salt or hash.
             Tested      The helper through --dry-run, and the sudoers line, were tested.  The BASIC programs and the C check were NOT
                        COMPILED OR RUN (the C had a syntax check only), and nor was a real run of the helper: the exchange has not
                        been seen to work against a live server.
             Messages    11450 to 11479 are new.
             Documents   The Administrator guide's SDNet page describes the credential model, what the file does and does not protect
                        against, both verbs, the helper and the checks in order; the TCL syntax card lists both verbs.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, SECOND SLICE (A):SET.SERVER CAN CONNECT ONCE AND SHOW YOU THE SERVER'S KEY HASH (S.65).

             Why         A server definition pins the hash of the other server's public key, and until now SDSYS had to type those 64
                        digits from a message, with nothing to say they were right.  set.server now takes the word connect in place of
                        the pin: set.server name host port account connect {read | write} {accounts a,b,c} {no.query}.  Every other
                        field is checked first, and nothing is dialled for a definition that would be refused.  SD then opens a TLS
                        connection to the host and port, waiting at most 5 seconds, reads the hash of the public key the server
                        presented and closes the connection at once.  It is a handshake only: nothing is sent to the server.  It shows
                        you the hash on a line of its own, tells you to compare it with the hash the other server's administrator gives
                        you by a way that does not use this network, and asks "Trust this key (y/n)?", which is no unless you answer y.
                        On y the definition is written with that hash; on n, or when the input ends, nothing is written.  Typing the 64
                        digits works as before and connects to nothing.
             The question  It is ALWAYS asked.  no.query still skips the question about replacing a definition that exists, and
                        only that one: a script that gives connect and no.query still has to answer whether the key is the right one,
                        because that answer is the point of connecting.  A replacement with connect asks the replacement question
                        first, before any connection, so answering no to it dials nothing.
             The hash    It is the SHA-256 of the server's PUBLIC KEY, not of the certificate, so a certificate renewed over the same key
                        has the same hash and the pin stays good.  The other server's administrator can read it from that server with
                        sudo /usr/local/sbin/sd-elevate tls-key, a new helper verb that prints the line "public key sha256: " and the 64
                        digits (it reads only the public key, takes no argument, and says "certificate: not yet generated" when the
                        server has taken no TLS connection yet); SETTINGS.REPORT shows the same line in its [api] section.  The
                        figure agrees with an independent computation on a test certificate, and set.server shows the same figure for
                        a test server.  The openssl pipeline for it by hand is in the SDNet page.  sd-elevate tls-show still prints the
                        certificate's fields and fingerprint, which change at a renewal; this hash does not.
             Failures    If the connection cannot be made (nothing listening, a wrong address, 5 seconds), if what answers does not
                        complete a TLS handshake, or if no hash can be read, a message of its own says which and nothing is written.
             Audit       The set.server line in the audit trail ends with pin=typed or pin=confirmed.  The hash itself is still not in it.
             SOCKET.INFO One new key for programs, SKT$INFO.TLS.SPKI (11): for a socket opened with OPEN.SOCKET and the SKT$TLS flag, the
                        same 64 lower case hex digits; a null string for any other socket.  Like the existing TLS key 8 it is SD's own.
             Messages    11412 and 11413 are reworded; 11440 to 11446 are new.
             Not there   Still no verb, statement or program connects to a server that is DEFINED; the connection above is the one
                        SDSYS makes, once, for set.server.  No user name or password for the remote account is asked for or stored,
                        there is no VOC pointer to a remote file, and the NET switch and the accounts list are still not consulted by
                        anything.  Nothing checks a pin on a later connection, because there is none.
             Documents   The Administrator guide's SDNet page describes the connect flow, its failure messages and the audit words, and
                        the Sockets page lists key 11; the TCL syntax card shows {pin | connect}.

17 Oct 26  SDNET, FIRST SLICE: THE NET SWITCH AND THE SERVER DEFINITIONS (S.65).  NOTHING CONNECTS YET.

             Why         SDNet, files on another SD server that a program opens and reads as if they were local, is being built in
                        slices.  This is the first.  It adds the permission an account will need and the list of servers SDSYS is
                        willing to have called, and nothing else: no program, verb or statement in SD opens a connection to a server,
                        and no remote file can be opened.  The switch and the definitions are in place, and can be set and listed, so
                        that the slices that follow have something to rest on.  The SDNet that was removed in September (a "server;file"
                        VOC entry, port 4245, passwords in sd.conf) is not back.
             NET switch  One more per-account switch, beside the shell, OS.EXECUTE, SD.ACCOUNTS, the three edit switches, ssh and the
                        API.  It is OFF for an account made from now on, and off for every account that already exists: nothing used
                        it, so there is no upgrade step.  SDSYS gives and takes it with modify.account name net-on and net-off, and
                        create.account user takes net-on.  After a change the command prints "Account fred has NET yes" (or no); if the
                        switch already had the value asked for it says only that the account already had that access.  Like sd-on, and
                        unlike the edit switches, it belongs to the account the person is IN: it is read at sign-on, again at logto
                        and given back when logto returns, and by the API server at every entry to an account.  It may be set on a
                        group account.  The administrator is never refused, and an SDSYS session over the local API is given it on.
                        The account archive keeps it for a user account, and restore.account gives it back only to an account it has
                        to create; an older archive restores the account with it off.  Today the switch changes nothing anyone can do.
             Servers     SDSYS defines the servers an account may one day reach; no user can.  Each definition is one line of the file
                        netservers in the SDSYS directory (mode 644): a name, a host, the PORT OF THE SERVER BEING DEFINED (chosen
                        when it is defined: 4247 for a full SD Core, 4249 for a Solo), the account to enter there, the SHA-256 hash of
                        the server's public key (the pin), a mode (read, unless write is given), the local accounts that may use it,
                        and the date.  The list of accounts is EMPTY unless SDSYS names some, so nobody may use a new definition,
                        and an account needs the NET switch as well.  The file is replaced whole, so it is never half written, at most
                        ten servers can be defined, and it holds no secret: a password or a key is never put in it.
             Verbs       set.server name host port account pin {read | write} {accounts a,b,c} {no.query} adds a definition, or
                        replaces one after a question (a replacement is the whole definition, the accounts list included); every
                        field is checked first and each wrong one has its own message, and a refusal writes nothing.  delete.server
                        name removes one.  list.servers {name} shows them, the pin cut short, or the whole pin and the date with a
                        name.  They are SDSYS's: they are in SDSYS's VOC and in no other account's, and each also refuses anybody but
                        the administrator.  Defining and deleting are written to the audit trail (set.server name=... and
                        delete.server name=..., without the pin).  Messages 11411 and 11412 to 11439 are new.
             Not there   The connection; (showing you the key hash a server presents for confirmation came with the entry above);
                        the user name and password for the remote account; the VOC pointer to a remote
                        file; and any check of the NET switch or the accounts list, because nothing yet asks them.  Writing to
                        a remote file is later again.  NETFILES in sd.conf still does nothing.
             Documents   The Administrator guide has a new page, SDNet, and the NET switch in Accounts and Security; the TCL syntax
                        card has the three verbs and net-on / net-off.

17 Oct 26  THE EDIT GATES: VOC.EDIT, BP.EDIT AND DICT.EDIT (S.64).

             Gates       Three more per-account switches, beside the shell, OS.EXECUTE, SD.ACCOUNTS, ssh and the API, say whether
                        the person who signed on to an account may SAVE A CHANGE to a VOC, to a program file (source or object
                        code) and to a dictionary.  They are off for an account made from now on.  When the installer is run by the
                        user whose own account already exists, the upgrade switches all three on, once, for every USER account in
                        the register, so that no such account loses what it had; group and other accounts are left off on purpose,
                        and if a different OS user installs over existing accounts the upgrade step is not run and they stay off.
                        SDSYS gives and takes them with modify.account name voc-on, voc-off, bp-on, bp-off, dict-on and dict-off,
                        and create.account takes voc-on, bp-on and dict-on.  The administrator is never refused, on a terminal or
                        in a batch job; an SDSYS session over the local API, which carries no administrator flag, is given all
                        three on when it signs on.  A switch takes effect when the person next signs on.  After a change the
                        command prints where all three stand; if the switch already had the value asked for it says only that the
                        account already had that access.
             Group accounts  A member of a group or other account's Linux group can sign on to it directly (sd -a name, a batch
                        job, or the API), and then that account's own switches decide; only logto leaves the earlier account's
                        switches alone.  SDSYS gives them to a group or other account with modify.account where members need them.
             Which files  A file is known by the name of the file that is OPEN, not the name that was typed, so a Q-pointer to a
                        VOC is caught: a VOC is a file called voc; a dictionary is any file whose name ends in .dic (voc.dic is
                        the dictionary of the VOC, not the VOC); a program file is bp, bp.out (the object code), or any name
                        ending in .bp or .bp.out.  Capitals do not matter.
             What stops  A program's WRITE, WRITEV, DELETE and CLEARFILE of such a file: an error that says which switch is
                        missing, or the ON ERROR status.  The verbs and statements that change a file the person names ask first,
                        before they lock or change anything, and give the reason: sed, ide and ced show it in their message line,
                        every other verb prints it and stops.  They are ed, edit, copy and copyp into the file or moving records
                        out of it, delete, clear.file, cname of a record, delete.file of a program file or of a dictionary on its
                        own, fixdir, format, map file, sp.view (its X key), set.trigger when it installs or removes a trigger,
                        copy.list into the file or with deleting from it, delete.list in, get.list deleting in, save.list in,
                        sort.list from, reformat to (and its Output file prompt), list pageseq, a delimited report written to a
                        pathname, cd data, generate, como on in, the .s and .d stack commands on an existing VOC record, and
                        create.file naming a file that already exists (adding a part, or converting to a multifile).  The private
                        catalogue directory counts as a program file.  Programs are also refused (status 3035, no message) by
                        create.file and opentemp of a new file inside such a place, ospath keys 5, 13 and 14, and the socket trace
                        file.  Over the API a client's writes, deletes and file clears are held to the same rule, and SDOpenSeq of
                        such a place opens read only.  cname will not take a program file out of its name, or put a file under
                        one, for a person without the switch, so a rename cannot be used to get round it.  OPENSEQ in such a
                        place opens the file read only and cannot create one; DELETESEQ, a print file made there and !oscopy or
                        !ioscopy into it are refused.  For these operating-system routes the name of the file being made counts
                        only if it is a directory or other than a plain file, so a plain file called sample.bp in an ordinary
                        directory is not held back.  Reading is never stopped.
             Compiling   Without bp.edit the basic, catalog, delete.catalog and decatalog commands are refused, since compiling
                        is how source becomes code that runs: otherwise source could be copied to any file and compiled.
             Internal programs  A program compiled in internal mode is exempt from the gates on files and on operating-system
                        paths, which SD's own commands need.  The "internal" mark is a bit in the program's own object file, so
                        until now a person who could put an object file where SD would run it (a directory file with a .out entry
                        in the VOC, or a run file) could set the bit and run it with every gate skipped, the shell, OS.EXECUTE and
                        sd-on gates included.  SD now honours the bit only for an object from the global catalogue, which only
                        SDSYS can write, or for the administrator; for any other object it is ignored when the object is loaded.
                        SD's own commands are all in the global catalogue and are not affected.  A program of your own that you
                        compiled in internal mode as SDSYS and run as an ordinary user from your account is now an ordinary
                        program there.
             LOGTO       The switches are those of the account the person SIGNED ON to (or, over the API, first entered); logto
                        does not change them, and unlike sh, os and sd the three edit switches are read at sign-on only.  A
                        person with a switch on keeps it in another account, and a person without it does not gain it there.
             Not stopped  What SD writes as a side effect of an ordinary command - a new pointer made by create.file, a new
                        saved sentence, a compiled I-type, the command stack - and anything done from the shell by an account
                        that has sh or os.execute.  The operating-system routes above are not looked at for an account that
                        holds os-on, when the path is outside the account's own directory.  The switches are about changing
                        these files by hand or by program.
             Backups      The account manifest records the three switches (edit.voc, edit.bp, edit.dict) for user accounts.
                        restore.account gives them back only to an account it has to create; an account that already exists
                        keeps its current switches, group and other accounts are not in the manifest, and a manifest from
                        before has none and gives none.
             After install  On a fresh install the installing user's own account starts with all three off like any new
                        account, and the installer prints the three modify.account lines.  A new account needs bp-on before it
                        can save or compile its first program, voc-on before it can save a paragraph in its VOC, and os-on as
                        well for nano and micro.  The user table of the shared memory has a new word, so SD is
                        restarted at the end of an upgrade, as always.  Windows Solo's and Linux Solo's VOC gate (the
                        administrator password) is the same idea for one account.

17 Oct 26  FILEINFO KEY 1021 AND THE PICK STYLE CALL "FILE NAME" (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH E).

             FL$WHO      FILEINFO(file, 1021) now answers as the openQM manual says FL$WHO does: the user numbers of
                        the other sessions that have the file open, with a field mark between them, and null when
                        there are none.  The caller is left out unless it has the file open through more than one file
                        variable.  Until now 1021 answered the Windows port's text form, "12 (don), 14 (ann)"; that is
                        at 1100 (FL$HOLDERS) and is what SD's own "cannot gain exclusive access" messages use.  A
                        program that asked FILEINFO for 1021 by number and wanted the text must ask 1100; programs
                        that use the name FL$HOLDERS or FL$WHO from keys.h are right once they are compiled again.
                        The Windows port still has the text form at 1021.
             CALL file   CALL "file name" (arguments) calls the program compiled into a file, without the catalogue:
                        the string is a file as the VOC names it, a space, and the program; the object directory is the
                        VOC entry file.out.  It can also be held in a variable (CALL @nm).  The file must be a VOC
                        name, found as OPEN finds it, so a Q-pointer follows the OS-access gate as for OPEN; an
                        operating system path, a program name beginning with $ ! * or _, a third word and an unknown
                        file are refused as an illegal call name, and a program that is not in the file as not found.
                        STATUS() is left as it was.

17 Oct 26  L-TYPE DICTIONARY LINKS IN I-TYPES, AND LINKS TO LINKS (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH D).

             Links       An L-type dictionary record names another file and how to find the record in it, and can now
                        be used inside an I-type as well as in a query: an I-type whose expression is TTL%TITLE, or
                        UPCASE(TTL%AUTHOR), or COPY : "/" : TTL%TITLE, fetches the item TITLE or AUTHOR of the file the
                        link TTL names, for the record the link's id expression gives, just as the same expression
                        written with TRANS() would.  Before, a name with a percent sign in an I-type stopped the
                        compile with an internal error message.
             Chains      A link may reach through another link: if READERS has a link OUT to BOOKS, and BOOKS has a
                        link TTL to TITLES, OUT%TTL%TITLE is the title of each book on loan, in a query or in an
                        I-type.  Up to ten links may follow one another.
             Queries     A link named in a query reached only D, I and C items one link away, and treated every
                        result as multivalued.  It now reaches A, S and E items too and follows links to links, and
                        it takes the column heading, format, conversion and association of the item it reaches.
             Field 4     An L record may have a fourth field, an expression that gives the name of the file actually
                        read, for a set of files that share one dictionary; field 3 is still the file whose dictionary
                        is searched.
             Messages    A link whose file has no dictionary, whose item is not in it, that has no id expression or
                        file name, or whose expressions have semicolons, is refused with a message naming the link or
                        the item (11401-11404 are new).  "Link item xx not found" used to name the link, not the item.
             Undefined   An I-type that named a field the dictionary does not have, other than one called F and a
                        number, aborted the compile with "Non-numeric where numeric required" in $ICOMP instead of
                        saying "Field 'xx' is not defined".  It says the second now.
             What it does not do.  A link is worked out when an I-type is compiled, so after a link is changed the
                        I-types that use it need CD (COMPILE.DICT) to follow it; a query that names the link is
                        compiled each time and does not.  The item name after the percent sign cannot contain a hyphen.
                        A record in the dictionary whose id contains the percent sign is found before any link.

17 Oct 26  RUN TIME ERRORS AS EXCEPTIONS, EXCEPTION GROUPS AND TRANSACTIONS IN TRY (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH C).

             SYS.*       Many errors that stop a program can now be caught with TRY / CATCH, under the names
                        openQM gives them: an unassigned variable is SYS.PROGRAM.DATATYPE.UNASSIGNED, divide by zero
                        SYS.PROGRAM.DIV_ZERO, a deadlock SYS.LOCKS.DEADLOCK, the record lock limit SYS.LOCKS.LIMIT,
                        a subroutine that is not catalogued SYS.PROGRAM.NOT_FOUND, a bad print unit
                        SYS.PRINTER.INVALID_UNIT, and about fifty more names for some hundred and twenty errors
                        (the list is on the program control page).
                        @EXCEPTION.DATA is the message the abort would have printed, which is also in @ABORT.MESSAGE.
                        When no handler can catch the exception - none written, or one the throw cannot reach past an
                        EXECUTE, a trigger or a dictionary expression - the error stops the program with the same
                        message as before, so a program that does not use TRY is not changed.  An error that is caught
                        prints and logs nothing.  A few errors have no name and still stop the program whatever
                        handlers there are: the failure of a transaction's commit and the refusals of the account
                        gates (OS access, SH, OS.EXECUTE).  Catching an error does not undo it: a write that failed has
                        not been made.
             Groups      An exception name is a path of components, and a CATCH takes everything below the name it
                        writes: CATCH SYS.LOCKS takes SYS.LOCKS.DEADLOCK, CATCH ACCOUNT takes ACCOUNT.INVALID.CLOSED.
                        Whole components only (CATCH ACC takes neither).  This was missing from TRY when it was added;
                        until now a CATCH took only the exact name.  Where one TRY has several clauses the first that
                        covers the name is used.
             Transactions   A transaction begun inside a TRY clause and still open when an exception leaves it is rolled
                        back, as the openQM manual says (it was not).  A transaction that was open before the TRY is
                        left open.  This applies to THROW as well as to the system exceptions.
             PDEBUG     A phantom that reaches the debugger with no PDEBUG waiting used to end.  If its program has a
                        handler for SYS.PROGRAM.NO_PDEBUG, or for a group above it, SYS$ANY or SYS$UNHANDLED, the
                        exception is thrown instead and the phantom carries on from the handler; with none it ends as
                        before.

16 Oct 26  LINE TERMINATOR MODES AND TRIGGERS ON DIRECTORY FILES (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH B).

             Line ends   A directory file, and a sequential file opened with OPENSEQ, can be put in one of four
                        line terminator modes, as openQM does it: FCONTROL(fvar, FC$CRLF, mode) sets one for that file
                        variable (it answers the mode the file was in; -1 only asks), and a letter in field 6 of the
                        F-type VOC entry chooses the mode for every OPEN or OPENSEQ of the file: L (mode 0, LF only, a CR
                        is data), C (mode 1, CR LF only, so a lone CR or LF is data and WRITE and WRITESEQ put CR LF),
                        B (mode 2, CR LF or LF, a lone CR kept) and X (mode 3, CR LF or LF, every CR thrown away).
                        READ and WRITE of a directory file and READSEQ and WRITESEQ follow the mode; a record longer
                        than the 31,744 bytes SD reads at a time is converted the same however it is cut.  A file
                        with no letter and no FCONTROL behaves exactly as before (READ ends a line at LF and keeps
                        every CR, READSEQ ends it at LF and drops a CR in front of it), so nothing changes for a
                        program that does not ask.  FC$CRLF used to stop the program with a message (11354, now
                        unused).  What it does not do: READBLK, WRITEBLK, OSREAD and OSWRITE never convert, MARK.MAPPING
                        OFF still turns every conversion off, and a hashed file has no line terminators (FCONTROL
                        answers 0 with STATUS 3004).  The manual's two spellings of the last mode, FC$CRLF.NOCR and
                        FC$CRLF.NO.CR, are both in keys.h.
             Triggers   A directory file can have a trigger function.  SET.TRIGGER works on it, LIST.TRIGGERS lists it
                        and FILEINFO(FL$TRIGGER) and (FL$TRG.MODES) answer for it.  The trigger is kept in a small
                        text file called %header% in the directory (trigger=name, modes=n), which SELECT, COUNT and
                        ANALYSE.FILE do not count as a record, CLEAR.FILE leaves, and no record id can name.  READ, WRITE,
                        DELETE and CLEARFILE call the function for the modes that are set, with the arguments a hashed
                        file's trigger gets; it can refuse the operation or change the data.  WRITESEQ, WRITEBLK,
                        OSWRITE and OSDELETE do not call it (openQM's limit).  A change takes effect when the file is
                        next opened.  Whoever can write to the directory can change %header%, as they can add or
                        remove records, so a trigger on a directory file is only as safe as the directory.  CLEAR.FILE
                        of a directory file also stopped losing a directory handle each time it ran.

16 Oct 26  NEWVOC.MODS, PHANTOM DEBUGGER @VARIABLES, QUERY IN / NOT.IN AND USER SORT SYSTEM (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH A).

             NEWVOC.MODS  The optional @SDSYS/newvoc.mods file (already applied to new accounts
                        and by UPDATE.ACCOUNTS) is now also applied to SDSYS's own VOC when SD
                        is installed, and is kept across an upgrade that keeps the accounts:
                        deletesdcore.sh puts it aside in /home/sd and installsdcore.sh puts it
                        back before the first bootstrap pass.
             PDEBUG     Evaluating an @variable (/@var) in the phantom debugger now returns the
                        value from the debugged phantom process rather than from the local
                        PDEBUG client session.  More @variables are known to it.
             IN, NOT.IN Query processor selection clause operators:
                          field IN {NO.CASE} listname
                          field IN {NO.CASE} "filename id"
                        and NOT.IN test whether a field value is present in a saved select list
                        in $SAVEDLISTS or in the fields of a named file record, with
                        support for NO.CASE, EVERY and WHEN clauses.  What it does not do: the
                        values in the list are compared as they are stored, with no input
                        conversion (a date field needs a list of day numbers), and an alternate
                        key index is never used.  IN and NOT.IN are now keywords.  After an
                        = sign an unquoted IN is still an ordinary value, but after OR or AND,
                        where the field and operator are repeated for you, an unquoted IN or
                        NOT.IN is the operator, so quote the value there.  A dictionary item
                        called IN wins over the keyword.  An empty list name ("") says so
                        (message 11400) instead of printing "To:".
             SORT(), SORTADD, SORTCLEAR, SORTDATA(), SORTNEXT()
                        Public BASIC sorting system supporting concurrent sort sessions via
                        sort variables (VARTYPE VT$SORT = 20; up to 255 open in one process, and
                        a sort is freed when its variable is overwritten or goes out of scope),
                        up to 32 keys with per-key SRT$ flags in syscom/keys.h (SRT$LEFT,
                        SRT$RIGHT, SRT$COMPOUND, SRT$RIGHT.FLOAT, SRT$STRICT, plus
                        SRT$DESCENDING, SRT$UNIQUE, SRT$NOCASE and SRT$DATA), and optional
                        key retrieval via SORTNEXT(sortvar, {MAT} keys) or item count limit via
                        SORTDATA(sortvar, max.items).  A BTREE statement given a sort variable
                        is refused.  A variable that is not a sort says "Not a sort variable"
                        (11398) and the 256th open sort says "Too many sorts are open at once"
                        (11399).  What it does not do: keys that compare as equal come out in
                        no promised order once a sort is large enough to spill to a work file
                        (newest first while it stays in memory), a mode above 4 is taken as 0,
                        and only the low byte of the flags is used.
             SORTING   The sort that SORT, SSELECT and the query processor use was reorganised to
                        share its code with the new one.  Its results are unchanged, with one
                        correction: a unique sort that spilled to work files now drops a
                        duplicate that lay in two different files, which the old merge kept.

16 Oct 26  CLASSES, GET, SYNC FILES, LOGTO RESET ALL, WIDE SORTS AND MORE (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCH 4).

             Classes  PUBLIC / PRIVATE var = value
                        A class variable, or a matrix, may be given a starting value: a
                        number, a string, CHAR(n) or one of @FM @VM @SM @TM @IM @AM @SVM
                        @TRUE @FALSE.  It is set when the object is made, before
                        CREATE.OBJECT runs.  It is a constant, not an expression.
                       CLASS c INHERITS other(values)  and  INHERITS other AS name(values)
                        pass constants to the inherited class's CREATE.OBJECT (at most 32).
                       RUN of a class module, or of its catalogue name, makes the object,
                        runs its MAIN and lets the object go.  CALL of a class is still
                        refused.  A class with no MAIN gets the usual object message.
                       A class with 32 names or more is searched through an index built
                        the first time it is needed; a program with a class of hundreds
                        of members ran many times faster.  Behaviour is the same.
             GET var {, n} {SETTING c} FROM device {UNTIL t} {RETURNING last} {WAITING s}
                        {THEN | ELSE}
                        Reads from a FIFO, port, character device or a file opened with
                        OPENSEQ, a character at a time, with a timeout for each wait.  The
                        terminator is kept in the data.  A timeout takes the ELSE and
                        leaves STATUS() at 0.  (GET inside a class still declares a public
                        function, as before.)
             OPEN / OPENPATH ... SYNC, FCONTROL(f, FC$SYNC, state), the S flag in field 6
                        of a file's VOC entry, FILEINFO(f, FL$SYNC)
                        Write a file's data to disk after every write and delete, for a
                        file that must survive a power failure.  Slower, by design;
                        a file without the flag behaves as it did.
             LOGTO name RESET ALL
                        From a stacked command level, casts off every level and runs the
                        new account's LOGIN at level 1.  At level 1 it is the same as
                        RESET.  Refused in an API session (message 11392).
             I-types  $MODE name{,name} ;
                        An I-type may begin with $MODE for CHANGE.NO.OVERLAP,
                        COUNT.OVERLAP, INDEX.OVERLAP, NOCASE.STRINGS, TIME.MS (and
                        COMPATIBLE.APPEND, which is accepted).  Not allowed in an I-type
                        that another one uses; any other mode is refused.
             OSPATH(path, OS$PARENT, OS$UID, OS$GID, OS$MODES, OS$IS.FILE, OS$IS.DIR)
                        New keys for the details of an operating-system path.  They
                        follow the OS-access gate: an account without it is refused as it
                        is for every other path function.
             ED        External commands: a line typed in ED that starts with an
                        external command name (EXTERNAL in $ED.OPTIONS) runs a program
                        on the record being edited.  See the ED page.
             Sorts     The disk sort handles records of any length SD can hold; the
                        lengths in the sort file are 32 bits where they were 16, so a sort
                        of long records is no longer cut off.  The count of a sort that
                        returns no data was one short when the last record ended the file.
             Hashed files  A split that finds the disk nearly full now stops before
                        the file's size is changed, with an entry in the log, instead of
                        leaving a part-split file.  It cannot undo a split that fails
                        after the new group has started to be used; that still needs a
                        file check.
             @(-6) @(-12) @(-14) @(-59)
                        Use the terminfo capabilities eblink, eprot, edim, erev and ebold
                        when the terminal's entry has them (add them in terminfo.mods),
                        and the plain attribute reset when it does not.  No entry that
                        ships with SD has them, so nothing changes until you add them.

             What these do NOT do: a class variable's value cannot be an expression; a
             timeout of GET is for each character, not for the whole read; the sync flag
             does not make a power failure safe for anything else on the machine; RESET ALL
             was not run in an API session (a test cannot host one).

16 Oct 26  THE DEBUGGER, ED, LIST MERGES AND ANALYSE.FILE (QM4 FIXES TAB, NEXT BATCH).

             Debugger  /*           shows every variable of the program, one to a line, the
                        name first.  (Paged; the manual's list you move about in and open
                        with Return is not built: use /name.)
                       ^            shows control characters as ^9 and back to ?.
                       window n     sets the data area of the full-screen debugger to
                        5 to 15 rows; WINDOW n in the VOC record $DEBUG.OPTIONS sets it
                        at the first stop.
                       set v = token  a token that stands for a number, a string or
                        char(n) may be the value (with $MODE DEBUG.EQUATES).
                       A program compiled for the debugger stops before each CASE
                        condition, so stepping shows the one being tried.
             ED        In edit values mode a line number carries the positions above it:
                        2.0001 is value 1 of field 2, 2.3.0001 subvalue 1 of value 3.
                        NO.EV.POSITION in $ED.OPTIONS turns it off.
             LIST.UNION, LIST.INTER, LIST.DIFF, MERGE.LIST
                        Find a key of one list in the other without scanning it: two lists
                        of 15000 keys merge in seconds, where the time grew with the
                        product of their lengths.  The results are the same.
             ANALYSE.FILE file STATISTICS
                        Also shows a histogram of group utilisation: how many groups use up
                        to 10%, 20% ... 100% of their size, and how many are over 100%
                        (have overflow blocks).
             Correlatives  A;NOCASE;expression makes the string operations of that one
                        correlative case insensitive.

             What these do NOT do: the debugger's variable list does not let you move
             about it and open an element.  The group histogram counts a group with its
             overflow blocks together.  Not done in this sitting: IN and NOT.IN in the query
             processor (SD has neither at all, so their saved-list-file form waits for
             them), the I-type $MODE prefix, class variable initialisers, RUN of a class,
             sort items over 32 KB.

16 Oct 26  SMALL FIXES AND OPTIONS (QM4 FIXES TAB, BATCHES 1 AND 2).

             NUMLOCKS      Range checked, 1 to 32767.  The lock table's cells are 16 bits,
                        so a larger value used to wrap; SD now refuses to start with it.
             PHANTOM       A phantom started while SD is suspended gives back its place in
                        the user table.  It used to stay there, looking like a process
                        that was running and could not be logged out.
             SELECT of a directory file
                        At the operating system's limit of open files the select now closes
                        the oldest cached file and tries again, where it used to answer with
                        an empty list and say nothing.
             SET.FILE      The target may be a Q-pointer, which makes a chain of pointers.
                        Not recommended, but it opens; every link is followed with the
                        account test it always had.
             CATALOG bp -name
                        A hyphen is a global catalogue prefix, like * ! _ and $.
                        DELETE.CATALOG -name removes it.
             CONFIGURE.FILE file DIRECTORY REPORTING
                        Shows how many records have been converted, every thousand and at
                        the end; IMMEDIATE shows the groups moved.  Nothing else changes.
             sdfix -Z      Clears the dead space at the end of each group (the bytes after
                        the used part of a block).  It reports how many blocks it cleared,
                        a second run clears none, and no record changes.
             dir_dict      Three items for a directory file's dictionary: DAYS (days since the
                        record was modified), DTM (date and time of the last modification)
                        and OWNER (the user name that owns it).
             OSPATH key 15  The owner's user name, for internal programs (DIR_DICT's OWNER).

             Looked at and found right already: a hangup at the break key prompt ends the
             process; the U format wraps in a report as T and L do; an inherited public
             variable is found beside a function of the same name.

             What these do NOT do: NUMLOCKS cannot be raised past 32767, so a file cannot
             hold more than that many locks at once (openQM's 65535 limit is not matched).
             sdfix -Z is for a file nobody has open.  dir_dict's items answer for a
             directory file only.  Not done in this sitting: LOGTO RESET ALL, and the five
             terminfo codes that end an attribute (eblink and the others), which need
             the extended part of a compiled terminfo entry to be read.

16 Oct 26  A FOURTH PER-ACCOUNT SWITCH: SD.ACCOUNTS.

             modify.account name sd-on | sd-off
                        With sd-on, an account's programs may open, list, copy,
                        delete and print into files by path under the directory of
                        any SD account the user could LOGTO (the register's group is
                        one the user is in, and the account is not suspended).
                        Reading and writing are one switch.  It may be set on a
                        group account.

             What it does NOT do: it is off for a new account.  The other
             account's VOC (voc, voc.dic) is never reachable this way, and a
             directory that is not an SD account's still needs os-on.  SDSYS is
             never refused, and os-on implies sd-on.  An account archive does not
             carry the setting, so a restored account starts with it off.

16 Oct 26  $MODE AUTOLOCK AND OPTION INHERIT.OWNERSHIP.

             $MODE AUTOLOCK
                        A WRITE, MATWRITE or DELETE that does not hold a lock on
                        the record takes an update lock, waiting for it, makes the
                        change and lets the lock go as a plain WRITE does.  For
                        programs written for environments that lock for you; not
                        recommended.  WRITEV is not changed.
             OPTION INHERIT.OWNERSHIP
                        CREATE.FILE gives the new file, and what is in it, the
                        owner and group of the account directory instead of the
                        user's own.  The system lets a user give a file only to a
                        group they belong to, so in practice it is the group: the
                        account's.  Where it refuses nothing is said.

             What these do NOT do: both are off unless asked for.  INHERIT.OWNERSHIP
             is an administrator's choice (set it in the account's LOGIN
             paragraph): the members of the account's group then have on the new
             file what they have on the account.  It does not change files that
             already exist.

16 Oct 26  MODE AND RANGE IN A QUERY.

             LIST file MODE field         the value that comes most often
             LIST file RANGE field        the largest value less the smallest
                        Like MEDIAN: the figure is at the end of the report and at
                        each breakpoint, and NO.NULLS leaves the nulls out.  MODE
                        takes any values; when several come equally often it shows
                        the first.  RANGE takes numbers only.

             What it does NOT do: they are two new words, so a dictionary item
             called MODE or RANGE can no longer be named in a query
             plainly (the same is true of MEDIAN).  Accounts get the words from
             UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, which the installer runs.

16 Oct 26  QUERY PROCESSOR: VERTICAL REPORTS, $QUERY.DEFAULTS, WHEN, BY.EXP; $BASIC.OPTIONS SEARCH; IF IN A DICTIONARY ITEM.

             LIST file field ... VERTICALLY {FMT {"name.format"} {,"data.format"}}
                        The name column of a vertical report can be given a
                        format (FMT "25L", or FIT for a column as wide as the
                        longest heading) and the values a data format (FMT
                        ,"20L"), which every item that is not right justified in
                        its dictionary entry and has no FMT of its own takes.  A
                        record $VLIST in the dictionary or the VOC (X, then the name
                        format or FIT, then the data format) sets them for every
                        vertical report of that file; the command line wins.  A
                        second value of a multivalued field now repeats the colon,
                        and a line that wrapped has none, so the two can be told
                        apart.
             $QUERY.DEFAULTS
                        Field 3 holds default words for SELECT, SSELECT and SEARCH,
                        as field 2 does for LIST and SORT, and field 4 the format of
                        the id column the query processor makes up when the
                        dictionary has no @ID (a width and L, R, T or C).
             WHEN field
                        With no operator it keeps the values that are not null; it
                        used to keep the null ones.  WHEN field BETWEEN a b compared
                        only the first value of a multivalued field; it now compares
                        them all.  UNLIKE NO.CASE ignores the case of the pattern.
             BY.EXP f1 BY.EXP f2
                        More than one exploded sort, provided the fields are in the
                        same association; one outside it is refused.
             $BASIC.OPTIONS: SEARCH file.list
                        The files an $INCLUDE with no file name looks in, in order,
                        @ being the file the source is in.  Without it: that file,
                        then SYSCOM, as before.
             A or S dictionary item, field 8
                        May begin with an IF expression, which is taken as an
                        A-correlative without the A; in front.  It was run as a
                        conversion code and gave the field's own value.

             What these do NOT do: VERTICALLY FMT and $VLIST do not change a
             report that is not vertical.  The data format leaves a right
             justified item and an item with its own FMT alone.  The SEARCH list
             of a C-type dictionary item has no @ (it has no source file).

16 Oct 26  THE APPLICATION EVENT PROFILER.

             PROFILER ON {USER n} {event ...}
             PROFILER OFF {USER n}
             PROFILER {DISPLAY} {USER n}
                        Records what the programs of a session do and where: the
                        events CALL, OPEN, READ (READU, READV, MATREAD...), WRITE,
                        DELETE, OPENSEQ, READSEQ, WRITESEQ, EXECUTE (the first 32
                        characters of the command) and OBJECT (a call of a class
                        module's object), counted by what they were on (the file,
                        the subroutine, the command), the program and its line
                        number.  DISPLAY shows the event types with their counts;
                        type the number of a line to go down to the names, then the
                        programs, then the line numbers, B to go back and Q to
                        leave.  Only the administrator can profile another user's
                        session (USER n): a profile holds the files and commands of
                        that user.

             What it does NOT do: openQM's display uses the cursor keys, this one
             asks for a line number.  The profile lives in memory while it is on
             and is put in the $ipc file when it is turned off, not in a file in a
             temporary directory, so it is gone when SD is restarted.  It holds
             4,000 different events and says how many it could not log.  A
             user's program is shown by the pathname of its object code.  A
             session needs the new version of SD running: the installer restarts it.

16 Oct 26  OPTION STACKED.ACCOUNT.

             OPTION STACKED.ACCOUNT
                        An EXECUTE of a command that does a LOGTO now leaves
                        the process in the account it began in.  Without the
                        option it stays in the account the command went to, as
                        it always did.  The revert undoes LOGTO's change and
                        does not make a new entry: no membership test, no
                        ON.LOGTO or LOGIN paragraph, and the account's SH and
                        OS.EXECUTE switches go back to the ones the first
                        account had, so nothing the visited account granted is
                        kept.

             What it does NOT do: a LOGTO typed at the prompt is not reverted,
             and what an aborted EXECUTE leaves was not measured.  The option
             is off unless you set it.

16 Oct 26  A REPORT AS XML.

             LIST file field ... XML {ELEMENTS} {WITH.DTD | DTD.ONLY}
                                     {WITH.SCHEMA | SCHEMA.ONLY}
                        The report is an XML document: the declaration, a root
                        element ROOT, and an element for each record named for
                        the file.  A single valued field is an attribute of it,
                        a row of an association is a tag named for the
                        association, and a multivalued field in no association
                        is a tag for each value, named for the field with _MV
                        added.  ELEMENTS makes every field an element of its
                        own.  WITH.DTD puts a DOCTYPE with the DTD before the
                        data, WITH.SCHEMA an XML Schema document before it, and
                        DTD.ONLY and SCHEMA.ONLY give the DTD or the schema and
                        no data.  Any query verb that reports takes it.

             What it does NOT do: the openQM manual shows the data and not the
             DTD or the schema, so those two are SD's own.  An XML report
             cannot have breakpoints, totals or other arithmetic (it says so
             and stops); a heading, a footing, DET.SUP and PAN are ignored.
             A field with no value is left out.  A character in a field name
             that XML does not allow in a name becomes an underscore.  The six
             new words are in the VOC of an account after UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, which
             the installer runs.

16 Oct 26  STDFIL.SHARED, DEBUG.EQUATES AND COMMAND.LEVEL.LOCKS.

             $MODE STDFIL.SHARED
                        The default file variable, @STDFIL, as one variable
                        for every program and subroutine of a command level.
                        A subroutine you CALL uses the file the main program
                        opened, and a file it opens is the main program's
                        default afterwards.  An EXECUTE starts a command level
                        of its own with a fresh @STDFIL and gives the caller's
                        back when it returns.  $MODE STDFIL (one for each
                        program) is as it was, and the two together are STDFIL.
             $MODE DEBUG.EQUATES
                        A program compiled for the debugger with this mode keeps
                        its EQUATE and $DEFINE tokens in the object.  The
                        debugger then shows, SETs and WATCHes a token equated to
                        a variable, a matrix element or a field of a dynamic
                        array variable, and shows a token of a number, a string
                        or CHAR(n) as NAME = value.
             OPTION COMMAND.LEVEL.LOCKS
                        A file variable that goes (a subroutine returns) no
                        longer takes its locks with it: the file is kept open,
                        out of sight, until the program is back at the command
                        processor, or the EXECUTE that started the level ends.

             What these do NOT do: STDFIL.SHARED uses a named common block
             called $STDFIL, so do not declare one of your own.  DEBUG.EQUATES
             makes the object larger, and a token of a constant cannot be SET
             or WATCHed.  COMMAND.LEVEL.LOCKS does not change CLOSE, which still
             closes at once, and it holds a file open for as long as a lock on it
             is held, so a program that opens many files in a loop and drops them
             holds them all until it ends.  The option is off unless you set it.

16 Oct 26  RESTRICTED IN A CLASS MODULE.

             RESTRICTED var {READONLY}, var ...
             RESTRICTED SUBROUTINE name{(args)} ... END
             RESTRICTED FUNCTION name{(args)} ... END
                        A name between PUBLIC and PRIVATE: it is the class's
                        own and every class that inherits it can use it
                        through the inherited object; the main program, an
                        unrelated class and a sibling that shares only the
                        parent cannot - they are told "Unrecognised
                        property/method name", as for a private name.  An
                        object that inherits the class does not pass its
                        restricted names on to the caller.  READONLY holds as
                        it does for PUBLIC.  A name is all public or all
                        restricted: a PUBLIC SUBROUTINE foo beside a
                        RESTRICTED FUNCTION foo is a compile error.

             What it does NOT do: an initial value (RESTRICTED x = 1) is not
             accepted - PUBLIC and PRIVATE do not take one either.  RESTRICTED
             is a reserved word inside a program now.  A class compiled before
             this release has no restricted names; recompiling a class that
             uses the new word is what makes one.

16 Oct 26  LOCK.TRACER.

             LOCK.TRACER ON / OFF     (administrator only)
                        While it is on, every lock any session takes or lets
                        go is added to the file locktrace in the system
                        directory: a record update lock, a read lock, one
                        raised or lowered, a file lock, and each release.  ON
                        empties the file first.  It shows when a hold-up began
                        and which program, on which line, took the lock.
             LOCK.TRACER {USER n} {LPTR {n}} {NO.PAGE}
                        The log as a report: date and time, user number,
                        action, program, line, file and record id.  USER n
                        shows one session.  LPTR prints it.
             The file is made with mode 0620 in the system directory's
             group: the administrator reads it, the users of SD append to
             it.  A link in its place is refused.  Each line is value mark
             delimited (epoch time, user, pathname, record id, action number,
             program's object, offset in hexadecimal, line number) for a
             program of your own to read.

             What it does NOT do: it logs everything, SD's own locks (a login
             takes $ipc, the command processor $command.stack) as well, and
             every lock costs a file write, so use it for short spells.  A
             lock that is refused or has to wait is not logged until it is
             granted.  The locks a killed or lost session leaves are cleared
             by the cleanup without a line.  openQM's report needs a terminal
             132 wide; this one is 120.

16 Oct 26  CLEANUP, PHANTOMS AND PHANTOM USER.

             CLEANUP=n (sd.conf)
                        Seconds between the checks that look for sessions that
                        ended without logging out (a killed terminal, a lost
                        connection) and clear them out of the user table.  30
                        to 3600; if the line is not there it is 300, the five
                        minutes it always was.  The daemon wakes every ten
                        seconds, so an interval is kept to within ten seconds.
                        CONFIG lists it, CONFIG('CLEANUP') reads it.  A very
                        short interval costs a pass over the user table that
                        often.
             PHANTOMS=u,n (sd.conf)
                        Keeps n user numbers from u for phantoms started with
                        USER.  Nobody else is given one of them: not a session,
                        not an API session, not a phantom started without USER.
                        It must lie between 1 and the highest user number and
                        must not overlap FIXUSERS or PORTMAP, or SD will not
                        start.  CONFIG lists it, CONFIG('PHANTOMS') reads it
                        ("u,n", null when none are kept).
             PHANTOM USER n command
                        The phantom is user number n, one of the numbers
                        PHANTOMS keeps.  It is refused, and no phantom is
                        made, when n is not a number SD has kept, or when a
                        phantom (or anyone) already has it.
             What changed besides: two blocks of kept user numbers side by
             side (FIXUSERS then PORTMAP, now PHANTOMS too) used to hand out
             the first number of the second block, because the number after a
             skipped block was not looked at again; both are skipped now.

             What it does NOT do: with USPLIT=1 the numbers PHANTOMS names are
             not kept (USPLIT ignores FIXUSERS, PHANTOMS, PORTMAP and USERPOOL),
             though USER n still works for a free number in them.  A change to
             either line takes an SD restart.  CLEANUP only says how often the
             check runs; what a check does is as before.

15 Oct 26  CLIENT LIBRARY HEADERS FOR C# AND PHP.

             The client library's headers for programs in other languages
             are installed in /usr/local/sdsys/syscom.  Two more are there:
             sdclient.cs, for C# and .NET (P/Invoke), and sdclient.php, for
             PHP 7.4 and later with the FFI extension.  Like the six before
             them (C, Basic, PureBasic, Gambas 3, Free Pascal, Python) each
             declares every function of the library with its parameters.
             The C# file passes strings as bytes, so that SD's field, value
             and subvalue marks arrive unharmed, and names the library
             "sdclilib", so that the same file works with sdclilib.dll on a
             Windows client and sdclilib.so on a Linux one.

             What it does NOT do: neither file has been run - no .NET or PHP
             runtime was available when they were written.  They are checked
             against the library's header (every function, every parameter
             and result type, the calling convention, the status constants,
             and the PHP declarations read by a C compiler beside the header),
             which is not the same as a program calling the library.  A Java
             header is not provided (a Java program can call the C library).

15 Oct 26  SD -STATUS ON A SERVER THAT RECORDED NO START TIME.

             The text sd -status prints when the running SD has no start
             time ("not recorded (started by an older build)") was one byte
             longer than the buffer it was copied into.  The compiler warned
             about it on every build.  The buffer is larger now, so the
             overrun cannot happen.  Nothing else changes in the output.

15 Oct 26  $MODE SELECTV: THE SELECT LIST VARIABLE IS OPTIONAL.

             A program that begins $MODE SELECTV (SELECT then builds a select
             list variable, as SELECTV does) no longer has to name the
             variable.  SELECT f, SELECTV f and SSELECTV f with no TO put the
             list in the program's own default select list variable, and
             READNEXT with no FROM reads that variable.  A READNEXT before any
             SELECT takes its ELSE clause.  Together with $MODE PICK.SELECT,
             EXECUTE 'SELECT ...' followed by SELECT f leaves the executed
             list in the default variable.

             What it does NOT do: without the mode SELECTV and SSELECTV with
             no TO are still a compile error and READNEXT still reads list 0;
             CLEARSELECT with no list number still clears list 0 (it cannot
             name the default variable); a list made by EXECUTE is in list 0,
             which a bare READNEXT does not read in this mode (READNEXT id
             FROM 0 does).  The default variable belongs to the program: a
             LOCAL SUBROUTINE shares it, a subroutine you CALL has its own.

15 Oct 26  LISTF, LISTFL AND LISTFR: MULTIFILES AND WITH / WITHOUT.

             A multifile is now listed with a line for each subfile: the type
             column has a value for each subfile, so that each type is on the
             line of that subfile's pathname, and the first begins with Mult
             (Mult DH, then Dir, for a multifile of a hashed and a directory
             subfile).  The type column is wider (12 characters) and the
             description narrower to make room.  The three verbs also take
             WITH or WITHOUT TRIGGER, ENCRYPTION and REPLICATION, as LISTF
             WITH TRIGGER for the files that have a trigger function.

             What it does NOT do: SD has no encryption and no replication, so
             WITH ENCRYPTION and WITH REPLICATION select nothing and WITHOUT
             selects every file.  Directory files cannot have a trigger in
             SD, so none shows a T.

15 Oct 26  SOCKET TRACING.

             SET.SOCKET.MODE(skt, SKT$INFO.TRACE, path) writes a log of the
             data sent and received on the socket: SEND or RECV and the byte
             count, then the data sixteen bytes to a line in hexadecimal and
             as characters.  A null path, or closing the socket, ends it; an
             existing file is overwritten.  The log can hold passwords, so it
             is owner-only (0600), a link at the path is not followed, and the
             path is held to the OS-access gate: an account without OS.EXECUTE
             can trace only into its own directory (SET.SOCKET.MODE gives 0).

             What it does NOT do: on a TLS socket it logs the data as the
             program sees it, not the encrypted bytes; SKT$INFO.TRACE is 10,
             SD's own number.

15 Oct 26  FCONTROL() FOR PROGRAMS, AND LIST.COMMON DETAIL.

             FCONTROL(file.var, action {, qualifier}).  A program can now use
             the openQM 4 function, with its action numbers (the FC$ tokens
             are in keys.h):
             2  FC$SET.SEQ.KEY n   the next id WRITE ... CREATING.SEQKEY gives
                                    out; returns 1
             3  FC$GET.SEQ.KEY     takes that id and moves the counter on, to
                                    reserve an id without writing a record
             5                      field access: always 1 (SD has no field
                                    level encryption)
             6  FC$VALID.ID id     1 if the id could be written to this file
             7  FC$MINMOD n        the minimum modulus of a hashed file
             2, 3 and 7 are refused on a file opened read only (STATUS()
             3018, nothing changed) and on a file that is not hashed (3005).
             FC$SYNC (1) and FC$CRLF (4) stop the program with a message:
             SD has no forced-write mode and no line terminator modes, and an
             answer that looked like success would be worse than no answer.
             The function used inside SD for file control, also called
             FCONTROL, is now FCONTROL.INT, and its FC$ tokens FCI$; programs
             of your own never used it (it is for internal programs only).

             LIST.COMMON DETAIL shows beside each named common block the name
             of the program that created it.  What it does NOT do: the name is
             that of the object file's program as SD records it, which is the
             program that first ran the COMMON statement in the session.

15 Oct 26  TWO FAULTS IN THE RESTRICTED CLIENT SESSION (SDCLIENT 1 AND 2).

             - A client that asked SDExecute to run a command in a session
               restricted with SDCLIENT 1 or 2 was refused, but the refusal
               text was copied past the end of a one byte buffer in the
               client library, which could end the client program with a
               heap error.  SDExecute now copies only what the reply holds.
             - With SDCLIENT 2 only subroutines flagged $sdcall may be called
               from a client.  Any other subroutine was reported as refused
               and still ran: the refusal was sent but the call went on.  It
               now stops at the refusal.

             What it does NOT do: SDCLIENT 0 (the default) is unchanged.

15 Oct 26  ED AND SED TAKE CREATING.SEQKEY, AND THE RECORD-ID COUNTER NO
           LONGER GIVES OUT AN ID TWICE.

             ED and SED.  CREATING.SEQKEY can be given in place of a record
             id: ED file CREATING.SEQKEY, SED DICT file CREATING.SEQKEY, or
             typed at SED's Record name prompt.  The editor takes the file's
             next sequential id - the counter WRITE ... CREATING.SEQKEY uses -
             and shows it like any record id.  The id is taken when the edit
             begins, so a record that is never filed leaves a gap in the
             sequence, and an id already used by a record is skipped.  A file
             that is not a hashed file, such as a directory file, is refused
             with a message, as is a file opened read only.  CREATING.SEQKEY
             in quotes is an ordinary record id.

             The counter.  A file that gave out record ids with WRITE ...
             CREATING.SEQKEY could lose its counter, and so hand out an id
             that was already in use, when something else wrote the file's
             header from a copy it had read earlier: closing, writing,
             splitting or clearing the file, creating or deleting an index
             (the long one), setting a journal or a trigger.  Those now write
             back only the part of the header they own.  A header lock left
             held on two failure paths of FCONTROL, which stalled the
             CREATING.SEQKEY writes of that process, is released, and the
             upgrade of a file's case handling now carries the counter into
             the rebuilt file.  A refused write to a read-only file no longer
             uses up an id.  sdfix's H command now
             shows the next id the file will give out (Next seq id) and warns
             if it is negative.

             An id in use is skipped.  WRITE ... CREATING.SEQKEY (and the
             editors, and SDWriteSeqKey over the API) now looks at the file
             before it uses an id: if a record already has that id - a file
             made by COPY or COPYP, whose counter starts at the first id even
             when numeric-id records were copied in, or records written by a
             program that chose its own numbers - the id is skipped and the
             next one is tried, so the record is not overwritten.  openQM does
             not look; this is a deliberate difference.  The skipped numbers
             leave gaps.  CLEAR.FILE keeps the counter, so the ids carry on
             after a clear (CONFIGURE.FILE file NEXT 1 starts them again).

             What it does NOT do: it does not make a copy carry the source
             file's counter, and a transaction looks only at the records
             already committed to the file (an id this process took earlier is
             never given out twice in any case).

15 Oct 26  THE CLIENT LIBRARY MAY BE USED FROM SEVERAL THREADS.

             Until now the client library (sdclilib.so) kept the current
             session, its one transfer buffer and the arguments of SDCallx
             in plain globals, so two threads of one program interleaved
             them and a program with more than one thread could not use it.
             Now:
             - The current session is each thread's own: SDSetSession and
               SDGetSession act on the calling thread, so two threads can
               work on two sessions at the same time.  A thread starts on
               session 0; a thread given a session by another selects it
               with SDSetSession first.
             - Connects from several threads at the same moment each get a
               slot of their own, and a local server's pipes are not handed
               to another thread's server.
             - The transfer buffer and the SDCallx arguments are per thread
               and are released when the thread ends.
             - A connection that fails keeps its slot for the thread that
               made it, so SDError() still says why after another thread
               connected and disconnected.  It is given back by that
               thread's next connect, SDSetSession or SDDisconnect, by its
               end, or by SDDisconnectAll.
             - Host names are looked up with getaddrinfo (the error number
               in the message is the one gethostbyname gave), and a network
               socket is close-on-exec, so a server started by another
               thread does not hold it open.

             What it does NOT do: it does not make one session safe for two
             threads at once - a program that can reach a session from more
             than one thread takes its own lock round each action, and an
             SDExecute with the SDRespond calls that answer it is one
             action.  A thread that used to rely on the process-wide
             current session now selects its session with SDSetSession.

15 Oct 26  SEQUENTIAL FILES OVER THE API, AND WRITE ... CREATING.SEQKEY.

             SEQUENTIAL FILES.  The client library (and the BASIC class
             sdclient and the six language headers) can open, read, write and
             position an operating system file, as OPENSEQ and its statements
             do, over requests 61 to 67:
             SDOpenSeq(file, id, modes)
                        With an id, file is a directory file and id the item
                        in it; with a null id, file is a pathname.  Returns a
                        file number (they start at 10001, apart from SDOpen's)
                        or 0 with SDStatus() and SDError().  modes adds up:
                        2 give up if another process has it, 16 read only,
                        256 append, 512 overwrite, 1024 flush after every
                        write, 4096 shared.
             SDReadSeq(fno, err)  SDReadBlk(fno, bytes, actual)
             SDWriteSeq(fno, rec)  SDWriteBlk(fno, rec, bytes)
             SDSeek(fno, offset, relto)  SDWeofSeq(fno)
                        A line or a block read or written, a position, a
                        truncation.  SDClose closes the file.
             THE GATES.  A session restricted with SDCLIENT cannot open any
             file.  A pathname outside the account's own directory is refused
             unless the account holds os-on, as OPENSEQ refuses it to any
             program: the API server is an internal program, which the kernel
             does not hold to that gate, so it asks the gate itself.

             WRITE / WRITEU ... CREATING.SEQKEY id.var.  A hashed file now
             keeps a counter in its header (4 bytes in a header block that was
             never full; older files hold zero, which reads as 1, and an older
             SD ignores it).  The statement writes the record under the
             counter's next value and gives that id, as text, to the
             variable; the update lock is released after a WRITE and kept by a
             WRITEU; in a transaction the id is taken at the WRITE.  A
             directory file is refused.  CONFIGURE.FILE file NEXT n sets the
             counter and NEXT QUERY shows it; FILEINFO(file, 1031) is the id
             of the last record written this way; rebuilding a file with
             CONFIGURE.FILE carries the counter over.
             (Unlike openQM, SD skips an id that already names a record - see
             the entry above.)
             SDWriteSeqKey(fno, rec) and SDWriteuSeqKey(fno, rec) do the same
             over the API and return the id.  The CREATING.SEQKEY option of
             the ED and SED editors is the entry above it; sdfix's check of
             the counter against the records is not done.

15 Oct 26  QMCLIENT: DICTIONARY ITEMS AND CONVERSIONS OVER THE API, SEVEN
           FIXES, AND A HEADER FOR EACH OF SIX LANGUAGES.

             NEW FUNCTIONS of the client library (and the BASIC class
             sdclient), requests 55 to 60 of the protocol:
             SDEvaluate(dictfno, names, data, id)
                        Evaluates dictionary items on the server against a
                        record you give it: data becomes @RECORD, id becomes
                        @ID.  dictfno is the number SDOpen("DICT file") gave.
                        Several names, separated by value marks, give their
                        answers joined by item marks.  D, A, S, C and I items
                        all work.  An I-type or a correlative that has not
                        been compiled is compiled for the call; the dictionary
                        is NOT written to.  An item that is not in the
                        dictionary gives the null string; a phrase, or an
                        expression that will not compile, gives the null
                        string and SDError() says which.
             SDEvalConv(dictfno, names, data, id)
                        The same, and each item's output conversion is applied
                        to every value of its answer.
             SDIConv(data, code) SDOConv(data, code)
                        ICONV() and OCONV() on the server, which has every
                        conversion code.
             SDIConvs(data, code) SDOConvs(data, code)
                        ICONVS() and OCONVS(): each element of a dynamic array
                        in turn.  After any of the four SDStatus() is the
                        conversion's own status.
             SDRecordlocked(fno, id)
                        RECORDLOCKED(): -3 -2 -1 another user holds a file,
                        update or read lock, 0 not locked, 1 2 3 this session
                        holds a read, update or file lock.  (The server has
                        answered this request for a long time; there was no
                        client function to ask it.)
             A session that the administrator has restricted with SDCLIENT
             cannot open a file, so it cannot evaluate a dictionary item; and
             it is refused a conversion code that reads a file (T with a file)
             or calls a program (U).  Ordinary codes are not affected.

             FIXES, each of them found by a test that failed first:
             * More than four sessions.  A program could hold four at once and
               the fifth SDConnect failed.  It is now 1024; the server's own
               user table (USERPOOL, 1023) is the real limit.
             * A session waiting for input was damaged when another session
               of the same program connected and disconnected: the client
               freed the buffer they share, and the answer to the prompt never
               reached the command.
             * A local session whose server had ended (AUTOLOGOUT ran out, or
               it was killed) made the client wait for ever on its next
               request, because the client kept the server's ends of its own
               pipes open; and a write to it could end the program with
               SIGPIPE.  The request now fails at once and says so.  AUTOLOGOUT
               itself needed no change: an API session can run the command and
               is logged out after the idle time, as a terminal is.
             * SDDcount used only the first character of its delimiter.  A
               delimiter of several characters is counted as DCOUNT() counts
               it.
             * The MASTER.LOGIN paragraph of the SDSYS VOC is now run for an
               API session, once, when it first enters an account and before
               that account's LOGIN, as for a terminal.  There is none unless
               an administrator writes one.
             * The BASIC class sdclient: a connect() that failed to reach the
               server left error empty and ServerStatus unset.  ServerStatus
               is now the status of the failed open and error names the host
               and port.
             * The PureBasic header declared SDMarkMapping and never defined
               it, declared SDLocate as returning a string where the library
               returns a number, and SDRevision could be called before the
               library was open.
             Checked and found right, with a test added so that they stay so:
             SDChange on overlapping strings, and SDLogto's return value.

             HEADERS.  sdsys/syscom now holds a header for each of six
             languages, all declaring every function of the library:
             sdclilib.h (C), sdclient.bas (Basic), sdclient.pb (PureBasic),
             and new today sdclient.module (Gambas 3), sdclilibbind.pas (Free
             Pascal and Lazarus; written out in full, with the routines that
             load and release the library) and sdclient.py (Python 3, ctypes).
             The C header was missing SDCallx, SDGetArg, SDDebug, SDLogto and
             SDRecordlock, and the PureBasic header SDCallx0 to SDCallx20,
             SDGetArg, SDLogto and SDDebug; they are there now.  None of them
             declares SDConnectUDS (removed) or the two package functions
             (not supported).  The Free Pascal unit has not been compiled, nor
             the Gambas module loaded: neither compiler is on the machine they
             were written on.

15 Oct 26  QMCLIENT: SIX MORE API FUNCTIONS.

             The client library (sdclilib.so, the BASIC class sdclient and the
             Basic / PureBasic declarations) gains:
             SDRevision()   Before a connection, the client's revision; once
                        connected, the client's and the server's, as two
                        values.  A server that does not know the request is
                        reported as 0.0-0.
             SDGetVar(name) The value of an @-variable of the session on the
                        server, as a string; the @ may be left off.  Names the
                        server knows: SELECTED, LOGNAME, USER, WHO, PATH,
                        USERNO, TTY, DS, SENTENCE, COMMAND, SYSTEM.RETURN.CODE,
                        USER.RETURN.CODE, DATE, TIME, ID, ANS and USER0 to
                        USER4.  Any other name gives the null string.
             SDSystem(key)  SYSTEM(key) on the server.
             SDIndices(fno, name)
                        INDICES() on the server: with a null name the index
                        names of the file, otherwise the details of that index.
             SDTrans(file, id, field, action) and SDRTrans(...)
                        TRANS() on the server.  Field is a dictionary name, a
                        field number or -1 for the whole record; action C
                        (the id if there is no record) or X (null; the default).
                        SDTrans lowers the marks of each record's answer one
                        level and joins a list of ids' answers with value
                        marks; SDRTrans leaves the marks as they are.  The file
                        and its dictionary are opened as OPEN opens them for the
                        API: not at all for an SDNet or SDCLIENT session, and
                        a file marked trusted is refused.  The B action (open
                        in binary) is not supported.
             SDTxn(mode)    Starts (1), commits (2) or aborts (3) a transaction
                        on the server, or (4) commits one without making it
                        durable (below).  A commit or abort with no
                        transaction running, or a mode out of range, is
                        refused with a message.
             The new requests are numbers 49 to 54 of the protocol; the
             Windows port's client stops at 48.  (QMEvalConv and the
             QMIConv / QMOConv family follow, in the entry above.)

             NON-DURABLE TRANSACTIONS.  The COMMIT of a transaction nested in
             another normally writes its updates to the files at once, and
             they stay written if the outer transaction is rolled back.  With
             $MODE NON.DURABLE.TXN in the program (or SDTxn mode 4) the
             nested COMMIT writes nothing: its updates, deletes and locks pass
             to the outer transaction, which writes them when it commits and
             discards them if it is rolled back.  A later write of the same
             record by the nested transaction replaces the outer one's.  A
             COMMIT of a transaction that is not nested is unchanged.

15 Oct 26  INDEX SCANS, DISABLE.INDEX, BIG LETTERS, PHANTOM GROUP AND MORE.

             BSCAN value {, ids} FROM file.var {, key} USING index
                    {RESET} {BY A | D} THEN / ELSE
                        Scans an alternate key index entry by entry, left
                        to right (BY A, the default) or right to left.  With
                        a key it goes to that entry or, if the index has none,
                        returns the nearest one in the direction of the scan
                        and takes ELSE with STATUS() 2.  RESET starts at the
                        extreme left or right.  THEN means an entry was found
                        (STATUS() 1); ELSE with STATUS() 0 is the end of the
                        index, 3 a file that is not a dynamic file, 4 an index
                        the file has not, 5 a sequence other than A or D.
             ROOT file.name, index TO scan.var THEN / ELSE
             KEY( operator, scan.var, index.key, item.id ) THEN / ELSE
                        The Pick way of the same thing.  ROOT makes the scan
                        variable (it is a string: the file name and the index
                        name; ELSE with STATUS() 3019 for an index the file
                        has not).  KEY takes C (first entry at or beyond the
                        key), L (the last entry), N (the next), P (the
                        previous), R (the first id of the key), V (is this
                        key and id in the index) and X (all the ids of the
                        key, field mark delimited), in upper or lower case.
                        Failure is ELSE with STATUS() 3030 past either end,
                        1 for no such entry, 2 for any other operator.  ROOT
                        takes the name of an index only, not an A-correlative
                        that an index was built from.  A program that has a
                        matrix called KEY still gets its matrix.  Numbered
                        list 10 is the scratch list of all of these.
             SELECTLEFTV and SELECTRIGHTV index FROM file.var
                    {SETTING key} TO list.var
                        SELECTLEFT and SELECTRIGHT into a select list
                        variable.
             DISABLE.INDEX file index...   or   DISABLE.INDEX file ALL
                        Stops an index being kept up to date and stops the
                        query processor using it, for a large load of records
                        or a batch that would change most of them.  BUILD.INDEX
                        builds it again and switches it on; until then LIST.INDEX
                        shows it as needing a build.  Like DELETE.INDEX it
                        needs the file to itself: an index switched off while
                        another session had the file open would go on being
                        used, and kept up, there.  (openQM later dropped that
                        need; SD does not.)
             BLOCK.PRINT text   and   BLOCK.TERM text
                        Print text, or show it on the terminal, in letters
                        seven rows high drawn with W.  Eight letters fit an
                        80 column line; longer text breaks at a space where it
                        can.  Letters, digits and . , ! ? - : / + = * ( ) ' have
                        shapes, small letters print as capitals, anything
                        else as a question mark.  openQM's $BIGLTRS record,
                        which changes the drawing character, is not read.
             FILE.STAT {DICT} {file.list} {LPTR} {NO.PAGE}
                        One line for each file of the account (or of the
                        select list, or of the names given): type, current
                        and minimum modulus, group size, record count, bytes
                        in the primary and overflow subfiles, and Y or N for
                        indices, trigger, statistics, case insensitive ids,
                        NO.RESIZE, encryption, replication and journalling.
                        The last three are always N here.  openQM can also
                        report on the files of other accounts; SD does not.
             SWAPMARKS(string)
                        The string as it is, which is what openQM gives on a
                        system without its extended character set.
             SYSTEM(1071)
                        After OSREAD, the STATUS array of the file that was
                        read: permissions, size, links, owner, inode, times,
                        pathname and type.  Null before any OSREAD and after
                        one that failed.
             OPTION QUERY.MERGE.PRINT
                        A query sent to printer 0 while the printer is already
                        on leaves it on at the end of the report, so that the
                        report and what the program prints round it are one
                        job.  Without it the report closes the job, as before.
             PHANTOM GROUP command
                        The phantom is logged out when the process that
                        started it ends.  SYSTEM(1073) lists the user numbers
                        of those grouped with this process (SYSTEM(1058) lists
                        all its phantoms) and LISTU shows a G after the parent
                        number of a grouped phantom.  A parent that goes before
                        the phantom has finished starting takes it with it.
             SET.QUEUE queue, width, depth, top.margin, bottom.margin,
                    mode {, options}   and   SP.ASSIGN {options} {, setptr}
                        Pick style form queues.  SET.QUEUE (0 to 999) keeps
                        what SETPTR would be given for a print unit; mode is
                        1 (printer), 3 (hold file) or 6 (both), the options
                        are SETPTR's.  SET.QUEUE queue shows one queue,
                        SET.QUEUE DISPLAY {LPTR} all of them, SET.QUEUE
                        DELETING queue removes one.  SP.ASSIGN F3 sets print
                        unit 0 from queue 3; n copies, R unit, H (hold file:
                        with S mode 3, without it mode 6), O (keep open) and
                        a SETPTR list after a comma can follow, as in
                        "SP.ASSIGN 2R4HF3,BANNER ""Sales""".  SP.ASSIGN with
                        nothing says which queue unit 0 has.  Options of the
                        queue that do not belong to the mode used (AT for a
                        hold file, AS for printing) are left out.
                        GETPU(PU$FORM.QUEUE, unit) and SYSTEM(1066) give the
                        queue last assigned to a unit and to any unit (-1 if
                        none).  THE FORMS ARE KEPT IN THE ACCOUNT, in the
                        directory file $FORMS, not in one file shared by every
                        account as openQM does: a queue names a printer and a
                        hold file, and a definition any account could change
                        would change where everybody's reports go.  An account
                        can point its own $FORMS entry at another's.
             FSTAT.USER {TOP} {USER n} {LPTR {unit}} {NO.PAGE}
                        File activity of each session: reads, writes and
                        deletes since it started (TOP puts the busiest
                        first); USER n adds opens, clears, selects, index
                        reads, writes and deletes, splits and merges.  An
                        administrator sees every session, anyone else the
                        sessions of their own login name.  It shows the
                        figures once; openQM's repeats every two seconds.
             @PSTAT     Text a program can leave in @PSTAT for PSTAT to show
                        under the command and the call stack of that process
                        ("PSTAT USER n").  Marks in it print as spaces; at
                        most 4000 characters.

15 Oct 26  SELECT.SOCKET() AND SELECTINDEXV.

             SELECT.SOCKET(matrix, milliseconds)
                        Waits on the sockets held in the elements of a
                        dimensioned matrix.  What to watch is set with
                        SET.SOCKET.MODE(skt, SKT$INFO.EVENTS, events), the
                        events being SKT$READ.EVENT 1 (data, a connection
                        waiting on a server socket, or the peer closing),
                        SKT$WRITE.EVENT 2 and SKT$EXCEPTION.EVENT 4.  The
                        result has a field for each element: the letters R, W
                        and E found there.  A time-out is a null string with
                        STATUS() 1011.  SKT$INFO.EVENTS is 9: openQM's manual
                        gives no number, so it is SD's own.  A TLS socket is
                        watched at the TCP connection.
             SELECTINDEXV index {, value} FROM file.var TO list.var
                        SELECTINDEX into a select list variable.  Numbered
                        list 10 is its scratch list and is empty afterwards.

15 Oct 26  INHIBIT.LOGIN, AND A KILLED BACKUP NO LONGER LOCKS EVERYBODY OUT.

             INHIBIT.LOGIN ON / OFF     (administrator only)
                        Stops new sessions - the command prompt and the API
                        alike - while maintenance is done: they are told who
                        has logins inhibited and to try again later.  Sessions
                        already running are not touched, and nor are the
                        phantoms the administrator starts.  With neither word
                        it shows the state.  @SYSTEM.RETURN.CODE is the user
                        number of the session that set it, or zero.
                        Not as openQM has it: the administrator can always
                        sign in, from a new session, and any administrator
                        session can switch it off - openQM keeps out
                        everybody, the administrator's own second session
                        included, and a lost terminal would mean restarting
                        SD.  If the session that set it is killed the
                        inhibit lapses by itself.
             A killed BACKUP.ACCOUNT or RESTORE.ACCOUNT: the hold that keeps
                        new sessions out while one runs was kept for as long
                        as the killed session's row stayed in the user table,
                        which is until "sd -cleanup".  It now asks whether
                        the process is still there.

15 Oct 26  DIGEST(), SET.TIMEZONE, KEEP.SELECT, MATWRITECSV, ANALYSE.INDEX,
           SET.MODE, FIND.FILE, FIND.PROGRAM, COMMAND.LOGGING, USPLIT AND
           SYSTEM(20).

             MATWRITECSV matrix TO file.var THEN / ELSE
                        One CSV line of every element of the matrix, each
                        quoted as WRITECSV quotes, joined with the CSV.MODE
                        separator, written to a file opened with OPENSEQ.  A
                        failed write takes ELSE.  openQM's ENCODING clause
                        and ON ERROR are not supported.

             DIGEST(string, is.file, mode)
                        The digest as lower case hexadecimal, for the modes
                        MD5, SHA1, SHA256, SHA384 and SHA512.  With is.file
                        true the string is the pathname of a file, read in
                        blocks; an account without the OS gate can read only
                        files inside its own directory, as OPENSEQ.  MD4 is
                        not there: it is broken, and OpenSSL no longer ships
                        it by default.  A null string comes back with STATUS()
                        saying why (1 the mode, 3003 no such file, 3035 not
                        permitted, 1008 not a file).
             SET.TIMEZONE zone      and      CONFIG TIMEZONE zone
                        The zone MVDATE(), MVTIME(), MVDATE.TIME() and
                        MVEPOCH() convert in, for this session only; CONFIG()
                        gives it back.  DATE(), TIME() and the clock are not
                        moved.  The zone has to be a name this machine has
                        (a file under /usr/share/zoneinfo, GMT or UTC):
                        openQM takes any name and gives UTC for a mistyped
                        one, so a refused name is STATUS() 2 here.  A null
                        string goes back to the process's own TZ.
             KEEP.SELECT            With OPTION CLEAR.SELECT the command
                        processor clears list 0 when a command that found
                        it active ends; a program that means to leave what
                        is left of it says KEEP.SELECT and it stays.
             ANALYSE.INDEX file {index {STATISTICS} {DETAIL}} {LPTR {n}}
                        With the file name alone, every index with its
                        number of indexed values and the most records any
                        one value has.  With an index name, the totals and
                        how the values spread over the number of records
                        they point at; DETAIL lists every value.  The
                        index's node sizes, which openQM shows with
                        STATISTICS, are not read: LIST.INDEX shows the same
                        figures with each index's definition.
             SET.MODE file {ALL | FROM n | id...} {NO.QUERY} mode
                        The permission bits of records of a directory file,
                        mode being three octal digits.  Only the rwx bits
                        (no set-id, no sticky bit), only regular files, and
                        a record that is a link is not followed.
             FIND.FILE {DICT} file  What the file is, its pathname, and
                        whether its record ids ignore case, it has indices,
                        triggers, is read only.
             FIND.PROGRAM name {DETAIL}
                        The local, private and global catalogue entries of a
                        program, in the order they are loaded, with when each
                        was compiled and what it is.  DETAIL adds the object
                        size and flags; SD's objects carry no compiler
                        version or source name, so those are not shown.
             COMMAND.LOGGING ON / OFF
                        A log of every command this session runs, with where
                        it came from (User, Cmd, PA, Exec): a tab separated
                        file clog.<user number>.log in the account directory,
                        mode 0600, added to and never emptied.  openQM puts
                        it in TEMPDIR; SD does not, because TEMPDIR is /tmp
                        unless sd.conf says otherwise and another user could
                        make the file first.  There is no global CMDLOG
                        parameter and no !CLOG hook.
             USPLIT=1 (sd.conf)     Interactive users take the lowest free
                        user number and phantoms and API sessions the
                        highest, so that the two never meet.  FIXUSERS,
                        PORTMAP and USERPOOL are ignored while it is on.
             SYSTEM(20)             How many print jobs have been passed to
                        the operating system's spooler since SD started, all
                        sessions together.  (openQM gives a job reference;
                        the spooler's own job number is not read back.)

15 Oct 26  SD BASIC FUNCTIONS AND STATEMENTS THAT OPENQM 4 HAS.

             Names and results follow the openQM 4 manual.  Only what SD does
             differently, or does not do, is written here.
             CEIL(v)  FLOOR(v)  ROUNDUP(v, step)  ROUNDDOWN(v, step)
                        Whole numbers above and below; the last two round
                        away from zero and towards it in steps of step.  A
                        dynamic array gives a dynamic array.
             IS.ALNUM  IS.ALPHA  IS.DIGIT  IS.GRAPH  IS.MARK  IS.SPACE
                        True if the first character of the argument is of
                        that kind; a null string is false.
             LEFT(s, n)  RIGHT(s, n)  Same as s[1,n] and s[n]; a count under
                        1 is taken as 1 for LEFT and 0 for RIGHT.
             LAST(s, d)  LASTS(array, d)  The final element of a delimited
                        string.
             NV(s)  NS(s)  The value or subvalue position numbers in place
                        of the values or subvalues.
             TRIMWS  MATCHESS  MATCHFIELDS  TRIMW, MATCHES and MATCHFIELD
                        over a dynamic array.
             BOOL(v)    A Boolean: false for a null string and for any
                        number that is zero (0, 0.0, 00), true for the rest,
                        the text false included.
             IS.NULL(v) True for the SQL null (JNULL()), not for a null
                        string.
             NUMERIC(v) The value as a number, as v + 0.
             ARG.PRESENT(name)  Whether the caller passed that argument.
             REUSING(name)      Whether that argument was passed with
                        REUSE().
             SORT.COMPARE(a, b, mode {, no.case})  -1, 0 or 1 by the rule in
                        mode: 0 SRT$LEFT, 1 SRT$RIGHT, 2 SRT$COMPOUND, 3
                        SRT$RIGHT.FLOAT, 4 SRT$STRICT, to which 16
                        (SRT$DESCENDING) and 64 (SRT$NOCASE) may be added.
             COMPARES(a, b {, justification})  COMPARE() over two dynamic
                        arrays, with an answer of the same shape.  The SRT$
                        and ML$ names are in SYSCOM KEYS.H.
             MERGELIST(list1, list2, modes)  The manual does not give the
                        values of ML$INTER, ML$UNION, ML$DIFF and ML$UNSORTED,
                        so SD has chosen 8, 16, 24 and 32.  The older
                        CALL !MERGELIST subroutine, with its own words for
                        the modes, is unchanged.
             CHILD(userno)  True if that user number is a phantom this
                        process started and which is still running.
             SIGNAL(userno)  Raises the signal in another process; the
                        other process reads and clears it with SYSTEM(1062).
                        It is true if that process was signalled.  SD signals
                        only a process of the same login user, or any
                        process when the caller is the administrator; the
                        manual has no such rule.
             PARSE(s, pattern, delimiter)  Matches as MATCHES does and
                        returns the string with the delimiter put between the
                        parts that matched each element of the pattern.  The
                        three dots are the same as 0X.  INMAT() is not set by
                        it.
             RMVD(array, code)  REMOVE() that stops only at field, value and
                        subvalue marks: a text mark or an item mark is part
                        of the data.
             TERMINFO.EX(name)  What TERMINFO(name) gives.  openQM lets a
                        capability have several strings; SD's terminal
                        database has one, so there is one value.
             TTYGET()  TTYSET settings  The terminal modes that PTERM sets,
                        as an array of eleven fields that TTYSET puts back.
                        Fields 1 to 5 are the manual's; 6 to 11 (mark input,
                        erase key, control character filter, binary in and
                        out, Telnet) are SD's, so that saving and restoring
                        leaves nothing changed.
             REVREMOVE item FROM array SETTING code  REMOVE going backwards.
                        SETREM n ON array puts the pointer at the nth
                        character first.  The code is the mark that comes
                        before the item, and 0 for the first.
             GROUPSTORE new IN s USING i, n {, delimiter}  The statement for
                        s = FIELDSTORE(s, delimiter, i, n, new).
             SEND s {:} TO file THEN / ELSE  WRITESEQ, or with the colon
                        WRITEBLK, to a file opened with OPENSEQ.
             ACCOUNT.PATH(account)  VOCPATH(file {, dict.flag})
                        The pathname of an account, and of a file named in
                        the VOC (the dictionary part with DICT in front or a
                        true second argument).  A path is told only where
                        the account could open it: its own directory and the
                        files in it always, anything else only to an account
                        that has the OS access gate; otherwise a null string,
                        which for ACCOUNT.PATH is also the answer for an
                        account that does not exist.
             OSREAD var FROM path THEN / ELSE  OSWRITE data TO path
             OSDELETE path
                        A whole operating system file read, written and
                        deleted by name, byte for byte.  They are made of
                        OPENSEQ and DELETESEQ, so an account that has no
                        OS access reaches only its own directory by them,
                        and one that has reaches what the user can.  OSWRITE
                        replaces the file and cuts it to the length of the
                        data; OSDELETE removes a file and not, as openQM's
                        statement does, a directory.  The ON ERROR clause is
                        not supported: STATUS() says what happened.
             CASING ON / OFF  Case sensitivity of string comparisons for the
                        rest of the program, as $NOCASE.STRINGS does for all
                        of it; SYSTEM(1086) tells which.

15 Oct 26  SELECT LIST VARIABLES AND NUMBERED LISTS: FIVE STATEMENTS AND A VERB.

             FORMLISTV array TO var       Makes a select list variable.
             GETLISTV name TO var {SETTING count} THEN / ELSE
                        A saved list into a select list variable.
             WRITELIST array TO name      A saved list written from an array
                        (ON works as well as TO).
             MOVELIST n TO m {KEEP}  MOVELISTV n TO var {KEEP}
                        Move a numbered list, or one in a variable, to
                        another; the ids already read are not moved.
             MOVE.LIST n TO m {KEEP}      The same as a command.
             EXECUTE.LIST command {; command ...}   Runs the commands for
                        each id of list 0 in turn, a pair of empty round
                        brackets standing for the id.  All the ids are taken
                        out of the list first.
             LIST.PHANTOMS {USER name}    The phantoms and no one else.  The
                        pool word of openQM is refused: SD has no pools of
                        phantoms.

15 Oct 26  NINE NEW $MODE WORDS AND DEFAULT VALUES FOR ARGUMENTS.

             $MODE  CHANGE.NO.OVERLAP  COUNT.OVERLAP  INDEX.OVERLAP
                        Whether CHANGE, EREPLACE, SWAP, COUNT, COUNTS, INDEX
                        and INDEXS see occurrences that overlap.
             PICK.CONVERT       CONVERT(string, from, to).
             PICK.SELECT        SELECTV moves a default list to its target.
             TRAP.UNUSED.MAIN   TRAP.UNUSED for the program's own variables.
             EXPLICIT           A variable has to be named in a DIM statement
                        before it is used.
             CONDITIONAL.STATEMENTS  Accepted; it changes nothing, since SD
                        always allows READ, WRITESEQ, LOCATE, SEEK and the
                        rest of the openQM list as the condition of WHILE
                        and UNTIL.
             DEFAULT.UNASS.ARGS  An argument passed unassigned takes its
                        default.
             SUBROUTINE name(a, b = 7)  A default value (a number, a string,
                        @TRUE or @FALSE) for an argument that the caller
                        leaves out; the program needs VAR.ARGS, or the mode
                        above.

15 Oct 26  SYSTEM() KEYS 14, 19, 21, 45, 1048, 1049, 1062, 1067, 1086, 1088.

             14 characters waiting at the terminal (a snapshot for piped
             input), 19 and 21 unique values from the date and time, 45 the
             name of the running program, 1048 a sequence number unique across
             all sessions until SD restarts, 1049 whether HUSH is on, 1062 the
             SIGNAL flag (reading it clears it), 1067 the default group size,
             1086 whether string comparisons are case sensitive, 1088 where
             MAXIMUM() or MINIMUM() found its element.  The names are
             SYS$... in SYSCOM KEYS.H.
             MAXIMUM() and MINIMUM() give the same answers as before.

15 Oct 26  QUERY PROCESSOR: MEDIAN AND RPT.  OPTIONS CORRELATIVE.NOCASE AND
           CORRELATIVE.REUSE.

             MEDIAN     Beside TOTAL and AVERAGE: the middle value (the mean
                        of the two in the middle when there is an even
                        number).
             RPT        Repeats a single valued field on each line of a
                        report on a multivalued one.
             OPTION CORRELATIVE.NOCASE  String operations in an I-type or
                        correlative ignore case.
             OPTION CORRELATIVE.REUSE   Its array operators act as if REUSE()
                        had been applied to each operand.  Both are read when
                        the correlative runs, not when it was compiled.

15 Oct 26  SIX NEW OPTIONS: GIT, DIR.DTM, KEEP.OLD.OBJECT, NO.PHANTOM.NOTIFY,
           SPOOL.COMMAND AND ASSOC.ALL.

             Each is off until OPTION name ON, and is the setting of one
             thing; none changes a lot at once.
             GIT          A directory file keeps .gitignore and .gitattributes
                          under their own names on the disk.  Without it every
                          name that begins with a dot has the dot replaced by
                          %D, which git does not read.  Only those two names;
                          any other name with a leading dot is mapped as ever.
             DIR.DTM      Writing a record to a directory file moves the
                          directory's date and time modified.  The system
                          moves it only when a name is added or removed, so
                          a program or a backup that looks for a changed
                          directory missed a record that was replaced.
                          Closing a written sequential file does not do it.
             KEEP.OLD.OBJECT  A compilation that fails leaves the previous
                          object code where it was, instead of a program
                          that no longer runs.  The listing of the failed
                          compile is not put back.
             NO.PHANTOM.NOTIFY  Set in a phantom, it stops the message that
                          the phantom sends its parent when it ends.
             SPOOL.COMMAND  Shows the operating system command that prints a
                          job, just before it runs.  A diagnostic aid.
             ASSOC.ALL    The query processor ignores the associations in the
                          dictionary and treats every field as one
                          association: with BY.EXP on one multivalued field,
                          each line shows the matching value of the others,
                          not all of them.

14 Oct 26  A K ENTRY OVER A LOCALLY CATALOGUED PROGRAM.

             A VOC entry of type K whose fields 3 onwards hold another entry
             hands the command over to that entry.  When that entry was the V
             CS entry of a locally catalogued subroutine, the command stopped
             with "Unable to load" and the K entry's own name, because the
             object is looked up by the name of its own VOC entry, not the
             name typed.  It now runs.  The name used is the last part of the
             object's pathname, which is the name it was catalogued under;
             a program catalogued under a call name different from its program
             name is not covered.

14 Oct 26  !SORT CAN SORT A SELECT LIST VARIABLE.

             CALL !SORT(list, result, mode)  When list is a select list
                        variable (from SELECTV, FORMLISTV and the like) its
                        ids are read out and sorted, instead of the call
                        stopping with "Data cannot be converted to a string".
                        The result is the sorted ids as a field mark
                        delimited list; the modes are as before.  The ids are
                        used up in the variable, as with any READNEXT.  The
                        call also no longer moves the REMOVE pointer of an
                        ordinary list passed to it.

14 Oct 26  THE DEBUGGER'S WATCH TAKES A CONDITION.

             WATCH variable operator value
                        The program stops only when the value just assigned
                        meets the condition; otherwise it carries on without
                        showing anything.  The operators are =, #, <, >, <=,
                        >=, their word forms EQ, NE, LT, GT, LE, GE, and
                        MATCHES.  A value in quotes is that text; a number
                        compares as a number and anything else as text.
                        A WATCH with no condition stops at every assignment as
                        before, a new WATCH replaces the old one with its
                        condition, and UNWATCH clears both.  LIKE and UNLIKE
                        are not supported.  A condition on a local variable
                        works while the program that set it is the one running.

14 Oct 26  STATUS() AFTER END TRANSACTION, AND A TIMEOUT FOR OPEN.SOCKET.

             A program can now test STATUS() straight after END TRANSACTION:
             it is 0 when the transaction committed and 1021 (ER_ROLLBACK)
             when it was rolled back, whether by ROLLBACK or by running into
             END TRANSACTION without a COMMIT.  A commit that fails does not
             come back as an error code (ER_COMMIT_FAIL, 1022, is defined for
             it): a failed commit still stops the program with its message, as
             before.

             OPEN.SOCKET(address, port, flags, timeout)  The optional fourth
                        argument is the number of seconds to wait for the
                        connection; when it runs out the call fails with
                        STATUS() 7005 as any failed connect does.  Zero, or no
                        argument, is as it was.  Until now the argument was an
                        unused place for a security context and had to be
                        zero.  A connect that failed also left its socket
                        open; it is closed now.

14 Oct 26  EPOCH, MVDATE, MVTIME, MVDATE.TIME AND MVEPOCH.

             EPOCH()        The time now as a time zone independent number of
                            seconds since 1 January 1970 UTC (the same as
                            SYSTEM(1035)).
             MVDATE(e)      The multivalue date (days since 31 December 1967)
                            of epoch value e, MVTIME(e) the time of day in
                            seconds since midnight, MVDATE.TIME(e) both,
                            joined by an underscore, as 15020_84690.
             MVEPOCH(s)     The epoch value of a date and time written as one
                            string "date_time", and MVEPOCH(date, time) the
                            same with the two apart.
                        The zone is the one the session runs in (the TZ
                        setting of the machine), as DATE() and TIME() use, and
                        summer time is allowed for.  Epoch values are 32 bit
                        (13 December 1901 to 19 January 2038): a value outside
                        that, a time of a day or more, or something that is not
                        a number gives the null string.  A fraction of a second
                        is cut off.  There is no SET.TIMEZONE yet, so a program
                        cannot choose a zone of its own.

14 Oct 26  SSELECTV.

             SSELECTV file TO listvar {RIGHT.ALIGNED} {DESCENDING}
                        Selects the record ids of a file (or the items of a
                        dynamic array) in sorted order into a select list
                        variable, as SSELECT does into a numbered list, and as
                        SELECTV does without the sort.  The sort options are
                        those of SSELECT and may come before or after TO.
                        TO is required.

14 Oct 26  INPUTNULL AND INPUTTRAP.

             INPUTNULL expr  A single character, typed alone as the answer to
                        INPUT @ or INPUTFIELD, makes the variable a null string
                        (what was echoed stays on the screen).  A null
                        expression cancels it, and so does returning to the
                        command prompt.
             INPUTTRAP expr GOTO label{, label...}  and  GOSUB
                        Characters (up to 16) that, typed alone as the answer
                        to INPUT @ or INPUTFIELD, send the program to a label:
                        the first character to the first label, and so on, and
                        a character past the end of the labels to the last
                        one.  The variable is set to null.  With GOSUB the
                        RETURN comes back to the statement after the INPUTTRAP,
                        not to the one after the INPUT.  A null expression
                        cancels the trap.  The trap belongs to the program that
                        set it: a subroutine it calls is not trapped, and
                        neither is a plain INPUT.  When a character is named by
                        both statements, INPUTNULL wins.  If the INPUT @ has a
                        THEN or ELSE clause, neither runs when the trap is
                        taken.

14 Oct 26  A FILL EXPRESSION FOR INPUT.

             INPUT var, length, fill  and  INPUT @(x, y) : var, length, fill
                        The first character of the fill string is shown in
                        every cell of the field that has no character in it, so
                        the width of the field is clear while you type; the
                        erase key puts it back.  When the entry is finished
                        the cells left over take the second character (a blank
                        if the fill has only one).  A third character leaves
                        the cursor at the end of the field instead of after
                        the last character typed.  The fill needs a length,
                        and cannot be used with HIDDEN (refused when the
                        program is compiled).  Without a fill nothing changes.

14 Oct 26  INPUT ... RAW.

             INPUT var, length RAW  Reads exactly length characters and puts
                        them in the variable just as they arrive: a newline
                        or an erase key is data, not an instruction, and
                        nothing is echoed.  The length is required (a program
                        without one is refused when it is compiled).  Items in
                        the DATA queue are taken whole, as for any INPUT.
                        UPCASE and TIMEOUT still work with it.  INPUT @ does
                        not take RAW.

14 Oct 26  OPENTEMP.

             OPENTEMP {pathname} {ECS} {NON.TRANSACTIONAL} TO file.var
                        Makes a dynamic file and opens it; the file is deleted
                        when the file variable is closed (or goes out of
                        scope).  Leave the pathname out, or give a null
                        string, and SD makes the file in its temporary
                        directory (the TEMPDIR setting) under a name made from
                        the process number and a counter.  A name that exists
                        already is refused, never taken.  ECS is accepted and
                        ignored.  The file's updates are never part of a
                        transaction, so that closing it really deletes it.
                        SECURITY: both the temporary directory and a pathname
                        you name are outside the account, so they need the
                        account's OS-access permission, as OPENPATH does; an
                        account without it can use OPENTEMP only with a name
                        inside its own directory, and gets the ELSE clause for
                        the others.  A session that is killed leaves its
                        temporary file behind.

14 Oct 26  INPUTIF.

             INPUTIF var {, length} THEN / ELSE  and  INPUTIF @(x, y) : var, length
                        Reads a line only if one is already there, from the
                        DATA queue or typed ahead on the keyboard; otherwise
                        it takes the ELSE clause at once, writes no prompt and
                        leaves the variable as it was.  The options of INPUT
                        and INPUT @ (HIDDEN, NO.ECHO, UPCASE and so on) work
                        with it.  When SD's input is a pipe or a file rather
                        than a terminal there is always something to read (the
                        end of the file counts), so INPUTIF behaves as INPUT
                        there.

14 Oct 26  EXECUTE ... STACKLIST.

             EXECUTE command STACKLIST  Keeps the default select list as it
                        was, the place READNEXT had reached included, and puts
                        it back when the command returns.  The command is
                        given the list as usual and may read it, change it or
                        replace it; the program has its own again afterwards.
                        Without STACKLIST whatever the command leaves in the
                        default list is what the program finds, as before.
                        It cannot be combined with PASSLIST or
                        RTNLIST (those give or take a list), which is refused
                        when the program is compiled.  With the
                        EXECUTE.CLEARLIST mode the command starts with the
                        list empty and the program still gets its own back.

14 Oct 26  FLUSH.DH.CACHE.

             FLUSH.DH.CACHE {LOCAL | NO.WAIT}  Makes every process close the
                        dynamic files it keeps in its cache of recently closed
                        files, for a program that is about to do something
                        (outside SD) that fails while a file is still open
                        somewhere.  The statement waits for the other
                        processes to say they have done it and gives up after
                        four seconds; a process that is waiting at an INPUT
                        does not answer until a key arrives, so it costs the
                        four seconds.  NO.WAIT asks and carries on; LOCAL
                        flushes only the process it is in.

14 Oct 26  INPUT NO.ECHO.

             INPUT, INPUT @ and INPUTFIELD take NO.ECHO  What the user types is
                        not shown: INPUT answer NO.ECHO.  The new line that
                        ends the entry still appears.  INPUT HIDDEN shows
                        asterisks; NO.ECHO shows nothing (with INPUT @, the
                        field stays blank), and the two together show nothing.
                        UPCASE, TIMEOUT and the other options work with it.

14 Oct 26  LOCATE CAN FORCE CASE.

             The BY string of LOCATE takes C and N  C forces a case sensitive
                        comparison and N a case insensitive one, whatever the
                        program was compiled to do: LOCATE 'banana' IN list<0>
                        BY 'ALN' SETTING p finds 'Banana' in a list kept in
                        alphabetical order without regard to case.  The
                        letters may come in any order and in either case
                        (NAL, aln); N alone does an unsorted search ignoring
                        case.  A BY string without C or N behaves as before.
                        One change in passing: a string whose first letter is
                        L or R (LAN) used to be read as "no sort", and is now
                        ascending, as the letter A in it says.

14 Oct 26  CSV.MODE.

             CSV.MODE mode {, delim}  Sets how PRINTCSV and WRITECSV build a
                        line of comma separated values.  Mode 1 (the default)
                        encloses an item in double quotes only if it holds a
                        double quote or the delimiter; mode 2 encloses every
                        item that is not empty or a number; mode 3 encloses
                        every item, an empty one too.  A double quote inside an
                        enclosed item is written twice in every mode.  The
                        optional delimiter replaces the comma (a null string
                        is a comma, and only the first character of a longer
                        string is used).  A mode other than 1, 2 or 3 is
                        ignored.
             SYSTEM(1043) and SYSTEM(1046)  Give the current mode and delimiter.
             The setting goes with the program: a subroutine you call starts
             with yours, a change it makes is undone when it returns, and an
             EXECUTEd command starts at the defaults and gives yours back
             afterwards.  READCSV, INPUTCSV and DPARSE.CSV are not affected;
             they take their delimiter as before.
             FORMCSV() is still not a function an ordinary program can call;
             PRINTCSV and WRITECSV use it.

14 Oct 26  CASE INSENSITIVE INDICES, AND TRANS() ON A FILE THAT WILL NOT OPEN.

             CREATE.INDEX and MAKE.INDEX take NO.CASE  The index treats keys
                        that differ only in case as one key, so a lookup of
                        'APPLE' finds the records holding 'Apple' and 'apple'
                        too.  Without NO.CASE an index is as it was.  Building
                        such an index (BUILD.INDEX, or MAKE.INDEX, which
                        builds) now keeps every record: the kernel already
                        compared these keys without case, but the build kept
                        only the last spelling's records, which no one could
                        meet before because there was no way to ask for it.
             TRANS(), RTRANS() and XLATE() on a file that cannot be opened
                        Follow the action code, as they do for a missing
                        record: X (the default) gives the null string and says
                        nothing, V gives null and prints "Cannot open", C gives
                        the record id.  They used to print the message every
                        time whatever the code was.  A program that relied on
                        the message with no code now sees nothing; add V.

14 Oct 26  FOUR SMALL ADDITIONS TO SD BASIC.

             ~= operator  Compares two values and ignores case: 'ABC' ~= 'abc'
                        is true.  The plain = is as it was.  Two values that are
                        both numbers are compared as numbers, as with =.
             COMPARE( a, b, justification )  The justification may now have an I
                        after its L or R (as in 'LI') to compare ignoring case.
                        Without the I, nothing changes.
             PRECISION INHERIT  The program takes the precision of the program
                        that called it.  A number after PRECISION works as before.
             INMAT() after MATBUILD and MATWRITE  Returns the index of the last
                        element that went into the string or record, or 0 when
                        the zero element of the matrix was added at the end.

             The kernel's list of operations gained one entry for ~= (the
             extended opcode CFE5), so programs compiled before this build and
             after it are not interchangeable if they use it; none did.

14 Oct 26  THE USER TABLE, THE HOT SPOT MONITOR AND PSTAT.

             LISTU and !LISTU show the account  Each user's line now ends with
                        the account the user is in (": jack, sales"), and the
                        ninth value of !LISTU is the account.  A user moves
                        into another account with LOGTO and the table follows;
                        a phantom starts in its parent's account.  The account
                        is shown to the administrator and, for anybody else,
                        only for the sessions of the login user who is asking;
                        another user's account is left blank.  (LISTU itself
                        is still the administrator's verb: an ordinary account
                        has !LISTU, from a program.)
             LISTU DETAIL  Adds a line above the report: the interactive users,
                        the API sessions, the phantoms that count as licensed,
                        the other phantoms, and the most users there have been
                        at once since SD started.  SYSTEM(1056) gives the same
                        five numbers.  SD has no licence count, so the third
                        number is whatever the system marks as chargeable.
             HSM  The Hot Spot Monitor counts each program's opens, reads,
                        writes and deletes as well as its calls and processor
                        time, and the report has a column for each.  What a
                        program asks for through the OPEN of a name (its VOC
                        lookup) or through READV and WRITEV (which read and
                        write the record themselves) is not counted against it.
             PSTAT  For a process that is waiting for a lock it also shows
                        when the wait began, as LIST.READU DETAIL already did.

             None of this changes what a user can do.  The shared memory
             layout changed, so SD must be restarted by the installer, as for
             any new build.

14 Oct 26  SHOW.LIST, AND SMALLER CHANGES TO THE COMMAND LINE, CATALOG AND ED.

             SHOW.LIST {n}  A new verb: it lists the items of an active select
                        list a page at a time, each with an asterisk, and lets
                        you clear and set the asterisks (C and S with numbers,
                        ranges, VISIBLE or ALL, or just the number); T N P move
                        about, Q keeps what is starred, QC keeps nothing, ? is
                        help.  The list is made again from what is starred.
             Catalogue names  A program run by typing its catalogue name,
                        not the name of a VOC verb, now takes LPTR and NO.PAGE
                        as RUN does.  The words stay in the sentence for the
                        program.  A word in quotation marks is not a keyword.
             .C-  In the command stack editor, .C- undoes the .C just before it.
                        A .L or .? may come between; anything else ends the
                        chance.
             CATALOG ... LOCAL  When the VOC already holds a local catalogue
                        pointer under that name and it points at a different
                        object file, you are asked before it is overwritten
                        (Return is no); NO.QUERY goes ahead as before, and the
                        same file again does not ask.
             ED  EV on an I-type that holds quoted text, brackets and
                        semicolons no longer corrupts the expression (a quoted
                        string was cut from the wrong place).  Text, subvalue
                        and value marks can be typed into ED without NPRINT;
                        the field and item marks still need it, because one in
                        a line would split the line unseen.

14 Oct 26  A BATCH OF SMALLER ADDITIONS (SD BASIC, THE QUERY PROCESSOR AND
           OPTION), EACH OFF UNLESS ASKED FOR.

             $MODE STDFIL  The default file variable: with it, OPEN, READ,
                        MATREAD, WRITE, MATWRITE, DELETE, CLOSE, CLEARFILE,
                        FILELOCK, FILEUNLOCK, RELEASE and SELECT may be written
                        without a file variable and use one the program does
                        not name, as in some other multivalue systems.
             $MODE WRITE.DELETE.THEN.ELSE  WRITE, WRITEU, MATWRITE, MATWRITEU,
                        DELETE and DELETEU take THEN and ELSE clauses; a trigger
                        that refuses the write or delete takes ELSE, and the
                        trigger is told it is being called with an error trap
                        set.  A statement with neither clause is as it was.
             CLASS ... INHERITS name AS variable  The object variable of an
                        inherited class can be given a name of its own.
             Method references  An empty pair of brackets is accepted after a
                        method name in a statement (obj->method()), as it
                        already was in an expression.
             Query X breakpoint control  The X option on a break field makes
                        the previous value of the field available in @BPV for
                        the I-types of that line.
             OPTION PAGINATE.ON.HEADING  With RUN ... NO.PAGE, a heading or a
                        footing still turns pagination on.
             OPTION QUERY.FOOTER.PAUSE  A report with a page footer waits for
                        RETURN after its last footer.
             OPTION FORCE.RELOAD  An unload event (compiling or cataloguing a
                        program) also refreshes the subroutine links that a
                        running program holds, so the next CALL loads the
                        current object code.
             OPTION NUMERIC.EXPONENT  Numbers in exponent form, such as
                        123.45E-2, are numeric.
             OPTION INHERIT.MFILE.PATH  CREATE.FILE puts a new subfile of a
                        multifile in the directory of the default subfile.

           None of them changes anything for a program or an account that
           does not ask for it.

14 Oct 26  NEW $MODE SETTING: PARTIAL.EXPRESSIONS.

           A program compiled with $MODE PARTIAL.EXPRESSIONS stops
           evaluating a logical expression as soon as its value is known:
           in A AND B the B is not evaluated when A is false, and in A OR B
           it is not evaluated when A is true.  That makes tests such as
           IF IDX MATCHES '1-2N' AND S<IDX> # 0 safe, and it means that a
           function with a side effect on the right hand side runs only
           when it is needed.  The value of every expression is the same as
           without the setting; only what is evaluated changes.  Without
           the setting both sides are always evaluated, as they were.

14 Oct 26  NEW $MODE SETTING: EXECUTE.CLEARLIST.

           A program compiled with $MODE EXECUTE.CLEARLIST clears the
           default select list (list 0) before the command of each EXECUTE
           statement runs, so the command does not pick up a list the
           program happened to have active.  An EXECUTE with a PASSLIST
           clause, with or without a variable, still passes the list in.
           Without the setting the list is passed into the command as it
           always was.

14 Oct 26  NEW OPTION: CHAINED.SELECT.

           With OPTION CHAINED.SELECT in effect, a GET.LIST, QSELECT,
           SEARCH, SELECT, SSELECT or SELECTINDEX that leaves an active
           select list takes the first line of the data queue and runs it
           as the next command, the way a CHAIN would: DATA 'LIST STOCK'
           followed by EXECUTE 'SELECT SALES' lists the selected records.
           When the select finds nothing the queue is cleared, so the
           commands that were waiting for a list do not run on nothing.
           Without the option the data queue is left alone, as it was.

14 Oct 26  NEW OPTION: PICK.ML.CONV.MASK.

           With OPTION PICK.ML.CONV.MASK in effect, a left bracket in an ML
           or MR conversion code no longer asks for negative values to be
           shown in round brackets: it starts a format mask that is
           enclosed in round brackets, as in MR2(#10) or ML2,(%8), which is
           how some other multivalue systems write it.  The mask is read as
           if the brackets were not there, in output and in input
           conversion.  The other negative value styles (+ - < C D) and
           the P, Z and T options are as they were, and a program that
           does not set the option sees no change.

14 Oct 26  NEW $MODE SETTING: PICK.READNEXT.

           A program compiled with $MODE PICK.READNEXT keeps the value of
           the id variable when a READNEXT takes its ELSE clause (the list
           is exhausted, or no list is active).  Without the setting the
           variable is set to the null string, as it always was.  Programs
           written for other multivalue systems often read the last id
           after the loop that read the list.  It applies to the plain
           forms of READNEXT; the forms that return value and subvalue
           positions are unchanged.

14 Oct 26  NEW $MODE SETTING: OPEN.FAIL.ZERO.FVAR.

           A program compiled with $MODE OPEN.FAIL.ZERO.FVAR gets the
           integer 0 in the file variable of an OPEN or OPENPATH that
           fails, where it is otherwise left unassigned.  Some programs
           written for other multivalue systems rely on that, and an
           unassigned variable stops them with a run-time error the moment
           they test it.  The ELSE clause is taken as always and an OPEN
           that works is not touched.  Nothing changes for a program that
           does not ask for it.

14 Oct 26  A DEVELOPMENT ENVIRONMENT: THE IDE VERB.

           IDE opens one full-screen program for working on BASIC programs
           and the other text records of an account, built on the screen
           library.  Type IDE at the command prompt, or IDE file record to
           start on a record.

             The screen  A menu bar on the top row (F10 or Esc opens it), a
                        tree of the account's files on the left, the editor
                        on the right with a tab for each record that is open,
                        a table of the compiler's errors (or of search
                        results) under it, and a status line that names the
                        record, the cursor's line and column and a star when
                        it has unsaved changes.
             The tree   Return on a file lists its records (the first 2000)
                        and Return on it again closes the list.  Return on a
                        record opens it.  A letter jumps to the next entry
                        that starts with it.
             The editor It is the screen library's text area, so its keys
                        are the ones SCREEN MEMO has: selection, cut, copy
                        and paste, undo and redo, find, line numbers.  The
                        text is coloured as SD BASIC, C, Python, JSON, shell
                        or Markdown by the file and record name.
             Build      F7 saves and compiles, and the compiler's lines go in
                        the table; Return on one goes to its line, and F4
                        goes to the next.  F8 compiles and catalogues, F5
                        compiles and runs, F9 compiles with debugging and
                        runs the debugger.  Run and debug give the terminal
                        to the program and come back to the IDE when you
                        press Return.  Nothing runs if the compile failed.
             Search     Ctrl-R finds a text in every record of the file the
                        tree is on (the first 2000 records, 500 lines) and
                        lists the lines in the same table; Return opens the
                        record at the line.
             Saving     F2 or Ctrl-S writes the record.  If somebody else
                        changed it since the IDE read it, the IDE asks before
                        it overwrites.  Closing a record or leaving with
                        unsaved changes asks whether to save them.

           On a UTF-8 terminal (LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE or LANG says UTF-8) the
           IDE draws its frame, menus and scroll bars with line-drawing
           characters; anywhere else it uses + - | as before.  The colours
           are the blue theme: a light-grey bar, white text on black for the
           tree and the editor, and a light-blue column between them.

           The keys follow SED where the two do the same thing: Ctrl-S
           saves, Ctrl-Q quits, Ctrl-L goes to a line, Ctrl-X, Ctrl-C and
           Ctrl-V cut, copy and paste, Ctrl-Z and Ctrl-Y undo and redo,
           Ctrl-F finds forward and Ctrl-B backward (Return on the question
           repeats the last text), Ctrl-R replaces a text through the record
           and Ctrl-W writes the text to another record.  Find in all the
           records of a file is F11.  In the text area of every program
           that uses SCREEN MEMO (and so in the IDE) a word back and on are
           now Ctrl-P and Ctrl-U: they were Ctrl-B and Ctrl-F, which are
           SED's find keys.

           What it does not do: it does not lock records while they are open
           (the check at saving is what protects against a second editor),
           it has no replace across records, no project files and no
           completion.  It follows the size of the window, with a minimum of
           60 columns by 16 lines.  SED is unchanged and stays for those who
           want its own keys.

13 Oct 26  THE ACCUTERM SUPPORT IS GONE, AND THE DEBUGGER AND IMMEDIATE
           MESSAGES WORK ON ANY TERMINAL.

           SD used to keep a part of its screen handling for AccuTerm, a
           Windows terminal emulator: it asked the terminal to remember the
           screen under a pop-up, to report the mouse and to rebind the
           function keys, with escape sequences only AccuTerm understood.  On
           every other terminal that code did nothing, which meant two things
           did not work there: the debugger had no full-screen display, and a
           message sent with MESSAGE ... IMMEDIATE was never shown.

           That code, and the eleven AccuTerm terminal types (vt100-at,
           vt220-at, vt320-at, vt420-at, wy50-at, wy60-at, adds4000-at,
           vp-at, viewpoint60-at, addsviewpoint-at and pcmon-at, with their
           aliases), has been removed.  In its place:

             The debugger  On a terminal that has an alternate screen (xterm
                        and the terminals that behave like it) the debugger
                        takes the whole screen: the title, the source with its
                        line numbers, the line about to run, breakpoints
                        marked, five lines of messages and the command line,
                        drawn in the screen library's styles.  The program's
                        own screen is kept by the terminal and comes back when
                        the debugger lets go.  F1 shows the help in a box, F4
                        shows the program's screen until a key.  A terminal
                        without an alternate screen, and a session with no
                        terminal, get the line-by-line debugger as before.
             MESSAGE ... IMMEDIATE  The message is shown in a box over the
                        alternate screen (or as lines and "Press return to
                        continue" on a terminal without one).
             OPTION DEBUG.REBIND.KEYS  Still accepted, no longer does anything.

           A user on AccuTerm should set the terminal type to xterm, or vt100:
           the -at types are no longer known.

           SDTerm and SDConsole, Ladybridge's own Windows terminal programs,
           are not provided with SD and are no longer supported either: the
           sdterm terminal type, its @(-29), @(-30) and @(-256) sequences, the
           window size LOGIN sent it and the TERM DISPLAY line about it are
           gone.  SAVE.SCREEN() and RESTORE.SCREEN still exist and do nothing
           that the terminal could see (they asked it to keep the picture, which
           only those two terminal programs could).

13 Oct 26  A NEW ACCOUNT STARTS WITH NO SHELL, NO OS.EXECUTE, NO SSH AND NO API;
           THE ADMINISTRATOR TURNS EACH ON, ACCOUNT BY ACCOUNT.

           SD is delivered with the tighter setting, and SDSYS loosens it for
           the accounts that need it.  Four switches, each kept per account:

             ssh, api   The two remote doors.  CREATE.ACCOUNT USER name ssh,
                        api or both gives them (none, the default, gives
                        neither); MODIFY.ACCOUNT name ssh / api / both / none
                        changes them later, either way.  A new account used to
                        get both without being asked.
             sh-on      The SH verb and "!".
             os-on      OS.EXECUTE in a BASIC program, nano and micro, the
                        embedded Python, and reaching a file or directory of
                        the operating system by its path outside the account's
                        own directory: OPENSEQ and OPENPATH, DIR() and
                        SELECT.DIR (listing a directory), DELETESEQ of a path,
                        !OSCOPY, !IOSCOPY and the OSCOPY verb, and printing
                        into a file by path (SETPTR ... AS PATHNAME, or the
                        SETPU statement).

           CREATE.ACCOUNT USER name sh-on os-on gives them at creation, and
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT name sh-on, sh-off, os-on or os-off changes either
           later; the two are independent, so sh-off leaves OS.EXECUTE alone.
           After either command SD says where the account now stands ("Account
           name has SH yes, OS.EXECUTE no").  A change takes effect the next
           time the account is entered.

           An account without os-on can still open every file it can reach
           through its VOC - dictionaries and other SD files included - and any
           file inside its own directory, as long as the VOC pointer leads
           somewhere it may go (the next paragraphs).  What it can no longer do
           is open
           /etc, another account's directory, or a symbolic link that points
           out of its own, by any of the ways above; a program is told the
           open was refused (status 3035), so OPEN ... ELSE works as before,
           and DIR() gives nothing and sets STATUS().  SH and OS.EXECUTE give
           a refusal in words: "name is not permitted to use the operating
           system shell" or "...to use OS.EXECUTE"; the OSCOPY and SELECT.DIR
           verbs and SETPTR say "name has no OS access, so a file or directory
           outside its own account cannot be reached by pathname (SDSYS can
           give it: modify.account name os-on)".

           DIR() now sets STATUS() to 0 when it works and to a code when the
           directory cannot be opened.  It used to leave whatever the last
           operation had set, so SELECT.DIR on an empty directory could say
           "Cannot read directory".

           Upgrading keeps what accounts have.  The installer switches SH and
           OS.EXECUTE on, once, for every account that exists when you upgrade
           (ssh and the API are left as they are), so nobody loses a command
           they were using; the administrator can then turn them off one
           account at a time, and a later install does not turn them back on.
           A fresh install makes the installing user's own account with
           everything off too, like any other new account, and says how to
           turn it on: sign in as sdsys and give it what it needs with
           modify.account.  SDSYS is never refused.

           A VOC pointer is held to the same rule (owner: "try to make
           tighter ... q pointers should only be allowed into an account the
           user can logto in any case").  An account used to be able to write a
           file pointer into its own VOC, or make one with CREATE.FILE ...
           PATHNAME, naming any directory its Linux user could read, and open
           it as an SD file.  Now, for everybody but SDSYS:

             - a file pointer must lead inside the account's own directory,
               or inside the account a Q-pointer was followed into, or be the
               pointer SD itself puts in every VOC (the same id and the same
               path as in NEWVOC: syscom, $map, sd.voclib, dict.dict, voc,
               newvoc, $acc); an account that holds os-on may go anywhere its
               Linux user can.  Otherwise the open is refused with status 3035.
             - a Q-pointer into a registered account is followed only if the
               user could LOGTO that account - a member of its group, and the
               account not suspended - whether or not the account holds os-on.
               So a Q-pointer made with SET.FILE into somebody else's account
               works only for a user who may enter it.  The two pointers every
               VOC starts with are the exception, because they are exactly
               what SD put there: sd.accounts, which leads into SDSYS (an
               account nobody can LOGTO), and md.
             - a Q-pointer that names a path instead of an account is an
               operating-system path, and the os-on question.

           What this changes for an existing site: a Q-pointer that leads into
           an account its owner can no longer enter stops opening, and so does
           a hand-made file pointer to a directory outside the account in an
           account that does not hold os-on.  Accounts that existed at an
           upgrade hold os-on, so their pointers keep working.

13 Oct 26  SD READS THE WINDOW SIZE; MORE FOR THE SCREEN STATEMENT; COLOUR IN
           SED; CED AND THE DEBUGGER DRAWN WITH THE SCREEN LIBRARY (1.2-0).

           The window size.  SD took the size of the screen from the terminal
           definition, 120 columns by 36 lines, unless TERM said otherwise, so
           a window of any other size was wrong from the first line.  It now
           reads the size of the terminal's window when a session starts and
           again whenever the window is resized, and @CRTWIDE and @CRTHIGH
           follow it.  The TERM 120,36 that LOGIN used to run is gone.  A
           program waiting in SCREEN WAIT is told by a "resize" event and can
           lay its screen out again.  What a program wrote with PRINT or CRT
           is not drawn again, only what the screen library drew.  A session
           with no terminal gets the size it got before.  SED is the exception:
           it is 120 columns by 36 lines whatever the window is (below).

           Default theme.  SCREENTHEME=name in sd.conf (mono, classic or blue)
           is the theme a SCREEN program gets when it does not choose one.
           Unset, it is mono, as before.

           More on the screen, each used by SCREEN statements and by nothing
           else, so a program that does not use them is not changed:

             SCREEN TREE       a list of labelled nodes, each with a depth,
                               opened and closed with the arrow keys, the space
                               bar or the mouse.  The program gets the code of
                               the current node.
             SCREEN SCROLLBAR  a scroll bar of its own, upright or lying down,
                               for a program that draws the content itself.
                               Each move is a "change" event.
             Table             F3 (or a click on a heading) sorts by the current
                               column, numbers by value; with MULTI the space
                               bar marks rows; Left and Right choose a column
                               and scroll sideways; < and > narrow and widen it.
                               SCREEN GET still answers the number of the row
                               in the rows the program gave, however they are
                               shown.
             Text area         WRAP breaks long lines at the width.  Ctrl-G sets
                               a mark, Ctrl-X, Ctrl-C and Ctrl-V cut, copy and
                               paste (the clipboard is shared between text
                               areas), Ctrl-Z and Ctrl-Y undo and redo for up
                               to 100 steps, Ctrl-T finds and Ctrl-N finds
                               again.  A viewer can select, copy and find.
             Menus             An item may be disabled (dim, the cursor goes
                               past it) or checked, and may open a submenu,
                               three levels deep.
             SCREEN OPEN ... LINES   draws boxes, forms and dialogues with the
                               terminal's line-drawing characters.
             SCREEN CLIP       keeps what is drawn inside a rectangle.
             SCREEN SET        takes WRAP, READONLY and VISIBLE.

           SED can colour the text it shows.  It is off until asked for, and
           with it off the screen is drawn exactly as before.  The command
           SYNTAX (or the Tools menu) turns it on and off, SYNTAX LIGHT and
           SYNTAX DARK choose the palette for a light or a dark background, and
           the words SYNTAX, NO.SYNTAX and SYNTAX.LIGHT in &sed.options& say
           what it starts as.  It knows SD BASIC, C, JSON, Markdown, shell and
           Python, picking the language from the name of the file, the record
           id and the first line; a record of the file &sed.syntax& adds a
           language of the site's own or replaces one.  A comment or string
           that goes on to the next line colours the lines below it.  A
           terminal that cannot show colour is told so and carries on without
           it.

           SED has undo and redo: Ctrl-Z and Ctrl-Y, and Undo and Redo at the
           top of the Edit menu.  A run of typing, of backspaces or of deletes
           on one line is one step; each other edit is a step, a command such
           as FORMAT or EXPAND.TABS included.  The cursor goes back to where the
           change began.  It undoes the screen, not the file, and holds 100
           steps (about 8 million characters); it starts again with each record
           and when another buffer is shown.  "Nothing to undo" and "Nothing to
           redo" say there is nothing.

           SED takes the mouse in a terminal that reports it (xterm and the
           like).  A click puts the cursor on the text, a click on a title of
           the bar opens that menu and a click on an item chooses it, and the
           wheel moves the cursor three lines.  It is on: the terminal's own
           selection then needs Shift.  The command MOUSE OFF, Tools > Mouse or
           NO.MOUSE in &sed.options& turn it off.  SED asks the terminal for the
           reports only while it waits for a key and for the menu.

           SED is 120 columns by 36 lines, SD's own default terminal size,
           whatever the window is, as it was until 12 October (when it was made
           80 by 12 at least, which let it draw off the edge of a small window
           and go wrong when a window was resized).  A window that says it is
           smaller than 120 by 36 is refused: "SED needs a window of at least
           120 columns by 36 lines; this one is 80 by 24".  A bigger window
           shows SED in its top left corner.  A session with no window to ask
           (a pipe, a phantom) is 120 by 36 as before.

           A drop-down menu with more entries than the screen has rows below
           its title (SCREEN MENU and SCREEN MENUBAR, on a short window) shows
           the rows that fit, scrolls as the cursor moves, and marks the cut
           edges with ^ and v.  The rows that did not fit used to be left off
           the screen though the cursor still moved over them.

           SCREEN MEMO takes SYNTAX name (the text is drawn in the syntax
           styles of the theme in force, for SD BASIC, C, JSON, Markdown, shell,
           Python or a language of the site's own) and LNUM (line numbers down
           the left).  The mono theme has no syntax styles, so a program that
           asks for colour should choose the dark, light, classic or blue
           theme.  A program that uses SCREEN MEMO must be compiled again.

           CED shows the elements of a collection as a table with a scroll bar,
           and asks its questions (a value, a name, a type, Save changes?) in
           boxes over the list.  The keys are the same.  The debugger's full
           screen is drawn with the library as well (see the entry above).

12 Oct 26  THE SCREEN STATEMENT GETS COLOUR, TABS, TEXT AREAS, TABLES AND THE
           MOUSE, AND COLOUR WORKS ON THE LINUX CONSOLE TERMINAL TYPE (1.2-0).

           Colour.  Everything the SCREEN statement draws is drawn in a named
           style - window, highlight, bar, hotkey, field and so on - and a
           THEME says what each style looks like:

               screen theme 'classic' returning name else crt 'no colour here'
               screen style 'highlight', 'red', 'black', 'bold underline'

           Three themes ship: mono, which uses only reverse video and
           underline and reads on any terminal, classic (dark text on light
           dialogues over a blue desktop) and blue.  A program that never asks
           gets mono, which looks exactly as before, so nothing changes for
           it.  A colour theme is refused (the ELSE branch) on a terminal that
           cannot show colour and in a phantom.  SCREEN CLEAR, TEXT and BOX
           draw in the theme's desktop colour, a theme can be changed while
           something is on the screen (the screen is drawn again from the
           library's picture of it), and SCREEN CLOSE clears a coloured desktop
           and puts the theme back to mono.  There are eight colours and bold
           for bright ones.  STYLE changes one style of the theme in force;
           it refuses a colour or flag it does not know and changes nothing.

           Why colour needed a terminal fix.  SD's own colour codes - @(-37, n)
           and @(-38, n), and the TERM COLOUR command - read the terminfo
           capabilities setf and setb, and the shipped linux and ansi entries
           had only the standard setaf and setab.  Terminals that report
           xterm-256color, screen-256color and the like get the linux entry,
           so colour printed nothing for most users.  Both entries now carry
           setf and setb: TERM COLOUR works on them too.

           More things for a form:

               screen tabs f, 'tabs', 'General':@vm:'Address':@vm:'Notes' at 2,1
               screen page f, 1
               screen entry f, 'name' at 10,3 width 20
               screen page f, 2
               screen entry f, 'street' at 10,3 width 20
               screen page f, 0
               screen button f, 'ok', '&OK' at 10,11 default

           A strip of tabs; the widgets added after SCREEN PAGE f, n belong to
           tab n and are drawn, take the focus and answer keys only while that
           tab is chosen.  The values of every page still reach the program.
           SCREEN WAIT reports a change event when another tab is chosen.

               screen memo f, 'notes', text at 2,2 size 40,8
               screen memo f, 'help', text at 2,2 size 40,8 readonly

           A text area of several lines, one line to a field: typing, Return,
           Backspace, Delete, Ctrl-A, Ctrl-E, Ctrl-K, the arrows, Home, End and
           the page keys edit and move through it; a long line scrolls
           sideways (it is not wrapped) and a scroll bar shows where you are.
           With READONLY it is a viewer.  It has no undo, selection or search.

               screen table f, 'people', 'Name':@sm:'10L':@vm:'Qty':@sm:'4R', rows at 2,2 size 36,8

           A table: a heading, columns with a width and left, right or
           centred alignment, rows one to a field and cells one to a value,
           one current row, a scroll bar.  SCREEN SET f, id ITEMS rows replaces
           the data.  The scroll bar of a long list now has a thumb as long as
           the part showing, where it was a single cell.

           The mouse.  SCREEN OPEN now asks an xterm-compatible terminal to
           report the mouse, and SCREEN CLOSE turns that off again (SCREEN
           OPEN NOMOUSE leaves it off).  Forms, menus and dialogues take clicks
           with no change to the program: a click presses a button, ticks a
           check box, chooses a radio button, a tab, a row of a list or table,
           an entry of a menu or pick list, and puts the cursor in an entry
           field or text area; the wheel scrolls a list, table or text area; a
           click outside a menu or pick list closes it.  A program that reads
           keys itself sees the key "mouse" from SCREEN KEY, and SCREEN MOUSE
           says what happened (press, release, drag or wheel, the button, the
           cell and the shift, alt and control keys).  The Linux console has no
           mouse reporting and ignores the request.  SED and the other
           full-screen editors do not use the mouse.  A program that is stopped
           without SCREEN CLOSE (a break, an error) leaves the terminal
           reporting and the mouse then types garbage at the prompt until the
           terminal is reset; KEYCODE also no longer rings the bell and types
           "0;12;5M" when such a report arrives.

           Not there yet: changing the layout when the terminal is resized (SD
           does not read the window size - it is what TERM says, 120 by 36
           unless set); sorting, wrapping and undo in the text area and table.

12 Oct 26  THE SCREEN STATEMENT: MENUS, DIALOGUES AND FORMS FROM BASIC (1.2-0).

           A BASIC program can now draw on the terminal with one new
           statement, SCREEN, followed by a verb, with no C, no extra package
           and no change to the program's own PRINT and INPUT:

               screen msgbox 'Delete this record?' title 'Confirm' buttons '&Yes,&No' returning n then ...
               screen menu items at 10,4 returning code
               screen pick cities title 'City' size 20,5 returning n
               screen ask 'Your name?' value 'Bob' width 12 returning s
               screen form title 'Customer' at 10,3 size 56,16 returning f
               screen entry f, 'name' at 12,2 width 30 value 'Smith'
               screen button f, 'ok', '&OK' at 18,14 default
               screen run f returning pressed then ...
               screen get f, 'name' returning name

           The verbs are open, close, clear, text, box, cursor, refresh, size
           and key (the screen itself); msgbox, menu, menubar, pick and ask
           (dialogues that ask one thing and give back the answer); and form,
           label, frame, entry, check, radio, list, button, run, get, set,
           focus and values (a form holds fields, check boxes, radio buttons,
           lists and buttons; Tab and Shift-Tab, the arrows, Return for the
           default button, Esc to cancel and a button's letter work as they do
           in a dialogue box).  Clauses (TITLE, AT, SIZE, DEFAULT ...) can be
           written in any order and left out; RETURNING names the variable
           that gets the answer; THEN is taken when the user finished and ELSE
           when the user cancelled (Esc), the terminal went away, or there is
           no terminal at all (a phantom or the API).  A form is a data
           collection, so JBUILD writes one out and JPARSE reads it back.

           After SCREEN OPEN the library keeps a picture of what it has drawn,
           so a dialogue over your text puts every character and its
           highlighting back when it closes.  Text you print with PRINT or CRT
           is not in that picture: draw it with SCREEN TEXT, or SCREEN REFRESH
           will not bring it back.  Before SCREEN OPEN a dialogue still works,
           but leaves blanks where it was.  The statement is documented, with
           what it asks of each verb, in sd64/screenlib-design.md.

           A program that already uses screen, title, buttons, default,
           returning or the like as variable names is not affected.
           (Colours, tabs, a text area, a table and the mouse came the same
           day: see the entry above.)  Shift-Tab is now a key on an xterm (its
           terminfo entry lacked kcbt).

12 Oct 26  SED, THE FULL SCREEN EDITOR, IS BACK, WITH DROP-DOWN MENUS (1.2-0).

           SED edits a record of any file (SED file record, or with an active
           select list).  It was removed because its Emacs-style keys are hard
           to remember.  It is back with a menu bar on the top line - File,
           Edit, Search, Buffer, Window, Tools - opened with F10, or with Esc
           pressed on its own.  Left and right move between the menus, up and
           down between the items, Return runs the item, Esc closes the menu,
           and a letter picks the item with that letter.  Each item shows the
           key that does the same thing, read from the key bindings in use
           (Ctrl-X Ctrl-S for Save, Esc G for Goto line), so the menus teach the
           keys.  An item that needs an answer (a search string, a line number)
           asks on SED's usual prompt line.  Quitting a changed record asks in
           a small message box (No is the default).  Esc followed at once by a
           letter is still the editor's own Esc-key command.

           The keys are now the ones most editors have: Ctrl-S saves, Ctrl-Q
           quits (a changed record asks first), Ctrl-O opens a record, Ctrl-W
           writes it under another name, Ctrl-C, Ctrl-X and Ctrl-V copy, cut
           and paste the text between the mark (Esc .) and the cursor, Ctrl-F
           and Ctrl-B search forward and backward, Ctrl-R replaces, Ctrl-L goes
           to a line, Ctrl-G gives up a question, and the Backspace key erases
           backwards.  The shell's line keys stay (Ctrl-A, Ctrl-E, Ctrl-N,
           Ctrl-P, Ctrl-D, Ctrl-K).  Ctrl-X is no longer a prefix, so the old
           Ctrl-X sequences are gone: the actions that had one are reached
           through the menu, which shows the key of every item that has one.
           To keep the old Emacs layout, put EMACS in field 3 of the record
           &sed.options& in your VOC.  SED still has no undo.

           SED needs a terminal of at least 80 columns by 12 lines; it used
           to insist on 120 by 36.  It does not change what SED edits or how
           the keys work.  The record names it reads for its options and key
           bindings are now lower case (&sed.options&, &sed.bindings&).

           The menus are built on the screen library that the SCREEN statement
           (the entry above) gives to BASIC programs.  It does not change
           anything else.

12 Oct 26  FOUR SMALL FIXES (1.2-0, openQM compatibility).

           CLEAR.FILE on a file that has NO.RESIZE set (CONFIGURE.FILE ...
           NO.RESIZE) empties it but keeps the size it had; it used to send the
           file back to its minimum size, as if the flag were not there.

           LIST.ITEM and SORT.ITEM number the lines of an item with more than
           999 fields with as many digits as the last number needs (0001 to
           1200, say); they used to cut the number to three digits.  An item of
           999 fields or fewer is numbered as before.

           The debugger tells you when it cannot find the source of the
           program it was asked to debug, and then lets the program run on.
           A record named $DEBUG.OPTIONS in your VOC containing the line
           NO.SOURCE.WARNING (one keyword to a line) leaves that message out.
           No other keyword is looked for.

           The terminfo entries for the ADDS 4000 terminal, adds4000 and
           adds4000-at, now say that the terminal leaves a character cell for
           a highlight marker (xmc#1), as the other terminals of its kind do.

           Two things in the same group of openQM changes were found to be
           already so and are now tested: CT opens a file whose name is in
           lower case, and SOCKET.INFO(var, SKT$INFO.OPEN) answers 0 for a
           variable that is not a socket, whether it holds a number, a string
           or nothing, instead of stopping the program.

12 Oct 26  SIX SMALL BASIC ADDITIONS (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; the compiler
           and the kernel).

           OPEN and OPENPATH take NON.TRANSACTIONAL, alone or with READONLY and
           in either order: OPEN "ORDERS" NON.TRANSACTIONAL TO F ...  A write
           through that file variable is made at once and is not undone when a
           transaction it happened in is rolled back.  It is for the one open
           only; the same file opened without it behaves as before.

           A trailing substring can be assigned to: S[3] = "XY" overwrites the
           last three characters of S.  A value that is too long loses its
           tail and one that is too short is padded with spaces; a count above
           the length of S is the length, and below 1 is 1.  It works on matrix
           elements too, and S[3, 2] = ... is as it was.

           REDIMENSION (short form REDIM) changes the size of a matrix that
           was passed into a subroutine as an argument, which DIM has never
           done: the caller's matrix is the one resized, and keeps its values
           as DIM keeps them.  Elsewhere REDIM is DIM.

           A matrix passed as an argument, MAT A, no longer needs a DIM
           statement in the subroutine or LOCAL SUBROUTINE that receives it:
           how many subscripts it has is read from how it is used.  A matrix
           that is not an argument and is never dimensioned is still refused.

           IF MAT A = MAT B THEN ... compares two matrices.  They are equal
           only if they have the same shape and every element is the same
           string; = and # both work.  A and B are compared as the text of
           their elements, so 1 and 1.0 count as different.

           A READ, READU, READL, READV, READVU, READVL, READNEXT, READSEQ,
           READBLK, READLIST, OPEN, OPENPATH or OPENSEQ statement may be the
           condition of a WHILE or UNTIL, as in LOOP WHILE READNEXT ID FROM 1
           DO ... REPEAT: the condition is true if the statement would have
           taken its THEN branch.  Such a statement cannot be given THEN or
           ELSE clauses of its own there; that is a compile error.  It is not
           yet allowed for the other statements that have THEN and ELSE
           (LOCATE, FIND and the like).

12 Oct 26  FILE HANDLING: READ-ONLY AND NON-TRANSACTIONAL OPENS FROM THE VOC,
           A LOCK ON A HELD PRINT FILE, AND BLOCK DEVICES (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility; a kernel change).

           Field 6 of a file's VOC entry (the F-type entry) can now hold mode
           letters.  R opens the file read-only whenever it is opened through
           that entry, so a write to it fails as it would on a file opened
           with the READONLY option; N opens it non-transactionally, so a write
           to it stays when a transaction that wrote it is rolled back.  The letters may be given
           together (NR) and in either case, and they apply to a file reached
           through a Q-pointer as well.  They affect that open only, as the
           OPEN statement's own options do.  The other letters the openQM
           manual lists for field 6 (B, C, L, M, S and X) are not acted on, as
           before.

           A print unit in mode 3 (SETPTR ... 3), whose output goes to a record
           of the $HOLD file under the default name or one given with AS name,
           now holds an update lock on that record from the moment the file is
           made until the unit is closed.  Another process that reads the
           record with a lock, or writes it, waits until the output is complete.
           A unit printing to AS PATHNAME is outside any SD file and is not
           locked; nor is a unit above 255.  A process that asks for the lock
           while another holds it waits, which stops its print job until the
           holder lets go.

           OPENSEQ can open a block device, a floppy disk drive among them, as
           it opens an ordinary file; FILEINFO(FL$SEQTYPE) says 4 for it.  Who
           may open one is decided by the operating system's permissions, and
           an ordinary user cannot read most of them: this has been tried only
           as far as the open being no longer refused as "not a sequential
           file".

           A command that needs exclusive access to a file (CNAME, CONFIGURE.FILE,
           DELETE.INDEX and the like) already stopped when another session had the
           file open and named the users holding it; that is now tested.

           The ids of records in a directory file may now contain bytes 128 to
           255, which they could not (anything above 126 was refused as an
           illegal record id).  They are stored in the file name as they are
           given, so an id written with a UTF-8 e-acute is a file name with
           those two bytes.  Control characters (below 32) and DEL (127) are
           still refused, and so are the system's own mark characters (248 to
           255), which are not ordinary characters.  This is the one place the
           rule that SD is English only has been lifted, at the owner's say,
           and it adds no other language support: SD does not interpret the
           bytes, fold their case or sort them in any national order.

12 Oct 26  LOCK AND DUMP DIAGNOSTICS: WHEN A LOCK WAS TAKEN, DEADLOCK=2,
           SYSTEM(1083) AND OBJECTS IN A PROCESS DUMP (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility; a kernel change).

           The kernel now notes the time at which each record lock and file
           lock was taken and each wait began.  LIST.READU DETAIL shows it under
           the lock - "Locked 13 Oct 26 01:56:40" - and, with WAIT, under each
           waiting process - "Wait since 13 Oct 26 01:56:42" - in the time zone
           of the user who gave the command.  Without DETAIL nothing changes.
           A shared lock that is raised to an update lock keeps the time it was
           first taken.  The zone's offset is the one in force when the command
           is given, so a lock taken before a daylight saving change is shown an
           hour out after it, as other times SD reports are.

           DEADLOCK=2 is new.  DEADLOCK=1, as before, stops a process that
           would wait for a record lock held by a process that is itself
           waiting for it, and shows the locks involved.  DEADLOCK=2 does that
           and also writes every lock in the system to a file, deadlock.n,
           where n is the number of the process that was stopped.  It goes
           where a process dump goes: DUMPDIR, or the SDSYS directory, and only
           its owner can read it.  Each lock takes four lines, with a blank line
           between locks: the user number and name; the file number and its
           pathname; the lock type (RU, RL, FX or SX) and, for a record lock,
           the record id; and when it was taken.  The process is told where the
           file is.  DEADLOCK now takes 0, 1 or 2 and refuses other values when
           SD starts; any value but 0 used to be taken as 1.  The default is
           still 0, which lets a deadlock happen.

           SYSTEM(1083) returns the last deadlock the process was in, for a
           program that wants to report it after the abort: a field for each
           process in it, of three values - its user number, the pathname of the
           file and the record id of the lock it was waiting for, the id left
           out for a file lock.  It is null until there has been one.  A record
           id is as the lock table holds it, in capitals in a file that does not
           tell the cases apart.  It is the process that was stopped that can
           ask; the process that was left waiting did not see the deadlock.

           A process dump now shows what an object variable holds: under
           "OBJ: Object" come the object's public and private variables, with
           their names, one to a line like any other variable.  The names are
           found from the class module by the size of its group of variables, so
           a class that also has a COMMON block of exactly the same number of
           variables could show the wrong names (never the wrong values).  An
           object that holds an object is followed four levels down.

           A process that is waiting because the lock table is full still
           answers a request to log it out.  That was already so and is now
           tested.  What is worth knowing is that with the table completely
           full no other session can give a command at all, each command taking
           a lock for an instant to look itself up, so the LOGOUT command cannot
           be given; from the operating system, "sd -k n" logs user n out
           without needing a lock.  NUMLOCKS is the table's size.

           Not done: the openQM 4.0.9 dump of the other process in a deadlock
           (it is waiting, and SD has no way to make it dump itself while
           waiting), and the exception openQM can raise for a deadlock, which
           waits for system events to become exceptions.

12 Oct 26  FIVE NEW sd.conf PARAMETERS, A LONGER CALL NAME AND A NAME FOR THE
           STARTUP PROCESS (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a kernel change).

           Each parameter below is optional.  With none of them in sd.conf
           SD behaves exactly as before, because every default is the number
           that used to be fixed.  A value outside the range given makes SD
           refuse to start and name the parameter, as it does for the others.
           CONFIG lists them; CONFIG() reads them in a program.

             MAXRLOCK=n   The most record locks one process may hold, 0..100000.
                          0, the default, is no limit.  A process that asks
                          for one lock more stops with "Record lock limit
                          (MAXRLOCK) reached", or, with an ON ERROR clause,
                          gets STATUS() 3060 and carries on.  It covers READU,
                          READL, READVU, RECORDLOCKU and OPENSEQ.  Asking again
                          for a lock the process already has is not a new lock,
                          and a release gives the place back.  There is no
                          way to except one account or one user, and no limit
                          on file locks.  openQM can instead raise a
                          SYS.LOCKS.LIMIT exception for TRY / CATCH; SD's
                          exceptions are only the ones a program THROWs, so
                          here the process stops or gets 3060 (the system
                          events as exceptions item is still to do).  CONFIG MAXRLOCK n changes it for the
                          process that gives the command.

             DHCACHE=n    How many closed dynamic files SD keeps open ready for
                          the next OPEN, 0..1024.  The default is 10, which is
                          what it always was.  0 keeps none.  CONFIG DHCACHE n
                          changes it for that process; note that EXECUTE empties
                          the cache, so set it before the OPENs that matter.

             LGNWAIT=n    When every user place is taken, wait up to n tenths
                          of a second for one to be freed instead of refusing
                          the login at once, 0..32767.  The default 0 refuses at
                          once, as before.  A login that gives up is refused
                          with "User limit reached" as it always was.  SYSTEM(1036)
                          is how long this process waited, in tenths, and
                          SYSTEM(1055) counts since SD started, as fields:
                          logins that had to wait, that then got in, waiting
                          now, the most waiting at once, and those that gave
                          up.  It waits in tenth-second steps, so it is not a
                          queue: whoever looks first after a place is freed
                          gets it.

             PWDELAY=n    Seconds the API server waits after a refused login
                          before it answers, 0..3600.  The default is 3 (the
                          openQM default is 5, and SD already had 3 fixed).  A
                          longer delay slows anyone guessing passwords; 0
                          removes the slowing and is the administrator's
                          choice to make.

             USERPOOL=n   The highest user number SD hands out, 0..16383, or
                          NUMUSERS if that is larger.  The default 1023 is what
                          it always was.  0 means the lowest free number is
                          used again each time instead of cycling through the
                          pool: a user number is reused as soon as it is free.
                          SYSTEM(1053) returns this highest number (before
                          this change it returned NUMUSERS, which was never the
                          highest number SD handed out).

           A process that SD starts for the STARTUP parameter now has @TTY set to
           "startup", so a program can tell that it was started that way.  What
           the startup process runs does not inherit this.  STARTUP itself is
           unchanged: it still takes a command only, and runs as SDSYS.

           The longest name a subroutine can be catalogued and CALLed by is the
           lower of 128 and MAXIDLEN.  MAXIDLEN is 63 unless you raise it, so
           nothing changes until you do; CATALOG, $CATALOG, CALL and the API
           server all use the same limit.  A program name also has to leave room
           for ".LIS" inside MAXIDLEN, because BASIC writes a listing record under
           it: at the default a program whose name is 60 or more characters could
           not be compiled before and still cannot.  The client libraries keep
           63, because a client cannot know the server's MAXIDLEN.

           Not done: IPADDR, to listen on one address only (SD opens no listening
           port of its own - sshd does - so there is nothing for it to bind); STARTUP taking an account, user and password
           (that puts a password in sd.conf, which is for the owner to decide);
           and switching FSYNC off while an index is built - an index build
           makes only four fsync calls in all, so there is nothing to save.

12 Oct 26  MORE FILEINFO() AND SYSTEM() KEYS, NUMBERED AS THE OPENQM MANUAL
           NUMBERS THEM (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a kernel change).

           FILEINFO() gains the keys the openQM 4 manual lists from 1022 to
           1040.  The ones that report something real in SD:

             FL$SEQTYPE (1023)    a sequential file's kind: 1 serial port, 2
                                  character device (/dev/null), 3 FIFO, 4
                                  block device; 0 for a plain file.  Only the
                                  character device and the plain file have
                                  been tried.
             FL$INDEX.SCAN (1030) 1 for a hashed file
             FL$NON.TXN (1032)    opened as non-transactional
             FL$COLLECTION (1035) a data collection file
             FL$TXN.UPDATE (1038) written or deleted in the current
                                  transaction
             FL$LOCKS (1039)      the locks you hold in the file, one to a
                                  field: RU or RL, a value mark and the record
                                  id; FX or SX for a file lock

           The rest answer as openQM does for a file without the feature: no
           QMNet (FL$NETFILE 1022), replication, ECS, distributed file, SYNC,
           temporary file or integrity constraint (all 0), and a null string
           for the replication list, ECS map, encoding, sequential key, forced
           id casing and owner.  FL$COLLECTION was 1022 for a few days; the
           manual gives 1022 to FL$NETFILE and 1035 to FL$COLLECTION, so it has
           moved.  A program that used the number 1022 for it, instead of the
           name, must change; none was released.  FL$HOLDERS (1021) is
           unchanged and is not the manual's FL$WHO: it gives text, "12 (don)",
           where FL$WHO gives a list of user numbers.

           SYSTEM() gains, by the manual's numbers: 13 (always 1), 16 (the
           command level, @LEVEL), 22 (the user number, @USERNO), 33 (the name
           of the program that called this subroutine), 1019 (the time zone's
           offset from UTC in seconds), 1033 (COMO active), 1035 (seconds
           since 1 Jan 1970 UTC), 1037 (every open file and the users that
           have it open), 1038 (login time as an epoch value), 1052 / 1057 /
           1058 (a phantom's children, its parent), 1053 (the highest user
           number), 1065 (the transaction cache: transaction number, file
           number, record id and W or D for each cached write or delete), 1070
           (PRECISION), 1076 (the CALL depth), 1077 / 1078 (the user's group
           names and numbers), 1081 and 1082 (milliseconds since 1970 and since
           midnight).  Keys 1034, 1040, 1044, 1051, 1054 and 1080 answer 0 or
           null: COMO cannot be suspended, file names are case sensitive on
           Linux, there is no ECS mode or USB installation or package, and no
           Proc.

           The array the STATUS statement and FILEINFO(f, FL$STATUS) return
           is now the manual's: the dates and times of last access and
           modification (fields 13 to 16) are in the user's time zone rather
           than UTC, and the operating system file name is repeated in field
           27 and the two times are given again as epoch values in fields 34
           and 35, which makes the array 35 fields long, not 21.  For a hashed
           file the later of the primary and overflow subfiles' times is used.
           OSPATH(path, 20) (OS$INFO) gives the same array for any operating
           system path, null if it is not there; OS$FULLPATHX is another name
           for OS$FULLPATH, which already keeps the case on Linux.  OSPATH() is
           still a function only the system's own programs may call, and SD's
           other OSPATH key numbers are the older ones, not the manual's.

           GETPU() gains keys 1020 to 1025: PU$FORM.QUEUE (0: there is no
           SP.ASSIGN), PU$FORM.FEED (the string sent between pages, CR FF or
           just FF with SETPTR ... FORM.FEED FF), PU$ENCODING and PU$FONT
           (null), PU$PATHNAME (the file the output is going to while one is
           open) and PU$FONT.SIZE (0).  A $SYSTEM subroutine for your own
           SYSTEM() keys, catalogued globally (CATALOG bp system GLOBAL $),
           was already given the key correctly; it is now tested.

           Not done: FL$WHO (see above), FL$LAST.PART (SD has no distributed
           files), OS$SET.LINK and the other OSPATH keys that take a third
           argument, FC$CRLF, SYSTEM(1071), which describes the file just read
           by OSREAD (SD has no OSREAD), and the many other SYSTEM() keys in
           the manual that need features SD does not have, such as 1073
           (phantoms grouped with their parent) and 1066 (SP.ASSIGN).  The
           lock table holds record ids in upper case, so FL$LOCKS shows them
           that way, as GETLOCKS() does.

12 Oct 26  A COLLECTION THAT HOLDS A FILE VARIABLE CAN BE WRITTEN TO A
           COLLECTION FILE (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a kernel change).

           A data collection in a program can hold things a file has no way to
           keep, such as a file variable.  Writing a collection like that to a
           data collection file used to fail with error 1019.  The openQM
           manual says those elements are stored as SQL-style nulls, and now
           they are: the WRITE succeeds, the other elements are stored as they
           were, and the element reads back as a null.

           What it does not change: JBUILD() of such a collection still fails
           with 1019 unless the JB$INVALID mode is given, which asks for a
           null in the same way; a collection that contains itself still
           cannot be written (1019); and only a collection or JSON text that
           is an object or an array can be written to a collection file.

12 Oct 26  TWO NEW FORMS OF FOR / NEXT (1.2-0, openQM compatibility).

             FOR i = 5, 7, 22                    the loop runs once for each
                ...                              value, in turn
             NEXT i

             FOR EACH v IN string {DELIMITER d}  the loop runs once for each
                ...                              value in a list
             NEXT v

           The first form takes constants or expressions: FOR i = a, a * 2,
           max(b, 3).  Each expression is worked out only when its turn
           comes.  A FOR with no TO that used to be a syntax error is now a
           list of values, so FOR i = 4 is a loop that runs once.

           FOR EACH walks the values of a list.  Without DELIMITER every mark
           (field, value, subvalue and so on) separates two values, as it does
           for REMOVE, which is what it uses; with DELIMITER only that
           character does, and any marks inside a value are part of it.  The
           list is copied when the loop starts, so changing the original in
           the loop has no effect.  A null string makes no pass at all; a null
           value inside the list, or after a final delimiter, is a pass with a
           null value.  This was a choice: the openQM manual does not say what
           a null string does.  The control variable must be a simple
           variable name (not an array element) in FOR EACH; the list form
           takes an array element as the classic FOR does.

           In both forms WHILE, UNTIL, CONTINUE, EXIT and NEXT work as in the
           classic loop, a variable called EACH is still an ordinary control
           variable (FOR EACH = 1 TO 5 is unchanged), and the control
           variable keeps the last value it was given on leaving the loop.
           $MODE FOR.STORE.BEFORE.TEST is honoured.  Programs already
           compiled are not affected.

12 Oct 26  THE ELEMENT KEYWORD IN QUERIES (1.2-0, openQM compatibility).

             LIST file ELEMENT "detail/*/prodno" ...
             LIST file ... WITH ELEMENT "ship" = "Dock B"
             SORT file ... BY ELEMENT "n"

           ELEMENT names an element of a data collection file by its path,
           the way A n or F n names a field, so a query no longer needs a
           dictionary item for every element it touches.  It can be used
           wherever a field name can: in the display list, in WITH and WHEN
           clauses, with BY, BY.DSND and BY.EXP, with SUM, TOTAL and BREAK.ON,
           and in COUNT and SELECT.  The usual qualifiers (AS, COL.HDG, FMT,
           CONV, MULTI.VALUE, SINGLE.VALUE) work on it as on any other field.

           The path is the one a collection variable takes: names and array
           positions separated by /, as in detail/1/prodno.  An * in place of
           a position stands for every element of that array, so
           detail/*/prodno gives a list of values, one for each element, and
           detail/*/serial/* a value for each element of detail with a
           subvalue for each of its serial numbers.  A path with an asterisk
           is multivalued.  Quotes are needed only for a path that holds a
           blank or a character the command parser splits on.  A name is case
           sensitive and keeps the case it is typed in; an element that is not
           there is a null value, not an error.

           With no FMT the item is 10 characters, left justified, and its
           heading is the path.  Left justified means that a sort or a test on
           a number compares characters, so BY ELEMENT "n" puts 10 before 3;
           add FMT "5R" (and AS, to sort by the formatted item) for numbers.

           ELEMENT works only on a data collection file; on any other file it
           says so (11160), and ELEMENT with no path says "ELEMENT path not
           found where expected" (11161).  ELEMENT is now a keyword, like
           EVAL, so a dictionary item that happens to be called ELEMENT should
           be given another name.

12 Oct 26  THE DATA COLLECTION EDITOR, CED AND !CED() (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility).

             CED {DICT} file {id ...}      edit records of a collection file,
                                           or JSON text in an ordinary file
             CALL !CED(collection)         edit a collection held in a variable

           A full-screen editor for a data collection.  It shows the elements
           of one level at a time: the name (or the position, in an array), the
           data type, and the value, or the number of elements of a collection
           or an array.  A > marks the selected line.  The path of the level
           being shown is at the bottom.

             Up, Down, Page up/down   move; Home and End go to the first and
                                      last element
             Return                   open a collection or array; change the
                                      value of anything else (Return keeps the
                                      change, Esc leaves it)
             Esc                      back to the level above
             F3                       rename the element
             F4                       change its type: only the types the
                                      value can become are offered
             F5 or Ctrl-C, F6 or      copy, cut and paste an element (paste
             Ctrl-X, F7 or Ctrl-V     asks for the name, or the position in
                                      an array)
             Ins or Ctrl-I            insert an element: name, type, value
             Del                      delete the element
             Ctrl-S                   save, and carry on
             Q                        leave (at the top level only)
             Ctrl-Q                   leave from any level
             F1 or ?                  list the keys

           Leaving with changes asks "Save changes (Y/N)?".  With several ids
           Q goes on to the next record and Ctrl-Q stops.  With no ids the
           active select list is used, and with none the ids are asked for
           until a null one is entered.  A record that is not there is a new
           one: the editor asks whether it is a collection or an array.  A
           record of an ordinary file must hold a JSON object or array, and is
           written back as JSON text; one that does not is reported and left
           alone.  The record is locked while it is open.  !CED() changes the
           variable it is given if the changes are kept (Ctrl-S, or Y when
           leaving) and leaves it as it was if they are not.

           An element whose name is empty or holds a slash is shown with ? for
           its type and cannot be changed in the editor, because a path cannot
           name it.  Ctrl-C is a key in the editor: the terminal's break
           character and the break key are switched off while it runs and back
           on when it ends.  If its input ends (the terminal hangs up) it
           leaves without saving.  A null cannot be edited; F4 changes its
           type.

11 Oct 26  ED, COPY, COPYP, COMPARE.FILES AND TRIGGERS WORK ON A DATA
           COLLECTION FILE (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a kernel change, so it
           needs a rebuild).

             ED file id                  edit a record's JSON, a line to an element
             COPY FROM coll TO other ids   the JSON text goes into a file that is
                                           not a collection file, and the JSON
                                           text of a record goes into a
                                           collection file
             COPYP file ids              the same, with the destination asked
                                           at the To: prompt; (T shows a
                                           collection record as JSON
             COMPARE.FILES one two       records compared as their JSON text

           All three stopped with "Data cannot be converted to a string" when
           they met a record of a data collection file, because a program that
           reads such a record is given a collection, not text.  ED (and EDIT)
           now shows the record as CT does, one element to a line, and files the
           lines back as JSON text; the file makes a collection of it again and
           refuses it, with "Write error 1020 - Data not saved", if it is not a
           JSON object or array, leaving the stored record as it was.  The same
           holds for UNLOAD into a collection file, and LOAD of lines from a
           collection record gives the lines of its JSON.  COPY from a
           collection file to a file that is not one writes the JSON text, in
           the compact form the file itself holds; to another collection file
           it copies the collection, as before.  Text that is not JSON copied
           into a collection file is refused, "Error 1020 writing record", and
           stops the copy, as any write error does (with the D option of COPYP
           the source record that was not copied is not deleted).  COPYP and
           COPY.LISTP do all of this too; they stopped with the same message.
           The openQM manual says a copy must be between two collection files
           or two files that are not; SD also copies between the two kinds, as
           a deliberate addition, so that a collection file can be exported to
           (or loaded from) text files, which a directory file holds as files
           on disk.  COMPARE.FILES compares
           collection records by their JSON text, in which the names are always
           in order, so two equal collections are the same.

           Triggers on a collection file now behave as the records do.  A
           pre-write trigger was given a collection when the program wrote one
           but the text when it wrote text, and the post-write trigger was given
           something that was not a string or a collection: a trigger that used
           the record there stopped the whole process with "Fault type 11".
           Both triggers, and the read trigger, are now always given the
           collection.  Text written to a collection file is made into its
           collection before the trigger runs, so text that is not a JSON object
           or array is refused (error 1020) without the trigger seeing it, and
           what a pre-write trigger changes is what is stored.  The file holds
           the compact JSON of the collection, whatever layout the text had;
           a program reading the record gets the same collection as before.
           A trigger that refuses a write (a non-zero @TRIGGER.RETURN.CODE)
           stops it as usual, and the post-write trigger does not run.

           The API server now sends a client the JSON text of a record it reads
           from a collection file; it would have failed to send the collection.
           This one has been compiled but not yet run against a live API server.

           The other file commands already worked on these files and still do:
           CONFIGURE.FILE, CLEAR.FILE, DELETE, COUNT, LIST.ITEM, SORT.ITEM,
           DUMP and CT.  Not changed: MICRO and NANO, which need a terminal and
           were not tried on a collection record, READV by field number on a
           collection file (a collection has no fields), and ANALYSE.FILE, which
           does not say that a file holds collections.

11 Oct 26  THE DEBUGGER SHOWS A DATA COLLECTION (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a
           kernel change, so it needs a rebuild).

             /c                      the collection c: kind, elements, JSON
             /c{name}                the element a path names
             /c{a/3/k}   /c{'a','3'}  the same path written either way
             DUMP c{sub} pathname    the element's JSON, to a file

           The debugger knew nothing of collections: /c showed an empty line
           for one.  It now says "Collection (9 elements)"
           or "Array (3 elements)" and gives the JSON text, cut off after 10,240
           characters with "..." (DUMP writes all of it).  A path in braces
           shows one element: a number or a string as the debugger shows any
           variable, a collection or array as above, and a JSON true, false or
           null as "Boolean: true", "Boolean: false" or "Null", which the
           program itself sees as the integer 1 or 0 and an empty string.  The
           path is written as in a program, with slashes or commas between the
           names and quotes optional.  The variable name is not case sensitive,
           as before, but the path is.  An element that is not there gives
           "Element not found in the collection"; a path on a variable that is
           not a collection, or an index on a collection, gives the existing
           "Index not allowed with scalar variable".  A collection that
           contains itself is reported as "(cannot be shown)" and the debugger
           carries on.  WATCH on an element is not provided, and the help
           screens are unchanged.

11 Oct 26  AN INDEX NOW FOLLOWS THE RECORD ID AS THE FILE HOLDS IT (a kernel
           change, so it needs a rebuild).  The fault is in L1.1-3, where every
           file is case insensitive and so every index was affected.

           An alternate key index (CREATE.INDEX, BUILD.INDEX) was kept up to
           date with an upper case copy of the record id, while BUILD.INDEX
           and everything else use the id as the file holds it, which in SD
           is normally lower case.  So once an index had been built, a record
           written to the file was entered in it as R4 when the file held r4;
           deleting a record left it in the index, because the delete looked
           for R3 and the index held r3; and changing a record left its old
           entry where it was and added a second one under the upper case id.
           A selection through the index then listed ids that were not in the
           file, or records that had gone.  The index now gets the id the file
           holds, whatever case the program used to write or delete it.

           A related change: record ids are not case sensitive, so writing R2
           over a record held as r2 replaces it.  The record kept the spelling
           it was first written with, unless the new data was a different size,
           when it took the spelling of the program that wrote it.  It now
           always keeps the first spelling.

           An index that was damaged by the old behaviour is not repaired by
           the update: BUILD.INDEX file ITEM rebuilds it from the file.

13 Oct 26  INDICES ON E-TYPE ITEMS (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a kernel
           change, so it needs a rebuild).

             CREATE.INDEX file ITEM            ITEM may be an E-type item
             BUILD.INDEX file ITEM
             SELECTINDEX ITEM, value           as for any index

           An E-type item of a data collection file can be indexed as a D-type
           item can, including one whose path has an asterisk (every element
           becomes a key).  The index is kept up to date as records are written
           and deleted, and a query with a WITH clause on the item uses it.  A
           D-type item with a field number is refused on a collection file, and
           an E-type item on any other file.

13 Oct 26  LINKED COLLECTIONS AND TRANS() (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a
           kernel change, so it needs a rebuild).

             ok = EXPAND(c{'path'})            join the record a link names
             ok = EXPAND(c{'path'}, filevar)   ...with a default file
             TRANS('file', id, 'ITEM', 'X')    an E-type item, by name
             TRANS(file, @ID, ITEM, 'X')       ...or in an I-type
             @RECORD{'a/b'}                    in an I-type, of a collection

           A link is a string in a collection of the form "filename:id", or
           ":id" when EXPAND() is given a file variable.  EXPAND() reads that
           record, which should be a data collection file's, and puts the
           collection it gives where the link was.  It returns 1 if the element
           is now a collection (including when it already was) and 0 if it is
           not a link, the file or the record is missing, or the record is not a
           collection, leaving the element as it was.  The file name is up to the
           first colon, so an id may have colons in it.  What is joined on is a
           copy; writing the parent back is up to the program.

           TRANS() on a data collection file accepts an E-type dictionary item
           for its third argument, and a program may now name any D or E-type
           item as a string: TRANS('CLIENTS', id, 'NAME', 'X').  In an I-type
           the item's name can be written without quotes as before.  A multivalued
           (asterisk) path comes back as it does from any TRANS(), with the
           marks lowered one level.  A D-type item with a field number gives a
           null string on such a file, and field -1 gives the whole record as JSON.

           In an I-type, a name that is an E-type item gives that element of the
           record, and a collection element reference can be written after any
           value: @RECORD{'city'}, @RECORD{'items', 1, 'p'}.  The query commands
           now keep the collection as @RECORD for a collection file, so an I-type
           sees it as a whole.

13 Oct 26  DATA COLLECTION FILES (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a kernel
           change, so it needs a rebuild).

             CREATE.FILE name COLLECTION       a file whose records are collections
             READ c FROM f, id                 c is a collection
             WRITE c ON f, id                  c is written as JSON text
             FILEINFO(f, FL$COLLECTION)        1 for such a file
             LIST file NAME CITY               E-type dictionary items
             CT file id                        shows the record as JSON
             OCONV/ICONV 'HU'                  see the entry below

           A collection file is an ordinary hashed file whose records are held
           as JSON text, so locking, transactions, the file size controls and
           everything else underneath work as they do for any file.  READ,
           READU and READL give the record as a collection and WRITE and
           WRITEU take one; WRITE also accepts text, provided it is a JSON
           object or array, and refuses anything else.  A collection holding a
           value with no JSON form cannot be written.  A read or write trigger
           is called with the collection.  MATREAD is refused, and READV or
           WRITEV of a field number gives the usual error since there are no
           fields.  The dictionary of the file is an ordinary one.

           A new kind of dictionary item, E, is a D-type item whose field 2 is
           an element path instead of a field number: E, path, conversion,
           heading, format, S or M, association.  The path may have up to two
           asterisks, which make the item multivalued.  The query commands (LIST,
           SORT, SELECT, COUNT, SEARCH and the rest) use E-type items as they
           use D-type ones, in headings, WITH clauses, BY clauses and
           conversions.  A D-type item with a field number above 0 is refused
           on a collection file with a message that says to use an E-type item,
           and an E-type item is refused on any other file; @ID works as
           always.  SEARCH looks at the JSON text of the record.

           CT on a collection file shows the record as JSON, one element to a
           line.  JBUILD() has a mode for the same layout, JB$PRETTY (128), an
           addition of SD's.

           Not yet: TRANS() and I-type items that use an E-type item, indices on
           E-type items, linked collections (EXPAND), the collection editor
           and XPARSE().

13 Oct 26  COLLECTIONS: BOOLEANS AND NULLS, OPERATORS ON AN ELEMENT, THE HU
           CONVERSION (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a kernel change, so it
           needs a rebuild).

             c{'on'} = JBOOLEAN(1)             store true (JBOOLEAN(0): false)
             c{'none'} = JNULL()               store a JSON null
             JTYPE(value)                      "boolean", "null", "number",
                                               "string", "collection",
                                               "array" or "other"
             c{'n'} += 5     -= *= /= :=       change an element in place
             OCONV(name, 'HU')  ICONV(name, 'HU')   escape and unescape

           SD has no boolean or null variable type, so these are ordinary
           values that carry a mark: JBOOLEAN(x) is 1 or 0 (false for 0,
           a null string and the text 0 or false in any case, true for
           anything else), JNULL() is a null string.  A collection element
           stored from one keeps its kind, JBUILD() writes true, false or
           null for it, and reading it back gives the mark again, so JTYPE()
           can tell.  The mark is lost the moment you do anything to the
           value: JBOOLEAN(1) + 1 is just 2.

           c{'n'} += 5 and the others read the element, apply the operator
           and store the result.  An element that is not there is a null
           string to start from, so += starts at 0 and := at nothing, and
           the levels of the path are made as for any assignment.

           OCONV(s, 'HU') writes everything but letters, digits and - . _ ~
           as %xx with lower case hex digits, so a URL can be a name in a
           collection without its slashes being taken for a path; ICONV
           undoes it and leaves a % that is not followed by two hex digits,
           and a +, as they are.

13 Oct 26  JSON: JPARSE() AND JBUILD() (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a
           kernel change, so it needs a rebuild).

             c = JPARSE(text)                  JSON text into a collection
             c = JPARSE(text, mode)
             text = JBUILD(c)                  and back
             text = JBUILD(c, mode)

           JPARSE() reads a JSON object or array.  An object becomes a
           collection, an array an array, a string a string, a number an
           integer when it is whole and fits in 32 bits and a floating point
           number otherwise.  true and false are kept as true and false and
           null as null, so JBUILD() writes them back as they were; read from
           the collection they are 1, 0 and a null string.  Spaces, tabs,
           returns, line feeds and all five mark characters between elements
           are ignored.  Names and strings may be in single or double quotes.
           \uXXXX is the single byte XX when it is 255 or less (so marks and
           8 bit characters come back as they went out) and UTF-8 above that.
           Nesting is limited to 500 levels.  A name that appears twice takes
           the later value.

           If the text is not JSON, or is not an object or an array,
           JPARSE() returns a null string, STATUS() is 1020 (ER$PARSE.ERROR)
           and INMAT() is the position of the character where it gave up.

           JBUILD() writes compact text with no spaces and the names in
           order.  Strings are escaped; marks, control characters and 8 bit
           characters become \u00XX, which JPARSE() reads back as the same
           byte.  A value with no JSON form (a file variable, say) or a
           collection that contains itself makes it return a null string with
           STATUS() 1019 (ER$BUILD.ERROR).

           The modes are added together and are named in the KEYS.H include:

             JP$NULL            a null is read as a null string
             JP$UNQUOTED.NAME   names may have no quotes
             JB$INVALID         a value with no JSON form is written as null
             JB$NULL            a null string is written as null
             JB$NUM             a string that is a JSON number is written as one
             JB$SQUOTE.NAME     names in single quotes
             JB$NOQUOTE.NAME    names with no quotes
             JB$SQUOTE.VALUE    strings in single quotes
             JB$NOQUOTE.VALUE   strings with no quotes

           The values (1, 2, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64) are SD's own.

13 Oct 26  ARRAYS IN DATA COLLECTIONS (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a
           kernel change, so it needs a rebuild).

             c{'list'} = MAT()                 an empty array
             c{'list'} = MAT(matrix)           a copy of a dimensioned matrix
             c{'list'} = MAT(matrix, 10)       at most 10 elements of it
             c{'list', n} = value              set element n (or add n+1)
             c{'list', -1} = value             add on the end
             INS value AS c{'list', n}         open a gap at n
             DEL c{'list', n}                  take n out, renumbering
             MAT target = MAT c{'list'}        copy it into a matrix
             INMAT(c{'list'})                  how many elements it has
             c{'orders/*/qty'}                 one value from every element

           An array in a collection is numbered from 1 and has no gaps:
           element n can only be set when 1 to n-1 exist, and a negative
           position adds on the end.  A matrix of two dimensions is copied
           row by row.  An element of a matrix nobody assigned becomes a null
           string.  INS and DEL renumber everything after them.  INS on a
           collection that is not an array just puts the element in, and
           INS MAT() AS c{'list'} puts in an empty array.

           An asterisk in a path stands for every element of an array, and
           the answer is a dynamic array: one value for each element, and
           with a second asterisk one subvalue for each element of the array
           below.  An element that does not have the item leaves its place
           empty.  Up to two asterisks may be used and only to read; setting
           or deleting through one is refused.

           AS is now a reserved word in expressions (it ends the value in
           INS value AS ...), so a variable called AS can no longer follow a
           value as a format.

13 Oct 26  DATA COLLECTIONS, FIRST PART (1.2-0, openQM compatibility; a
           kernel change, so it needs a rebuild).

             c = COLLECTION()                  a new, empty collection
             d = COLLECTION(c)                 an independent copy
             c{'name'} = value                 put an element in
             value = c{'name'}                 read it back
             DEL c{'name'}                     take it out
             ELEMENT.EXISTS(c, 'name')         1 if it is there, else 0
             ENUMERATE(c)                      its names, sorted

           A data collection is a set of named values, like a dynamic array
           whose elements are found by name instead of position.  The value
           of an element can be a number, a string or another collection, so
           collections nest as deeply as memory allows.  Names are case
           sensitive and ENUMERATE() gives them in simple left-to-right
           order, capitals before lower case.

           The braces take a name, several names or a path.  c{'a','b'},
           c{'a'}{'b'} and c{'a/b'} are all the same element, and the forms
           can be mixed.  Setting an element creates whatever levels are
           missing on the way; DEL of a level removes everything below it.
           Reading an element that is not there gives a null string and
           sets STATUS() to 1004.

           Assigning a collection (d = c) does not copy it: both names refer
           to the same collection, as with a file variable.  Use
           COLLECTION(c) for a copy; it copies nested collections as well.

           This is the first part.  Not there yet: arrays inside a
           collection, JPARSE() and JBUILD() for JSON, collection files, the
           collection editor.  A collection that contains itself is never
           freed, which is how openQM behaves too.

13 Oct 26  FIELD AND VSLICE TAKE MORE, CATALOG ... GLOBAL c, THREE SHOW
           CHANGES AND SP.VIEW SORTING (1.2-0, openQM compatibility;
           VSLICE needs a rebuild for its new opcode, the rest is BASIC).

           SP.VIEW file BY field (or BY.DSND, with NO.CASE before or after
           the field name) sorts the list of records it offers.  It works as
           SELECT does, so the field is a dictionary item of the file, and
           like openQM's it replaces the default select list.  In a record,
           Return now pages forward (it did nothing) and Esc-Z pages back.

           FIELD(string, delimiter, n, *) and string[delimiter, n, *]
           return everything from the n-th field to the end.  Before, the
           last number had to be a count.  An asterisk anywhere else in an
           expression is still a multiplication.

           VSLICE(string, vpos, svpos) takes a slice of subvalues.  With a
           vpos of 1 or more it comes from that value only; with a vpos
           below 1 it comes from every value, and the value marks that keep
           the positions are kept.  Marks are written only in front of data,
           so empty values and fields at the end are dropped.  With svpos
           below 1 and vpos of 1 or more it is the ordinary value slice.
           VSLICE(string, vpos) is unchanged.

           CATALOG file name GLOBAL c puts the prefix character c (one of
           * ! _ $) in front of every call name that has none of its own, so
           a whole select list can be catalogued globally with a prefix.  A
           name that already has a prefix keeps it.  It does not accept a
           hyphen as a prefix.

           SHOW: B moves to the last page (T was the only way to move far).
           The keyword SORT.SUP stops SHOW sorting its list by record id when
           there is no BY clause, which matters when the order the file gives
           is the order wanted; a BY clause still sorts.  Record ids given on
           the command line that are not in the file are now listed, one per
           line as 'id' not found, and SHOW stops without drawing the screen
           or making a list.  Before, they were skipped without a word.

12 Oct 26  PTERM ERASE, CTRL.FILTER AND QUERY (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundle A-13; a kernel change, so it needs a
           rebuild).

             PTERM ERASE ON | OFF | n | ^c | DEFAULT
             PTERM CTRL.FILTER ON | OFF
             PTERM QUERY

           ERASE chooses the key that rubs out a character in INPUT.  At the
           start of a session INPUT accepts ctrl-H, DEL and whatever the
           terminal type names (kbs), all together.  PTERM ERASE then makes
           one key the erase key and nothing else: ON is ctrl-H (character
           8), n is a character number (1 to 31, or 127 for DEL), ^c is the
           control key written with its letter (^D, ^H, ...), OFF means INPUT
           has no erase key at all, so the keys that were erase keys are
           ordinary characters.  DEFAULT, an addition, goes back to how a
           session starts.  PTERM ERASE on its own says which it is.  INPUT@
           is not affected; it keeps using the terminal type.

           CTRL.FILTER ON makes INPUT and INPUT@ throw away the control
           characters that are not keys they use (the erase key and Return
           still work).  It is set for one command level: a program run with
           EXECUTE starts with it off, and the level that set it has it on
           again when the program returns.  Off is how a session starts.

           QUERY shows what keys send, which is what you need to write a
           terminfo entry.  Press a key and it shows the bytes in
           hexadecimal, as characters (a dot for those that do not print) and
           for a control key or a sequence the way terminfo writes it (\E[A
           for ESC [ A).  A space ends it.  The mark translation of PTERM MARK
           is held off while it runs.

           PTERM DISPLAY now reports the erase key and the control filter.

           What this does not do.  A terminal whose keys send their bytes
           with a long pause between them would show one key as several, since
           QUERY treats bytes within a tenth of a second as one key.  And
           what a pipe delivers all at once, QUERY shows as one key.

12 Oct 26  PTERM MARK (1.2-0, openQM compatibility bundle A-13; a kernel
           change, so it needs a rebuild).

             PTERM MARK ON
             PTERM MARK OFF
             PTERM MARK

           With MARK ON, three control keys typed at the keyboard are
           turned into the delimiters a program cannot otherwise get from a
           terminal: ctrl-\ (character 28) becomes a subvalue mark,
           ctrl-] (29) a value mark and ctrl-^ (30) a field mark.  It
           applies to everything read from the keyboard, so INPUT, KEYIN()
           and the rest see the marks.  PTERM MARK on its own says whether
           it is on, and PTERM DISPLAY now reports it.  It is off when a
           session starts.

           What this does not do.  It is set for the session only, there is
           no configuration value to start it on, and it changes only these
           three keys.  The openQM PTERM page also has CODEPAGE, which is for
           Windows consoles only, and ENCODING, which is not wanted here.

12 Oct 26  SCAN, SEARCH ... DISPLAY AND STRINGS (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundle A-3; new VOC entries, added to an account by
           UPDATE.ACCOUNT, and a change to the query processor).

             SCAN {DICT} file {selection} {ids} {ALL} {NO.CASE}
                  {ALL.MATCH} {FOR "text" ...} {STRINGS file record}
             SEARCH file ... DISPLAY

           SEARCH selects the records that hold a text string.  Now it can
           also show where: with DISPLAY, for each record it selects it
           prints the record id, then each line of the record that holds
           any of the strings, with the line number:

             SEARCH BP FOR "!SORT" DISPLAY
             SCAN BP FOR "!SORT"          (the same thing)

             prog1
             0012: call !sort(list, 'AL')
             0040: if x then call !sort(other, 'DR')

           A line is a field of the record; the value, subvalue and text
           marks in it are shown as ], \ and [.  The records are still
           selected as select list 0, and the usual count follows.  SCAN is
           SEARCH with DISPLAY and takes the same words: a selection
           clause, record ids, FROM, ALL (search the id too), NO.CASE,
           ALL.MATCH and FOR.  As before, with no FOR it asks for strings
           until a blank answer.

           New for both, and so for SEARCH without DISPLAY too:

             STRINGS file record

           takes the search strings from a record: each field is one
           string, and a null field is ignored.  It adds to any given with
           FOR.  A file or record that is not there is reported and nothing
           is searched.

           What this does not do.  DISPLAY shows the lines of a record that
           hold a string; with ALL.MATCH, it still shows the lines holding
           any one of them.  With NO.MATCH it shows nothing, since the
           records selected hold none.  Up to 100 strings are used (20
           before).

12 Oct 26  HOLD.FILE.LOGGER, $SETPTR.DEFAULTS AND @SEQNO (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundle A-6; a kernel and system-routine change,
           so it needs a rebuild).

           HOLD.FILE.LOGGER.  When a print unit in mode 3 (hold file) or
           mode 6 (hold file and printer) opens its file, and again when
           it closes it, SD calls a catalogued subroutine named
           HOLD.FILE.LOGGER if there is one:

             SUBROUTINE HOLD.FILE.LOGGER(UNIT, OPENING, PATHNAME)

           UNIT is the print unit, OPENING is 1 for an open and 0 for a
           close, and PATHNAME is the name of the file as SD holds it,
           such as $hold/P0 or the pathname given to AS PATHNAME.  It can
           keep a log of print jobs or act once a job has been closed.
           Nothing happens if there is no such subroutine, and a plain
           printer job (mode 1) never calls it.

           $SETPTR.DEFAULTS.  An X-type record of that name in the VOC of
           the account, or if there is none in the system's VOC, gives
           options that SETPTR takes as if they had been written straight
           after the mode.  Field 2 is for mode 1, field 4 for mode 3,
           field 6 for mode 5, and so on: field mode + 1.  A record
           reading

             X
             NEWLINE CRLF, EJECT

           makes every SETPTR into mode 1 behave as if NEWLINE CRLF and
           EJECT had been written.  Options in the command itself come
           after them and so win.  It applies to a SETPTR with no options
           at all (SETPTR 0,,,,,3) as well.

           @SEQNO, GETPU(PU$SEQNO, unit).  The number the last AS NEXT
           hold file was given, as it appears in the file name (0012, not
           12).  @SEQNO is the last one this process made, whichever unit;
           GETPU is the unit's own.  Both are null until a NEXT job has
           started.

           What this does not do.  The ENCODING option of the same manual
           page is not here.  HOLD.FILE.LOGGER is called for a hold file
           only, not for the temporary file a printer job is spooled
           from.

12 Oct 26  SETPTR FORM.FEED, AND THE DIGITS OF THE NEXT NUMBER (1.2-0,
           openQM compatibility bundle A-6; a kernel change, so it needs a
           rebuild).

             SETPTR unit, ... , FORM.FEED FF
             SETPTR unit, ... , FORM.FEED CRFF

           Chooses the sequence sent between pages of print data and wherever
           the data holds a form feed: FF is the form feed alone, CRFF a
           carriage return then the form feed.  CRFF is the default, and what
           SD has always sent.  SETPTR unit,DISPLAY now ends its list with
           the setting, FORM.FEED FF or FORM.FEED CRFF, as it does for
           NEWLINE, so a captured setting restores it.

           The AS NEXT option of SETPTR numbers successive hold file items
           from a counter in the $NEXT record of the dictionary of $HOLD.
           Field 2 of that record is the next number.  Field 3 may now say
           how many digits the number has, from 3 to 9; without it, or with
           anything else, it has four.  The numbers run from 1 up to the
           highest the digits hold (9999 for four) and then start again.

           Correction: the numbers used to start again after 1000, not
           9999, in the four digits they were written in.  A site that
           depended on that gets the longer run now; set field 3 to 3 for a
           run of 1 to 999.

           (The ENCODING option the same manual lists is not here; see the
           next entry for the rest of what the manual describes.)

12 Oct 26  SP.VIEW CAN DELETE THE ITEM IT IS SHOWING (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundle A-6).

           While SP.VIEW shows a record, X now deletes it.  It asks first
           ("Delete item from file (Y/N)?") and only Y or y deletes; any
           other key leaves the item as it was.  A record that another user
           holds locked is reported and left alone.  Opened from the pick
           list of a file, the item is taken off the list when it goes and
           the cursor stays near where it was; opened by name, the viewer
           ends, since there is nothing left to show.  The prompt line
           now lists X-delete among the keys.

           It works on whatever file SP.VIEW has open, not only $hold, and
           with the access of the user who runs it, like DELETE.

           What this does not do.  The openQM manual also describes a sort
           clause (SP.VIEW file BY field) and a Return key that pages
           forward while a record is shown; SP.VIEW here has neither.

12 Oct 26  TERM SETS THE DEFAULT PRINTER'S PAGE SIZE (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundle A-6).

             TERM {columns} {, lines} {, prt.cols} {, prt.lines} {type}

           TERM already set the width and depth of the display.  It now
           takes two more numbers for the default printer, print unit 0:

             TERM 120,32               the screen 120 x 32
             TERM ,,132,66             the printer 132 x 66, the screen
                                       as it was
             TERM ,,,50                the printer's depth alone

           A number may be left out if its comma is not, and a number left
           out stays as it is.  The widths must be 20 to 32767 and the
           depths 10 to 32767, for the printer as for the screen.  With no
           arguments TERM now shows the printer's width and depth after the
           screen's.  TERM DEFAULT is unchanged: it sets the screen to 120
           x 36, which is what a session starts with (the openQM manual
           says 80 x 24; SD does not), and leaves the printer alone.

           Also corrected: a screen depth was checked against the largest
           width instead of the largest depth; both are 32767, so nothing
           that worked before stops working.

12 Oct 26  SELECTINDEX COMMAND (1.2-0, openQM compatibility bundle A-7; a
           new VOC entry, added to an account by UPDATE.ACCOUNT).

             SELECTINDEX {DICT} filename indexname {value} {TO n}

           The command form of the SELECTINDEX statement, for the command
           prompt and paragraphs.  Without a value it builds select list 0
           (or n) from the values held in the alternate key index; with
           one, from the ids of the records that have that value.  It says
           how many it selected, as SELECT does.  The index name is found
           as typed, then in lower and then in upper case, and the file name
           the same.

             SELECTINDEX orders customer.name
             SELECTINDEX orders customer.name Smith
             LIST orders

           What this does not do.  The value is matched as it is stored in
           the index, so for a date or a number it is the internal form, as
           in the statement.  The file must be a dynamic file with the index
           built (BUILD.INDEX), and an index that has been created but not
           built selects nothing.  TO n is an addition: the openQM text
           describes the command without it.

           Also corrected: SELECT.DIR and GET.LIST, given TO with no list
           number, said "Error writing saved list record"; they now say
           "Select list number missing or invalid".

12 Oct 26  COPY.LISTP (1.2-0, openQM compatibility bundle A-7; a new VOC
           entry, added to an account by UPDATE.ACCOUNT).

             COPY.LISTP {{DICT} src.file} {id.list | *} {(options}

           The Pick form of COPY.LIST, and the same program as COPYP: the
           options (B D I N O P S T) and the "To:" question are COPYP's.
           What differs is that the source file is $savedlists, where saved
           select lists are kept, unless one is named, so
           COPY.LISTP mylist asks where to copy the list called mylist, and
           COPY.LISTP * (T shows every saved list.

           When the command names two or more words before the options, the
           first is taken as the source file if it opens as a file, and
           every word is a list name if it does not.  A file and a list of
           the same name are therefore told apart by writing the file name
           first (COPY.LISTP $savedlists mylist).  After DICT the next word
           is always the file.

           What this does not do.  The openQM description given for this
           command reads like COPYP's own; the rule for telling a file name
           from a list name is SD's.  COPYP itself is unchanged, and still
           needs its file name.

           A related defect found on the way: the COPYP record in the
           installer's bootstrap VOC (voc_template) had a description where
           its type should be.  It is a verb record now, as it is in the
           Windows port.

12 Oct 26  LISTDICT (1.2-0, openQM compatibility bundle A-8; a new VOC
           entry, added to an account by UPDATE.ACCOUNT).

             LISTDICT filename {LPTR}

           Lists the dictionary of a file in the form Pick systems use: one
           line for each A, D, I and S record, with its type, its field
           number or expression, conversion, column heading, format, S/M
           and association, in that order of type.  LPTR sends the listing
           to the printer instead of the screen.

           It is a paragraph over LIST DICT, so the layout is the one LIST
           DICT gives and the columns follow the DICT.DICT dictionary.  What
           it adds is the choice of record types: phrases and every other
           type are left out.  LIST DICT filename still lists everything.

           What this does not do.  The openQM text says only that the
           column widths follow the screen width; the layout here is SD's
           own, not a copy of openQM's page.  LPTR must be the third word
           of the command.

12 Oct 26  TRY / CATCH / THROW, CAUGHT() AND @EXCEPTION (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundle B-4; a kernel and compiler change, so it
           needs a rebuild.  Programs compiled before it are not affected).

           An exception is a named event that a program raises with THROW
           and handles with TRY / CATCH, wherever in the program, or in the
           subroutines it CALLs, the THROW happens.  The program leaves
           every level below the TRY, then goes on in the CATCH clause that
           names the exception:

             LOOP
                READNEXT ACC.ID ELSE EXIT
                TRY
                   CALL PROCESS.ACCOUNT
                CATCH ACCOUNT.INVALID
                   DISPLAY 'Account ' : ACC.ID : ' is invalid'
                END
             REPEAT

             TRY {statement}
                statements
             CATCH exception {qualifiers} {, exception {qualifiers} ...}
                statements
             {CATCH ... any number of further clauses}
             END

             THROW exception {, data}

           The exception name is not case sensitive and may be up to 63
           characters.  In CATCH it may be written in quotes or without
           them; in THROW it is any expression.  A TRY may have several
           CATCH clauses and one clause may name several exceptions; the
           first clause that names it is the one used.  Two special names
           can be used in CATCH: SYS$ANY takes any exception that no clause
           names, and SYS$UNHANDLED takes an exception that no TRY anywhere
           in the program's call chain names or takes by SYS$ANY.

           In the CATCH clause these hold what was thrown:

             @EXCEPTION          the name, as it was written in THROW
             @EXCEPTION.DATA    the data given to THROW, null if none
             @EXCEPTION.ORIGIN  the name of the program that threw it, a
                                field mark and the line number of the
                                THROW (-1 where the program has no line
                                table), the program being named as error
                                messages and SYSTEM(1002) name it
             @EXCEPTION.STACK   the call stack, in the form of SYSTEM(1002),
                                when the CATCH name was followed by
                                SAVING.STACK; null otherwise

           Two qualifiers may follow an exception name in CATCH, and apply
           to that name only: DUMPING writes a process dump file, as PDUMP
           does, and SAVING.STACK fills @EXCEPTION.STACK; both are done
           when the exception is thrown, before the program leaves the
           levels below the TRY.  $MODE EXCEPTION.SAVE.STACK gives every
           CATCH in the program SAVING.STACK.

             CAUGHT(exception {, modes})

           is 1 if a TRY that is active now (in this program or in one that
           called it) has a CATCH for that exception, 0 if not, so that a
           subroutine can find out before it throws.  Modes adds up what
           else counts: 1 a SYS$ANY handler, 2 a SYS$UNHANDLED handler;
           without it only a clause that names the exception counts.

           An exception that nothing handles stops the program, with
           "Unhandled exception name" and the data after a colon, as a run
           time error does.

           What this does not do.  Errors SD itself finds, such as dividing
           by zero or a file that cannot be opened, are not exceptions and
           still stop the program as before; only THROW raises one.  An
           exception does not pass through EXECUTE, a trigger or a
           recursive program: in there it is unhandled unless a TRY inside
           handles it.  The program must not jump into or out of a TRY
           clause with GOTO, EXIT or CONTINUE (openQM has the same rule);
           the handler stays set until the program returns.  RETURN from a
           GOSUB or a CALL inside the clause is fine.  At most 64 TRY
           clauses may be active at once.  Objects that the levels left
           behind hold are not given a DESTROY.OBJECT call.  openQM's
           !EXC.STACK subroutine is not provided.  TRY, CATCH, THROW and
           CAUGHT are now words of the language: a program that used one of
           them as the name of an array or a function needs it renamed.  A
           plain variable of that name is not affected.

12 Oct 26  INMAT() AFTER MATCHES AND MATCHFIELD (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundle B-4, fifth part; a kernel change, so it
           needs a rebuild but no recompile of programs).

           A MATCHES template may hold several patterns separated by value
           marks; the string matches if any one of them does.  Until now
           there was no way to tell which one.  After MATCHES or
           MATCHFIELD(), INMAT() is now the number of the pattern that
           matched, counting from 1 in the order they are written in the
           template, and 0 when none of them matched:

             IF 'ab12' MATCHES '3N':@VM:'3A':@VM:'2A2N' THEN
                CRT INMAT()                 ;* 3
             END

           The first pattern that matches is the one reported, as it is the
           one used.  A template with a single pattern gives 1 when it
           matches.

           What this does not do.  INMAT() is also what MATPARSE, MATREAD
           and OPEN leave behind, so a MATCHES between one of those and the
           INMAT() that reads its result replaces that result: read INMAT()
           straight after the statement it belongs to.  SD has no
           MATCHESS() or PARSE() function, so those are not covered.

12 Oct 26  EREPLACE() (1.2-0, openQM compatibility bundle B-4, fourth part;
           a new opcode, so it needs a rebuild and the compiler recompiled).

             EREPLACE(string, old, new {, occurrence {, start}})
                          The same as CHANGE() when old is not null: every
                          occurrence of old is replaced by new, or
                          "occurrence" of them (less than 1 means all)
                          starting at the "start"th.  When old is null, new
                          is put in front of the string.  A null new
                          deletes.

             FIX(number {, precision {, mode}})
                          The number as a string with exactly "precision"
                          decimal places, trailing zeros kept: rounded to the
                          nearest (halves away from zero) when mode is 0 or
                          left out, cut off when mode is not 0.  Without a
                          precision the program's PRECISION is used.
                          FIX(37.73629273, 2) is 37.74 and with mode 1 it is
                          37.73.

             MATSTR(matrix)
                          A dynamic array from a whole dimensioned matrix:
                          for a two dimensional matrix a field for each row
                          and a value for each column; for a one
                          dimensional one a field for each element, as
                          MATBUILD gives (trailing nulls left off).  The
                          zero element is not used and marks already in the
                          data are not escaped.  It also works on a matrix
                          passed into a subroutine.

           What this does not do.  FIX works to 14 decimal places and to
           numbers of about 18 digits; beyond that it uses the plain C
           formatting.  For exact decimal arithmetic use SADD and the other
           S functions.

12 Oct 26  SETPTR INFORM, EJECT AND AS ... IN (1.2-0, openQM compatibility
           bundle A-6, second part; the print code changed in the kernel, so
           it needs a rebuild).  Syntax is SD's own where openQM's is not
           given.  SETPTR options are separated by commas.

             SETPTR 0,80,60,0,0,3,AS job,INFORM
                          Says "Print output is going to $hold/job" when the
                          job starts (modes 3 and 6).
             SETPTR ...,EJECT
                          Ends the job with a form feed, if anything was
                          printed.
             SETPTR ...,AS job IN dirfile
                          The job is held in the directory file named
                          instead of $HOLD.

12 Oct 26  FIVE NEW SUBROUTINES AND A SIZE FROM DIR() (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundle B-4, third part; DIR() is kernel code, so it
           needs a rebuild).  Where openQM has a function, SD's own
           programs have the same thing as a function only inside SD, so
           these are subroutines an application can CALL.

             CALL !MERGELIST(result, list1, list2, mode)
                          Merges two field mark lists in order.  Mode words:
                          U union (default), A all with duplicates, I in
                          both, D in list 1 not list 2, X in one only; AL,
                          DL, AR or DR for the order; N if the lists are not
                          in order.  A null list is just an empty one.
             CALL !OPTION(number, state)
                          Reads (state null) or sets (state 0 or 1) one of
                          the OPTION flags, by its number, OPT.xxx in
                          INT$KEYS.H.
             CALL !VOCPATH(name, path, status)
                          The full pathname that OPEN would use for a VOC
                          file name, following Q-pointers; DICT name works.
             CALL !TESTLOCK(n, owner)
                          The user number holding task lock n, or 0.
             DIR(path)    Each entry now has a third value, the size in
                          bytes: name, F or D, size.  Programs that took the
                          first two values are unaffected.

12 Oct 26  REBUILD.ALL.INDICES, REDO AND A PHANTOM STATEMENT (1.2-0, the
           openQM list's unbundled items).  Syntax is SD's own.

             REBUILD.ALL.INDICES {NO.QUERY}
                          Rebuilds the indices of every file in the account
                          that has any (BUILD.INDEX file ALL for each).  It
                          asks first unless NO.QUERY is given.
             REDO {TIMES n} {EVERY s} command
                          Runs the command again and again: n times in all
                          (default: until the break key), a run starting
                          every s seconds (default 1; whole seconds).
             PHANTOM "command" in a program
                          A statement that starts a phantom, the same as
                          EXECUTE "PHANTOM command" and with the same
                          CAPTURING, RETURNING and similar clauses.  The
                          user number is in @SYSTEM.RETURN.CODE.

12 Oct 26  STRING ARITHMETIC, REGEX() AND A PRIVATE VOCABULARY (1.2-0, the
           openQM list's unbundled items; the kernel has six new functions,
           so it needs a rebuild and the compiler recompiled).

             SADD(a, b)   Add, subtract, multiply and divide numbers held as
             SSUB(a, b)   strings, of any length, with an optional sign and a
             SMUL(a, b)   decimal point.  There is no rounding and no
             SDIV(a, b)   floating point: SADD("0.1", "0.2") is exactly 0.3,
                          and SMUL of two 20 digit numbers gives all 40
                          digits.  SDIV gives 15 decimal places, cut off
                          (not rounded), with trailing zeros dropped; a zero
                          divisor is a run-time error.
             SCMP(a, b)   -1, 0 or 1 as a is less than, equal to or greater
                          than b, numerically.
             REGEX(s, p)  1 if the extended regular expression p matches
                          anywhere in s, else 0.  Begin the pattern with
                          (?i) to ignore case.  A pattern that is not valid
                          stops the program with the reason.
             $PRIVATE.VOC A VOC record  X / filename  names a file laid out
                          as the VOC is; a command that is nowhere in the
                          VOC is looked for there last, so an application
                          can keep its own commands out of the account's VOC.

           What this does not do.  A string that is not a number counts as
           zero in the S functions rather than raising an error.  REGEX uses
           the C library's POSIX syntax, not Perl's: no \d, no lazy
           quantifiers.

12 Oct 26  PHANTOM OPTIONS, PDUMP, LOGOUT GROUP (1.2-0, openQM compatibility
           bundles B-7, B-6 and B-8, first parts; the kernel gained an
           opcode, so it needs a rebuild and the compiler recompiled).  The
           openQM notes give no syntax, so this is SD's own.

             PHANTOM NO.MSG command   Does not display "Started phantom".
             PHANTOM DATA command     Hands this process's DATA queue to the
                                      phantom (the queue here is emptied);
                                      the phantom's INPUT reads it.
             PHANTOM LOG file command The phantom's log record is made in the
                                      directory file named, not in $COMO.
                                      The words may come in any order, in
                                      front of the command.
             CALL !PHLOG(user, name)  The log record of the phantom with that
                                      user number (the newest PH<user>_...
                                      in $COMO), or null.
             LOGOUT n GROUP           Logs out process n and every phantom it
                                      started.
             PDUMP                    A BASIC statement: writes a process
                                      dump file of the running process and
                                      goes on.
             Process dump files       Show the length of each string
                                      variable, and list the environment
                                      (a value is left out when its name has
                                      PASS, SECRET, TOKEN or KEY in it).
             Q-pointers               A Q-pointer in the VOC whose file name
                                      (field 3) is null now points at the
                                      account's VOC.

           What this does not do.  PHANTOM NO.MSG hides the start message
           only; SD prints no message in the parent when a phantom ends.
           Of the rest of the diagnostic list (lock times, deadlock detail,
           object variables in a dump) nothing is built.

12 Oct 26  FOUR BASIC ADDITIONS (1.2-0, openQM compatibility bundle B-5,
           second part; the kernel gained an opcode and OPENSEQ changed, so
           it needs a rebuild and the compiler recompiled).  Syntax is SD's
           own where the openQM notes give none.

             CLEARCOMMON name        Sets the variables of the named common
                                     block to zero.  CLEARCOMMON alone still
                                     clears the unnamed block.
             $MODE TIME.MS           TIME() then returns the seconds since
                                     midnight to the millisecond, as a
                                     number with a fraction (it is
                                     SYSTEM(1020) / 1000).
             OPENSEQ file,id CREATING TO f
                                     Makes the file at once if it is not
                                     there, and takes the THEN clause.
             OPENSEQ file,id SHARED TO f
                                     Opens for appending (and makes it if
                                     needed) so that several processes can
                                     add to the same file, an audit log for
                                     example: each WRITESEQ is one whole
                                     line at the end of the file, and no
                                     process overwrites another's.  It is
                                     not buffered, so it is slower than a
                                     plain APPEND.

           What this does not do.  SYSTEM(12) is not changed by TIME.MS;
           the other openQM statements (INPUT options, INPUTIF, OPENTEMP,
           EXECUTE STACKLIST, FLUSH.DH.CACHE, CSV.MODE, FOR EACH, the ~=
           operator and the rest) are not built yet.

12 Oct 26  CREATE.FILE COMMENT, MULTIFILE AND RELATIVE PATHNAME (1.2-0,
           openQM compatibility bundle A-8, second part).  Syntax is SD's
           own.

             CREATE.FILE name COMMENT "text"
                            Puts the text in the VOC record after the type
                            code, where a person listing the VOC sees it.
             CREATE.FILE name MULTIFILE
                            Makes the data portion a multifile with one
                            subfile named for the file, as CREATE.FILE
                            name,name would.
             CREATE.FILE name PATHNAME dir
                            A relative dir is now kept relative in the VOC
                            record, so the account can be moved or copied.
                            A full path is stored as before.

           Also found and not changed: SD's SET.TRIGGER already refuses a
           file that is not dynamic, so LIST.TRIGGERS has no trigger on a
           directory file to show.  FSTAT already takes file names and DICT.

12 Oct 26  MD5(), SAVED LISTS IN ANOTHER FILE, INDEX OPTIONS (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundles B-4 and A-7, second part; MD5 is a kernel
           function, so it needs a rebuild and the compiler recompiled).

             MD5(string)    The MD5 digest of the string, as 32 lower case
                            hexadecimal characters:  MD5("abc") is
                            900150983cd24fb0d6963f7d28e17f72.  MD5 is not
                            secure against a determined attacker; use it to
                            check data, not to protect passwords.
             SAVE.LIST name IN file
             GET.LIST name IN file
             DELETE.LIST name IN file
                            Use the file named, instead of $SAVEDLISTS, for
                            the saved lists.  SAVE.LIST does not create it.
             CREATE.INDEX file field ... DELETING
             MAKE.INDEX   file field ... DELETING
                            Delete an index of the same name that is already
                            there first, instead of refusing.
             $INDEX.PATH    A VOC record  X / pathname  makes that the place
                            new indices go when CREATE.INDEX or MAKE.INDEX
                            has no PATHNAME.  The directory must not exist
                            yet; the first index makes it.
             LIST.INDEX file ALL LPTR {n}
                            Sends the report to a printer.

           What this does not do.  The other new openQM functions in the
           list (EREPLACE, FIX, MERGELIST, JBUILD, CAUGHT and the rest) are
           not built.

12 Oct 26  CATALOGUE AND MAP OPTIONS, TWO NEW OPTIONS (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundles A-10 and A-4, second part; the kernel's
           option array grew, so it needs a rebuild).  Syntax is SD's own.

             MAP LOCAL           Lists the programs catalogued locally in
                                 the account (marked L), as MAP lists the
                                 global (*) and private ones.
             MAP NO.PAGE         Heading once, no page breaks, on the screen.
             CATALOG ... LOCAL RELATIVE
                                 Stores the object file's path relative to
                                 the account when it lies below it, so the
                                 account can be moved or copied.
             OPTION CATALOGUE.LOCAL
                                 CATALOG and DELETE.CATALOG use the local
                                 catalogue when no mode is given.  A name
                                 with a global prefix is still global.
             OPTION DIR.SEL.OMIT.HIDDEN
                                 SELECT of a directory file leaves out the
                                 names that begin with a dot.

           What this does not do.  Asking before a local catalogue entry is
           pointed at a different object file is not built: it would stop an
           unattended install.  A prefix given apart from the call name, and
           a hyphen prefix, need compiler support and are not built.

12 Oct 26  COMMAND LANGUAGE: COMO, IF IN AND EXISTS, INLINE PROMPTS (1.2-0,
           openQM compatibility bundle A-9, second part; the kernel's COMO
           statement changed, so it needs a rebuild).  Syntax is SD's own.

             COMO ON record APPEND   Adds to the record if it exists
                                     instead of replacing it.
             COMO ON record IN file  Writes the record in the directory file
                                     named, not in $COMO.
             IF EXISTS file record THEN ...      In a paragraph: true if the
             IF NOT.EXISTS file record THEN ...  record is in the file.
             IF value IN a b c THEN ...          True if the value is one of
             IF value NOT.IN a b c THEN ...      the words after IN.
             ... NO.CASE                         Put after IN's list, or
                                                 after the second value of
                                                 =, <>, < and so on, to
                                                 ignore case.
             <<a,Prompt>>   An inline prompt's control letters (A, C, F, I,
                            L, R, S, U, SUBR, SYSTEM) may now be lower case.
             <<A,"Prompt, with a comma">>
                            Quotes round the prompt text let it hold commas.

           What this does not do.  Record names given to COMO are mapped as
           for any directory file only if the session has name mapping on.
           The other command-language items in the openQM list (LOGTO RESET
           ALL, the command stack changes, running a catalogued program by
           name) are not built.

12 Oct 26  FILE UTILITIES, COMMON BLOCKS, AUTOMATIC INCLUDES AND NAMES WITH
           TWO UNDERSCORES (1.2-0, openQM compatibility bundles A-1 and
           A-14, second part).  The openQM notes give the purpose of these
           and not the syntax, so the syntax below is SD's own.  The BASIC
           tokenizer changed in the kernel, so it needs a rebuild.

             OSCOPY source target {OVERWRITE | APPEND}
                          Copies an operating system file.  It will not
                          replace a target that exists unless you say
                          OVERWRITE, and APPEND adds the source to the end
                          of the target.  !OSCOPY(source, target, mode,
                          status) is the same for programs (mode 0, 1, 2).
             !IOSCOPY(source, target, status)
                          Copies a file or a whole directory tree.
             COMPARE.FILES file1 file2 {DETAIL}
                          Counts the records only in one, only in the
                          other, in both but different, and the same;
                          DETAIL lists the ids.  DICT file names a
                          dictionary.  The exit value is 0 when the files
                          are alike.
             !MULTISORT(data, key, order)
                          Sorts several related lists (one to a field,
                          elements separated by value marks) by one of
                          them and keeps the others in step.
             FIXDIR file {id ...}
                          Takes the carriage returns out of directory file
                          items that came from a Windows system.
             DELETE.COMMON ALL EXCEPT name ...
                          Deletes every named common block but those
                          listed.
             DELETE        A record another user holds locked is now
                          reported and skipped; DELETE used to wait for it.
             newvoc.mods   If SDSYS has a file of that name, its records
                          are copied over the standard VOC records in each
                          new or updated account.  An account's own record
                          marked [locked] is left alone.  (Built; not yet
                          run on an install.)
             $BASIC.OPTIONS  A line INCLUDE record (or INCLUDE file record)
                          makes the compiler include that record at the top
                          of every program it compiles.  Line numbers in
                          error messages and the debugger are unchanged.
             BASIC names   a__b, with two or more underscores together, is
                          now a valid name.

           What this does not do.  OSCOPY and IOSCOPY run cp, so they have
           the user's own permissions and refuse a path with a single quote
           in it.  FIXDIR rewrites a record only if it holds a carriage
           return.

11 Oct 26  SSELECT SORT OPTIONS AND SSELECTN (1.2-0, openQM compatibility
           bundle B-5, first part; the kernel gained an opcode, so it needs
           a rebuild and the BASIC compiler recompiled).

             SSELECT file RIGHT.ALIGNED     Sorts the ids as numbers where
                                            they are numbers (9, 10, 100)
                                            instead of as text (10, 100, 9).
             SSELECT file DESCENDING        Sorts the other way round.
             SSELECT file TO n ...          Both options may be given, in
                                            either order, before TO.
             SSELECTN file TO n             Same as SSELECT file TO n.

           A program compiled before this change keeps working: the old
           SSELECT instruction still sorts left aligned and ascending.

           What this does not do.  The options go BEFORE "TO n": after it
           the list number would take the option word as part of itself.
           SSELECTV is not built.  The rest of the openQM statement list
           (CLEARCOMMON name, EXECUTE STACKLIST, FLUSH.DH.CACHE,
           CSV.MODE, FOR EACH delimiters, OPENTEMP, INPUT options,
           INPUTIF, the ~= operator and the $MODE settings) is not.

11 Oct 26  SIX MULTIVALUED FUNCTIONS (1.2-0, openQM compatibility bundle
           B-4, first part; the kernel's opcode tables changed, so it needs
           a rebuild and the BASIC compiler recompiled).

             ADDS(a, b)    Adds a and b element by element, where each is
                           a dynamic array.  A list that runs out first
                           counts as zero.
             SUBS(a, b)    Subtracts.
             MULS(a, b)    Multiplies.
             DIVS(a, b)    Divides.
             QUOTES(a)     Puts double quotes round every element of a
             DQUOTES(a)    (the two names are the same function).

           They work on field, value and sub-value marks the way the
           existing ANDS, CATS and MODS do.

           What this does not do.  SQUOTES is not built: it would need a
           new opcode of its own rather than a variant of an existing
           one.  MD5, EREPLACE, FIX, MERGELIST, JBUILD, CAUGHT, VOCPATH,
           MATSTR and the others in the openQM list are not built; some
           need syntax or a return format the release notes do not give.

11 Oct 26  CONFIGURATION DEFAULTS, INVCASE AND sd -status (1.2-0, openQM
           compatibility bundle B-1; the kernel changed, so it needs a
           rebuild).

             INTPREC    The default is now 11 (it was 13).  Thirteen
                        significant figures was found to be too many: the
                        last two or three of a calculated number showed
                        binary rounding noise.  A site that wants the old
                        value sets INTPREC=13 in the configuration file.
             SORTMEM    The default is now 4096 kb (it was 1024): sorts of
                        up to 4 Mb are done in memory, which is faster.
                        A site short of memory sets SORTMEM lower.
             INVCASE    New.  INVCASE=1 in the configuration file turns
                        terminal input case inversion on when a session
                        starts.  The default is 0 (off), as it has been
                        since 13 September, so nothing changes unless the
                        administrator asks.
             sd -status Prints the version, the time SD was started and the
                        number of interactive, phantom and API processes
                        against the user limit.  It needs no privilege.

           What this does not do.  The other parameters in the openQM
           list (LGNWAIT, DHCACHE, PWDELAY, MAXRLOCK, IPADDR, USERPOOL,
           the STARTUP extensions, a longer catalogued call name) are not
           built.  -quiet was already accepted; SD has no VOC update
           query for it to suppress.

11 Oct 26  FOUR NEW SYSTEM() KEYS (1.2-0, openQM compatibility bundle B-2,
           first part; the kernel changed, so it needs a rebuild).

             SYSTEM(35)    The number of users logged in now, phantoms and
                           the API server's sessions included.
             SYSTEM(1016)  The number of user slots still free: the user
                           limit (SYSTEM(1013)) less SYSTEM(35).
             SYSTEM(1045)  The time SD was started, as a Unix epoch value
                           (seconds since 1 January 1970).
             SYSTEM(1047)  A new globally unique identifier, 36 characters
                           such as 3f2c9a52-...; every call returns a new one.

           The names SYS$USERS, SYS$START_TIME and SYS$GUID are in KEYS.H.
           1016 had a name but no value before; it returned nothing useful.

           What this does not do.  The other new keys in the openQM list
           (1036, 1037, 1040, 1065, 1071, 1073 and the FILEINFO() and
           GETPU() ones) are not built; some of them need a facility SD
           does not have.

11 Oct 26  TWO BASIC DEFECTS FIXED (1.2-0, the bug-fix audit against the
           reported openQM defects; the kernel changed, so it needs a
           rebuild).

             SEEK from the end   SEEK file, -3, 2 failed with error 3028
                                 although a negative offset from the end of
                                 a sequential file is the usual way to say
                                 "three bytes before the end".  It now
                                 positions there.  A positive offset with 2
                                 meant the same thing in SD before and still
                                 does, so no existing program changes; an
                                 offset that lands before the start of the
                                 file is still refused.
             LOCATE, right       LOCATE 5 IN s BY 'AR' where s held a single
             justified           item that was text, such as 'abc', said
                                 position 2.  Text sorts after numbers, so
                                 the number goes first: it now says 1, as it
                                 already did when the list held two or more
                                 text items.

           What this does not do.  The other reported defects were tried
           and SD does not have them; the list is in the header of
           gplbld/sandbox-audit-b9.py.  Those that need sockets, a
           distributed file or an encoding with a byte order mark could not
           be tried here.

11 Oct 26  FIVE CONVERSION CODE ADDITIONS AND TRIMW (1.2-0, tenth of the
           openQM compatibility bundles, and the first that changes the
           kernel's conversion and string code; it needs a rebuild).

             MCN- and MC/N-   MCN keeps only the digits; MCN- also keeps a
                              minus sign at the start of the string.  MC/N-
                              deletes the digits and that minus.
             MCS              Capitalises the first letter of each sentence
                              and leaves the rest as it is.
             MCP*  MCP'*'     MCP shows a non-printing character as a dot.
                              A character after MCP, bare or in single or
                              double quotes, is shown instead.
             MT with M        The value is milliseconds since midnight
                              (MTSM, MTHSM).  The options H, S and M may be
                              in any order.
             MD options       The negative value action (+ - < ( C D) and P,
                              Z and T may come in any order.  They had to be
                              in that order before.
             TRIMW(s)         Like TRIM but for tabs, line feeds and the other
                              white space as well as spaces; a run inside the
                              string becomes one space.

           What this does not do.  TRIMWS, the multivalued form, is not
           built; the SYSTEM(), FILEINFO() and other new keys, the other new
           functions and statements, and the $MODE runtime settings still
           need the kernel and are listed in PROJECT_STATUS.

11 Oct 26  DECATALOG, LIST.TERM.TYPES, CT ID.SUP AND NUM.SUP, A SPELLING OF
           $PRIVATE.CATALOG, AND TWO DEBUGGER COMMANDS (1.2-0, ninth of the
           openQM compatibility bundles: the small items of the command
           language, catalogue, editor, debugger and terminal groups).

             DECATALOG name ...    A new command.  Deletes the local
                                   catalogue entry and the object code it
                                   points to; DELETE.CATALOG ... LOCAL
                                   deletes only the entry.  A file name in
                                   front of the program name is accepted and
                                   ignored.
             LIST.TERM.TYPES       A new command.  Lists the terminal types
                                   SD has a definition for (the names under
                                   terminfo in the SD directory) and how
                                   many there are.
             CT ... ID.SUP         Leaves out the "file record" line.
             CT ... NUM.SUP        Leaves out the line numbers.
             CT DICT file item     The default display of a compiled
                                   dictionary item no longer shows the object
                                   code after the 15 lines of the item; HEX
                                   and BINARY still show all of it.
             $PRIVATE.CATALOG      The VOC record may be spelt this way as
                                   well as $PRIVATE.CATALOGUE.
             Debugger BRK          With no line number, lists the
                                   breakpoints that are set.
             Debugger blank line   Repeats the last command (not while
                                   walking an array or the source, where it
                                   steps on).

           The debugger changes are built but not exercised: they need a
           terminal.

           What this does not do.  The paragraph IF with EXISTS, IN and
           NO.CASE, COMO appending or naming another file, the stack editor's
           .C- and the stacks directory, CATALOGUE RELATIVE and the prefix
           option, the MAP options, the editor and SHOW and LIST.ITEM items,
           the WATCH condition, WINDOW, /*, the PTERM and terminfo items,
           DELETE.COMMON with an exception list, COMPARE.FILES, iOSCOPY,
           !MULTISORT and a NEWVOC.MODS file are not built: the release notes
           give no syntax, or the part of SD they change is not here.

11 Oct 26  SET.TRIGGER WORKS AGAIN, AND GAINS ALL; LIST.TRIGGERS; FSTAT'S
           COLUMNS ARE WIDER (1.2-0, eighth of the openQM compatibility
           bundles).

           SET.TRIGGER refused every function name.  Since the call-name
           change of 14 September it lower-cased the name and then checked it
           against upper-case letters, so a file could not be given a trigger
           with the command at all ("Illegal trigger function name").  It
           checks the upper-case form now.  A trigger set from a BASIC
           program or an older release was not affected.

             SET.TRIGGER file name ALL   Every trigger mode (pre- and
                             post-write, pre- and post-delete, read, pre-
                             and post-clear).
             LIST.TRIGGERS {file ...}    A new command: every file of the
                             account that has a trigger function, with the
                             function and its modes; with names, only those
                             files.  Dictionaries are looked at as well.
             FSTAT           The columns of the statistics are twelve wide,
                             not ten, so the counts of a fast disk fit.

           What this does not do.  FILE.STAT, LISTDICT, the LISTF WITH
           clause, CONFIGURE.FILE REPORTING and NO.MAP, a comment on
           CREATE.FILE, its MULTIFILE option, relative PATHNAME, the ANALYSE.FILE
           histogram and SET.FILE with chained Q-pointers are not built: the
           release notes give no syntax for them, or the command does not exist
           here to extend.

11 Oct 26  SAVED SELECT LISTS: KEEP, DELETING, COUNT.SUP, A DEFAULT NAME,
           SORT.LIST AND SELECT.DIR (1.2-0, seventh of the openQM
           compatibility bundles; the sixth is the one line below it).

             SAVE.LIST name KEEP    Leaves the active select list as it was.
                                    SAVE.LIST has always emptied it.
             GET.LIST name DELETING Deletes the saved list once it is loaded.
             COUNT.SUP              On GET.LIST and SAVE.LIST: do not print
                                    the count of records.
             SAVE.LIST, GET.LIST    The name may be left out.  The list is
                                    then called after its number: SAVE.LIST
                                    saves list 0 as "0", and GET.LIST 0
                                    brings it back.  GET.LIST TO 2 and
                                    SAVE.LIST FROM 2 name the list number as
                                    before.
             SORT.LIST name         A new command.  Sorts a saved list into
                                    ascending order, as strings, in place.
                                    FROM file names another list file.
             SELECT.DIR pathname    A new command.  Makes a select list (0, or
                                    TO n) of the names of the files and
                                    subdirectories in an operating system
                                    directory.

           What this does not do.  There is no SELECT.DIR option to keep only
           files or only directories, and SORT.LIST has no descending order or
           numeric order.  SHOW.LIST, COPY.LISTP, the SELECTINDEX command, the
           index commands' new options, LIST.INDEX to a printer and the
           alternative list file on SAVE.LIST and GET.LIST are not built; the
           release notes do not give their syntax.

11 Oct 26  SP.VIEW SHOWS WHERE OUTPUT GOES FOR PRINT UNIT MODES 0, 4, 5 AND 6
           (1.2-0, sixth of the openQM compatibility bundles).

           When SP.VIEW asks which printer to use it shows the print unit's
           destination.  It did that for modes 1 and 3 only; for a unit in
           mode 0 (the screen), 4 (standard error), 5 (the auxiliary port) or
           6 (a printer and a hold file) the line was blank.  It now says what
           the destination is.  The other print system items in the release
           notes (SETPTR INFORM, EJECT, the form feed sequence, SET.QUEUE, the
           printer page size in TERM) change the print system in the kernel
           and are not built.

10 Oct 26  WHO USER, PSTAT USER name, A LOCK HOLDER'S NAME IN PSTAT, AND
           !LISTU (1.2-0, fifth of the openQM compatibility bundles).

             WHO USER        Also shows your login name after the user
                             number and the account.
             PSTAT USER name USER already took a user number.  A name that is
                             not a number now selects the processes whose
                             login name it is; if there are none the command
                             says so ("No process is running for user xx").
             PSTAT           When a process is waiting for a lock, the line
                             that says which user holds it now shows that
                             user's login name after the number.
             !LISTU          A subroutine for programs: CALL !LISTU(data)
                             returns the user table behind LISTU, one field
                             a user in user number order, each with the
                             values user number, pid, IP address, flags,
                             parent user number, login name, terminal, login
                             time.  The flags are the USR$ bits of INT$KEYS.H.

           What this does not do.  LISTU still does not show the account a
           user is in, nor the number of processes of each kind, nor which
           phantoms are grouped with their parent; PSTAT and LIST.READU do not
           say when a process began waiting for a lock; and the hot spot
           monitor has no input/output counters.  Each needs the kernel to
           record something it does not record now.

10 Oct 26  SIX NEW OPTION NAMES FOR COMPATIBILITY WITH OTHER MULTIVALUE SYSTEMS
           (1.2-0, fourth of the openQM compatibility bundles).

           All are off, as every option is until OPTION turns it on, so an
           existing application sees no change.  OPTION with no name lists them.
           (The kernel's table of options grew by six, so this release must be
           rebuilt, not just re-catalogued.)

             QUERY.STRING.COMP   In a query, EQ and NE always compare as
                                 strings.  By default "1", "1.0" and "01" are
                                 all equal to 1, as numbers.
             QUERY.STR.COMP.ALL  The same for every relation (LT, GT ...) on
                                 data from a right aligned dictionary item,
                                 which otherwise compare as numbers when both
                                 sides look like numbers.
             QUERY.SORT.NO.CASE  Sorts in the query processor ignore case.
                                 QUERY.NO.CASE already did this and more
                                 (it makes selection ignore case too).
             BACKSLASH.NOT.QUOTE A backslash on a command line is an ordinary
                                 character, not the start of a quoted string.
             DEFAULT.MV          A dictionary item whose single/multivalue
                                 flag is blank is treated as multivalued.
             CLEAR.SELECT        Select list 0 that was waiting when a command
                                 started is cleared when that command
                                 finishes, unless the command made it.

           What this does not do.  FORCE.RELOAD, QUERY.MERGE.PRINT,
           PAGINATE.ON.HEADING, QUERY.FOOTER.PAUSE, NUMERIC.EXPONENT,
           DIR.SEL.OMIT.HIDDEN, INHERIT.MFILE.PATH and the OPTION D3 and
           MVBASE shortcuts are not built; each needs a change in the kernel
           or the print system, or the release notes do not say which options
           the shortcuts set.

10 Oct 26  THE QUERY PROCESSOR: TWELVE ADDITIONS FROM OTHER MULTIVALUE SYSTEMS
           (1.2-0, third of the openQM compatibility bundles).

           LIST, SORT, SELECT, COUNT, REFORMAT and SEARCH.  None of this
           changes a query that does not use it, except where it says so.

           New keywords (they are VOC records, so an account made before this
           release gets them from the usual account update):
             ABSENT.IGNORE   In LIST, COUNT and REFORMAT, do not print the
                             "'xx' not found" list for ids that are not in
                             the file.
             SORTED.SAMPLE n Like SAMPLE, but the n records are counted after
                             the sort, so BY num SORTED.SAMPLE 2 is the two
                             records with the lowest num.  SAMPLE 2 BY num
                             sorts the first two it meets.
             COL.HDR.SPACE   A blank line under the column headings of a
                             tabular report.
             FOR             SEARCH file FOR "text" "text" gives the strings
                             on the command line instead of asking for them.
             REPORTING       On COUNT and SELECT: a line "n record(s)
                             examined" for every thousand records examined.

           Existing keywords that now do more:
             FROM NONE       Ignores an active select list 0 and goes to the
                             file.  (REQUIRE.SELECT with FROM NONE is
                             refused, because no list is being used.)
             NO.PAGE         May now be given on any query command; it was
                             accepted only on LIST and its relatives.
             CSV 3           Every item is quoted, numbers too.  CSV 1 and
                             CSV 2 are as they were.
             BREAK.ON "'N'"  Starts a new page and numbers it 1 again, which
                             "'P'" does not.
             BREAK.ON "'LU'" The L (suppress the break line) is ignored when
                             the U (underline) is also given.
             SEARCH ... ALL  Also searches the record id.
             SELECT file field  A field name that no clause uses is now
                             SAVING MULTIVALUED field, so the list holds the
                             values of the field and not the record ids.
                             THIS CHANGES an old SELECT that named a field it
                             did not otherwise use; add no field name, or use
                             SAVING, to say what you mean.
             REFORMAT ... @RECORD  Appends the whole of the source record, as
                             it is, to the new record.

           What this does not do.  SEARCH with strings from a file, the
           DISPLAY option and the SCAN command, VERTICALLY FMT, the X
           breakpoint code and @BPV, $VLIST, the longer $QUERY.DEFAULTS and
           the unbroken-line marker in vertical reports are not built: the
           release notes give the name but not the syntax, and the exact
           spelling matters for compatibility.  The query bug fixes in the
           release notes could not be reproduced here.

10 Oct 26  DICTIONARY EXPRESSIONS: SIX THINGS THEY COULD NOT DO BEFORE
           (1.2-0, second of the openQM compatibility bundles).

           These are in I-types and in the compiled forms of A, S and C items.
           Nothing that compiled before changes its answer.

           IF with a part missing.  IF cond THEN a  and  IF cond ELSE b  are
           now accepted, and the part that is not there gives the null string.
           Before, either was a compile error ("ELSE not found where expected
           in conditional expression").  IF with neither part is still refused.

           Field by number.  A name that is F and a number, such as F4, is
           field 4 when the dictionary has no item of that name.  An item that
           is called F4 still wins.

           A format after a value.  F1 '6"0"R'  with no operator between the
           value and the format string is the format operation, as it is in
           BASIC.  It was a compile error.

           Hyphens in names.  If an item is called HY-X, the expression HY-X + 1
           now reads it.  The compiler first tries a name, a minus and another
           name or number as one id, and only if no dictionary item has that id
           is the minus a subtraction.  So F1-F2 is still F1 minus F2 unless
           someone has made an item called F1-F2.

           TRANS() of an A or S item.  TRANS(file, key, name, ...) used to
           accept only a D-type or I-type name.  A or S items are now accepted,
           and so is C-type: one with no correlative gives its field, the
           others give the value they compute against the record read.

           Divide by zero.  Inside a dictionary expression, dividing by zero
           now gives zero with no error and no warning.  It used to stop the
           report with "Divide by zero error in dictionary expression".  This
           is the one change in the kernel (op_arith.c).  A BASIC program is
           not affected: OPTION DIV.ZERO.WARNING still decides what it does.

           What this does not do.  Dictionary link records, multi-level links
           and the CALL correlative's @ACALL.FNO are not in SD (there is
           nothing to extend), and the C-type {name} construct, which the
           openQM release notes list as fixed, already worked here.

10 Oct 26  THE BASIC COMPILER LEARNS SOME THINGS OTHER MULTIVALUE SYSTEMS
           ALREADY DO (1.2-0, first of the openQM compatibility bundles).

           These are all in the compiler and its BASIC command.  None of them
           changes a program that does not use them, and none needs the
           kernel rebuilt.

           $MODE and $BASIC.OPTIONS.  $MODE takes more than one mode name,
           separated by commas, each with an optional leading minus to turn
           it off:  $MODE PICK.SUBSTR, -OPTIONAL.FINAL.END.  The MODE line of
           a $BASIC.OPTIONS record takes the same list, and a leading minus
           there turns a mode off, which was not possible before.
           A new mode, IMPLIED.STOP, makes the code the compiler adds after
           the last END a STOP instead of a RETURN, so a subroutine that runs
           off its end stops the program rather than returning.  Another,
           STRICT.EQUATE, makes the compiler warn when a name given to EQUATE
           or $DEFINE is also a statement or function name (the warning is
           message 11038; it is a warning, the program still compiles).

           New @ tokens.  @LINE is the source line number it stands on, and
           @BUILD.TIME is the time the program was compiled, in seconds since
           1 January 1970 in the compiling session's local time.

           $IFDEF and $IFNDEF accept AND and OR between token names, for
           example $IFDEF SD AND SD.LINUX.  AND binds tighter than OR; there
           is no NOT and no brackets, and $IFNDEF is the opposite of the same
           test with $IFDEF.  The single-name form is unchanged.

           NO.QUERY on cataloguing.  The BASIC command, the $CATALOGUE
           directive and the CATALOGUE line of $BASIC.OPTIONS now take
           NO.QUERY, which stops the compiler asking "Program is also in the
           private catalogue. Remove (y/<n>)?" and the two like it when it
           catalogues a program (the old entries are removed without asking,
           exactly as CATALOGUE NO.QUERY already does).

           What this does not do.  A variable name with two underscores in a
           row (a__b) is still refused, because the tokenizer is in the
           kernel and is not part of this change.  The $MODE names
           PICK.READNEXT, OPEN.FAIL.ZERO.FVAR and WRITE.DELETE.THEN.ELSE are
           not added: they change what the running program does, not just
           what the compiler writes.  These are planned with the kernel
           bundle that follows.

10 Oct 26  THE INSTALLER SAYS WHICH SOURCE IT IS DOWNLOADING.

           installsdcore.sh told you it was downloading "the main branch" even
           when the copy in a release package, which is pinned to a release
           tag, was installing that tag.  The two messages now name what will
           be cloned: "v1.1-3" in L1.1-3's package, "main" in the repository's
           own script.  Nothing about what is installed changed; the L1.1-3
           package installs the L1.1-3 tag, as it always did.

L1.1-3 - 9 Oct 2026
-------------
Changes since L1.1-1.  L1.1-3 pairs with SD Core for Windows W1.1-3.

09 Oct 26  THE UNINSTALL SCRIPT NO LONGER STOPS HALF WAY WHEN YOU KEEP YOUR
           ACCOUNTS.

           deletesdcore.sh, asked to keep your existing accounts, copies the
           account register to /home/sd/accounts and then lists it.  The copy
           is made by root, and on the Ubuntu 26.04 test computer where this
           was found it came out owned by root with mode 750, which you cannot
           read.  The listing, which ran as you, was refused, and the script
           ended there: your accounts were saved, but nothing else had been
           removed and no message said why.  The listing is now run with sudo
           like the copy before it, and the script goes on to remove SD.
           Found by deleting and reinstalling SD on test computers.  Nothing
           about the saved accounts changed, and this has no effect on a
           delete that does not keep the accounts.

08 Oct 26  SD'S OWN COMMANDS ARE WRITTEN IN LOWER CASE IN ITS MESSAGES AND USAGE LINES.

           The 7 October change put the audit trail in lower case.  This takes
           the same rule to the text SD shows you when it names one of its own
           commands or a word that command takes.  A refusal that used to say
           "Rename or delete one of them, then run CONFIGURE.FILE again." now
           says "run configure.file again."  The syntax lines read, for example,
               Syntax: backup.account name [name ...] [to directory]
               Syntax: restore.account archive name [name ...] {no.query}
               Command Syntax: create.account user <user name> {ssh | api | both | none} {no.query}
           It covers create.account, modify.account, modify.password,
           backup.account, restore.account, set.backup.directory, settings.report,
           configure.file with no.case, update.accounts with all, build.index,
           remove.home, logto, break and umask, in 28 messages and the usage text
           of create.account and modify.account.

           What it does NOT do: the words you type are unchanged (SD has always
           accepted either case), and SD's BASIC and query-language error
           messages still write the language's own keywords, such as IF, CASE,
           ON and WITH, in capitals, because those name the language rather than
           a command.  A script that reads this text and matches the old capitals
           has to match the new spelling, or ignore case.

08 Oct 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT WARNS WHEN A ROUTE HAS NOTHING BEHIND IT, AND NO LONGER
           MAKES AN ACCOUNT WHOSE PASSWORD WAS REFUSED.

           Two changes, both matching SD Core for Windows.

           First, a new user account gets the ssh and API routes unless you name
           one.  If the account is given ssh and this computer has no ssh server
           installed, or is given the API and the API listener is off, the verb
           now says so after the route line, for example
               zzjoan is set up to use the API, but this machine provides no API
               listener yet. The account cannot connect until it is turned on
               (remote.api on) - the grant is already in place and takes effect then.
           It is a warning only: the account is still made and the route is
           still recorded, and it works the day the server or listener is turned
           on.  The ssh test is whether the ssh server is installed, not whether
           it is running at that moment.

           Second, if the password for a new user cannot be set and you answer n
           at "Retry", the Linux user that was just made is now removed again and
           nothing is created ("An account must have a password. Nothing was
           created.").  Before this, the account was made around a user who had
           no password.  If the removal itself fails, the verb says the user is
           still on the computer and gives the userdel command that removes it.

           What it does NOT do: it does not touch accounts that already exist, and
           it does not change a user SD did not create (an existing Linux user is
           still refused, or attached, exactly as before).  RESTORE.ACCOUNT, which
           makes a missing account with the same code, now stops the same way when
           a new user's password is refused.  This was compiled on a test copy of
           SD; the two warnings and the removal have not yet been run on an
           installed system.

07 Oct 26  THE AUDIT TRAIL IS WRITTEN IN LOWER CASE.

           Every line SD adds to /usr/local/sdsys/audit now has its event words
           in lower case: create.account, delete.account, restore.account,
           modify.account add, delete, suspend, unsuspended and route, remote.api,
           remote.ssh, logto and login, and the event names elevation granted,
           elevation refused, account refused, api refused, login refused and
           logto refused.  A line reads, for example,
               login account=sdsys
               logto refused account=sdsys reason=sdsys is entered by its own session only
               modify.account route api account=zzrel1
           This is the rule that commands are lower case wherever SD writes them,
           which SD Core for Windows set first and the lower-case work of 7 October
           finished here; the audit trail had kept upper case from the start.
           What follows an equals sign is unchanged: the account name as it is
           registered, the text of a reason=, and what a caller typed in command=.

           What it does NOT do: it does not rewrite the lines already in your
           audit file, so one file can hold both spellings until it is rotated.
           Anything of yours that reads the trail by matching the old upper-case
           words (ELEVATION REFUSED, API REFUSED, LOGIN ...) has to match the new
           ones, or match without regard to case.  The messages SD shows on
           screen are not changed by this.

           This was measured on a test copy of SD: sessions that sign in and are
           refused SDSYS wrote "login account=sdsys" and "logto refused account=sdsys
           reason=..." where the previous build wrote "LOGIN" and "LOGTO REFUSED".
           A new check reads every audit statement in the programs (26) and fails
           on an upper-case event word, and six deliberate breakages of the
           programs were each caught.  The witness rows that wait for these lines
           were changed and have not been run.

07 Oct 26  UPDATE.ACCOUNTS ALL NO LONGER TELLS YOU TO START SD WITH SUDO.

           When UPDATE.ACCOUNTS ALL is typed anywhere but in an administrator's
           session as SDSYS, SD refuses and says so.  The refusal ended with
           "Start SD with sudo - that lands you in SDSYS - and run it again".
           That advice is from before 18 September: running SD as root is now
           refused outright ("root is not SD's administrator"), so following it
           led to a second refusal.  It now says what the other refusals say:
           log in as sdsys at the machine's keyboard (or a desktop-sharing view
           of it), run sd from that session, and run it again.

           What it does NOT do: the refusal itself is unchanged, and the
           installer, which runs the update with its own privileges, is
           unaffected.  No other message SD prints advises starting SD with
           sudo (the editor's hint to install a missing editor package with
           sudo is a different matter).

07 Oct 26  A PASSWORD THAT IS TAKEN IS ANSWERED "Password accepted." EVERYWHERE.

           Setting a password with MODIFY.PASSWORD used to answer in two ways:
           "Password accepted" (no full stop) when the installer asked for it,
           and "Password set for account <name>" at an ordinary prompt.  It now
           answers "Password accepted." both times, which is the line SD Core
           for Windows has printed since 21 September, so a script or a person
           waiting for the success text sees the same words on both.  The
           account is no longer repeated on success (you typed it on the
           command line); a failure still names it.

           What it does NOT do: the failure message, the prompts and the blank
           line the installer shows before the answer are unchanged, and the
           prompts still differ in wording between the two ports (Repeat new
           password: here, Type it again: there).

           This was measured on a test copy of SD: the old build printed
           "Password set for account sdsys" and the new one "Password
           accepted." at an ordinary prompt and, after the blank line, with the
           installer's quiet option.  The verifier rows that waited for the old
           words were changed to wait for the new ones; they have not been run.

07 Oct 26  BACKSPACE ERASES AT AN INPUT PROMPT, WHICHEVER KEY YOUR TERMINAL
           SENDS FOR IT.

           A terminal sends either Ctrl-H or Delete for the Backspace key, and
           which one it sends does not always match what its terminal type says
           (a vt100 type expects Ctrl-H, but most terminal programs on Linux
           send Delete).  The SD command line coped with both from 14
           September.  The INPUT statement did not: with the key it was not
           expecting, Backspace did not erase, it ADDED a character to what you
           had typed.  At a password prompt (MODIFY.PASSWORD, and every program
           that asks for a hidden entry) a typing mistake could therefore not be
           corrected, and trying to correct it made the entry longer, with a
           star on the screen for the extra character.  Both keys erase now, as
           well as whatever key the terminal type names, and the erase on the
           screen is back, blank, back.  This is the change SD Core for Windows
           made on 22 August.

           What it does NOT do: nothing else about typing at a prompt changes,
           and a terminal type that names some third key for Backspace still
           works, because its key stays in the set.  The installer's Linux
           password for sdsys is read by the shell, not by SD, and was never
           affected; its two SD password prompts are SD's own INPUT HIDDEN and
           were.

           This was measured on a test copy of SD, not yet on an install.  A
           program asks INPUT for one entry and INPUT HIDDEN for another and
           prints what it got; each of three terminal types (vt100, linux,
           xterm) was sent "ab", Backspace, "c" with each of the two keys, and
           the answer had to be "ac".  Before the change vt100 with Delete,
           linux with Ctrl-H and xterm with Ctrl-H gave "ab", the key and "c"
           (12 of 40 rows failed); after it all 40 passed, including the
           control that sends no Backspace at all and the check of what the
           screen was told.

07 Oct 26  THE INSTALLER NOW LEAVES SD RUNNING.

           The last thing the installer does is start SD.  Until now it ended
           with SD stopped: it starts SD for each password step and stops it
           again, and the stop did not go through the service manager, so the
           service still read "active" while no SD was running.  "sd" then
           answered "SD has not been started", and "systemctl start sd" did
           nothing, until the computer was rebooted or the service was
           restarted by hand.  The installer now runs "systemctl restart
           sd.service" after the last password step, and its closing text says
           "SD is running now." when that worked.  If the restart fails the
           install still finishes, says "SD did not start" and prints the
           command that tries again.

           What it does NOT do: the advice to reboot stays, because the
           groups you were added to take effect at your next login, and a
           reboot is still the sure way to have them.  Nothing about how SD
           starts at boot changed.

           This was measured on the installer's own text, not on an install:
           the restart block run the way the installer runs it, with a failing
           restart as well as a working one, and its place in the script
           (after the last password step, before the closing text, and no
           stop after it).  11 of 11 rows passed, and six deliberate
           breakages of the installer (the restart turned back into a start,
           removed, made fatal, a stop put after it, the "running" line shown
           unconditionally, the retry command dropped) were each caught.  On
           the owner's computer the same restart typed by hand started SD from
           exactly the stopped state the installer left.  Not measured: the
           installer ending with SD running, which needs the next reinstall.

07 Oct 26  SDSYS'S OWN VOC HAS THE SOUNDEX KEYWORD, AND THE BATCH-JOB LIST HAS
           A DICTIONARY.

           The keyword "~" (find names that sound like ...) is in the VOC of
           every ordinary account again (restored on 6 October).  SDSYS's own VOC is built
           from a second set of records, VOC_TEMPLATE, and that set had no
           record for it, so a query in SDSYS could not use it.  The record
           (%T, the same bytes as the ordinary one) is there now.  SD Core for
           Windows found the same omission in its own tree the same day and
           made the same change.

           BATCH.JOBS, the list of commands each account may run from the
           command line, is a directory file the installer makes, and it had
           no dictionary, so a LIST of it could name no field.  It now has
           one (batch.jobs.dic: the account as the id, and "command", which
           holds one command per line), made by the first bootstrap pass and
           filled by the same step that fills the other dictionaries.  This
           is how SD Core for Windows has had it since 22 August, from the
           same records.  What it does not do: nothing adds a VOC entry for
           BATCH.JOBS, so you still reach it the way you did, by its path.

           Also: a message of several lines is shown a line at a time.  The
           refusal you get when you try to enter SDSYS from another account
           ("The SDSYS account is entered only by running SD as the sdsys OS
           user ... from a local session") was printed as one string, and
           the break between its two lines came out as a stray replacement
           character; the same was true of the three refusals of a command
           run from the command line (a command not on the account's list,
           one with arguments, one that is not a paragraph or sentence).
           This was measured for the first: LOGTO SDSYS printed the two lines
           run together with the stray character before, and on two lines
           after.  The other three were compiled, not run.

           This was measured on a test copy of SD: a new SDSYS VOC holds "~"
           (type K, "Keyword to test soundex code"), and the dictionary
           directory exists with its three records after the bootstrap, which
           passed all four steps.  Not run on an install.

07 Oct 26  THE PROGRAM NAMES IN THE SHIPPED VOCS ARE LOWER CASE.

           Each verb in a VOC names the program that runs it in the third
           line of its record: $ALIAS, $CATALOG, $QPROC and so on.  They are
           now $alias, $catalog, $qproc, in every record of both shipped VOCs
           (the one every account gets and the one SDSYS uses), 189 records in
           all, which is how SD Core for Windows has them.  Nothing you type
           changes, and the verbs run as before, because a program name was
           already looked up in lower case.  What you see is LIST.ITEM or CT
           on a VOC record, or a COPY of one, showing the lower-case name; an
           account gets it when UPDATE.ACCOUNTS brings its VOC forward.
           Twenty-seven programs that SD ships (the Python call set, the
           password-hash helpers, the two that switch the process's user
           and OPTION) named their include files in capitals, as KEYS.H;
           they say keys.h now, with no change to what they do.  The step goes a little past the port: eight of these
           records (COUNT and NLS in each VOC, and four verbs of SDSYS's own:
           SETTINGS.REPORT, BACKUP.ACCOUNT, RESTORE.ACCOUNT and
           SET.BACKUP.DIRECTORY) are still in capitals in the Windows copy,
           and are lower case here.

           This was measured on a test copy of SD built from the tree: it
           compiled 218 programs with no errors and the four earlier checks
           of this series (26, 25, 28 and 42 rows) passed again.  A check of
           the shipped files finds no VOC record whose program name has a
           capital in it.

07 Oct 26  AN UPGRADE MAKES YOUR OLD FILES CASE INSENSITIVE, AND YOUR PRIVATE
           CATALOGUE LOWER CASE.

           When you reinstall and keep your accounts, the installer now walks
           every kept account after it has brought the VOCs forward.  Each
           of the account's files (the data and dictionary parts its VOC names,
           then the VOC itself, last) is first read, looking for two ids that
           differ only by case.  A file with none is rebuilt case insensitive
           and keeps every record.  A file that has a pair is left exactly as
           it was and named, with the pair, so you can rename or delete one id
           and convert that file with CONFIGURE.FILE NO.CASE; a file with
           indexes is named and left for CONFIGURE.FILE too.  The report is
           printed and kept in /var/tmp/sdcore-install-nocase.log.  A pair is
           a warning, not a failure, and the install goes on.  This is the
           second half of the step SD Core for Windows took on 14 September
           (its change 407fa2e4).

           Two things are Linux's own.  A rebuilt file is a new file, so each
           account is converted with the installer acting as the account's
           owner (your Linux user for a user account, sdsys for a group
           account); an account that cannot be acted as is skipped and named.
           The files come out group writable (mode 664).  And each account's
           private catalogue is made lower case: the step that made every
           program name lower case left an entry catalogued before it, as
           cat/ZZSUB, where a call no longer finds it ("Unable to load"),
           because a name that differs only by case is a different file on
           Linux.  cat/ZZSUB is renamed cat/zzsub unless cat/zzsub exists
           already, in which case both are named and left for you.

           What it does NOT do: SDSYS's own files are not converted, because
           the install makes them new; an account that is neither a user nor
           a group account is named and skipped; a file another session has
           open is left and named; a file that an account's VOC names but that
           lies outside that account's own directory (another user's, say), or
           under SDSYS, is left and named in a list at the end, because
           converting it as this user would change who owns it (the real path
           is used, so a link into SDSYS does not get past); it does not touch
           a directory file; and it runs only during an install, because it
           needs the installer's root session (the program refuses to switch
           owner for anyone else).

           The first run of this on a real install (7 October) stopped at the
           first account with "Error 3001 opening file", and the install
           carried on without converting anything, losing nothing.  An
           account's VOC names some of SDSYS's own files ($map, dict.dic,
           voc.dic and the like), and the walk, already acting as the
           account's owner, tried to open them to look inside; the owner
           may not.  It now decides from a file's real path, before opening
           anything, that a file under SDSYS or outside the account is not
           its to touch, and counts and names it.  A file of the account's
           own that cannot be opened, or an account whose VOC cannot, is
           reported with its error number ("Cannot open ... It was left as it
           is."), counted among the files that could not be read or rebuilt,
           and the walk goes on to the next one.  It does not try to make
           such a file readable.

           The next run on a real install, the same morning, ran to the end:
           the account's VOC and its $hold.dic were rebuilt, owned by the
           account's user and group, mode 664.  Its report was untidy, and
           that is fixed too: two VOC records may name one file, which was
           counted and listed twice (the account's own VOC is named by its
           own record and was then converted again as the last step, hence
           "Converted 2 of 3"; voc.dic was named three times), and directory files (SDSYS's syscom and newvoc, the
           account's own directory) were listed as files to convert with
           CONFIGURE.FILE, which cannot convert a directory.  A file is now
           judged once per account, and a path that is a directory file is
           passed over in silence as before.

           This was measured on a test copy of SD, not yet on an install.  A
           fixture account with five case-sensitive files (a VOC, a data file
           with ids Alpha, beta and GAMMA, a file with jack and JACK, and two
           dictionaries) and a private catalogue with ZZSUB1, Mixed, a lower
           case entry and a pair ZZPAIR and zzpair: a read-only run (CHECK)
           changed nothing and named the pair and the two renames; the real
           run converted four of the five files, kept all three records of
           the data file, read ALPHA back as "one", left the file with jack
           and JACK holding both and case sensitive, renamed ZZSUB1 and
           Mixed with their bytes intact and kept both of ZZPAIR and zzpair,
           left the new files with the owner and group of the ones they
           replaced, made them mode 664 and did not touch SDSYS; a second run
           converted none.  Three accounts it could not convert were each
           named with the reason.  Three more files the VOC named, one in
           another directory, one under SDSYS and one under SDSYS that the
           account could not open at all, were left whole and named; an
           account's own file and another account's VOC that could not be
           opened were each reported and the walk went on, and a VOC record
           naming a file already named, a directory under SDSYS and the
           account's own directory were each left out of the counts and the
           list.  57 of 57 rows passed (46 before the first install run).  Putting the old order
           back, open first and judge afterwards, made the walk stop with
           the owner's own "Error 3001 opening file"; taking out the report
           and carry on for a file, and again for a VOC, failed 21 and 29
           rows.  Deleting the pair test for a trial let it rebuild the pair
           file and lose JACK, and the rows caught that; taking out the catalogue step failed the
           four catalogue rows and nothing else; taking out the test for the
           other directory and SDSYS let both files be converted and failed
           six rows.  Not measured: an install, where the owner is another
           user than the one running the test.

07 Oct 26  NO TWO RECORD IDS IN A FILE MAY DIFFER ONLY BY CASE.

           Every file SD creates now treats record ids without regard to
           case, so a file can hold "jack" or "JACK" but never both: writing
           one when the other is there updates the same record.  The system
           files (the VOC and the dictionaries) were already built that way;
           it now holds for every file, including your own.  CREATE.FILE and
           CONFIGURE.FILE no longer accept the CASE keyword, and say why
           (message 10176).  CONFIGURE.FILE used to carry a file's case
           setting forward unchanged; now a rebuild of an older case-sensitive
           file makes it case insensitive, and if the file holds two ids that
           differ only by case it refuses (message 10177), names both, and
           leaves the file exactly as it was, because a rebuild would have
           kept only one of them without a word.  This is the step SD Core for
           Windows took on 14 September (its change 72d4a533).

           The same step fixed two programs that would have deleted what
           they had just written once the VOC ignores case: CATALOG, run a
           second time on a name it had already catalogued, wrote the entry
           and then deleted it as an "old capital-letter entry", so no entry
           was left and 3029 had said "added" (the Windows port measured
           this on its own copy; here the guard went in with the change);
           and the program that writes the
           shipped dictionaries at installation renamed an old upper-case id by
           writing the new one and deleting the old one, which on a file that
           ignores case is the same record.  CATALOG now does that rename only
           on a VOC that is still case sensitive, and the dictionary writer
           deletes the old id first on a file that is not.

           What it does NOT do: it does not convert a file that already
           exists.  An upgrade keeps your files as they were, case sensitive,
           and they stay that way until the conversion that follows this
           change (or CONFIGURE.FILE on the file).  Nor does it touch a
           directory file: on Linux a name that differs only by case is a
           different file in the operating system, which Windows hides and
           Linux does not, so records of bp, cat and every other directory
           file are still looked up exactly by the kernel; the programs that
           take a name from you (BASIC, CATALOG and the like) still try it as
           typed, then lower case, then upper case, as before.  That
           difference is the operating system's, not a choice.

           This was measured on test copies of SD built before and after.  A
           file made with CREATE.FILE, a record written as jack and then as
           JACK: before, two records; after, one, stored jack.  The flag was
           read from the file itself, not inferred from the count, on the data
           part, the dictionary part and the account's VOC: all three case
           insensitive after, none before.  CREATE.FILE ... CASE was refused
           with message 10176, printed a line at a time, and made no file.
           A case-sensitive file built in internal mode with jack and JACK in
           it was refused by CONFIGURE.FILE with message 10177 naming both and
           still held both records and the case-sensitive flag afterwards; the
           same file with only jack converted, kept its record, and read case
           insensitive.  A create.file statement in an ordinary program that
           asked for a case-sensitive file still got a case-insensitive one;
           when the internal-mode test in the kernel was taken out for a trial
           that one row failed and the others passed.  26 of 26 rows passed.

           Also, in passing: message 10176 and 10177 are several lines, and
           showing them with one DISPLAY put raw field marks on the screen;
           they are shown a line at a time, as the root-entry refusals are.

07 Oct 26  A PROGRAM, SUBROUTINE AND CATALOGUE NAME IS LOWER CASE EVERYWHERE
           SD KEEPS IT.

           Until now SD wrote the name of a catalogued program in capitals:
           a CATALOG made the VOC entry ZZSUB and the file cat/ZZSUB, the
           global catalogue held $CPROC and !USERNAME, the program library
           held the names of the built-in subroutines in capitals, and the
           messages said "ZZSUB added to private catalogue".  Every one of
           them is now lower case - zzsub, $cproc, !username - and the
           messages print the name that way.  You can still type a name in
           any case: CALL, CATALOG and DELETE.CATALOG turn what you typed to
           lower case before they look.  This is the step of making every
           name in SD lower case that SD Core for Windows took on 14 September
           (its change 4d8136b2); the two products now agree on it.

           It changes the SD program and the SD kernel together, so an
           install replaces both: a new kernel over an old catalogue, or the
           reverse, would look for $cproc in the wrong case and find nothing.
           The installer replaces both.

           CATALOG over an entry that was catalogued before this step does
           the right thing: it finds the old capital-letter entry, writes the
           new lower-case one and removes the old one, so you are left with
           one entry and not two.  DELETE.CATALOG finds an old capital-letter
           local entry too.  Two things beside that: CPROC decides which
           commands need administrator rights (CREATE.ACCOUNT, DELETE.ACCOUNT,
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT, MODIFY.PASSWORD, RESTORE.ACCOUNT) by looking their
           catalogue name up in a list written in capitals, and a lower-case
           catalogue name would have matched nothing and run the command
           without the rights it needs, silently; it now compares in capitals
           whatever case the VOC holds.  A file's trigger name, stored inside
           the file by an earlier version in capitals, is turned to lower case
           when the file is opened.

           What it does NOT do: it does not rename anything you catalogued
           before.  On Linux a name that differs only in case is a different
           file, so a program of your own catalogued as ZZSUB, with its object
           in cat/ZZSUB, is no longer found after this step ("Unable to load
           'zzsub'", measured on a test copy of SD) until you run CATALOG on
           it again.  The dictionaries of your own files and the other
           records you own are unchanged.  SD's own catalogue is rebuilt by
           the install, so nothing of SD's needs doing.

           This was measured on a test copy of SD built from this tree and on
           one built from the tree before it.  A script reads names back
           case-sensitively: the global catalogue held no capital letter in
           any of its 158 entries (before: all 158), the program library's 55
           names held none (before: all 55), CATALOG said "zzcc3ap added to private
           catalogue" and cat/ held exactly zzcc3ap (before: ZZCC3AP), the
           runtime named a missing subroutine as '!zznosuchsub3a', a local
           entry stored as zzcl3a was found by a call typed ZZCL3A, and
           CATALOG and DELETE.CATALOG over an entry stored as ZZCL3A left and
           removed exactly one.  29 of 29 rows passed; the same script against
           the earlier build failed 15.  Two rows of it were made to fail on
           purpose, by taking the old-entry handling out of DELETE.CATALOG and
           CATALOG in turn, and each failed exactly where it should.

06 Oct 26  THE DICTIONARIES SD SHIPS NOW NAME THEIR ITEMS IN LOWER CASE, AND
           "LIST ACCOUNTS" SAYS SUSPENSION, NOT TIER.

           The step before this one made every dictionary read find an item
           whether it was stored in lower or upper case.  This step uses it:
           the items SD itself supplies for its own files (the dictionaries of
           VOC, ACCOUNTS, $MAP, $HOLD, DICT and BATCH.JOBS) are now stored in
           lower case, so type instead of TYPE, f1 instead of F1, @id instead
           of @ID, data.name instead of DATA.NAME, and the field names inside
           their I-types are lower case too.  A file made with CREATE.FILE, and
           a new account's VOC dictionary, get their default item as @id, and
           message 6129 says so.  Queries you already write in capitals keep
           working: the exact name is tried first, then the other case.

           An upgrade does not leave the old names behind.  The program that
           writes these dictionaries during installation used to add each item
           and delete nothing, so an upgraded VOC dictionary would have held
           TYPE and type side by side.  It now deletes an older item that
           differs from the new one only in case, after writing the new one,
           and prints "REPLACED OLD ID: voc.dic TYPE BY type" for each.  An
           item of your own that matches no shipped name is left alone.

           The ACCOUNTS dictionary still described a "Tier" field and a "Was"
           field from the account tiers that were removed on 18 September, so
           LIST ACCOUNTS showed a column headed "Tier" that actually held the
           word SUSPENDED or nothing.  It now has one field, "Suspension".
           An upgraded system keeps its old TIER and PRIOR.TIER items
           (nothing deletes an item SD no longer ships); they are harmless.

           This was measured on test copies of SD.  A fresh one listed
           DICT VOC as @id, name, f1, data.name, type and so on, none in
           capitals.  To rehearse an upgrade, TYPE and @ID were planted beside
           type and @id, with one item of our own; the installer program was
           run again; the dictionary went from 16 items to 19 to 17, two
           REPLACED lines were printed, and our own item was still there.

           What it does not do: this is still only the dictionaries.  File
           names, program and call names and the case of ids in new files are
           the following steps, and a data file made with CREATE.FILE is
           still case sensitive.  An existing system is converted only for
           the shipped dictionaries named above, not for dictionaries of your
           own files.

06 Oct 26  A DICTIONARY ITEM IS FOUND WHETHER IT WAS STORED IN LOWER OR UPPER
           CASE (FIRST STEP OF MAKING EVERY NAME LOWER CASE).

           A dictionary item named in a query, CD, a SHOW, an I-type or a
           CREATE.INDEX was looked up exactly as typed, so a field stored as
           f1 was not found when you typed F1, and an I-type whose expression
           named F1 and F3 could not be compiled against a dictionary that
           stored f1 and f3.  The lookup now tries the name as typed, then in
           lower case, then in upper case, in the programs that read
           dictionaries (the query processor, ACOMP, BCOMP, CD, CNAME,
           GENERATE, ICOMP, LISTI, MKINDX, SHOW and the counter that
           numbers hold-file records).  This is the first of the steps that
           SD Core for Windows took to make every name in SD lower case; it
           changes nothing that already works, because the exact name is tried
           first, and it has to come before any dictionary is renamed.

           This was measured on test copies of SD built before and after.
           With a dictionary storing f1 in lower case, LIST file F1 used a
           15-character default heading before ("F1............") and the
           item's own heading after ("F1..."), and CD of an I-type naming F1
           and F3 failed before and compiled and evaluated after.  Fifteen
           ordinary queries (COUNT, LIST, SORT, SELECT, LIST DICT, with and
           without conditions) printed identical output before and after.

           What it does not do: nothing is renamed yet.  The dictionaries SD
           ships still hold their names in upper case, new files are still
           case sensitive, program and call names are still stored in upper
           case, and the conversion tool for existing installations is not
           here.  SHOW, MKINDX, LISTI, GENERATE and the hold-file counter were
           compiled, not run.

06 Oct 26  THE SOUNDEX TEST "~" WORKS AGAIN IN EVERY ACCOUNT'S VOC.

           A query such as LIST CUSTOMERS WITH NAME ~ "smith" (find names that
           sound like smith) needs the keyword "~" in the account's VOC.  On
           SD Core for Linux that keyword was missing from every account
           created or updated since the file names were put into lower case on
           13 September.  Its source record is a file in sdsys/newvoc whose
           name has to be %T, in capitals: the loader turns a leading %T into
           "~" and does not recognise %t.  The record had been renamed to %t
           by mistake, from a comment that said lower case (that comment, in
           sd.h and in verify-nocase.py, was wrong and is corrected).  The
           mistake was reported by the SD Core for Windows agent, who could
           see %T in its own tree.

           This was measured on a test copy of SD: the VOC of an ordinary
           account held none of "~", "%t" or "%T" before, and holds "~"
           (type K, "Keyword to test soundex code") after.  The other
           punctuation keywords (=, <>, >, <, %) were present both times.

           What it does not do: an account that already exists does not gain
           the keyword by itself.  UPDATE.ACCOUNTS adds it, and the installer
           runs UPDATE.ACCOUNTS ALL on an upgrade of the full product.

06 Oct 26  SUSPENDING AN ACCOUNT NOW LOCKS ITS REGISTER RECORD AND REPORTS A
           FAILED WRITE.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT name SUSPENDED and UNSUSPENDED wrote back the account
           record that the command had read when it started.  If another
           administrator changed the same account in between, that change was
           lost, and a write that failed was not noticed.  The record is now
           read again under a lock just before the flag is written, and a
           failed write says "Unable to change the state of name; its state in
           the register is unchanged".  This is how SD Core for Windows has
           done it.  Run on a test copy of SD: suspend, a second suspend
           ("already SUSPENDED; nothing changed"), unsuspend and a second
           unsuspend behave as before, and the audit lines are unchanged.

           What it does not do: the failed-write message was not provoked (no
           way to make that write fail was found), and suspending SDSYS is
           still refused as an invalid account name.

06 Oct 26  THE AUDIT TRAIL NOW SAYS WHAT A SINGLE-COMMAND SIGN-ON RAN, AND
           RECORDS TWO REFUSALS AT THE API DOOR.

           A sign-on that is a single command line (sd 'COUNT VOC', as a
           scheduled job runs) used to write the same line as an interactive
           one: "LOGIN account=sdsys".  It now adds the command:
           "LOGIN account=sdsys command=COUNT VOC".  An interactive sign-on is
           unchanged.  This was run on a test copy of SD and both lines were
           read from its audit file.

           When an API client signs in correctly but is then refused entry to
           the account it asked for, the client sees only "User not allowed in
           requested account" (the text is the same for every reason, so a
           probe learns nothing).  The audit trail now records why, in the
           words SD Core for Windows uses: "ACCOUNT REFUSED branch=2 suspended
           account=name" when the account is suspended, and "ACCOUNT REFUSED
           branch=4 not.in.group user=name group=group account=name" when the
           user is not in the account's group.

           What it does not do: the API lines were compiled but not run, as
           the test copy has no API client; the client's message is unchanged;
           and a refusal because the account directory cannot be entered is
           still not recorded.

06 Oct 26  THE KERNEL KEY THAT ASKS "WHOSE LOGIN IS THIS" MOVED FROM 65 TO 91.

           SD's internal programs ask the kernel a few questions by number.
           One of them, K$LOGIN.UID ("does this session's login belong to
           user x?", used when SDSYS is entered), was number 65 here, and
           number 65 on SD Core for Windows meant a different question ("did
           this process start elevated?").  A program written for both ports
           would have asked the wrong question on one of them without any
           error.  It is now 91, in the block set aside for this port (90-99);
           SD Core for Windows keeps 58-89.  The audit tool found the clash,
           and now finds none.

           This was run on a test copy of SD: a small internal program
           printed K$LOGIN.UID as 65 before and 91 after, and the kernel
           answered the question correctly at the new number.

           What it does not do: nothing changes for anyone who only types
           SD commands.  The kernel program and GPL.BP must be installed
           together, which the installer already does; a BASIC program of
           your own that is marked $internal and uses the number 65 directly
           (instead of K$LOGIN.UID) must change it.

06 Oct 26  THE EMBEDDED-PYTHON ERROR MESSAGES ARE SPELT CORRECTLY.

           The text that goes with the Python error numbers (-12001 to
           -12034, and two of the -104xx user-switch errors) had typing
           mistakes: "interperter not initiialized", "concatinate", "proess",
           "could get __main__ dictionary" for "could not get".  They are now
           worded as SD Core for Windows words them, for example "interpreter
           not initialised", "failed to concatenate python strings" and
           "could not get __main__ dictionary".  No error number or value
           changed, so a program that tests STATUS() against SYSCOM ERR.H is
           unaffected; only the words shown by ERRTEXT change.

           The text lives in gplsrc/err.h and the two header files that carry
           it, SYSCOM ERR.H and the ERRTEXT include, are regenerated from it
           by ERRGEN.  Run on a test copy of SD, ERRGEN on the old source
           reproduced the old files exactly, and on the new source changed
           only these words.

           What it does not do: it does not add the Windows-only server codes
           (3042, 4100, 4101), and it does not reword any message outside the
           Python and user-switch groups.

06 Oct 26  CONFIG NO LONGER LISTS CREATUSR.

           CREATUSR was an sd.conf setting that let CREATE.ACCOUNT create a
           Linux user.  Since the account tiers were removed nothing reads it:
           CREATE.ACCOUNT always makes the Linux user a USER account needs.
           CONFIG still printed "CREATUSR  1", which suggested that changing
           it would do something.  The line is gone from CONFIG's output, as
           it is on SD Core for Windows, which dropped it on 16 August.  This
           was compared on a test copy of SD: the output differs by that one
           line and nothing else.

           What it does not do: an sd.conf that still has a CREATUSR line is
           still accepted, and the CONFIG() function still answers for it, so
           no existing installation is affected.  STARTUP stays in the list
           (it runs here, which it could not on Windows), and there is no
           APIPORT to list here: the API port is fixed at 4247 and REMOTE.API
           with no word reports whether it is on, local or off.

06 Oct 26  PY_LISTCREATE IS THE NAME OF THE PYTHON LIST CONSTRUCTOR ON BOTH
           PORTS, AND PY_LISTCRTE STILL WORKS.

           The embedded-Python function that makes an empty Python list was
           added here on 22 September as PY_LISTCRTE.  SD Core for Windows had
           added the same function a week earlier as PY_LISTCREATE, so a BASIC
           program that called one name failed on the other port with
           "Unable to load '!PY_LISTCREATE' object code".  Both names now work
           on both ports, and PY_LISTCREATE is the one the documentation will
           use.  Both are also declared in the sdpyfunc.h header, which until
           now did not declare PY_LISTCRTE at all, so including the header gave
           access to every other Python function but not this one.

           This was run on a test copy of SD: PY_LISTCREATE made a list,
           appended to it and read it back, a second call with the same name
           returned -12038, an empty name returned 1, and PY_LISTCRTE behaved
           the same.  On the build before the change, the new name failed as
           above.

           What it does not do: it does not rename or remove anything, and the
           User and Administrator guides still name only PY_LISTCRTE until the
           documentation is revised.

06 Oct 26  SIGNING IN NO LONGER ASKS "UPDATE VOC TO NEW RELEASE" WHEN AN
           ACCOUNT'S VOC IS FROM AN OLDER RELEASE.

           When an account's VOC had been made by an older release of SD, the
           first sign-in after the upgrade stopped and asked "Update VOC to
           new release (y/<n>)?".  A session with no one at the keyboard (a
           script driving SD, an installer step) could not answer it.  This
           was measured on a test copy of SD: a VOC marked as release L0.0-1
           produced the question at sign-in.  SD now signs in without asking.
           Every upgrade already runs UPDATE.ACCOUNTS ALL, which refreshes
           every account's VOC, so nothing is left stale by an upgrade; an
           account whose VOC is still old can be refreshed with
           UPDATE.ACCOUNTS.  SD Core for Windows made the same change on 16
           September, at the owner's wish to proceed silently.

           What it does not do: it does not refresh the VOC by itself at
           sign-in, and it does not warn that the VOC is old.

06 Oct 26  CREATING, DELETING AND RESTORING AN ACCOUNT NOW LEAVE A RECORD IN
           THE AUDIT TRAIL.

           The audit trail (the "audit" file in the SDSYS directory) already
           recorded logins, refused logins, LOGTO, MODIFY.ACCOUNT changes and
           REMOTE.API and REMOTE.SSH.  It did not record the three verbs that
           make or remove an account, which are the ones an administrator
           most often needs to answer "who did that, and when".  Each now
           writes one line, in the same shape as the others:

               CREATE.ACCOUNT account=name type=USER
               DELETE.ACCOUNT account=name
               RESTORE.ACCOUNT account=name archive=SD-host-name-stamp.zip

           The line is written only when the verb finished: a creation that
           was refused, a deletion that was abandoned at the question, and an
           account that was not restored leave nothing.  RESTORE.ACCOUNT
           writes one line per account restored, naming the archive by its
           file name.  SD Core for Windows is taking the same three strings.

           This was measured on a test copy of SD: a created and then deleted
           account produced the first two lines.  The RESTORE.ACCOUNT line is
           built and compiled but was not run, because the test copy cannot
           make a real backup of an account that has a Linux group.

           What it does not do: it does not record a refused attempt, and it
           does not record who did it beyond the user name and process that
           the audit trail already stamps on every line.

06 Oct 26  PRESSING ENTER AT THE "OVERWRITE VOC RECORD" PROMPT NOW MEANS NO,
           AND THREE MESSAGES SAY WHAT THEY SHOULD.

           The .S command saves a command from the stack as a VOC record.  If
           the name is already a VOC record, SD asks "Overwrite VOC record
           'name'?".  Pressing Enter, or reaching the end of a script that was
           feeding SD, did not answer it: SD asked again, for ever, and the
           session had to be killed.  This was measured on a test copy of SD:
           the session never returned.  Enter now means no, the record is left
           alone, and the prompt shows "(y/<n>)" like every other yes-or-no
           question.  The same loop was closed at the other prompts earlier;
           this one had been missed.  Two more were found the same way and
           fixed alike: DELETE file ALL ("Delete all records in file?") and
           CLEAR.FILE's "Use file 'name'?".  Measured on a test copy of SD for
           DELETE ... ALL: the session never returned, and the lines typed
           after the prompt were swallowed as its answers.  Now Enter means
           no and the records are kept.

           Three messages were brought into line with SD Core for Windows.  F1
           at an empty prompt names the LISTV command, not "list voc".  The
           note that UPDATE.ACCOUNTS ALL is the form an upgrade runs now says
           so.  When DELETE.ACCOUNT cannot remove an account's directory, the
           message now says everything else was done and gives the usual
           reasons: a file system mounted inside it (SD does not cross file
           systems), or a file SD was not allowed to remove.

           What it does not do: CLEAR.FILE's prompt only appears when the file
           name was typed in a different case from the file's own, which is rare
           here, so that one was changed the same way but not run.  The printer
           prompt in SPOOL's viewer already has a default answer.  SD Core for
           Windows has the same two loops and has not changed them yet.

06 Oct 26  COPY ... DELETING NO LONGER DELETES A RECORD THAT WAS COPIED ONTO
           ITSELF.

           COPY FROM file TO file id,id OVERWRITING DELETING copies a record
           and then deletes the original.  When the source and the target
           were the same record (the same file and the same id, for example
           COPY FROM customers TO customers jack,jack OVERWRITING DELETING),
           the copy landed on the original and the delete then removed the
           only copy.  The command said "1 record(s) copied and deleted" and
           the record was gone.  This was measured on a test copy of SD built
           from this release: after the command, COUNT of the file said 0.

           COPY now leaves a record alone when it was copied onto itself and
           says so: "Record 'jack' was copied onto itself, so it was not
           deleted."  The same holds in a file that ignores case (CREATE.FILE
           ... NO.CASE) and in a directory file, where jack and JACK are one
           record.  In an ordinary file, jack and JACK are two records, so
           copying one to the other and deleting the original still works.
           Copying to a different id or to a different file is unchanged.

           The closing line says what happened.  When DELETING skipped
           records, it reads, for example, "1 record(s) copied, 0 deleted. 1
           record(s) copied onto themselves were left in place."  When
           nothing was skipped, the line is the usual "n record(s) copied and
           deleted."

           What it does not do: it does not stop you copying a record onto
           itself without DELETING; that has always been harmless and is
           unchanged.

05 Oct 26  RESTORE.ACCOUNT LATEST NOW CHOOSES THE NEWEST BACKUP THAT REALLY
           HOLDS THE ACCOUNT, AND SAYS WHEN A NEWER BACKUP DOES NOT.

           RESTORE.ACCOUNT LATEST name [name ...] used to choose from the
           names of the backup files.  A backup of everything (ALL), or of
           several accounts, has no account names in its file name, so it
           counted for every account.  If you asked for an account that was
           not in the newest backup of that kind, SD chose that backup anyway
           and then refused it, even with an older backup of your account in
           the same directory.  This was measured on an Ubuntu 26.04 test
           machine.

           SD now opens each backup the name might fit, reads the list of
           accounts it holds (nothing is unpacked) and takes the newest one
           that holds every account you named.  If a newer backup made on
           this computer does not hold the account, or cannot be read, SD
           says so before it asks whether to go ahead:

               The most recent backup made on this computer, <file>, does
               not hold <account> (or cannot be read). The newest backup that
               does is <path>

           You can still answer n.  If no backup holds the account, SD says
           "No backup of <account> made on this computer was found in
           <directory>." and changes nothing.

           RESTORE.ACCOUNT LATEST ALL is as before: it takes the newest
           backup made with ALL, and a newer backup of one account is not a
           reason to warn.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it looks only at backups made on this
           computer, in the directory saved by SET.BACKUP.DIRECTORY, and it
           still checks the chosen backup in full before anything is
           changed.  A backup that cannot be read is skipped, not repaired.

04 Oct 26  EVERY FAILED API LOGIN IS NOW RECORDED, EVEN IF THE CLIENT HANGS UP EARLY.

           When a program tried to log in to the API with a wrong password,
           SD waited three seconds before answering, to slow down guessing, and
           wrote the failed attempt to the audit trail only after that wait.
           A program that hung up during the three seconds left no record at
           all.  This was measured on Debian 13 and on Fedora 44: five of five
           early disconnects wrote nothing, while a client that waited wrote
           one.  Anyone guessing passwords over the API could therefore choose
           not to be recorded.

           SD now writes the record first and then waits.  The three-second
           wait is unchanged, and so is everything else about a failed login:
           the message, the refusal and the audit text.  Successful logins are
           not affected.

04 Oct 26  THE INSTALLER'S CLOSING MESSAGE IS CORRECTED.

           After "Reboot to assure that group memberships are updated" the
           installer tells you to "enter 'sd'" to connect.  It printed the
           name as \'sd\', backslashes included.  It now prints 'sd'.

           The message also said only that sdsys cannot log in over ssh.  It
           now says the other half as well: the API admits sdsys only from a
           process running as sdsys on the same computer, never from another
           one.  That is how SD already behaved; only the message was
           incomplete.  Nothing else about the install changed.

04 Oct 26  THE INSTALLER CHECKS THE COMPUTER IS ONLINE BEFORE IT STARTS.

           The installer downloads SD and the build packages, so it needs the
           internet.  On a computer that was offline it used to ask its
           questions, ask for your password and then fail part-way through the
           packages.  It now checks first and stops at once, saying the
           computer is not online and that nothing has been changed.

           This matters when the installer is run from a USB stick: the stick
           carries the installer and the documentation, not SD itself, and the
           computer it is plugged into must still be online.  Run it with
           "bash", because a stick formatted FAT, exFAT or NTFS cannot mark a
           file executable.

           The check only looks for a route to github.com.  It is skipped when
           a proxy is set in the environment (https_proxy, HTTPS_PROXY,
           all_proxy or ALL_PROXY).  If your proxy is set up only for the
           package manager and the check stops you wrongly, run the installer
           as "SD_SKIP_NET_CHECK=1 bash installsdcore.sh".  It does not make
           the installer work offline.

04 Oct 26  DEBIAN, UBUNTU AND FEDORA ONLY.

           The installer now supports Debian, Ubuntu (and derivatives such as
           Linux Mint) and Fedora, and refuses anything else by name before
           it installs a package.  Arch and openSUSE were listed but had
           never been installed on; their package steps are removed rather
           than shipped untested.  RHEL and its clones (Rocky, Alma), which
           the installer used to treat as Fedora, are refused as well: they
           were never a target.  Nothing changes on a computer already
           running SD Core.

           The installer also installs the "openssl" command, which SD Core
           uses to show the API certificate.  Fedora Workstation (the desktop
           edition) does not include it, unlike Fedora Server.

04 Oct 26  THE FIREWALL RULES WORK ON FEDORA (FIREWALLD) AS WELL AS UBUNTU (UFW).

           Opening the API or ssh to other computers - at install, or later
           with REMOTE.API ON and REMOTE.SSH ON - added a rule only to ufw,
           the firewall Debian and Ubuntu use.  Fedora uses firewalld, so
           there the listener opened but the firewall kept every other
           computer out, and the install said nothing about it.

           The installer, REMOTE.API, REMOTE.SSH and the uninstaller now use
           whichever of the two the computer runs: the API's port 4247 and
           ssh's port 22 are added to firewalld's default zone and kept in
           its saved settings, so they survive a restart.  REMOTE.API SHOW
           and REMOTE.SSH SHOW name the firewall they read.  REMOTE.SSH OFF
           removes only the port 22 rule SD added; Fedora's own "ssh" service
           still lets ssh in, and the command says so rather than remove a
           rule SD did not make.  The uninstaller removes SD's 4247 rule from
           firewalld as it does from ufw.

           A computer with neither firewall is unchanged: the listener alone
           decides, as before.  Tested on Fedora 44 Server.

02 Oct 26  A RESTORED ACCOUNT KEEPS ITS DIRECTORY PERMISSIONS.

           RESTORE.ACCOUNT used to create every directory of the restored
           account readable and writable by its group (mode 775), whatever
           the directory's mode had been when BACKUP.ACCOUNT saved it, so an
           account whose directories were private (700) came back with them
           open to its group.  The backup always recorded each directory's
           mode; the restore now applies it, as it already did for files.
           The owner always keeps full access, and permission for everyone
           else to write is not restored.

           WHY: found on the first restore run on a real installation, where
           every directory of the account changed from 700 to 775.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not repair an account that was
           already restored the old way; restore it again from the same
           backup.  A backup made by SD Core for Windows carries no Unix
           permissions, so the directories of an account restored from one
           stay 775, as before.

02 Oct 26  RESTORE.ACCOUNT LATEST: RESTORE THE MOST RECENT BACKUP WITHOUT
           NAMING IT.

           RESTORE.ACCOUNT LATEST name [name ...] and RESTORE.ACCOUNT LATEST
           ALL (each with the usual NO.QUERY) put the word LATEST where the
           archive name goes.  SD looks in the directory saved by
           SET.BACKUP.DIRECTORY, picks the newest backup made on this
           computer that holds every account you named, says which one it
           chose ("The most recent backup is ..."), and restores from it as
           it would have if you had typed its name.  For ALL it takes the
           newest backup that was made with ALL.  If there is none it says
           so and changes nothing.  The same command is in SD Core for
           Windows.

           WHY: a backup's name holds the computer, the accounts and the
           time, which is a lot to type when you only want the last one.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it never picks a backup made on another
           computer, and it only restores the accounts you named from a
           backup of several.  Archive names always end in .zip, so LATEST
           cannot be mistaken for one.  (How it decides which backup holds
           an account was changed on 05 Oct 26: see that entry.)

02 Oct 26  THE WINDOWS CLIENT LIBRARY EXAMPLE IS GONE.

           examples/windows.c (a Visual Studio port of the C client library)
           and gplsrc/sdclient.c (the Windows client DLL source, which this
           product never built) are removed.  SD Core for Windows has its own
           client library.  The Python API example no longer has a Windows
           library path.  Nothing installed changes.

02 Oct 26  THE INSTALLER AND UNINSTALLER HAVE NEW NAMES.

           installsdai.sh is now installsdcore.sh, and deletesdai.sh is now
           deletesdcore.sh.  They do exactly what they did before; only the
           file names changed.  SD Core Solo's are installsdsolo.sh and
           deletesdsolo.sh.

           WHY: the names now say which product each one is for.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not touch an installed system.  If
           you kept a copy of the old installer or uninstaller, it still
           works, but fetch the new names for a new install.

02 Oct 26  SET.BACKUP.DIRECTORY: SAY WHERE BACKUPS GO ONCE.

           SET.BACKUP.DIRECTORY directory saves the directory that
           BACKUP.ACCOUNT writes to and RESTORE.ACCOUNT reads from, so it need
           not be typed on every command.  It creates the directory if it is
           not there, checks that it can be written to, and keeps it in sd.conf
           (BACKUPDIR=), where it takes effect at once - no restart.  On its
           own, SET.BACKUP.DIRECTORY shows the saved directory and changes
           nothing.  BACKUP.ACCOUNT no longer needs TO: without it, it uses the
           saved directory, and if none is saved it asks for one and saves the
           answer exactly as SET.BACKUP.DIRECTORY would.  RESTORE.ACCOUNT does
           the same for an archive named without a directory.  TO directory,
           or an archive name with a directory in it, still works and changes
           nothing that is saved.  Only the administrator (SDSYS) may use it.

           A backup made on SD Core for Windows is now read correctly
           whichever kind of line ending its description file uses; before
           this, one with Windows line endings would have been refused.

           WHY: every backup and restore had to be given the same long path.
           SD Core for Windows has the same command.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: the directory must be given as a full path;
           a relative one is refused, because a setting that meant different
           places depending on where you were when you typed it would be a
           trap.  A directory that already exists is used as it is, wherever
           it is - on a USB drive, in the pCloud folder - and nothing about it
           is changed.  A missing one is made readable by SDSYS only, because a
           backup holds every account's files: by SDSYS itself wherever SDSYS
           may create it, and otherwise by SD's privileged helper, but ONLY
           below /media, /run/media, /mnt, /var/backups, /srv, /opt and
           /home/sdsys.  That limit is deliberate: a directory that SDSYS owned
           inside a place the system reads its configuration from
           (/etc/systemd/system/ssh.service.d, say) would let SDSYS run
           commands as root, and SDSYS never is root.  Anywhere else the
           command says so and creates nothing.  To allow another place, as an
           administrator list its directory, one per line, in
           /etc/sd-backup-roots (a file only root may own and write; SD
           ignores it otherwise), or create the directory yourself and give it
           to sdsys.  It does not move backups you already have.

           WHAT YOU MAY NEED TO DO: an earlier release refuses to start if
           sd.conf holds a BACKUPDIR line, so take the line out before going
           back to one.

02 Oct 26  SD CORE AND SD CORE SOLO CAN BE INSTALLED ON THE SAME COMPUTER, AND
           THE COMMAND YOU TYPE SAYS WHICH ONE YOU GET.

           sd starts SD Core, and sd is its only name.  SD Core Solo is
           started by sd-solo, which always starts Solo.  So a person who
           has both gets SD Core from sd and Solo from sd-solo.  SD Core for
           Windows works the same way.

           WHY: with their own shared-memory keys and API ports (see the
           entries below), nothing but the commands stopped the two being
           installed together.  Whichever of them came first on a user's PATH
           answered sd, which is the wrong way to choose.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not change which one ssh starts
           (each ssh route names its own program).  A person who is both a
           Solo user and a member of the sdusers group, and who connects over
           ssh, may get either, depending on the order of the two sshd
           settings; that combination has not been tested.

02 Oct 26  THE SD API NOW LISTENS ON PORT 4247 (IT WAS 4243).

           Programs that connect to SD through its API - the client library,
           and SD's own connections to another SD - now use port 4247 unless
           they name another.  The installer's "Allow API access" question,
           the firewall rule it adds, and REMOTE.API all use 4247.  The port
           is fixed: there is no setting to move it.

           WHY: 4243 is the port OpenQM and ScarletDME use, and the original
           SD uses 4245.  SD Core's full products now use 4247 and SD Core
           Solo uses 4249, so each can run on the same computer as the others.

           WHAT YOU MAY NEED TO DO: a client program that named port 4243
           must name 4247 (or name no port, and take the new default).  On a
           computer upgraded from an earlier release, a firewall rule for 4243
           may remain; the uninstaller now removes SD's 4247 rule and tells
           you about a 4243 one, but does not remove it, because another
           database may be using that port.

02 Oct 26  SD CORE NOW HAS ITS OWN SHARED-MEMORY KEYS, SO IT CAN RUN BESIDE
           SD CORE SOLO AND THE DATABASES IT CAME FROM.

           Every running SD keeps its user table and locks in a shared-memory
           segment and a set of semaphores, found through two fixed numbers.
           SD Core used the same two numbers as the SD it was built from, and
           as SD Core Solo, so only one of them could run on a machine at a
           time.  Each SD Core product now has its own pair (this one:
           0x53434C01 and 0x53434C02), outside the range used by OpenQM,
           ScarletDME and the original SD.

           WHY: the owner wants the four SD Core products - for Linux and
           Windows, full and Solo - able to run at the same time on one
           machine.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not make an SD started by an older
           release stop itself.  That release must stop it before this one is
           installed; uninstalling it first, as the installer already
           requires, does that.  (The API ports are separate too now, and SD
           Core Solo's installer accepts a computer that has this product -
           see the entries above.)

01 Oct 26  ACCOUNTS CAN BE BACKED UP AND RESTORED: BACKUP.ACCOUNT,
           RESTORE.ACCOUNT AND SETTINGS.REPORT.

           BACKUP.ACCOUNT name [name ...] TO directory (or ALL TO
           directory) writes one zip file, named for the machine, the
           accounts and the time, holding each account's files and a
           plain-text manifest of how the account was set up: its type,
           description, suspension, remote access and group members.
           RESTORE.ACCOUNT archive name [name ...] (or ALL) puts accounts
           back, over an account that exists or onto a machine that has
           never had it.  A restored account's paths are corrected to where
           it now lives, and programs it had in the global catalogue are
           catalogued again.  SETTINGS.REPORT [directory] writes, or shows,
           a plain-text record of the system's settings (sd.conf, ssh and
           API access, the accounts) for an administrator to keep.

           All three are for the administrator (SDSYS).  A backup or
           restore starts only when every other session has logged out,
           says who is still logged in if any are, and no one can log in -
           at the terminal, over ssh or through the API - until it has
           finished.  Every backup is checked as it is made: the files,
           bytes and directories of each account are counted before it is
           packed and compared with what was written, and a backup that does
           not match is deleted rather than kept.  A restore checks the whole
           archive against its manifest before it changes anything, lists
           what it will replace and create, and asks first.

           WHY: there was no way to back up or move an SD account.  Backups
           made here restore on SD Core for Windows and the other way round.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not back up SDSYS or the system's
           configuration (SETTINGS.REPORT records that, for reference only;
           nothing reads it back).  It carries no passwords: an account
           restored onto a machine that never had it asks for a new one, and
           an account that exists keeps its own.  It does not follow
           symbolic links inside an account; any it finds are named and left
           out.  A program recompiled after it was catalogued globally is not
           recognised as the account's and is not catalogued again.  A full
           system's backup restores only to a full system, and SD Core Solo's
           only to Solo.

L1.1-1 - 29 Sep 2026
-------------
Changes since L1.1-0.  L1.1-1 pairs with SD Core for Windows W1.1-1.

29 Sep 26  RUN WORKS FROM A DEEP FOLDER, AND TWO OVERRUNS ARE CLOSED.

           RUN refused any program whose full path was longer than 128
           characters, even though the file was there and readable: a
           program in a data file a few folders deep could not be run at
           all.  The limit is now 255 characters, the same as any other
           file path SD opens.  Beyond that, the file itself cannot be
           opened, and SD says so instead of running anything.

           Two places could write past the end of a buffer.  The error
           line "at line N of <program>" was added to a message without
           checking the room left, so a long message from a program with a
           long name could overrun it.  And the name recorded inside a
           loaded program was copied without a limit; it now keeps the
           last 128 characters of a longer path.  Neither was known to
           have happened, but both could have.

           The installer also checks its dictionary step properly.  It
           used to trust sd's exit code, and sd reports success even when
           the command it was given never ran, so an install could finish
           "successfully" with no dictionaries.  It now requires the step
           to finish with its own COMPLETE line and stops the install,
           saying why, if it does not or if the step reports an error.
           The step's full output is kept in
           /var/tmp/sdcore-install-pass3.log.

           WHY: found by SD Core Solo for Windows, which shares this code;
           the same defects were in SD Core for Windows and in the SD this
           was built from.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not change how RUN finds a
           program, and it changes nothing for a program under 128
           characters, which is every program in a normal install.

29 Sep 26  SD CORE IS ENGLISH ONLY, AND THE LANGUAGE MACHINERY IS GONE.

           L1.1-0 already took SET.LANGUAGE and LOAD.LANGUAGE out of
           every account's VOC, but the programs behind them, and the part
           of SD itself that looked up a message in another language
           first, were still installed.  Now they are removed: the
           SETLANG and LOADLANG programs, the messages only they used,
           and the kernel function that switched the message language.
           SD reads its messages from the one English message file, and
           builds its month and day names once when it starts.

           NLS IS BACK.  L1.1-0 also took the NLS command out of the VOC.
           It is restored: NLS shows the currency symbol, thousands
           separator and decimal separator, and NLS CURRENCY, THOUSANDS
           or DECIMAL followed by a value changes one for the session,
           and NLS DEFAULT puts back "$", "," and ".".  It has nothing to
           do with languages.  New accounts get it; an
           existing account gets it from UPDATE.ACCOUNTS.

           WHY: SD Core for Linux, SD Core for Windows and SD Core Solo
           for Windows are English-only products.  Support for other
           languages had been carried in from the SD they were built
           from without ever being decided on, and a feature nobody is
           meant to use is still something that can go wrong.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not remove the currency,
           thousands-separator and decimal-point settings.  A BASIC
           program can still read and change them with GETNLS() and
           SETNLS(), and the CURRENCY, THOUSANDS and DECIMAL keywords are
           still there.  It does not change any message, date or number
           that SD prints: they were English before and are the same now.

27 Sep 26  THE LICENCE TEXT NAMES THE RIGHT LICENCE.
           It said the install and delete scripts were under the "Black
           Oak" Model License.  The licence is the Blue Oak Model License
           1.0.0, as the scripts' own headers say.


L1.1-0 - 26 Sep 2026
-------------
The L is for Linux, and parallels the W of SD Core for Windows.  The number is
1.1 to match SD Core for Windows W1.1-0, the release this one is built to conform
with: SD Core for Linux tracks SD Core for Windows, and sharing the version
number makes it plain which pair of builds a screenshot or a support question
belongs to.

There was no shipped L1.0 - this is the FIRST release of SD Core for Linux,
numbered to line up with Windows rather than incremented from a Linux
predecessor.  1.0-1 and 1.0-2 were releases of the SD this work was built from,
not releases of this system.  So everything below is what L1.1-0 contains, not
what changed since some earlier Linux release.

Entries are newest first, by date.  This section is written for someone using
SD: what changed, why it mattered, and what it does not do.


22 Sep 26  A BASIC PROGRAM CAN NOW CREATE A PYTHON LIST, NOT JUST USE ONE.
           Embedded Python already let a BASIC program append an item to a
           list (PY_LISTAPPD), read one back (PY_LISTGETS) and empty one
           out (PY_LISTCLR) - but there was no way to create the list in
           the first place.  PY_LISTCRTE(listname) is now built and
           cataloged, so a program can make a new, empty list before it
           puts anything in it.

           WHY: the function was reserved and wired through to Python when
           embedded Python was written, but nothing in BASIC ever called
           it.  Found by SD Core for Windows, which hit the identical gap
           in its own verb-surface check and fixed it the same day.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not change PY_LISTAPPD,
           PY_LISTGETS or PY_LISTCLR, which already worked - it only adds
           the missing fourth verb.


20 Sep 26  A COMMAND RUN FROM THE COMMAND LINE NOW NEEDS TO BE ON A LIST.
           SD can be run as "sd 'SOME COMMAND'" - a single command, typed
           on the command line or run from a scheduled job, rather than a
           full sign-on session.  Until now that ran anything at all, for
           any account, the moment the account itself was reachable.

           IT NOW HAS TO BE ON THAT ACCOUNT'S OWN LIST, or the session is
           refused.  An administrator adds a command by name to the
           account's record in the SD system file "batch.jobs" - one
           command per line, nothing after it, and it has to be a
           paragraph or a sentence in that account, not an arbitrary
           program. Signing in as an administrator, at this machine, is
           unaffected - the list only limits a bare, unattended command
           line.

           WHY: an interactive sign-on has somebody present who can use
           judgement about what they type. A command run this way does
           not - it is meant for an unattended job, like one launched on a
           schedule - so it gets a list an administrator sets up in
           advance instead.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not change what an interactive
           session may do once signed on, and it does not apply to an
           administrator's own session, at this machine or otherwise. A
           new account's list starts empty, so nothing can be run this way
           until an administrator adds to it.


20 Sep 26  THE SDSYS ADMINISTRATOR ACCOUNT NOW APPEARS ON YOUR LOGIN
           SCREEN.
           SD has one administrator, SDSYS, and the way to use it is to log
           in to this computer as the Linux user "sdsys" and run sd from
           that session.  That is what SD tells you when it turns an
           administrator command away.

           It was not possible to follow that advice.  The installer created
           sdsys as a "system" account - the kind Linux uses for background
           services - and a login screen deliberately hides those, so the
           account SD kept pointing you at was never in the list.  It had a
           password, a home directory and a working shell; you simply could
           not get to it the ordinary way.

           It is now created as a normal account, so it appears in the login
           screen and the user switcher alongside everyone else, which is
           how SD Core for Windows has always behaved.

           NOTHING ELSE ABOUT IT CHANGES.  sdsys is still not an ordinary
           user account to work in, it is still not in the sudo group, it
           still cannot be reached over ssh or from another machine, and SD
           still refuses to administer unless the session really is a local
           sdsys login.  The only thing that changed is that you can find it.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not alter an sdsys account that
           already exists.  Reinstalling and keeping your accounts leaves the
           old hidden one as it was, because changing a live account's
           identity number would break the ownership of every file SD has
           installed.  The installer now says so when it sees one, and points
           you at a text console (Ctrl+Alt+F3), which reaches the hidden
           account perfectly well.  A delete-and-install that removes accounts
           creates it afresh, visible.


20 Sep 26  YOU CAN NOW TURN AN ACCOUNT'S ssh AND API ACCESS OFF, AND ON
           AGAIN.
           Every account can be reached two ways from elsewhere: by ssh,
           which lands the person straight in SD, and by the API, which is
           how a program connects.  Both are ON for a new account, which is
           almost always what you want - but not always, and until now
           there was no way to say so.

           Two verbs now take a route word: SSH, API, BOTH or NONE.

             MODIFY.ACCOUNT sales NONE     no remote access at all
             MODIFY.ACCOUNT sales API      programs may connect, people may not
             MODIFY.ACCOUNT sales SSH      the other way round
             MODIFY.ACCOUNT sales BOTH     back to the default

           CREATE.ACCOUNT takes the same word, and leaving it out means
           BOTH, so nothing you already type needs to change:

             CREATE.ACCOUNT USER sales          both routes, as before
             CREATE.ACCOUNT USER sales NONE     a local-only account

           IT GOES BOTH WAYS, WHICH IS THE POINT.  A setting you can only
           tighten is a trap: you would have to delete the account and make
           it again to undo a mistake.  Whatever you take away you can give
           back, in either direction, as often as you like.  SD tells you
           what the account ended up with each time, and says plainly when
           you have asked for the access it already had.

           WHAT HAPPENS TO SOMEONE WHO IS TURNED AWAY.  An ssh connection
           is refused as it arrives, with "<name> is not permitted to reach
           SD over ssh", and no tunnel can be opened through it either.  A
           program connecting to the API is told "<name> is not permitted
           to use the API".  Both refusals come after the password has been
           checked, so neither tells a stranger anything about whether an
           account exists.

           The two routes are independent: turning off ssh leaves the API
           working, and the other way round.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO.  It does not affect signing in at the
           machine itself - an account with NONE is still a normal SD
           account to anyone sitting at this computer, and that is the way
           back in if you shut yourself out of a remote one.  It is not a
           Linux login setting: it governs reaching SD, not what the
           account's Linux user may do.  It does not apply to SDSYS, which
           has never been reachable by ssh and reaches the API only from a
           session already signed in as sdsys on this machine.  And
           REMOTE.SSH and REMOTE.API, which say whether this computer can
           be reached from elsewhere at all, are unchanged and still have
           the last word: what you grant one account here cannot get past
           them.


20 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER NOW SAYS WHOSE PASSWORD IT IS ASKING FOR.
           Near the end of an install you are asked for three passwords,
           and it was not clear which account each one belonged to - two of
           them are sdsys's, and the third is your own, with your account's
           password asked for in between sdsys's two.

           Before the first of them the installer now lists all three, in
           the order they come:
             1. your own SD password  - for reaching SD over the API;
             2. sdsys's LINUX password - for signing in as SD's
                administrator at this machine;
             3. sdsys's SD password   - for reaching SD as the
                administrator over the API.
           Each prompt is headed "1 of 3", "2 of 3" or "3 of 3" and names
           the account and which of its two passwords it wants.  Where SD
           itself asks ("New password:"), the line above it names the
           account, because SD asks in the same words for either one.

           A REINSTALL NO LONGER REPLACES SDSYS'S LINUX PASSWORD BEHIND
           YOUR BACK.  If you reinstall and keep your accounts, the sdsys
           user is still there with the password you set.  The installer
           used to ask again and replace it, with no way to enter the one
           already in use.  It now keeps it, says so, and tells you that
           "sudo passwd sdsys" is how to change it on purpose.  The two SD
           passwords already behaved this way.

           The steps are quieter, too.  Each one is introduced by its own
           ruled block, rather than the only ruled block on screen being
           SD's "administration granted" notice arriving mid-step, where it
           read as a result.  When the installer runs SD to set a password
           it now runs it silently: no start and shutdown lines, no notice,
           and a successful entry answers "Password accepted".  A refusal
           still says everything - that is the case you have to act on.

           What this does NOT do: it does not change which passwords are
           asked for, and your own SD password is still required.  The
           password asked for at the very start of an install is your own
           Linux password, for sudo, and is not one of the three.

19 Sep 26  A REMOTE SDSYS SESSION IS NOW TURNED AWAY AT THE DOOR.
           Running sd as sdsys over ssh was already stopped - SD would not
           let the session into the SDSYS account - but it stopped it one
           step late, after the session had started.  SD now refuses such a
           session as it opens, and says why: SD administration needs a
           local session.  Log in as sdsys at the machine itself.

           This is a tightening of something that already worked, not the
           closing of a way in.  sdsys still cannot log in over ssh at all;
           that is set in the machine's ssh configuration, and this is SD's
           own half of the same door.

           What this does NOT do: it does not affect any other account, and
           it does not change how sdsys is administered locally.

19 Sep 26  SDSYS CAN USE THE API AGAIN, AND NOW HAS TWO PASSWORDS.
           The install now asks for a second password for sdsys: one is the
           Linux password that signs sdsys in at this machine, the other is
           an SD password that lets a program reach SD as the administrator
           through the API.  They are different passwords for different
           doors, and the install says so as it asks.

           It is optional.  Press Enter and sdsys simply has no API access,
           which is how every earlier release was delivered - until now SD
           refused to set an SDSYS password at all, so nothing could reach
           the administrator account through the API even on this machine.

           WHAT GUARDS IT.  SD admits sdsys over the API only when the
           operating system itself says the connection was opened by a
           process running as sdsys on this machine.  SD used to accept any
           connection that arrived from a local address, and that is not the
           same test: an ssh tunnel ends on this machine, so a connection
           forwarded from somewhere else looks local.  Anyone with a login
           here could have forwarded a port and offered sdsys's password.
           SD now asks the kernel who opened the socket, which a tunnel
           cannot disguise - it reports whoever owns the tunnel.

           What this does NOT do: it does not give sdsys API access from
           another computer, and it does not work over 127.0.0.1 either.
           Only SD's own local socket carries the proof SD needs, so a
           program wanting to connect as sdsys must use that socket and must
           be running as sdsys.  Every other account is unaffected and
           reaches the API exactly as before.  LOGTO SDSYS remains refused
           for everybody: the administrator account is entered by logging in
           as sdsys, or through the API as described here.

19 Sep 26  SD NOW REQUIRES A STRONG PASSWORD.
           Every password SD sets must be at least 8 characters long and
           contain a lower-case letter, an upper-case letter, a digit and a
           symbol (any other printable character, space included).  It
           applies to:
             - the SD password (MODIFY.PASSWORD, and the one the installer
               asks for);
             - the Linux password CREATE.ACCOUNT sets for a new account;
             - the sdsys password the installer asks for.
           Until now SD accepted whatever the machine's own rules allowed,
           and on many machines that is anything at all.  A password that
           gives access to the database should not be weaker than this,
           whatever the operating system is set to allow.  SD Core for
           Windows applies the same rule, word for word.

           A password that does not meet the rule is refused before you are
           asked to type it again, and counts as one of the three attempts.
           Both prompts now say which attempt you are on - MODIFY.PASSWORD
           and the one CREATE.ACCOUNT asks when it sets the account's Linux
           password.  The second of those had three attempts and no way to
           tell how many were left.

           What this does NOT do: it does not change passwords you already
           have, and it cannot govern a Linux password you change yourself
           outside SD (with passwd) - there the machine's own rules apply.
           The machine's rules still apply on top of SD's where they are
           stricter.  Only printable ASCII characters are accepted.

19 Sep 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT REMOVES THE ACCOUNT'S DIRECTORY, OR SAYS IT DID NOT.
           Deleting an account promised to remove its directory, and usually
           did - but anything the account's own user had made without group
           write permission (a folder made at the Linux prompt, for example)
           could not be removed by the administrator, and the rest of the
           directory was left behind without a word.  The directory is now
           removed through SD's privileged helper, which accepts exactly one
           account directory and nothing else.  If one is ever still there
           afterwards, SD says so (message 10919) and names it.

           The warning before a deletion also said a suspended account
           "can be given a tier again later" - tiers are gone.  It now says
           how to let a suspended account back in:
           modify.account <name> unsuspended.

19 Sep 26  UNSUSPEND IS NOW UNSUSPENDED.
           The word that lifts a suspension is  MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account>
           UNSUSPENDED, the same word SD Core for Windows uses, so the pair
           reads SUSPENDED / UNSUSPENDED on both systems.  The old spelling
           UNSUSPEND is no longer accepted; it is refused with the list of
           words that are.

19 Sep 26  MODIFY.PASSWORD CHECKS YOUR CURRENT PASSWORD FIRST.
           When you changed your own SD password, a wrong current password
           was only noticed after you had typed the new one twice; nothing
           was changed, but the new password was asked for as though the
           old one had been accepted.  It is now checked straight away, and
           a wrong one ends the command with "Password not changed."  The
           administrator setting somebody else's password is not asked for
           the old one, as before.

19 Sep 26  YOU CAN CHANGE YOUR OWN SD PASSWORD.
           Type  MODIFY.PASSWORD  in your own account.  SD asks for your
           current SD password, then the new one twice, all hidden.  Until
           now only the administrator could set SD passwords, even your own,
           and ordinary accounts did not have the command at all.

           The rule is the same as in SD Core for Windows: you can change
           only your own password; the administrator (logged in as sdsys)
           can change anyone's, and is not asked for the old one when
           setting somebody else's.  Both of you type the same command and
           see the same prompts.

           How it is done on Linux: the password store stays readable and
           writable by the administrator only.  When you change your own
           password, SD hands the new (already scrambled) password to a
           small helper that every SD user may run through sudo for exactly
           this and nothing else; the helper checks who you are from sudo,
           checks your current password, and changes only your record.  No
           password, old or new, ever appears on a command line.

           What this does NOT do: it does not change your Linux password,
           and it does not let you change anyone else's SD password.  A
           suspended account cannot change its password.

19 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER ASKS FOR YOUR SD PASSWORD AGAIN - AND IT IS REQUIRED.
           This reverses the 18 Sep entry below, the same night, for a
           reason that entry missed.  Logging in to Linux does reach your SD
           account at the machine - but every account except sdsys can also
           be reached remotely through the SD API, and that needs an SD
           password.  The installer's own account was the one account an
           install made without one, so the remote route it opened could not
           be used.

           The installer now asks for it near the end, hidden, twice.  It is
           NOT your Linux password and does not replace it: at the machine
           and over ssh you still sign in with your Linux password.  This one
           is only for programs that reach SD through its API, from this
           computer or another.

           If you leave the prompt without setting one, the installer asks
           again, up to three times, then says in red how to set it later
           (log in as sdsys and type  MODIFY.PASSWORD  with your user name).
           It cannot force you, and it says so rather than pretend.  A
           reinstall that keeps your accounts keeps your password and does
           not ask.  SD Core for Windows made the same change the same night.

           Also: two MODIFY.PASSWORD refusals still told you to use
           "su - sdsys", which SD now refuses; they now say to log in as
           sdsys at the machine.

19 Sep 26  YOUR SD ACCOUNT IS NAMED IN LOWER CASE EVEN IF YOUR LINUX LOGIN IS
           NOT.  The installer makes an SD account for the person who
           installs SD, on top of the Linux login they already have.  If
           that login had capital letters in it - "Don" rather than "don" -
           the account's register entry was lower case but its directory
           and its sdu_ group kept the capitals, so the three did not
           match.  Now all three are lower case; the files still belong to
           your Linux login, spelled as it is.

           The installer's internal step for this is now called ATTACH, the
           same name and the same command line SD Core for Windows uses.
           ATTACH cannot be typed at an SD prompt: it works only inside the
           installer, once, for the one person installing.

           What this does NOT do: it does not rename an account made by an
           earlier install.

18 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER NO LONGER ASKS FOR AN SD PASSWORD FOR YOUR ACCOUNT.
           This reverses the 15 Sep entry below.  The account the installer
           makes for you is reached by logging in to Linux and running sd -
           you have already given a password to get that far, so a second
           one bought nothing.  SD Core for Windows made the same change the
           same day, so the two installers now behave alike.

           The SD password still exists, and is still what programs using
           the SD API log in with.  If you need one, the administrator sets
           it: log in as sdsys and type  MODIFY.PASSWORD  with your user
           name.  A reinstall that keeps your accounts keeps any password
           already set.

           What this does NOT change: the installer still asks for the
           Linux password of the sdsys account, because logging in as sdsys
           is how SD is administered.

18 Sep 26  THE ACCOUNT MODEL IS NOW ONE ADMINISTRATOR AND ONE LEVEL.
           The tiered account structure - STANDARD, PROGRAMMER and
           ADMINISTRATOR, each with its own set of verbs - is gone.  Every
           account gets the same VOC: the full NEWVOC, which now also
           includes SH (the operating-system shell command) and !.  SH and
           OS.EXECUTE run at your own Linux permissions, with no SD gate
           behind them.

           There is ONE administrator, SDSYS.  It is not a rank that people
           are promoted into: it is the sdsys operating-system user, and SD
           gives it administration only when someone LOGS IN as sdsys, with
           its own password, at the machine itself (its keyboard, or a
           desktop-sharing view of it) and runs sd - not over ssh, not over
           the API from another machine, not by sudo or su from another
           user, and NOT by running sd as root, which is refused outright.  Every account except SDSYS can reach SD
           over ssh (ssh just starts SD) and through the API.

           The account verbs that moved people between levels (GRANT,
           REVOKE, LIST.GRANTS) are gone; giving a person access to an
           account is now the Linux group: usermod -aG sdu_<account> <user>.
           Suspending an account is unchanged in effect - MODIFY.ACCOUNT
           <account> SUSPENDED, and UNSUSPENDED brings it back - but it is
           now a flag on the account rather than a level change.

           What this does NOT do: it does not remove the ssh boundary (SD
           accounts still get SD instead of a shell over ssh, and the sdsys
           account cannot log in over the network at all), and it does not
           change who can read or write an account's files - those limits
           are Linux's own.

18 Sep 26  A BOUND IS PUT ON TWO NAME COPIES IN THE CLEANUP PROGRAM.
           clopts (the "sd -cleanup" bookkeeping) copied a user name from
           the shared system segment into a small buffer without a bound;
           both copies now copy at most the buffer's length and always end
           with a terminator.  Reported by the Windows port; could have
           crashed the cleanup in a damaged shared segment.

15 Sep 26  ONE ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE NOW NAMES THE CONFIGURATION FILE, FOR
           SD ITSELF AND FOR PROGRAMS THAT USE THE CLIENT LIBRARY.

           SD keeps its settings in /etc/sd.conf.  If you need it somewhere
           else - a test system beside a live one, say - you point SD at it
           with an environment variable, and that variable is SD_CONFIG.

           It used to be two variables that nobody could have guessed apart.
           SD itself read SCARLET_CONFIG, while the client library programs
           link against read SD_CONFIG, so setting either one moved half of
           your system to the new file and left the other half reading
           /etc/sd.conf - with no error to tell you.  SCARLET_CONFIG is no
           longer read at all; it was named after a project SD is not part
           of.

           WHAT TO DO: if you set SCARLET_CONFIG anywhere - a shell profile,
           a service file, a script - change it to SD_CONFIG.  If you have
           never set either, nothing changes: the default is still
           /etc/sd.conf, and it is used exactly as before.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not let SD find a configuration file
           on its own, and it does not change where the installer puts one.


15 Sep 26  REINSTALLING AND KEEPING YOUR ACCOUNTS NOW KEEPS THE SERVER'S
           NETWORK IDENTITY TOO.

           When a program connects to SD over the network, the connection is
           encrypted using an identity the server makes for itself the first
           time one arrives.  Until now, reinstalling threw that identity away
           even when you chose to keep your accounts, so the machine came back
           with all your accounts and passwords but a brand new identity.

           It is now kept whenever your accounts are kept, and removed only
           when you delete everything.  The delete script says which it did.

           WHY IT MATTERS EVEN THOUGH NOTHING COMPLAINS TODAY: no client
           program currently checks the server's identity, so nothing notices
           it changing.  When one does, a routine upgrade would otherwise look
           exactly like someone impersonating your server - and a warning that
           appears after every upgrade is one people learn to ignore.


15 Sep 26  DELETING AN ACCOUNT NO LONGER STOPS BECAUSE SOME OTHER ACCOUNT
           COULD NOT BE CHECKED.

           Before deleting an account, SD looks through every other account for
           references to the one going, and tells you about any it finds.  If
           one of those other accounts could not be opened - its directory had
           been removed, say - the whole command stopped with an error, before
           it had even asked you to confirm.  A check that only produces a
           warning was stopping the thing you had asked for.

           SD now names the account it could not look inside, says it was
           skipped, and gets on with the deletion.

           SD Core for Windows found this one and has the same change.


15 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER ENDS BY ASKING FOR YOUR SD PASSWORD.

           Programs that connect to SD through its API log in with an SD
           password.  It is separate from your Linux login password and is set
           with MODIFY.PASSWORD.  A new install used to finish with no SD
           password set for anyone, so nothing could use the API until an
           administrator set one by hand.

           The installer now asks for it at the end, for the account it makes
           for you.  It asks twice, to catch a typing slip.  Press Enter on an
           empty line to skip it; you can set one later by running  sudo sd
           and typing  MODIFY.PASSWORD  with your user name.  The install's
           closing summary says whether it was set, and how to set it if it
           was not.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not ask again when you reinstall and
           keep your accounts - the password you already set is kept.  And it
           does not ask for a password for SDSYS, which SD Core for Windows
           does: on Linux nothing would use one, because signing in never asks
           for an SD password and SDSYS cannot use the API.


15 Sep 26  REMOTE.API LOCAL AND OFF REMOVE THE FIREWALL RULE EVEN WHILE THE
           FIREWALL IS SWITCHED OFF.

           REMOTE.API LOCAL and REMOTE.API OFF are meant to take away the
           firewall rule that lets other computers reach port 4243.  On a
           machine where the ufw firewall was installed but not switched on,
           they left that rule in place and reported that there was none: SD
           asked ufw which rules were in force, and a switched-off ufw answers
           "none".  Nothing was exposed while ufw stayed off, but switching it
           on later would have opened port 4243 while SD said it was closed.

           SD now asks ufw which rules are configured, whether or not the
           firewall is running.  LOCAL and OFF remove the rule, ON finds it,
           and REMOTE.API and REMOTE.SSH report the rules that are really
           there.  REMOTE.SSH's warning about other rules that allow ssh reads
           the same list.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not switch the firewall on.  While ufw
           is off, the listener alone decides who can reach the API, and
           REMOTE.API reports the firewall as inactive.


15 Sep 26  THE API IS ENCRYPTED: EVERY CONNECTION IS TLS 1.3.

           Until now only the API login was protected.  The password never
           crossed the network, but everything after it did - every command,
           every record read or written, every result - as plain text that
           anyone able to see the traffic could read.  That was the weakness
           of the old QM-style API, and this closes it.

           Every connection to the SD API, over the network port (4243) and
           over the local socket alike, is now encrypted with TLS 1.3 before
           SD sends a single byte.  The login is also bound to that encrypted
           connection.  A machine placed between a program and the server
           cannot log in on the program's behalf or read what passes, even
           by pretending to be the server: the program's proof of its password
           is only good on the connection the program itself set up, and the
           program will not go on unless the server proves that it knows the
           account too.

           THE CLIENT LIBRARY (sdclilib, libsdcli) AND THE BASIC !sdclient
           CLASS do all of this automatically.  A program built against an
           older copy of the library cannot connect: the server waits for
           encryption to begin and closes the connection after ten seconds.
           Rebuild or relink it against the current library.

           The server's key is made on the first API connection, in
           /etc/sd-tls, and only root can read it.  The part of SD that
           handles the encrypted connection gives up root before it reads
           anything from the network and runs as the user nobody, so a flaw
           in it does not hand anyone root.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: programs do not yet check the server's
           certificate.  The bound login is what stops a machine in the middle
           from getting in or reading data, but such a machine could still
           collect a login attempt and try to guess the password offline.
           Use SD passwords that are long and not used anywhere else.

           Installing SD now needs the libssl-dev package, which the installer
           installs.


14 Sep 26  REMOTE.API AND REMOTE.SSH: CHANGE REMOTE ACCESS WITHOUT REINSTALLING.

           The installer asks whether other computers may reach this one over
           ssh and over the API, and until now the only way to change your
           answer was to install again.  Two administrator commands now do
           it, as they do in SD Core for Windows:

               remote.api on | local | off
               remote.ssh on | off

           REMOTE.API ON lets other computers connect to the SD API, LOCAL
           lets only this computer connect, and OFF turns the API off
           altogether.  REMOTE.SSH ON and OFF open and close port 22 in the
           firewall.  Either command with no word reports what the machine is
           doing now - read from the machine, not remembered.

           Unlike SD Core for Windows, changing the API setting does not
           restart SD or end anybody's session.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: REMOTE.SSH never starts or stops the ssh
           server itself, and on a machine whose firewall does not decide who
           reaches ssh (ufw not installed, not active, or letting everything
           in) it refuses and says so, because changing a rule there would
           change nothing.  Accounts still need an SD password, and for the
           API a grant (modify.account <account> api), whatever these say.


14 Sep 26  A FAILED OPENSEQ NO LONGER LEAVES ITS RECORD LOCKED.

           When OPENSEQ could not open a file the way it was asked - for
           example OPENSEQ ... OVERWRITE on a file you may only read, which SD
           now rightly reports as an error instead of pretending to empty it -
           the lock SD had taken on that file was never released.  It stayed
           after the program ended and after the session logged off, and
           nothing but restarting SD removed it; the next program to open the
           same file waited for a lock nobody held.

           The lock is now released whenever OPENSEQ fails.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: a successful OPENSEQ keeps its lock until
           CLOSESEQ, exactly as before.


14 Sep 26  THE API IS GRANTED PER ACCOUNT.

           Until now any SD user could log in through the API once they had
           an SD password.  The API now admits only accounts that have been
           given it.  An administrator always has it.  Anyone else gets it
           with

               modify.account <account> api

           and loses it with

               modify.account <account> none

           A refused login is told "<user> is not permitted to use the API"
           and the refusal is written to the audit trail.  SD Core for
           Windows works the same way.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT now asks for the decision up front: a new account
           that is not an administrator must say API or NONE, and nothing is
           created until it does.  Taking an administrator down to a lower
           tier must also say which it keeps, for example

               modify.account <account> programmer api

           Your own administrator account keeps the API: the installer adds
           every administrator to the new sdapi group.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: logging in at a terminal or over ssh.  ssh
           still follows the account's tier, so SSH and BOTH - the other two
           words SD Core for Windows accepts - are refused here with an
           explanation rather than quietly treated as API.


14 Sep 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR CANNOT USE THE API FROM ANOTHER MACHINE.

           The API now refuses an administrator's login unless the
           connection comes from this machine - 127.0.0.1 or the local
           socket.  An administrator's Linux user can reach root through SD's
           own tools, and the API admits a connection on the SD password
           alone, so an administrator login from the network was one password
           away from the whole machine.  SD Core for Windows has the same
           rule.  The refusal says what happened and what to do instead, and
           is written to the audit trail with the address it came from.

           "Administrator" means an account at the ADMINISTRATOR tier, or a
           Linux user in the sdadmin group, whichever is true.

           WHAT STILL WORKS: an administrator on this machine, including a
           local program connecting as one; standard and programmer accounts
           from anywhere the API is reachable; and an administrator from
           elsewhere through an ssh tunnel (ssh -L), which needs the account's
           Linux login before it gets as far as SD.

           ALSO: SD now records the address a TCP API connection came FROM.
           It used to record the server's own address - the one the client
           connected to - which says nothing about who connected.


14 Sep 26  THE APILOGIN SETTING IS RETIRED, WITH THE OLD API LOGIN IT CONTROLLED.

           APILOGIN in sd.conf chose how the old cleartext API login checked
           a connection: against the Linux password (1), or not at all for a
           local-socket caller, who was simply run as whichever Linux user had
           connected (0).  That login is gone - the API accepts only SCRAM,
           described below - so the setting has nothing left to choose, and
           the code behind both choices is removed from SD.

           CONFIG no longer lists APILOGIN, and a fresh sd.conf no longer
           contains it.  An sd.conf that still has the line keeps working: SD
           reads it and ignores it, so a kept configuration does not stop SD
           starting.

           SD no longer needs the libbsd library to build, and no longer links
           the system password-hashing library (libcrypt): the only thing that
           used either was the removed login.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the API's local socket (/tmp/sdsys/
           sdclient.socket) still works, including through an ssh tunnel, but
           a program connecting over it now logs in with SCRAM and the SD
           password exactly as over the network - it is no longer let in on
           the strength of which Linux user opened it.

14 Sep 26  THE API NO LONGER ACCEPTS A PASSWORD SENT IN CLEAR.

           Programs that connect to SD through its API used to send the
           account's password across the network as it was typed.  The API
           now accepts only a SCRAM-SHA-256 login, which proves the password
           without sending it, and in which the server must prove itself to
           the program as well - so a program cannot be fooled into logging
           in to something pretending to be SD.  This is the login SD Core for
           Windows uses.

           The client library shipped with SD (sdclilib), and the BASIC
           !sdclient class, already speak it.  A program built against an
           older copy of the library is refused with the message "Cleartext
           login is no longer supported", and needs the current library.

           THE PASSWORD IS THE SD PASSWORD, SET WITH MODIFY.PASSWORD (under
           sudo sd), not the Linux login password.

           SDConnectUDS, which connected over a local socket without a
           password, is removed from the client library: it depended on the
           old login.  Connect to 127.0.0.1 with SDConnect instead.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: logging in at a terminal or over ssh, which
           still uses the Linux password; and SDConnectLocal, which starts a
           session of your own and sends no password.

14 Sep 26  THE SD CLIENT LIBRARY NO LONGER SENDS YOUR PASSWORD.

           A program that connects to SD through the client library used to
           send the user's password across the network in plain text.  It now
           logs in with SCRAM-SHA-256, as SD Core for Windows does: it proves
           it knows the password without ever sending it, and it also checks
           that the server it reached really holds the account's credential,
           so a program pretending to be SD cannot collect passwords.

           THE PASSWORD IT USES IS THE SD PASSWORD, set with MODIFY.PASSWORD
           under sudo sd, not the Linux password.  An account with no SD
           password cannot be reached through the client library until one is
           set.  The library gains no dependency: it is still one file.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the library's functions and their arguments,
           so existing programs need no change - only the password they are
           given.  The old plain-text login is still accepted by the server
           for now; it will be removed in a later update.

14 Sep 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT NOW REMOVES THE ACCOUNT'S SD PASSWORD, AND
           MODIFY.PASSWORD SAYS STRAIGHT AWAY WHEN IT CANNOT RUN.

           Deleting an account left its SD password behind.  An account
           created later under the same name would have inherited it - the
           old password would have let someone into the new account through
           the API.  The password is now removed with the account.

           MODIFY.PASSWORD run without sudo sd asked for a new password twice
           before failing, because the password store is readable only by
           root.  It now says at once that it needs sudo sd, and asks for
           nothing.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: who may set a password, and how it is done
           under sudo sd.

14 Sep 26  MODIFY.PASSWORD NOW SETS SD'S OWN PASSWORD, AS ON WINDOWS.

           MODIFY.PASSWORD used to change the account's Linux password.  It
           now sets the password SD keeps for the account itself, the one the
           SD API is moving over to, as SD Core for Windows already does.  SD
           stores it only as keys derived from it, never as the password.
           Changing your own asks for the current one; changing another
           account's needs an administrator, and both need sudo sd, because
           the store is readable only by root.  A reinstall that keeps your
           accounts keeps these passwords too.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE YET: logging in through the API still checks
           the Linux password, until the next stage of this work.  The Linux
           password is set as before, by CREATE.ACCOUNT or with passwd.

14 Sep 26  A LONG PASSWORD NOW WORKS THROUGH THE API.

           A program connecting through the API could not use a password
           longer than 32 characters: the client library refused it as
           "Invalid password", and the server would have cut it short anyway.
           Linux puts no such limit on passwords, and neither does SD Core
           for Windows, so anyone with a long password was shut out of the
           API.  Both limits are gone.  An empty user name or password is now
           refused by the client straight away, as it is on Windows.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: user names are still limited to 32
           characters, and the password is still checked against Linux's own.

14 Sep 26  A PROGRAM CONNECTED THROUGH THE API NOW HAS THE SAME ACCESS AS ITS
           USER AT A TERMINAL.

           Linux decides what a person may read and write partly by the groups
           they belong to, and SD gives each account a group of its own.  A
           session started through the API took on the person's identity but
           none of their groups, so in an account they had been granted it
           could read but not change anything, where the same person at a
           terminal could.  The API now gives the session the person's groups
           when it logs them in.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: who may log in through the API and which
           accounts they may enter.  The session gets the groups the person
           already has in Linux and nothing more.

14 Sep 26  THE API NOW KEEPS THE SAME TIER RULE AS LOGTO.

           A grant may go down or sideways, never up.  LOGTO has enforced that
           since GRANT arrived, but a program connecting through the API
           reaches its account by a different road, and that road did not
           check.  So someone granted into an account that was later promoted
           above their own tier could still enter it through the API, with
           the higher tier's whole vocabulary.  The API now refuses them, as
           LOGTO does.

           The API also names the account in lower case, as it is everywhere
           else - WHO over the API used to print it in capitals.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: logging in through the API, and entering any
           account the person may enter with LOGTO.  The API still gives the
           same short refusal for every reason an account cannot be entered,
           so a caller cannot use it to learn which accounts exist.

14 Sep 26  GRANT'S WARNING NOW SAYS WHAT HAPPENS TO A SESSION ALREADY OPEN.

           GRANT tells you the person must log out of Linux and back in.  It
           went on to say that until they did, they could be let into the
           account and then refused by the filesystem.  A session that was
           already open is not refused: it is let in, but cannot change
           anything in the account, because Linux still sees it with the
           groups it had when it started.  The message now says so.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: GRANT and REVOKE themselves, and the advice
           to log out and back in, which is still what gives full access.

14 Sep 26  ONE CRASHED SESSION CAN NO LONGER FREEZE ALL OF SD.

           SD's processes share a small set of locks.  If a session died while
           holding one - killed, or crashing - the lock was never given back,
           and every other SD session then waited for it for ever; the only
           way out was a reboot.  Now the operating system gives the lock back
           when the process that held it ends, however it ended, and a session
           that crashes gives back its locks itself before doing anything else.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: normal locking.  The one cost is the reason
           this was a decision: after such a crash the next session may find
           the shared information that lock protected half-updated, where
           before it would have found SD frozen.


14 Sep 26  TWO MORE QUESTIONS NO LONGER WAIT FOR EVER WHEN NOBODY ANSWERS.

           "Use active select list?", asked by DELETE, COPY, CD, CT, ED and
           DELETE.FILE when a select list is active, now reads (y/<n>) and
           takes Enter as N in all six commands, which stops without changing
           anything.  Before, only DELETE.FILE did; the other five asked
           again for ever, beeping, when run from a script.

           DELETE.FILE on a multifile asked "Delete all data components?",
           and neither answer left the file alone: N still deleted its
           dictionary.  It now offers a third answer, C to cancel, which
           deletes nothing, and Enter means C.  Y and N do what they did.
           This matches SD Core for Windows.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: answering Y does exactly what it did.


14 Sep 26  BACKSPACE WORKS AT THE COMMAND LINE WHICHEVER BYTE YOUR TERMINAL
           SENDS.

           A terminal's backspace key sends either Ctrl-H or DEL.  SD only
           accepted the one its terminal type said to expect, so after TERM
           VT100 (which expects Ctrl-H) the backspace key of a normal Linux
           terminal, which sends DEL, did nothing at the command line.  Both
           are now accepted as backspace unless the terminal type gives one of
           them another meaning.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the full-screen editors handle keys
           themselves and are not affected.


14 Sep 26  RUN FINDS A PROGRAM WHATEVER CASE ITS NAME IS TYPED IN.

           BASIC stores a compiled program under a lower-case name, so
           "BASIC BP REPORT" compiles to bp.out/report.  RUN did not do the
           same, so "RUN BP REPORT" then answered "Program BP.OUT REPORT not
           found" and only "RUN BP report" worked.  RUN now looks for the name
           as typed, then in lower case, then in upper case, the same order SD
           uses for file and command names.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: a program is still run from the file you
           name, and a name that matches nothing still gets the same message.


14 Sep 26  PROCESS DUMPS HAVE THEIR OWN DIRECTORY, AND ONLY THEIR OWNER CAN READ
           ONE.

           PDUMP writes a file holding a session's variables, call stack and
           open files.  It used to be aimed at the system directory: an
           ordinary user's dump could not be written there at all ("Cannot
           open dump file"), and an administrator's landed where every user
           could read it.  Dumps now go to /usr/local/sdsys/dumps.  Any SD user
           can create one there, nobody but an administrator can list the
           directory, and each dump is readable only by the user whose session
           it is (and by root).  If a dump cannot be written, the message now
           says where and why.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: who may PDUMP which process.  The DUMPDIR
           setting in /etc/sd.conf still chooses the directory; an install
           that kept its old configuration has DUMPDIR added for it.


14 Sep 26  SIGNING ON AFTER A RELEASE CHANGE NO LONGER HANGS A SCRIPT.

           When an account's VOC was at an older release, sign-on asked
           "Update VOC to new release?" and accepted only Y or N.  Pressing
           Enter asked again, and a script or pipe driving sd - which has no
           answer to give - made it ask for ever, beeping each time.  The
           prompt now reads "Update VOC to new release (y/<n>)?", and Enter,
           or the end of input, means N.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: answering Y still updates the VOC.  N leaves
           it as it was, and you are asked again at the next sign-on.


14 Sep 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR CAN LOGTO AN ACCOUNT CREATED IN THE SAME SESSION.

           In a sudo sd session, LOGTO to an account whose Linux group did not
           exist when the session started failed with "Error 3001 opening
           file at line 2952 of $CPROC" and left you where you were - for
           example, CREATE.ACCOUNT followed by LOGTO to the new account.  A
           running program keeps the group memberships it started
           with, and SD never looked again.  SD now refreshes them when the
           session starts and after each CREATE.ACCOUNT, DELETE.ACCOUNT and
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: who may enter which account, and who owns the
           files SD writes.  A group change made outside SD while a session is
           open is still only seen after one of those commands, or in a new
           session.


14 Sep 26  RUN SAYS WHEN A PROGRAM'S PATH IS TOO LONG, AND WHAT THE LIMIT IS.

           When the full path to a compiled program was longer than 128
           characters - an account kept deep in the directory tree, say - RUN
           failed with "Invalid runfile pathname", which read as though the
           name had been typed wrongly.  It now says "Runfile pathname is
           longer than 128 characters".

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the limit itself is still 128; a path that
           long still cannot be run this way.  Catalogued programs are not
           affected.


14 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER NO LONGER WARNS THAT SD MAY NEED TWO REBOOTS TO START.

           Its closing message used to say that in rare cases SD would not
           start at boot until the computer had been restarted twice, or SD
           was started by hand.  That was the service fault fixed on 13 Sep
           (see "SD now stays running when the computer starts it" below), and
           SD has started and stayed running on every boot since, so the
           warning is gone.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: if SD is ever found stopped, starting it by
           hand still works - sudo /usr/local/sdsys/bin/sd -start.


13 Sep 26  THE TIER POLICY LISTS MOVED TO THEIR OWN FILE.

           The two lists that decide which verbs each account tier gets - the
           verbs a STANDARD account is denied, and the ones an ADMINISTRATOR
           gains on top - used to live in SDSYS newvoc as the records
           TIER.OMIT.STANDARD and TIER.ADD.ADMINISTRATOR.  They are not
           vocabulary entries, so they did not belong in a VOC file - it was
           also the last thing in newvoc whose name was not lower case.

           They now live in a dedicated file, SDSYS tier.policy, as the records
           omit.standard and add.administrator.  IF YOU CUSTOMISE what a tier
           starts with, edit them there.  Nothing else changes: each tier gets
           exactly the verbs it did before.


13 Sep 26  SD'S OWN PROGRAM SOURCES AND INCLUDE RECORDS HAVE LOWER-CASE NAMES:
           gpl.bp/cproc, gpl.bp/login, syscom/keys.h, syscom/err.h and the rest.

           Every record in the system's program file gpl.bp and include file
           syscom now has a lower-case name, so SD's own names follow the same
           rule as everything else on disk.  With this, every name SD ships -
           directories, VOC entries, programs and include records - is lower
           case, apart from two tier lists that are due to move out of the VOC.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: $INCLUDE SYSCOM KEYS.H, $INCLUDE
           KEYS.H and BASIC GPL.BP CPROC all still work, because the compiler
           finds an include or source record in either case.  Catalogued
           names - the ones CALL uses, such as !PARSER - are untouched.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: RUN takes a program's name exactly, so a program
           in gpl.bp is now run as RUN gpl.bp write_install_dicts, not in
           capitals.  A script that reads these files by their path -
           /usr/local/sdsys/syscom/KEYS.H - needs the lower-case name.


13 Sep 26  WHAT YOU TYPE IS NO LONGER CASE-FLIPPED IN AN ACCOUNT WITHOUT ITS
           LOGIN PARAGRAPH.

           SD started every session with "case inversion" on - typing list
           gave LIST and typing LIST gave list - and relied on each account's
           login paragraph (PTERM CASE NOINVERT) to turn it off again.  An
           account whose login paragraph had been removed or replaced typed
           with every letter flipped, which quietly breaks anything that takes
           a name exactly, such as COPY FROM VOC or a record id.

           Case inversion now starts off.  Nothing changes in an ordinary
           account, where it was already being turned off.

           TO USE CASE INVERSION deliberately, PTERM CASE INVERT still turns it
           on, for a session or in a login paragraph.


13 Sep 26  ACCOUNT NAMES ARE LOWER CASE, THE SYSTEM ACCOUNT INCLUDED: don, sdsys.

           An account's name is now stored in lower case, so it matches the
           Linux user it belongs to.  The system account is sdsys, the same as
           its Linux user.  @WHO, WHO and WHO.AM.I show the lower-case name.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: LOGTO SDSYS, LOGTO DON,
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT DON ..., DELETE.ACCOUNT, GRANT, SET.FILE and
           MODIFY.PASSWORD all accept the name in any case, as does a
           Q-pointer that names an account.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: a program that compares @WHO with a name in
           capitals - IF @WHO = 'SDSYS' - needs the lower-case name now.  And
           an account register from an earlier release has upper-case names
           and will not work with this one; there are none outside
           development, so delete and re-create any test installation.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: account directories were already lower case,
           and the names of Linux users and groups are untouched.


13 Sep 26  S AT A REPORT'S PAGE PROMPT NOW SCROLLS THE REST OF THE REPORT.

           Answering S ("Suppress pagination") at the "Action (...)" prompt of
           LIST, SORT and the other report commands looked as though it ended
           the report.  It did not: every remaining record was sent, but SD
           still cleared the screen and redrew the heading at every page, so
           on a real terminal the pages flashed past and only the last one was
           left on screen with the record count beneath it.

           S now behaves exactly as if the report had been given NO.PAGE from
           that point: the rest of the report scrolls up the terminal without
           clearing the screen or repeating the heading.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: RETURN or N still shows the next page, Q still
           quits and A still aborts, and a report sent to a printer or to a
           hold file was never affected.


13 Sep 26  THE LAST UPPER-CASE VOC ENTRIES SD SHIPS ARE LOWER CASE: $acc, $map,
           $release, and $command.stack in every new account.

           $release records the release your VOC is at, $acc points at the
           account's own directory, $map is where MAP.CAT writes, and
           $command.stack is the marker that tells SD to keep your command
           history between sessions.  With these, every VOC entry SD ships or
           creates is lower case.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: COUNT $ACC, LIST $MAP and
           MAP.CAT FILE $MAP work as before.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: SD looks for $release and $command.stack by their
           exact names when you sign in and sign off.  An account made before
           this release has them in upper case and would be refused at sign-in
           with "$release VOC record not found" - there are no such accounts
           outside development, so delete and re-create any test account.


13 Sep 26  THE SDSYS REGISTER RECORD IS INSTALLED WITH THE PERMISSIONS INTENDED.

           The installer meant the system account's register record to be
           readable but not writable by anyone but root, and set that - and
           then two later steps undid it, so it was installed with execute
           permission as well.  It is now set last, to read/write for root and
           read for everyone else, like every other account's record, and the
           installer prints what it set.


13 Sep 26  CREATE.FILE NOW NAMES A NEW FILE IN LOWER CASE, WHATEVER CASE YOU TYPE.

           CREATE.FILE ORDERS makes a file called orders: the VOC entry is
           orders, the directory on disk is orders and its dictionary is
           orders.dic.  Before this, the VOC entry kept the case you typed and
           the directory was always made in upper case, so on Linux, where a
           name's case is part of the name, CREATE.FILE ORDERS and
           CREATE.FILE orders could leave two different files.  A file now has
           one name.

           If a file of that name already exists in any case, CREATE.FILE
           finds it rather than making a second one.  DELETE.FILE ORDERS now
           finds orders without asking, and so does SET.FILE when it looks in
           another account.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: LIST ORDERS, COUNT ORDERS and
           OPEN "ORDERS" in a program all find orders, as they already found
           names in either case.  The compiled-program file BASIC makes is
           now bp.out on disk as well.

           TO USE THE NAME EXACTLY AS TYPED instead, set the option whose
           description is "Keep operating system file name case in
           CREATE.FILE" - it is listed by OPTION under the historical name
           CREATE.FILE.UPCASE, which is misleading: it keeps case, it does not
           upper-case.  With it set, CREATE.FILE uses the name you typed for
           the VOC entry and on disk.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: files that already exist keep their names, and
           the records inside a file keep theirs - SUE and sue are still two
           records.


13 Sep 26  EVERY ACCOUNT'S OWN FILES ARE NOW LOWER CASE ON DISK: voc, $hold,
           $hold.dic, $svlists and bp.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT now makes these names, and the system account's own
           voc and $hold follow them, so the directory SD makes for your VOC
           is voc and your BASIC programs go in bp.  The VOC entry for your
           program file is bp too.  These are the names SD Core for Windows
           uses.  Printing to the hold file writes into $hold.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: BASIC BP MYPROG, RUN BP MYPROG,
           SP.VIEW and GET.LIST all work as before, because names are found
           through the VOC in either case.

           FIXED ON THE WAY - "Data pathname 'BP.OUT' already exists".  If the
           first program you compiled in an account was typed with the file
           name in lower case (BASIC bp MYPROG), SD made its compiled-program
           file under a mixed-case name, bp.OUT, that it could never find
           again; every later BASIC BP stopped with that message until the
           file was deleted by hand.  The compiled-program file is now named
           from the file's VOC entry, so typing bp gives bp.out, and BASIC BP
           afterwards finds it.

           WHAT IS NOT CHANGED YET: CREATE.FILE still makes a new file's
           directory in upper case, so the compiled-program directory on disk
           is still BP.OUT behind the bp.out VOC entry.  That is the next
           change.  An account made before this one keeps its old names and
           will not work with this release - there are no such accounts
           outside development, so delete and re-create any test account.


13 Sep 26  THE SYSTEM'S PROGRAM DIRECTORIES ARE NOW LOWER CASE TOO: gpl.bp,
           gpl.bp.out, bp, bp.out and pcode.out.

           The second half of the move that renamed the data directories
           earlier today.  gpl.bp holds SD's own BASIC source and gpl.bp.out
           its compiled programs; bp and bp.out are the system account's own
           program files.  The system account's VOC entries now name them in
           lower case, which is also how SD Core for Windows names them.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: BASIC GPL.BP CPROC and
           $INCLUDE GPL.BP HEADER.H still work, because the file is found
           through the VOC in either case.  The programs inside keep their
           names - CPROC is still CPROC.

           WHAT IS NOT RENAMED YET: the BP file in YOUR account, which
           CREATE.ACCOUNT still makes as BP, and every account's own VOC and
           $HOLD.  Those move together, separately.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: a script that reaches SD's source by its path -
           /usr/local/sdsys/GPL.BP/CPROC, say - needs the lower-case name.


13 Sep 26  SD NOW STAYS RUNNING WHEN THE COMPUTER STARTS IT.

           Some of the time SD would start at boot and then be shut down again
           a second later, so the first person to type sd was told "SD has not
           been started".  The system service manager was watching the wrong
           process: when SD's background process set itself up, the service
           manager saw a short-lived helper finish, decided SD had stopped, and
           stopped it.  The service now counts SD as running once
           "sd -start" says it has started, and leaves it running until it is
           asked to stop.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: how SD is started and stopped by hand
           (sudo /usr/local/sdsys/bin/sd -start and -stop), and what it does
           once running.  systemctl status sd now shows "active (exited)"
           while SD is up; that is expected, and means the service is
           running, not that it has ended.


13 Sep 26  THE SYSTEM'S DATA DIRECTORIES ARE NOW LOWER CASE ON DISK.

           Under /usr/local/sdsys, the directories that hold SD's own data now
           have lower-case names: accounts, newvoc, voc_template, messages,
           syscom and sd.voclib, and the files the install creates - $ipc,
           $map, $map.dic, voc.dic, accounts.dic, dict.dic and dir_dict.  The
           VOC entries that point at them name the new paths, so
           CT VOC syscom now shows "2: @SDSYS/syscom".  These are the names SD
           Core for Windows uses.

           Why it matters: on Linux a file name's case is part of the name, so
           SYSCOM and syscom could otherwise both exist.  Giving each system
           directory one spelling, and the same spelling as the VOC entry, is
           a step towards SD having no name in two casings.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: COUNT SYSCOM and $INCLUDE SYSCOM
           KEYS.H work as before, because the VOC entry is found in either
           case.

           WHAT IS NOT RENAMED YET: GPL.BP, BP and PCODE.OUT, and the system
           account's own VOC and $HOLD, move separately.  The records inside
           these directories keep their names: the register still holds
           SDSYS, and syscom still holds KEYS.H.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: a script or program of your own that reaches one
           of these directories by its path - /usr/local/sdsys/ACCOUNTS, say -
           needs the lower-case name.  Anything that goes through the VOC is
           unaffected.


12 Sep 26  THE SYSTEM FILE POINTERS ARE NOW LOWER CASE IN THE VOC: voc, syscom,
           newvoc, dict.dict, md, sd.accounts, sd.voclib, and in the system
           account accounts, messages and qfile.

           As with the commands, what you type does not change: LIST VOC,
           COUNT SYSCOM and $INCLUDE SYSCOM KEYS.H all work as before, and
           CT VOC SYSCOM now answers with the heading "VOC syscom".

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the files themselves.  These are only the VOC
           entries' names; the directories on disk (SYSCOM, NEWVOC and the
           rest) keep their current names for now.

           THE SAME CAUTION AS BEFORE: a command that takes a record id exactly
           - COPY FROM VOC SYSCOM,MYPTR, or QSELECT VOC SYSCOM - now needs the
           lower-case id.  Names you open or type are found in either case.


12 Sep 26  SD'S COMMANDS ARE NOW LOWER CASE IN THE VOC: list, create.file,
           count, and the other 380 or so.

           Every verb, keyword, paragraph, phrase and sentence SD ships is now
           stored under a lower-case name.  WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE:
           LIST, list and List all run the same command, as they already did.
           What you will notice is what SD prints back - CT VOC LIST now
           answers with a heading of "VOC list".

           WHAT IS NOT RENAMED YET: file pointers (VOC, BP, QFILE, SYSCOM and
           the rest), and the $ records ($ACC, $MAP, $RELEASE).  Those move
           separately.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: a command that reads a VOC record by its exact
           name - COPY FROM VOC LIST,MYLIST, for example - now needs the
           lower-case name (COPY FROM VOC list,MYLIST).  Commands you TYPE are
           found in any case; a record id handed to COPY is taken as given.


12 Sep 26  THE HOLD FILE IS NOW CALLED $hold.

           The second system name to move to lower case.  The VOC entry for the
           file that holds deferred prints - where SETPTR mode 3 sends output,
           and what SP.VIEW reads - is now $hold, and SD prints that spelling:
           SETPTR 5 now shows "Hold file: $hold P5" rather than "$HOLD P5".

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: your held prints.  Only the VOC entry's name
           moved; the directory on disk that holds them is still $HOLD.


12 Sep 26  THE SAVED-LIST FILE IS NOW CALLED $savedlists, THE FIRST SYSTEM NAME
           TO MOVE TO LOWER CASE.

           SD's system names are moving to lower case, one group at a time.
           This is the first: the VOC entry for the file that SAVE.LIST,
           GET.LIST, DELETE.LIST, COPY.LIST, EDIT.LIST and SAVE.STACK use is
           now $savedlists.  A new account gets it under that name.

           You can still type $SAVEDLISTS: names are found in either case.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: your saved lists.  Only the name of the VOC
           entry moved; the directory that holds the lists is where it was,
           with everything in it.


12 Sep 26  A NAME NOW FINDS ITS VOC ENTRY WHETHER YOU TYPE IT IN UPPER OR
           LOWER CASE.

           SD has always forgiven a command typed in lower case: type "who" and
           it tries WHO.  It never forgave the other direction.  A VOC entry
           whose name is lower case could only be reached by typing it in lower
           case, so:

               CREATE.FILE orders
               COUNT orders       0 record(s) counted
               COUNT ORDERS       File not found

           CREATE.FILE had just made that entry, and the name it printed back
           was the one COUNT then could not find.  The same was true of a verb
           you saved under a lower-case name, and of a program that opens a
           file by name.

           Every place SD looks a name up now tries it exactly as typed, then
           in lower case, then in upper case - the commands you type, the
           query language (LIST, COUNT, SELECT and their keywords), BASIC's
           OPEN and OPENSEQ, a Q-pointer's target, RUN, the stacked-command
           editor, and the compiler's $INCLUDE.  An entry spelled exactly as
           you typed it still wins, so nothing that works today changes.

           This is the groundwork for SD's system names becoming lower case,
           which is still to come; it has to be in place first, or that change
           would stop every upper-case command from working.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not make the query language's RECORD
           ids case-insensitive.  LIST CUSTOMERS "SUE" still does not find a
           record called "sue" - on Linux those can be two different records,
           and SD will not guess between them.  (CT and ED already fell back to
           the upper-case id when the one you typed was not there; they now
           try lower case too, as they do for names.)


12 Sep 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT NOW FULLY REMOVES AN ADMINISTRATOR IT DELETES, EVEN
           WHEN THE LINUX USER STAYS.

           Most SD accounts own the Linux user behind them - SD created it, and
           DELETE.ACCOUNT removes it along with everything else.  But an account
           that was ADOPTED onto a Linux user SD did not create (the person who
           installed SD is the usual one) leaves that Linux user in place when
           the account is deleted, because the login and its files are not SD's
           to destroy.

           Until now, deleting such an account left that Linux user in SD's
           administrator group.  On Linux that group grants real power - a
           member can run SD's privileged helper without a password - so an
           administrator whose account you had deleted could still act as one,
           and from there reach the root account.  "I deleted that
           administrator" did not fully mean it.

           DELETE.ACCOUNT now strips the SD groups it granted - administrator
           first, then the general SD-user group - from a surviving Linux user,
           so a deleted account confers nothing afterwards.  The Linux user and
           its home are still left untouched; only the memberships SD itself
           added are removed.  Deleting an account whose Linux user SD created
           is unchanged: that user, and everything with it, still goes.


12 Sep 26  DELETE.FILE: PRESSING ENTER AT "USE ACTIVE SELECT LIST?" NOW MEANS
           NO.

           If you have a select list active and type DELETE.FILE with no file
           name, it offers to use the list:

               Use active select list (First item 'PAYROLL')?

           Pressing Enter used to do nothing at all - the question simply came
           back, for ever, until you typed Y or N.  In a script or a piped
           session there is nothing to type, so it never stopped: the same
           question scrolled past until something killed it.

           Enter now means N, which clears the list and deletes nothing.  That
           is the safe answer, and it matches the rest of this verb - every
           other question DELETE.FILE asks already takes N when you press
           Enter.

           THE QUESTION ITSELF STILL DOES NOT SAY SO, and that is deliberate
           rather than an oversight: the same wording is used by DELETE, CD,
           COPY, CT and ED, none of which takes a default yet, so adding
           "(y/<n>)" to it would be wrong in five places to be right in one.
           Those verbs are unchanged - in them, Enter still re-asks.


12 Sep 26  DELETE.FILE NO.QUERY NO LONGER ASKS A QUESTION AFTER PROMISING NOT
           TO.

           Deleting a file whose data lives in the system account - a VOC entry
           pointing at @SDSYS/something - does the safe thing under NO.QUERY:
           the VOC reference goes and the shared file is left alone, and it
           tells you so.  That part was right.  What followed it was not.  The
           verb then went on to ask

               OK to delete DATA portion '' (y/<n>)?

           three times, about a file with no name, after NO.QUERY had already
           been honoured.  Typed at a terminal it was a puzzling question you
           could answer with Enter.  In a script or a piped session it was
           worse: the prompt swallowed the next lines of input as its answers,
           so the commands after DELETE.FILE never ran and nothing said why.

           The right thing still happened underneath - the VOC entry went, the
           shared file stayed - which is exactly why this went unnoticed.  Only
           the route was wrong.

           IT DOES NOT CHANGE WHAT GETS DELETED.  NO.QUERY still declines to
           remove a file out of the system account, and still tells you the
           file is still there; delete it without NO.QUERY to be asked about
           the file itself.


12 Sep 26  MODIFY.PASSWORD SETS AN ACCOUNT'S PASSWORD FROM INSIDE SD.

               modify.password           - your own
               modify.password bob       - somebody else's (administrators)

           An SD account is a Linux user here, so its password is that user's
           Linux password: there is no second password to get out of step with
           the real one.

           SD NEVER SEES THE PASSWORD.  It does not prompt for one, hold one,
           or pass one to anything - it hands over to passwd, which does its
           own prompting.  And a password is never accepted on the command
           line: "modify.password bob hunter2" is refused rather than quietly
           ignoring the second word, because by the time you saw a prompt the
           password would already be in SD's command stack.

           Setting your own asks for your current one - Linux asks, not SD, so
           it follows whatever password rules this machine already has.
           Setting somebody else's needs administrator rights and does not ask
           for the old one, because an administrator resetting a forgotten
           password does not know it.

           ONLY ADMINISTRATOR ACCOUNTS HAVE THIS VERB, and that is a starting
           position rather than a rule about who deserves it.  SD ships its
           security tight and leaves you to loosen it deliberately: if you want
           one particular person to set their own password, copy the
           MODIFY.PASSWORD entry from VOC_TEMPLATE into that account's VOC.

           They can then change their own password and nothing else - asking
           for somebody else's is still refused, because that needs
           administrator rights and copying a verb does not confer any.  Remove
           the entry again and they are back where they were.

           Worth knowing if you do: an upgrade will not take the entry away
           again, but changing that account's tier will, because a tier change
           rebuilds the account's administrative verbs from scratch.

           What it does not do:

           It will not set a password for a group account, which has no Linux
           user of its own and so has nothing to set.

           It never touches the root account.  The user is always named
           explicitly, so the verb cannot be pointed at whoever happens to be
           running it.


11 Sep 26  UPGRADING NOW BRINGS YOUR ACCOUNTS' VOCs WITH IT.

           Installing a new release replaces the master lists SD builds new
           accounts from, but it never touched the accounts you already had.
           So every verb added since an account was created was simply not
           typeable in it, and there was no way to find out except by typing
           one and being told it was not in your VOC.

           An upgrade - a reinstall that keeps your accounts - now walks every
           registered account and brings its VOC up to the new release.

           ADMINISTRATOR ACCOUNTS GET THE ADMINISTRATOR VERBS THEY WERE
           MISSING, which is the half that had never worked at all.  Those
           verbs come from a different list, and nothing except creating the
           account or changing its tier had ever copied them.  On this very
           system the only registered administrator was missing eight of the
           eighteen - including create.account, delete.account, modify.account
           and update.accounts.

           It had gone unnoticed because administration is normally done with
           "sudo sd", which puts you in SDSYS, and SDSYS has every verb.  Sign
           on as yourself and the gaps appear.

           You can run the walk yourself at any time, from SDSYS:

               update.accounts all

           What it does not do:

           It never removes anything.  A VOC gains what the new release added
           and keeps everything it had, including records you have edited.
           Only accounts whose tier is ADMINISTRATOR receive administrator
           verbs; programmer and standard accounts are untouched by that part.

           It may ask, once per account, about a record whose type has changed
           between releases.  That question is unavoidable - SD will not
           silently replace a record of one kind with another - so an upgrade
           is worth watching rather than walking away from.


11 Sep 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT NO LONGER TAKES OVER AN EXISTING LINUX USER.

           SD accounts create their own Linux user.  That has been the model
           for a while, but the verb did not enforce it: if you typed

               create.account user bob

           and a Linux user called bob already existed, SD quietly attached an
           SD account to that person's existing login - no password set, no
           account created, nothing said.  It now refuses:

               Linux user bob already exists.  SD accounts create their own
               Linux user

           Pick a name SD will create, and SD will make the Linux user, set its
           password and build the account together.

           THE ONE EXCEPTION IS THE PERSON WHO INSTALLS SD, whose Linux login
           obviously already exists.  The installer adopts it, and that is the
           only adoption the system performs.

           AND A REFUSAL THAT NAMED THE WRONG THING IS FIXED.  Asking for
           no.query when the Linux user does not exist used to answer "Invalid
           user name", which sent you off to check a name that was perfectly
           good.  The real reason is that creating a Linux user means setting
           its password, and that needs a prompt, so no.query cannot work.  It
           now says so.

           What it does not do:

           There is no way to give an existing Linux user an SD account.  That
           is deliberate, not an oversight - it is what "SD accounts create
           their own Linux user" means.  The installer's adoption is gated on
           being run from inside the installer and cannot be reached from a
           command line, so typing ADOPT yourself answers "Unexpected token".

           Existing accounts are untouched.  This changes what CREATE.ACCOUNT
           will do next time, not anything already created.


11 Sep 26  GIVING SOMEBODY ELSE ONE OF YOUR ACCOUNTS: GRANT AND REVOKE.

           An administrator can let a second person into an account, take that
           access away again, and see who has it:

               grant payroll to bob
               revoke payroll from bob
               list.grants payroll

           Entry to an account has always been membership of the Linux group
           CREATE.ACCOUNT makes for it - sdu_payroll for account payroll - so
           these verbs edit that group and change nothing in the account's
           record.  Until now there was no verb for it at all: you added and
           removed members with MODIFY.ACCOUNT, and there was no way to ask who
           was in the group without leaving SD.

           A GRANT MAY GO DOWN OR SIDEWAYS, AND NEVER UP.  A programmer can be
           given a standard account; a standard user cannot be given a
           programmer account.  Your own tier has to be at least the tier of
           the account you are being let into.  Somebody with no SD account of
           their own has no tier to compare, so they cannot be granted one.

           NOBODY IS EVER GRANTED SDSYS.  It belongs to administrators, who
           reach it by logging in or with LOGTO.  It is not something a tier
           can be raised to obtain, and the refusal says so.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT ADD OBEYS THE SAME RULE, because it makes the same
           group change and would otherwise be the way round it.  DELETE does
           not: taking access away is always allowed.

           AND A PROMOTION NOW SAYS WHAT IT BROKE.  Raising an account's tier
           can turn people who were legitimately granted it into people SD will
           now refuse - the membership still looks perfectly normal from the
           outside.  MODIFY.ACCOUNT compares who could get in before and after
           the change and lists anybody the change stopped, with what to do
           about it.  Lowering a tier cannot break a grant, so it prints
           nothing.  If the group cannot be read at all it says THAT, rather
           than leaving a silence that looks like good news.

           What it does not do:

           The person must log out of Linux and back in before they can write
           in the account.  SD sees the grant at once and will let them in; the
           account's directory belongs to the group, and Linux gives a session
           the groups it had when it started.  Until they log in again they can
           be let in and then refused by the filesystem.  Revoking has the same
           delay in reverse - SD stops admitting them immediately, but their
           access to the files lasts until their next login.

           It does not stop an administrator making the same group change with
           usermod outside SD, and no rule here can.  SD applies the tier rule
           again at LOGTO, which is what actually holds.

           LIST.GRANTS reports the members of the group, which includes the
           account's own user and root.  Neither is a grant somebody made -
           CREATE.ACCOUNT puts them there - but hiding them would make the
           listing disagree with the group it is describing.

           The three verbs are new, so an administrator account created before
           this release does not have them, and neither UPDATE.ACCOUNTS nor
           re-typing the tier it already has will add them: a tier change only
           rewrites the VOC when the tier actually moves.  An administrator
           account created after this release has them from the start.  For an
           older one, move the tier down and back up:

               modify.account bob programmer
               modify.account bob administrator

           Do that to the account you are signed in as only if another
           administrator is registered, because the first command takes your
           own administrator rights away.


11 Sep 26  SD NOW KEEPS AN AUDIT TRAIL.

           The file "audit" in /usr/local/sdsys records every sign-on, every
           sign-on that was refused and why, every LOGTO and every refused
           LOGTO, the administrator rights sudo sd gives or refuses, refused
           API logins, and account changes made with MODIFY.ACCOUNT: members
           added and removed, tier changes (suspensions included) and SH /
           OS.EXECUTE grants.  Each line has the date, the time, the Linux
           user, the SD user number and the process:

              2026-09-11 14:02:07 user=don uid=3 pid=8624 LOGTO account=TSTD

           Under sudo sd the process belongs to root, so the line also names
           who ran sudo: "user=root sudo=don".

           The refusals are the interesting half - somebody who is not an
           administrator asking for SDSYS, or for an account they are not a
           member of.

           THIS IS NOT THE ERROR LOG.  The error log throws away its oldest
           half when it fills; the audit file is renamed with the date and time
           when SD starts and finds it over 1MB, and a new one begun, so nothing
           is discarded.  Removing old ones is your decision.

           SD USERS CAN ADD TO IT AND DO NOTHING ELSE.  They cannot read it,
           change or empty it, or rename or delete it - Linux itself refuses,
           because the file gives them write without read and is append-only
           (chattr +a).  Administrators read it with sudo.  To remove an old
           one: sudo chattr -a <file>, then delete it.  If the installer says
           it could not make the file append-only, your filesystem does not
           support it, and SD users could empty the file.

           Uninstalling with your accounts kept keeps the audit trail too, and
           reinstalling puts it back.

           What it does not do: Linux keeps its own record of logins and sudo
           use (the journal, or /var/log/auth.log), separate from this one and
           more complete; read the two together.


11 Sep 26  AN ACCOUNT CAN BE SUSPENDED.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT takes a fourth tier:

               modify.account bob suspended

           A suspended account loses all access to SD: the console, ssh, LOGTO
           from another account and the API all refuse it.  It is for an
           account that should stop working for a while - somebody on leave,
           a login being investigated - without deleting anything.  If you
           only want to stop somebody using SD, suspend the account rather
           than deleting it; DELETE.ACCOUNT now says so before it deletes.

           NOTHING IN LINUX CHANGES.  The Linux user, its password and its
           groups - sdadmin included - are left exactly as they are, and so is
           the account's VOC.  SD keeps the tier the account had, LIST
           SD.ACCOUNTS shows it, and suspending says all of this at the time.

           THERE IS NO "RESUME" KEYWORD.  Coming back names the tier:

               modify.account bob programmer

           and the VOC is brought from the tier it had to the one named.  An
           account can go from any tier to any other directly.

           What it does not do: an administrator can still LOGTO a suspended
           account, to look at it.  You cannot suspend the account you logged
           in to or the one you are standing in.  A session already inside the
           account when it is suspended carries on until it ends.


11 Sep 26  A PROGRAM CAN CREATE A SEQUENTIAL FILE AGAIN.

           The normal way for a program to create a file is OPENSEQ, which
           takes its ELSE branch because the file does not exist yet, then
           WRITESEQ, then CLOSESEQ.  Since a code clean-up earlier this year,
           that did not work: OPENSEQ threw away the file variable the moment
           it handed it to the program, so every WRITESEQ was refused with
           "not a sequential file", nothing was written, and the file was
           never created.  Worse, the lock OPENSEQ had taken on the file's
           name was kept, even through CLOSESEQ and logging out, with no
           session to own it: the next OPENSEQ of the same file waited for
           ever, only restarting SD cleared it, and listing the locks at that
           point could stop SD for every user.  OPENSEQ now keeps the file
           variable, as SD always did before the clean-up and as SD Core for
           Windows does, so the file is written and the lock is released when
           it is closed or the program ends.

           What it does not do: a lock already stranded this way is not
           cleared by installing this version; restart SD once.


11 Sep 26  SD ENDS A SESSION WHEN ITS INPUT RUNS OUT, INSTEAD OF SPINNING.

           When SD reads its commands from a pipe or a file - for example
           printf 'LISTU\n' | sd, or sd < commands - and the input ran out
           without an OFF, the command prompt did not stop.  It asked for the
           next command again and again, as fast as the machine could go,
           sending a bell character each time, until something killed the
           process.  It now logs the session out, exactly as OFF would.

           What it does not do: a session at a real terminal is unchanged -
           SD still waits for you to type.  A program that uses INPUT still
           receives an empty answer at the end of its input and decides for
           itself what that means.


11 Sep 26  A SESSION THAT DIED NO LONGER LOCKS A FILE FOR EVER.

           When a command needs a file to itself - BUILD.INDEX, DELETE.INDEX,
           CNAME, CONFIGURE.FILE, SET.TRIGGER - and cannot get it, it now names
           the file and the session holding it ("Holding it: 12 (don)"), and
           says what to do.  If nothing else has it open, it says the file is
           still referenced inside your own session instead.  LOGOUT n on a
           session whose process has already gone - a dropped ssh connection, a
           closed window - now reclaims its slot and releases every lock it
           held, and says so, instead of leaving it "(logout pending)" for ever.

           LIST.READU no longer crashes on a lock whose session has gone; it
           shows the owner as "(gone)".  The crash left SD's internal locks
           held, which stopped every other session.

           What it does not do: LOGOUT n on a session that is still running
           asks it to log out, as before; you can log out only your own
           sessions unless you are an administrator.


11 Sep 26  PROTECTING A VOC RECORD YOU HAVE CHANGED.

           If you have customised a record in your account's VOC - a sentence,
           a paragraph, a keyword, a file pointer - you can now keep it through
           a VOC update by putting [locked] in its first field, after the type
           code (for example "PA [locked] my nightly run").  The update leaves
           it as it is and then lists every record it left alone, so you know
           which corrections you have not received.

           What it does not do: it does not protect a verb.  A verb is what SD
           runs, and holding one back would leave your account calling a
           program this release has changed; a verb marked [locked] is updated
           anyway and listed separately.  To change what a command does, add
           your own verb under a name SD does not ship - updates never touch
           those.

           Also: when CREATE.ACCOUNT cannot set a new user's password it now
           says why - the two entries did not match or the system's password
           rules refused it, the name is one SD may not set, or the system's
           own status number - and it no longer offers a retry that cannot
           succeed.


11 Sep 26  LISTF SAYS WHAT THE SYSTEM FILES ARE.

           Ten of the file entries in the system account's VOC - ACCOUNTS, BP,
           BP.OUT, GPL.BP, GPL.BP.OUT, MESSAGES, NEWVOC, QFILE, $HOLD and the
           NEWVOC entry given to every account - now carry a description, so
           LISTF reads "File - Account register" or "File - BASIC program
           source" instead of a bare F.

           What it does not do: nothing about how these files are found or
           opened changes; SD reads only the first letter of that field as the
           type, as before.


11 Sep 26  CSV FILES FROM WINDOWS READ CORRECTLY.

           READSEQ now treats a CR LF pair as the end of a line, as it already
           treated LF alone.  A file written on Windows, or any CSV file that
           follows the CSV standard (which uses CR LF even on Linux), used to
           come back with a stray carriage return on the end of every line - and
           READCSV, which reads through READSEQ, put it on the last field of
           every row, so "B1" compared unequal to "B1".  A carriage return that
           is not followed by a line feed is still returned as data.

           What it does not do: files with ordinary Linux line endings read
           exactly as before, and WRITESEQ still writes LF alone.

           ED's return code for a command error is now negative, as every other
           command's is, so a program or paragraph that tests for a failed ED
           command sees one.


11 Sep 26  SIGNING ON FROM A MODERN TERMINAL.

           Most graphical terminals and ssh clients set TERM to a type such as
           xterm-256color, which SD does not ship a definition for.  Until now
           that left the sign-on sequence with no terminal capabilities at all:
           the screen was not cleared, and backspace at a prompt during sign-on
           erased nothing.  It looked fine once you reached the colon prompt,
           because your account's LOGIN paragraph sets the type again - but that
           runs too late for sign-on itself.  A type SD has no definition for now
           falls back to linux, the same type the LOGIN paragraph uses.

           What it does not do: it does not add definitions for more terminal
           types, and a TERM that SD does know is used exactly as before.


11 Sep 26  F1 SAYS SOMETHING.

           Pressing F1 at an empty command prompt used to do nothing at all,
           which looks like a broken keyboard.  It now says that SD has no
           built-in help system, that LIST VOC shows the commands your account
           has, and where this changelog is.

           What it does not do: it is not a help system, and HELP is still not a
           command; the message names only things that are on the machine.


11 Sep 26  .D AND AN UNFINISHED TRANSACTION.

           .D name, which deletes a saved sentence or paragraph from the VOC,
           now says "not found in VOC" when there is no such record.  It used to
           say nothing and act on whatever record had last been read, so .D with
           a mistyped name could offer to delete a different record.  It also no
           longer leaves a lock behind when the name is not found.

           A BASIC program with a BEGIN TRANSACTION that is never closed now
           fails to compile with "Unterminated transaction construct" instead
           of making the compiler run for ever.

           What it does not do: neither changes a program or paragraph that was
           already correct.


11 Sep 26  DELETE.FILE CAN RUN UNATTENDED.

           DELETE.FILE ... NO.QUERY no longer stops to ask a question.  When
           part of the file is in the system account it now deletes the VOC
           reference, leaves the file itself where it is, and says so (message
           10117) - it no longer assumes you meant yes to a question about other
           accounts' data.  Deleting a file whose name you typed in lower case
           no longer asks "OK to delete DATA portion" and then the same about
           the dictionary: those questions were caused only by the difference in
           case.  When the question is asked, it now shows that Enter means No,
           and it no longer asks again for ever when there is nobody to answer
           it - run from a script it used to print the question millions of
           times.  The same holds for "Use file" in DELETE.FILE, for the three
           "Program is also in ... catalogue. Remove?" questions in CATALOG, and
           for .D's "Delete VOC record?": each shows (y/<n>), and Enter means No.  An index held in a separate directory is now reported if it
           cannot be deleted (message 2636) instead of being left behind
           without a word.  And DELETE.FILE refuses to delete VOC itself however
           you type the name.

           What it does not do: NO.QUERY still does not delete a file whose data
           is somewhere other than its usual place without asking; FORCE is the
           way to say yes to that.


11 Sep 26  TWO MORE FIXES TAKEN FROM SD CORE FOR WINDOWS.

           QSELECT now names the select list it filled.  A successful QSELECT
           ended its message with a dangling "...to select list " and no number,
           although QSELECT takes TO n and the result can be in any list from 0
           to 10, so the one thing you need next was missing.  It now reads
           "14 record(s) selected to select list 3", as SELECT and NSELECT
           already did.

           DELETE.INDEX now accepts an index name typed in any case.  Index
           names are held in upper case, and LIST.INDEX folds case before it
           looks, but DELETE.INDEX did not: "delete.index zzak f1" answered
           "Unrecognised index name (f1)" for an index that LIST.INDEX showed as
           F1.  The two commands now agree.  A name that still matches nothing is
           reported exactly as you typed it.

           What it does not do: neither changes anything else about how the two
           commands select or delete; each was a wrong or missing answer.


10 Sep 26  SMALLER FIXES TAKEN FROM SD CORE FOR WINDOWS.

           CONFIG() given a name longer than eight characters returns an empty
           string with status 1004, as for any unknown name, instead of stopping
           the program.  A file in a directory file whose name holds a % that is
           not one of SD's own escapes is listed under its real name ("draft%1"
           rather than "draft"), and a name ending in % no longer makes SELECT
           read past the end of its buffer.  A WRITE inside a transaction with no
           lock held now says so (message 10151) instead of suggesting the disk
           may be full.  WRITESEQ to a port ends a line with CR and LF, not CR
           alone.  sdtic reports every terminal definition that fails to compile
           and exits with an error, and a bad definition no longer spoils the
           ones after it.  sd -start says so when it cannot start its daemon.

           What it does not do: none of this changes how SD is used or
           configured; each was a fault that either stopped a program or gave a
           wrong answer.

10 Sep 26  NANO JOINS MICRO, AND BOTH FULL-SCREEN EDITORS WORK THE SAME WAY.

           NANO <file> <record> edits a record in nano, as MICRO does in micro,
           and both highlight SD BASIC in a BP file.  They are now one program,
           taken from SD Core for Windows, and it brings three things MICRO did
           not have.  Value, subvalue and text marks show in the editor as ~~,
           ~` and ~! and turn back into marks when the record is saved, so a
           record holding marks is safe to edit.  The working copy in $HOLD is
           removed however the editor is left.  And a missing editor, a session
           with no terminal, or an account without OS-ON is told so before
           anything is written.  EDIT is still the line editor ED.

           What it does not do: nano's highlighting is installed for everyone
           only where /usr/share/nano exists; micro's is copied into each user's
           own configuration the first time they use MICRO.  A STANDARD account
           has neither verb.  A record whose name contains a single quote has to
           be edited with ED.


10 Sep 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT CAN REMOVE THE USER'S HOME DIRECTORY.

           DELETE.ACCOUNT <account> REMOVE.HOME also removes the home directory
           of the account's Linux user.  The one confirmation names the home
           directory, and afterwards SD says whether it really is gone.  Without
           REMOVE.HOME the home directory is left alone, as before.

           What it does not do: it removes a home directory only for a Linux user
           that SD itself created, and only when that home is the ordinary
           /home/<user>.  For any other user REMOVE.HOME is refused before the
           question is asked, and nothing of the user is removed.


10 Sep 26  A ROUND OF CHANGES TO MATCH SD CORE FOR WINDOWS.

           SD Core for Linux follows SD Core for Windows wherever the two do the
           same job.  An audit found places where they had drifted apart, and
           these are the ones a user will notice.

           Administration verbs are for administrators only.  SH, "!", CONFIG,
           LISTU, LIST.READU, LIST.LOCKS, CLEAR.LOCKS, LOCK, SET.DATE and
           CLEAN.ACCOUNT are no longer given to STANDARD or PROGRAMMER accounts;
           an ADMINISTRATOR account gets them.  An administrator who wants one
           person to have one of these can copy that verb into the person's VOC.

           UPDATE.ACCOUNT is now UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, the name SD Core for Windows
           uses.  UPDATE.ACCOUNTS ALL, typed in SDSYS by an administrator, updates
           every registered account without asking first and says how many it
           updated.  Any other word after the verb is refused.

           NLS, SET.LANGUAGE and LOAD.LANGUAGE are gone: SD is English only.

           Every yes/no question now shows which answer pressing Enter gives -
           "(y/<n>)" - and Enter gives it.  Several questions used to ask again
           for ever on an empty answer.  The default is always the answer that
           changes nothing.

           Accounts created from now on keep the descriptions on their VOC
           records ("File - Account register" rather than a bare "F"), and a VOC
           update no longer strips them.

           DELETE.ACCOUNT asks one question instead of up to three, and the
           question names what will be deleted.  It deletes the account's Linux
           user too, but only a user SD itself created: CREATE.ACCOUNT now marks
           the users it makes, and any other user - including the person who
           installed SD - is left in place, with a message saying so.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT takes SH-ON and OS-ON, so a new account can be given
           the shell or OS.EXECUTE when it is made.  MODIFY.ACCOUNT's SH-ON and
           OS-ON now report where both settings stand, and refuse a group
           account, which has no person of its own to grant them to.

           DELETE.CATALOG checks every name before deleting any, so a list that
           mixes your own entries with global ones deletes nothing instead of
           stopping half way.

           Several messages have new numbers, so that a message number means the
           same thing in SD Core for Linux as in SD Core for Windows.

           What it does not do: existing accounts keep the verbs and VOC records
           they already have - an update adds and replaces but never removes, and
           changing an account's tier is what brings its verbs into line.  Linux
           users created before this change carry no mark, so DELETE.ACCOUNT
           leaves them in place; remove one with userdel if it should go.
           DELETE.ACCOUNT does not remove the Linux user's home directory.


10 Sep 26  MICRO WORKS AGAIN, AND ADMINISTRATORS REACH THE SYSTEM WITHOUT SUDO.

           MICRO started the editor through SD's shell command, which is closed
           to anyone who has not been given shell access.  In an ordinary
           session it printed "not permitted to use the operating system shell"
           and then "Record was not saved" without ever opening the editor.  It
           now starts the editor itself, and says plainly when it cannot: the
           session has no terminal, the account may not run programs outside SD
           (MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> OS-ON grants that), or micro is not
           installed.  It also finds the file however its name is typed, so
           "micro bp test" edits BP.

           An administrator working in their own account without sudo now has
           the shell (SH and "!"), OS.EXECUTE and MICRO.  The rules already said
           an administrator's operating-system access comes with the tier and
           cannot be switched off, but in practice it was only there under
           "sudo sd".

           What it does not do: this access belongs to the administrator's own
           account.  After LOGTO into an account that is not an administrator's,
           the shell and MICRO follow that account's own settings.  A STANDARD
           account still has no MICRO verb.  MICRO will not edit a record whose
           name contains a single quote; use ED for that.


10 Sep 26  CHANGING AN ACCOUNT'S TIER NOW CHANGES ITS VERBS AT ONCE.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> STANDARD, PROGRAMMER or ADMINISTRATOR now
           brings the account's verbs into line with the new tier straight away.
           Before, the tier changed but the account kept exactly the verbs it
           had, so a STANDARD account raised to PROGRAMMER still could not
           compile, and a PROGRAMMER lowered to STANDARD still could.  Raising a
           tier copies in the verbs it adds; lowering one removes them.  The
           command reports how many VOC records it added, removed and left
           alone.

           What it does not do: when lowering a tier it removes a verb only if
           the account's record is still exactly as SD wrote it.  A record
           someone has edited or replaced under that name is left in place and
           counted as "left alone" - it is never silently destroyed, but it does
           stay usable.  File pointers and anything else the account has added
           are not touched.  If the account's VOC cannot be opened, the tier is
           not changed.


10 Sep 26  A VOC UPDATE NO LONGER UNDOES AN ACCOUNT'S TIER.

           After an upgrade, logging in to an account whose VOC is at an older
           release asks "Update VOC to new release?", and UPDATE.ACCOUNTS does the
           same thing on request.  Both copied the whole of the shipped VOC into
           the account, so a STANDARD account that answered Y got back every verb
           it had been created without - BASIC, CATALOG, RUN, the editors - and no
           privilege was needed to do it.  The update now applies the account's
           tier exactly as CREATE.ACCOUNT does.

           It also fixes an old fault in the same update: a verb whose record was
           identical to the one before it (CATALOGUE after CATALOG, for example)
           could be skipped, leaving the account without it.

           What it does not do: an update adds and replaces, it never removes, so
           an account already holding verbs its tier would not give it keeps them.
           An account created before tiers existed is updated with the full set.


10 Sep 26  ACCOUNTS NOW GET A VERB SET THAT MATCHES THEIR TIER.

           The verbs a new account receives now depend on its tier.  A STANDARD
           account gets the everyday verbs but not the ones for building or
           administering a system - no BASIC, CATALOG, RUN, the file and index
           commands, the editors, and so on: it can run an application, not build
           one.  A PROGRAMMER account gets the full set, and an ADMINISTRATOR
           account gets that plus the account-management verbs (CREATE.ACCOUNT,
           DELETE.ACCOUNT, MODIFY.ACCOUNT, UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, UNLOCK, and the other
           administration verbs listed in the entry above).

           What it does not do: an account created before this change keeps the
           verbs it was created with.  Changing its tier with MODIFY.ACCOUNT
           brings them into line (see the entries above).


10 Sep 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT NOW CREATES THE OPERATING-SYSTEM USER TOO.

           In SD a user account and its operating-system login are the same
           thing, so CREATE.ACCOUNT now creates the OS user as part of creating
           the account.  The OS user no longer has to exist first, and there is
           no longer a setting (CREATUSR) to turn this on - it just happens.  You
           are prompted for the new user's password.

           What it does not do: CREATE.ACCOUNT with NO.QUERY still will not create
           an OS user, because creating one needs the password prompt that
           NO.QUERY suppresses.  For an unattended CREATE.ACCOUNT ... NO.QUERY,
           pre-create the OS user; otherwise leave NO.QUERY off.


10 Sep 26  THE LINUX CLIENT LIBRARY HAS BEEN HARDENED.

           The library that programs use to reach SD over the network
           (sdclilib.so) has been replaced with a hardened version.  It now
           checks the length of what the server sends before trusting it,
           prevents a class of buffer overflows, copes with network reads that
           arrive in pieces or are interrupted, drops a connection cleanly if it
           loses sync instead of carrying on, enforces the server's maximum
           record size, and tells a server-side failure apart from a client-side
           one.  The default connection port is 4243.

           What it does not do: it does not change how you call the library -
           existing programs keep working unchanged.  It is the same library
           with sturdier handling of bad or partial input.


10 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER CAN NOW OPEN SSH AND THE NETWORK API TO OTHER COMPUTERS.

           The installer asks two questions.  "Allow ssh access from other
           computers?" - yes turns the ssh service on at every boot and opens
           port 22 in the firewall.  "Allow API access from other computers?" -
           yes opens the SD network API (the interface SD client programs use)
           on TCP port 4243 to the network.  Both default to no.

           Left at the defaults, ssh is untouched and the API listens only on
           this machine (127.0.0.1 port 4243): programs on the same computer can
           use it, nothing on the network can.  A remote program can still reach
           it by tunnelling over ssh.

           A connection to the API always has to log in with an SD user name and
           password, whether it arrives locally or over the network.

           What it does not do: it does not switch your firewall on.  If ufw is
           not running the "allow" rules are simply recorded for when it is.  It
           exposes nothing by default - you have to answer yes.


10 Sep 26  OVER SSH, NON-ADMINISTRATOR ACCOUNTS ARE NOW HELD INSIDE SD.

           SD can decide that a STANDARD or PROGRAMMER account may not reach the
           operating-system shell.  Until now that decision stopped at the door
           of SD: the same account could connect over ssh and land at an
           ordinary shell prompt, never entering SD, so the restriction did not
           hold at the edge of the machine.  The installer now adds a block to
           the ssh configuration that sends every non-administrator SD account
           straight into SD on an ssh login.  An administrator keeps a normal
           shell.  Someone SD has granted shell access (MODIFY.ACCOUNT SH-ON)
           still reaches one, through SD, as themselves.

           What it does not do: it does not change who may connect or log in -
           only what a non-administrator gets once connected.  It leaves local
           console logins alone.  Because the ssh session is handed straight to
           SD, file-copy tools that run over ssh (scp, sftp) no longer work for
           non-administrator accounts; that follows from the decision rather
           than being a separate setting.  If you have already customised your
           ssh configuration, the installer will NOT edit it - it tells you so
           and leaves it to you, rather than changing a file you are managing.
           Your original ssh configuration is kept as
           /etc/ssh/sshd_config.before-sd, and uninstalling removes the block.


10 Sep 26  A NEW INSTALL REGISTERS THE INSTALLING USER AS AN SD ADMINISTRATOR.

           Installing SD used to leave the person who ran the installer with an
           ordinary STANDARD account and no administrator registered at all.
           SD coped by granting administrator rights for the first session and
           saying that no administrator was registered.  That fallback still
           exists, but it is now a fallback: an install registers the
           installing user as an ADMINISTRATOR, which is both the account tier
           and membership of the sdadmin group.  An upgrade that keeps its
           existing accounts does not change any of them, so reinstalling does
           not promote anybody - and the sdadmin group survives that upgrade,
           so existing administrators do not have to be promoted again.

           The installer also now says plainly, at the start, if the person
           running it cannot use sudo, instead of failing part-way through with
           a system error.

           A fresh install no longer stops part-way through when /home/sd
           already exists - which it does when an uninstall kept your SD
           configuration.  The installer now makes the account directories
           whatever state /home/sd is in, instead of only when /home/sd is
           missing.

09 Sep 26  OPERATING-SYSTEM ACCESS IS NOW A PER-ACCOUNT PRIVILEGE.

           OS.EXECUTE - the SD BASIC statement that runs an operating-system
           command - and the SH verb and "!" at the command prompt are now off
           for everyone except administrators and SD's own system programs.
           Before this, any program could run OS.EXECUTE, so the boundary the
           account tiers are meant to draw did not hold: a standard or programmer
           account could reach a shell from a program it wrote, whatever its tier
           allowed inside SD.  A program that is neither an administrator's nor
           part of SD is now refused with "not permitted to use OS.EXECUTE", and
           a non-administrator using SH at the prompt is told they are "not
           permitted to use the operating system shell".

           An administrator can give either privilege to a named account:

               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> OS-ON      allow OS.EXECUTE
               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> OS-OFF     take it away
               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SH-ON      allow SH and "!"
               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SH-OFF     take it away

           The two are independent, so SH-OFF says nothing about OS.EXECUTE.
           Administrators always have both, so the four forms refuse an
           administrator account and say so.  A grant takes effect the next time
           the account is entered, not for a session already running in it.

09 Sep 26  SD BASIC IS SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTED IN THE EDITOR.

           MICRO now colours SD BASIC: keywords, strings, numbers and comments.
           The colours come from a file generated out of the compiler's own
           tables, so what is highlighted is what the compiler actually
           accepts, rather than a list somebody typed and then let drift.

           It only applies to records edited from a BP file - a program - which
           is how SD knows the record is BASIC.  A VOC or data record gets no
           colouring, which is the honest answer for something that is not a
           program.

           You do not have to do anything.  The first time you edit a program
           with MICRO, SD copies the colour file into your own micro
           configuration.  It is per-user because that is the only place micro
           reads them from, and it is copied rather than forced, so if you
           later edit that file yourself your version is kept until SD's own
           genuinely changes.

09 Sep 26  ACCOUNT MAINTENANCE NO LONGER CALLS sudo DIRECTLY, AND TWO THINGS
           THAT ONLY WORKED ON DEBIAN NOW WORK EVERYWHERE.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT, MODIFY.ACCOUNT and DELETE.ACCOUNT used to run eight
           different privileged commands through sudo - useradd, userdel,
           passwd, groupadd, groupdel, usermod, deluser and chmod.  None of
           those is restricted by its arguments, so a system that let SD run
           them let SD run them on anything: "sudo passwd root" is the whole
           machine.

           They now go through one command that SD owns and that checks what it
           is being asked to do.  It refuses root, SD's own sdsys account and
           every system account as a target; it will only touch groups that
           belong to SD, and never SD's own sdusers and sdadmin groups; and it
           resolves a path before setting the group bit on it, so a symlink
           cannot point it somewhere else.  If you administer SD you will see
           it name the command it resolved before it runs.

           TWO REAL BUGS GO WITH IT.  Removing a user from a group used the
           deluser command, which exists on Debian and Ubuntu and not on Arch,
           Fedora or RHEL - so MODIFY.ACCOUNT's DELETE, and demoting an
           administrator, simply failed there.  And creating an account group
           used "groupadd -U", which needs a recent shadow-utils.  Both now use
           gpasswd, which is present everywhere.

09 Sep 26  SETTING A PASSWORD AND CREATING AN OPERATING SYSTEM USER NOW REQUIRE
           ADMINISTRATOR RIGHTS IN THEIR OWN RIGHT.

           Two of SD's internal functions - the ones behind CREATE.ACCOUNT that
           add a Linux user and set its password - are in the global catalogue,
           which means any account can call them from a BASIC program.  Neither
           checked who was calling.  The only thing protecting them was that
           CREATE.ACCOUNT, the verb, checks - so the check was on the door and
           not on the room.

           A program written in an ordinary account could therefore make SD run
           "sudo passwd someone" or "sudo useradd".  On a machine where the
           caller can use sudo that succeeded; where they cannot, sudo stopped
           and asked for a password inside an SD session, which is not a place
           that can answer one.

           Both now refuse unless the caller is an SD administrator, and they
           report it the way they report every other failure - by returning
           false and setting STATUS().

09 Sep 26  THIS IS SD CORE, VERSION L1.0-0.

           The sign-on line now reads

               SD Core, the Essential Multivalue String Database, version L1.0-0

           and "sd --version" answers L1.0-0 to match.  It used to say 1.0-2,
           which is not this project's number at all - it is the release of the
           SD this work was built from.  The binary was telling you the version
           of something else.

           THE L IS DELIBERATE AND IT IS NOT GOING TO CATCH UP.  Our numbering
           is a separate sequence from upstream's, which is what the letter
           marks - exactly as SD Core for Windows uses W.  So upstream reaching
           1.0-3, or any later number, is not a version this system is behind.

           The "(AI modified)" that used to follow the version is gone.  It
           distinguished this from stock SD; the L does that now.

           If you have accounts created before this update, the first time you
           log in to one SD will tell you the account is at release level 1.0-2
           and ask whether to update it.  Answer Y.  That is the normal way an
           existing account takes on new VOC entries, and it is not a sign that
           anything is wrong.

09 Sep 26  sd -internal NOW REQUIRES root, AND SO DOES EVERY OTHER PRIVILEGED
           sd OPTION ON EVERY SYSTEM.

           -internal puts SD's compiler into the mode that accepts $INTERNAL
           programs, which are the ones allowed to call KERNEL.  It had no
           privilege check, so any user could compile a short program that
           granted itself SD administrator rights - which made the check
           described in the entry below, and every other check of those
           rights, something a user could simply step around.  It now asks the
           same question -i has always asked.

           If you compile SD's own internals - the programs in GPL.BP - you
           now need sudo to do it.  Installing SD is unaffected: the installer
           already ran these steps as root.  Nothing about compiling your own
           programs changes; ordinary BASIC never used this option.

           If you build SD from source and want that check out of your way
           while you work, build with

               make EXTRA_C_FLAGS=-DSD_DEV_BUILD

           A binary built that way says so - "sd --version" reports DEVELOPER
           BUILD and every use of -internal prints a line saying the check was
           skipped.  Do not install one, and if you are reporting a bug please
           include the "sd --version" output so we can see which you are on.
           A plain "make" is unaffected, and the installer always builds from
           a fresh checkout, so what you install is never a developer build.

           IN THE SAME CHANGE, "administrator" for these options means root and
           only root.  SD used to also accept membership of a group called
           admin.  SD does not create or manage a group of that name, and on
           some Linux distributions admin is an old name for the sudo group -
           so on those systems the option checks above were weaker than on
           others, with nothing to tell you which kind of system you were on.
           SD's own group, sdadmin, is deliberately not used here: these are
           operating system operations - start, stop, kill a user, restart,
           bootstrap install - rather than SD ones.

09 Sep 26  WHO COUNTS AS AN ADMINISTRATOR HAS CHANGED.  STARTING SD WITH sudo
           IS NO LONGER ENOUGH ON ITS OWN.

           Until now, any session that reached SD as root was an administrator.
           That is no longer the test.  An administrator is a person who is
           BOTH a member of the sdadmin group - the group SD's own sudoers rule
           names - AND recorded in SD's account register as an ADMINISTRATOR.
           Root is still necessary; it is no longer sufficient.  This is how SD
           Core for Windows has always worked: you can hold the operating
           system's administrator rights and still have no rights inside SD.

           You register an account as an administrator either when you create
           it or afterwards:

               CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> ADMINISTRATOR
               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> ADMINISTRATOR

           Either records the tier in the register and adds that person to the
           sdadmin group.  MODIFY.ACCOUNT also takes PROGRAMMER and STANDARD,
           and an account can move between the three in one command with no
           intermediate step.  Going down from ADMINISTRATOR removes the person
           from sdadmin again.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT is the one that matters on a system you already have,
           because CREATE.ACCOUNT refuses a name that is already in the account
           register - it will tell you the account exists.  You do not have to
           delete an account to change what it is.

           THE FIRST ADMINISTRATOR IS HANDLED FOR YOU, SO NOTHING TO DO BEFORE
           UPGRADING.  On a system where no account is registered as an
           administrator yet - which is every system installed before this
           update - starting SD with sudo still grants the rights, and SD
           prints a line saying that it has done so and how to register a real
           administrator.  That arm switches itself off the moment the first
           ADMINISTRATOR account exists.  You cannot be locked out by it.

           If your session is refused administrator rights, SD says so as it
           starts rather than leaving you to find out when a command fails -
           and it says WHICH of the two requirements you did not meet, because
           being told "you are not a registered administrator" when you are
           registered and merely missing from the group sends you to look in
           the wrong place.  There are three things it can tell you: you are
           not registered as an administrator; you are registered but are not
           in the sdadmin group; or the account register could not be read at
           all, which is not the same as it saying no.

           Whichever it is, it appears BELOW the sign-on banner, between two
           rule lines and with a blank line either side.  It used to print
           above the banner, where it read as one more line of the boilerplate
           everybody skips.

           Three checks now read the new test instead of asking whether the
           process is root, which was never the same question: CATALOG and
           DELETE.CATALOG on the global catalogue, and LOGTO SDSYS.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO.  It does not change what is in an account's
           VOC - every account still gets the same verbs whatever its tier -
           and it does not yet refuse a session to somebody who is not a
           registered SD user.  Accounts created before this update carry no
           tier, which is exactly the case the first-administrator arm above
           covers.

           SUSPENDED is not here yet.  SD Core for Windows has a fourth level
           that denies access while leaving the account intact, and this
           version does not: it would need the doors it is meant to close, and
           on Linux one of those is ssh, which is still being worked out.  A
           SUSPENDED that recorded a level and shut no door would be worse than
           not having it.

09 Sep 26  PROC, SED, UPDATE.RECORD AND MODIFY HAVE BEEN REMOVED.

           SED was the full-screen editor, and UPDATE.RECORD and MODIFY were
           full-screen record editors - MODIFY the one inherited from OpenQM.
           All three are gone, along with the PROC language.  LISTPQ, the
           sentence that listed the PROCs in a VOC, goes with PROC.

           A VOC item of type PQ now says that PROC is not supported instead of
           running.  Your PQ records are left alone: it is the interpreter that
           has gone, not the records, and SD says so by name rather than
           complaining that the record is invalid.

           ED, the line editor, is not affected and is now the way to edit a
           record.

           Four names are close enough to invite confusion and NONE of them is
           affected:

           * MODIFY.ACCOUNT and MODIFY.PASSWORD are different verbs with
             different programs behind them, and both remain.
           * LIST, COUNT, SELECT and SORT are the query processor, which is a
             different thing from PROC despite the similar name.
           * PROCREAD and PROCWRITE, the two BASIC statements, still compile.
             With the interpreter gone PROCREAD simply reports that it is not
             running inside a PROC, which is what it already did anywhere else.
             Nothing you have compiled stops loading.
           * The PHANTOM debugger is untouched.

           An account created before this update keeps whatever was in its VOC
           at the time - SD only ever adds records at an update, never removes
           them.

09 Sep 26  SD NO LONGER CLAIMS TO SUPPORT VIRTUAL FILE SYSTEMS.

           SD advertised a "virtual file system": you could write a VOC file
           pointer whose pathname began VFS:, and LISTF would report its type as
           VFS.  Nothing behind that was ever built.  No part of SD has ever
           looked at a pathname to see whether it begins with VFS:, no handler
           was ever loaded, and the three error codes reserved for VFS problems
           could not be raised by anything.  So the first thing you saw said the
           pointer was a valid virtual file system, and then OPEN refused it
           with an unrelated path error.

           The claim is now gone rather than the feature, because there was no
           feature.  FTYPE no longer returns VFS, the FL$TYPE.VFS file type is
           withdrawn, and a VFS: pathname is turned into an absolute path like
           any other - which is what SD always actually required.  The template
           class module for writing a VFS handler is no longer shipped.

           What this does not do: nothing that worked stops working.  A VFS:
           pointer failed to open before this change and still fails, only now
           it is described honestly from the start.  The three error numbers
           (3038, 3039, 3040) are retired rather than handed to something else,
           so a program that compares a status against one of those numbers
           still never matches - which is what it already did.  The names
           ER$VFS.NAME, ER$VFS.CLASS, ER$VFS.NGLBL and FL$TYPE.VFS are gone from
           the include files, so a program that spells one out will no longer
           compile cleanly; nothing shipped with SD used them.

09 Sep 26  SDNET - REMOTE FILE ACCESS OVER THE NETWORK - HAS BEEN REMOVED.

           SD could open a file held on another SD server by naming it
           server;file in a VOC pointer, over a private protocol on port 4245.
           That whole facility is gone, along with the verbs that configured it:
           SET.SERVER, DELETE.SERVER and LIST.SERVERS.  A VOC pointer written in
           the server;file form now fails to open like any other bad path.

           Why: it was an unmaintained network path, its per-server passwords
           were stored in sd.conf with a simple letter substitution that is not
           encryption, and it could not be turned off - the NETFILES setting was
           read at startup and then never consulted.  Removing it serves the
           smaller-system goal and closes a weak spot at the same time.

           The NETFILES line in sd.conf is still accepted and ignored, so an
           existing configuration file still loads.  If you used SDNet, remote
           access is no longer available; copy the data across instead.

09 Sep 26  THE TAPE AND RESTORE SUBSYSTEM HAS BEEN REMOVED.

           SD no longer ships the tape verbs - ACCOUNT.SAVE, ACCOUNT.RESTORE,
           FILE.SAVE, RESTORE.ACCOUNTS, SET.DEVICE, the T.* family (T.ATT,
           T.REW, T.DUMP and the rest) - or the programs behind them.  They were
           an optional part of the install: you were asked "Install TAPE and
           RESTORE subsystem?" and most installs said no.  The question is gone
           and so are the programs.

           This is part of making SD a smaller system.  Tape was a way of moving
           whole accounts to and from magnetic tape devices, which is not how
           anyone backs up or transfers data now; ordinary file copies and the
           account directory serve that purpose.  If you had installed it and
           relied on a T.* verb, it is no longer present.

09 Sep 26  ENCRYPTION, SDEXT AND EMBEDDED-PYTHON ERRORS NOW SHOW THEIR MESSAGE
           TEXT INSTEAD OF A BARE NUMBER.

           The error codes added for the encryption, SDEXT and embedded-Python
           features had no message text, so an error from any of them printed
           just a number - and the number carried the wrong sign, positive where
           every other SD error code is negative.  A program catching one by
           value would not have matched it.

           Both are fixed.  Those codes now read back the way the rest do: the
           right (negative) number and a sentence of text.  Nothing that already
           worked changes - these are the codes that showed nothing useful
           before.

           Behind this: the BASIC copies of the error, revision and opcode
           tables (SYSCOM/ERR.H, GPL.BP/ERRTEXT.H, GPL.BP/REVSTAMP.H,
           GPL.BP/OPCODES.H) are generated from the C sources rather than kept in
           step by hand, which is how they had drifted.  A build now checks they
           match and stops if they do not, so this cannot silently come back.

09 Sep 26  A TRANSACTION THAT FAILS PART WAY NOW PUTS BACK WHAT IT HAD
           ALREADY WRITTEN.

           A transaction is meant to be all or nothing.  Until now, if a COMMIT
           got part way through and then could not apply one of its actions - a
           record locked by something else, a read-only file, a disk problem -
           it stopped there and left everything it had already applied on disk.
           The records before the failure were written; the ones after it were
           not.  There was no way to undo it and nothing told you which was
           which.

           SD now records what each record held immediately before the commit
           overwrote it, and puts them back if the commit fails.  A record that
           existed is restored to its previous contents.  A record the
           transaction was creating is removed again, since it never existed.

           WHAT IS WRITTEN TO THE ERROR LOG.  Whether or not everything could be
           put back, SD writes a line to sdsys/errlog saying so:

               Transaction commit failed part way: 3 record(s) restored,
               1 removed, 0 could not be undone

           If a particular record could not be put back - because whatever
           stopped the commit is also stopping the repair - it is named
           individually, with its file, so you know exactly what to look at.  SD
           undoes everything else it can rather than stopping at the first
           problem.

           One thing worth knowing: the record the commit actually failed on is
           also restored, and so may appear in that count.  A write that fails
           half way can leave a record in a state nothing can describe, so SD
           puts it back rather than assuming it was untouched.

           AND A FAILED COMMIT NO LONGER HOLDS ITS RECORD LOCKS.  The locks a
           transaction took used to stay held for the life of the session after
           a commit failed, so the records nobody could finish committing were
           also records nobody could reach to repair.  They are now released as
           part of the same rollback.

08 Sep 26  A COMMIT INSIDE ANOTHER TRANSACTION NO LONGER THROWS AWAY THE
           OUTER TRANSACTION'S WRITES.

           If you began a transaction, wrote a record, began a second
           transaction inside it, and committed that inner one, the outer
           transaction was quietly abandoned at that point.  Its writes were
           then lost when you committed it - no error, no warning, nothing in
           the log.  The inner records landed and the outer ones did not.

           That is the one thing a transaction exists to prevent, so if you
           have avoided nesting transactions because results looked
           inconsistent, this is why.

           A commit now ends only its own transaction and puts you back in the
           one that contained it, which is what a rollback has always done.

           system(1008), the transaction nesting level, is also correct again.
           It only ever counted upwards before - raised when a transaction
           began and never lowered when one was committed - so after your first
           commit it could not be used to ask whether you were inside a
           transaction.  system(1007) was unaffected and stays the reliable
           test on older releases.

08 Sep 26  RECORDS WRITTEN OR DELETED INSIDE A TRANSACTION, IN A DIRECTORY
           FILE, WITH CERTAIN CHARACTERS IN THE RECORD ID.

           This affects directory files only, and only record ids containing
           one of these characters:

               *  ,  =  >  <  %  /  +  :  ;  ?  \  "

           or an id starting with a dot or a tilde.  In a directory file each
           record is a separate file on disk, and because those characters are
           awkward or illegal in a filename, SD stores them encoded - the id "="
           is held in a file called "%E", and SD converts back and forth for you
           so you never see it.

           That conversion was not being done for a write or a delete made
           inside a transaction.

           A WRITE inside a transaction stored the record under the raw id
           instead of the encoded one.  The write reported success, but a later
           read of the same id looked in the encoded place, found nothing, and
           reported the record as missing.

           A DELETE inside a transaction tried to remove the raw name, which was
           not there.  SD treats "already gone" as success, so the delete
           reported success and the record was still there afterwards.

           Both are fixed.  Neither needed anything to go wrong to happen - the
           transactions committed normally - and neither produced an error, a
           status or a log entry, so a site that hit this would have seen only a
           record that would not read back, or one that would not stay deleted.

           The same statements OUTSIDE a transaction were always correct, and
           records already on disk are unaffected.

08 Sep 26  A DELETE THAT FAILS INSIDE A TRANSACTION IS NO LONGER REPORTED AS
           DONE.

           Deleting a record from a directory file inside a transaction did not
           check whether the delete actually happened.  If the file was
           read-only, protected, or otherwise could not be removed, the commit
           reported success and the record stayed on disk - so a later read,
           select or list returned a record you had been told was gone.

           It now reports the error, the same way a delete outside a transaction
           always has.

08 Sep 26  A FAILURE WHILE EXTENDING AN INDEX NO LONGER DAMAGES THE INDEX.

           When SD needed a new block for an alternate key index and could not
           get one - a disk error, a full disk, a file held open by something
           else - it carried on and used block zero.  Block zero is the index
           header.  The write went over it, and the index was then permanently
           damaged: every select, list or query on that key read from a header
           that had a data block written on top of it.

           The error was always reported, but only after the damage was done,
           so the message named a temporary problem and left a permanent one.

           SD now stops the operation and reports the error before writing
           anything.  The index is left as it was.

           THERE WAS A SECOND WAY IN, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS IS MORE THAN A
           MISSING TEST.  One of the three ways the allocator can fail handed
           back a real node number rather than zero - the head of the free
           chain, which it had not removed from that chain.  Checking the
           answer at each of the seven places that ask for a node could not
           have caught that, because the answer looked valid.  The allocator
           now reports failure the same way on all three paths.

           A healthy disk never reached any of this, and nothing about normal
           indexing changes.  If you have an index that has been behaving oddly
           after a disk problem, rebuild it.

08 Sep 26  WEOFSEQ AND OPENSEQ ... OVERWRITE NO LONGER REPORT SUCCESS WHEN THEY
           FAILED TO SHORTEN THE FILE.

           Both of these do their work by truncating a file, and neither looked
           at whether the truncate had worked.

           WEOFSEQ put 0 - success - on the stack whatever happened, so a
           program had no way to find out.  What this costs is a stale tail:
           the old content past the new end survives, so a file written by a
           later and shorter run can still carry rows from an earlier, longer
           one.  A file produced that way is the answer somebody takes away,
           which is why this was worth fixing even though nobody has reported
           it.

           OPENSEQ ... OVERWRITE promises the previous content is gone.  If the
           truncate failed the old file stayed as it was, and later writes
           replaced only the front of it.

           Both now report the failure the way the rest of these statements
           already do: STATUS() gives the I/O error, an ON ERROR clause runs,
           and a statement written without one stops rather than carrying on.
           Nothing that used to work behaves differently - the change is only
           visible when the truncate actually fails, which needs a read-only
           file, a mandatory lock or a full disk.

08 Sep 26  ERROR MESSAGES ARE NO LONGER CUT OFF AFTER THE FIRST LINE.

           Anything SD reported as a program error was truncated at about 84
           characters, so a message written as three lines lost the last two -
           usually the part saying what to do about it.

           The room was always there: the buffer holds 241 characters and the
           limit had been set to 84 by a typing slip, a + where a * was meant.
           Long messages now print in full.

08 Sep 26  "sd -stop" NO LONGER RISKS KILLING THE PROGRAM THAT RAN IT.

           Stopping SD signals every session in its user table.  It did that
           without checking that the recorded process id was real, and a stale
           entry can carry a process id of 0 - which on Linux does not mean
           "nobody", it means EVERY PROCESS IN MY PROCESS GROUP.

           So "sd -stop" run from a script or a terminal could terminate the
           script, the shell, or anything else running alongside it, and say
           nothing about having done so.  SD now signals only a real process id.

           WHETHER YOU EVER HIT THIS depends on whether a session ever died in a
           way that left its entry behind with no process id.  Nothing about a
           normal stop changes.

08 Sep 26  "sd -cleanup" NOW RELEASES A DEAD SESSION'S TASK LOCKS.

           It already gave back the file, record and group locks a lost session
           was holding, but never its task locks - so a LOCK taken by a program
           that was then killed stayed taken, and no recovery an administrator
           had would shift it.  A nightly job guarded by LOCK 3 would not run
           again until SD was restarted.

           IF YOU HAVE BEEN RESTARTING SD TO CLEAR A STUCK TASK LOCK, you should
           not need to any more.  It was comparing the lock against the wrong
           session's number - the one running the cleanup rather than the one
           being cleaned up.

08 Sep 26  GLOBAL CATALOGUING NOW REQUIRES ADMINISTRATOR RIGHTS WHICHEVER WAY
           YOU ASK FOR IT, AND DELETE.CATALOG REQUIRES THEM AT ALL.

           CATALOG already required them for the spelled-out form,
           "CATALOG BP MYPROG GLOBAL".  It did NOT require them when the same
           thing was asked for by putting a *, !, _ or $ in front of the name -
           "CATALOG BP $MYPROG" - which is the form most people use.

           DELETE.CATALOG required nothing at all, by either route.

           THIS MATTERS BECAUSE THE GLOBAL CATALOGUE HOLDS THE PROGRAMS SD RUNS
           FOR EVERYBODY, $LOGIN among them.  Replacing one ran your code in
           every session on the machine, administrators included; deleting one
           stopped everybody signing in.

           NOTHING CHANGES FOR LOCAL AND PRIVATE CATALOGUING, which is what
           programmers use day to day:

               CATALOG BP MYPROG          private catalogue, this account
               CATALOG BP MYPROG LOCAL    this account's VOC

           Both still work, in any account you are allowed to enter.  The only
           thing you can no longer do as an ordinary user is catalogue or delete
           a program whose name starts with *, !, _ or $, because those
           characters mean "system-wide".  Name it without one.

08 Sep 26  "term default" NOW RESTORES 120 x 36, NOT 20 x 24.

           It was setting the page to the smallest size SD allows rather than
           the default, which wrapped every standard report.  It now sets the
           documented width and depth - the same size a session starts with when
           nothing else tells it otherwise.

08 Sep 26  SET.SOCKET.MODE CAN NOW TURN KEEP-ALIVE OFF.

           SET.SOCKET.MODE(skt, SKT$INFO.KEEP.ALIVE, 0) returned 1, for success,
           and switched keep-alive ON.  The value you passed was read and then
           discarded.  SOCKET.INFO(skt, SKT$INFO.KEEP.ALIVE) reads the socket
           itself and had been telling the truth all along, so the two disagreed.

           The setting is now whatever you asked for.  If you have code that
           worked around this by not calling it, the workaround is no longer
           needed.

08 Sep 26  ENCRYPT.FIELD HAS BEEN REMOVED.  IT COULD NEVER WORK.

           The verb was in the account template and pointed at a program called
           $CRYPTO which is not part of this distribution, so typing it answered

               Unable to load '$CRYPTO' object code at line 1550 of $CPROC

           which names neither the verb you typed nor anything you could act on.
           "Verb not found" is the honest answer, and that is what you get now.

           Nothing in the documentation referred to the verb and field encryption
           was not available through it before.  THE ENCRYPT KEYWORD IS A
           DIFFERENT THING and is untouched.


